"eventId":"0YoSvhnQYY2tWtmUCGQdm","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"n-P6J_2i6Hhj3bvpMhREg","createdAt":1792361118587,"updatedAt":1792361118587,"version":1},{"worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","factId":"Dmpf652sP3uZayt_2VuBj","characterId":"VYmV5MC0GNPpKG9aYeDrl","eventId":"QTNnUEyj1eh9MHQvCY99h","note":"","id":"ocn1aAl4k8rOyhmDghBfc","createdAt":1792361099147,"updatedAt":1792361099147,"version":1},{"worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","factId":"_7FLPC34giB2lyarzjJBe","characterId":"txMWhAmoQ5MKX1y3i93KU","eventId":"0YoSvhnQYY2tWtmUCGQdm","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"p5IUoPyAPSiqQjn2Pzr3k","createdAt":1792361118594,"updatedAt":1792361118594,"version":1},{"worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","factId":"7hsn4gqvEavnTba87Pk13","characterId":"_x741Dso4igMjm3KHsYLZ","eventId":"0YoSvhnQYY2tWtmUCGQdm","note":"","id":"pxrKT0YskzluowlQjj0OB","createdAt":1792365024830,"updatedAt":1792365024830,"version":1},{"worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","factId":"UeskDgvC49zf6OCHuiLKl","characterId":"5Rhfebe9llBmAD8a7c-7Z","eventId":"X5cj4zTjmev3PwWKCfkEx","note":"","id":"qXPf6z_lHIkj1t8ycNTGO","createdAt":1792362145992,"updatedAt":1792362145992,"version":1},{"worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","factId":"cKlgnfpO8q7Kqy0vDcSu9","characterId":"ovmu-1zRoQUHJcKMDmj3J","eventId":"MsLhDkYbkLalyMtBs3Twg","note":"","id":"r4I1bWyIDwRntpnuoz92U","createdAt":1792361573652,"updatedAt":1792361573652,"version":1},{"worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","factId":"UeskDgvC49zf6OCHuiLKl","characterId":"i-7K4GpRSz6BeCfFM6Xo4","eventId":"X5cj4zTjmev3PwWKCfkEx","note":"","id":"rDzmc0jkWborimIiHtO-6","createdAt":1792362145956,"updatedAt":1792362145956,"version":1},{"worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","factId":"1A9LiuphoOBJ5D9v0DnRG","characterId":"i-7K4GpRSz6BeCfFM6Xo4","eventId":"ZfvRJLOr4LOCgCSIL5URI","note":"","id":"tBAim1Hd1zxZH_hdd_bGt","createdAt":1792361207887,"updatedAt":1792361207887,"version":1},{"worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","factId":"_7FLPC34giB2lyarzjJBe","characterId":"VYmV5MC0GNPpKG9aYeDrl","eventId":"FCcx9EVdPWwkA6032mhg0","note":"","id":"uJxDLJLhcYzXniDzZVLQ7","createdAt":1792361757101,"updatedAt":1792361757101,"version":1},{"worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","factId":"Dmpf652sP3uZayt_2VuBj","characterId":"txMWhAmoQ5MKX1y3i93KU","eventId":"lIFNCQBy7DVD0TeJ_LzrI","note":"","id":"vDch5yTXS2cp4OBXbhssc","createdAt":1792361117239,"updatedAt":1792361117239,"version":1},{"worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","factId":"Dmpf652sP3uZayt_2VuBj","characterId":"5Rhfebe9llBmAD8a7c-7Z","eventId":"QTNnUEyj1eh9MHQvCY99h","note":"","id":"wTCbBfMGUbKNv9nqtwT_5","createdAt":1792361099126,"updatedAt":1792361099126,"version":1},{"worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","factId":"evrfDRU1v3AfWwA5mUW91","characterId":"i-7K4GpRSz6BeCfFM6Xo4","eventId":"VvxSkA1OXIEZRFdcspW_i","note":"","id":"x88mfPQvWQPE7ltvXoHmK","createdAt":1792362034690,"updatedAt":1792362034690,"version":1},{"worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","factId":"oiMavdu-0lei4c1BiDq7P","characterId":"VYmV5MC0GNPpKG9aYeDrl","eventId":"MsLhDkYbkLalyMtBs3Twg","note":"","id":"xZ6WZ_lsCtGaWDI9hZY5X","createdAt":1792361570582,"updatedAt":1792361570582,"version":1},{"worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","factId":"oiMavdu-0lei4c1BiDq7P","characterId":"_x741Dso4igMjm3KHsYLZ","eventId":"0YoSvhnQYY2tWtmUCGQdm","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"zCARGpouWQKc4ZImLn16v","createdAt":1792361785258,"updatedAt":1792361785258,"version":1}],"characterGoals":[],"sceneTexts":[{"id":"0096YFpI_8Hi82uq_24tv","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"WfzecJCXcnkhuVLjye4PH","text":"\"A girl,\" Dolfin said. \"How delightful.\"\n\nThe vine on the pergola had put out its first leaves since Matteo's last visit, small and crumpled and very green, like the hands of a newborn. Dolfin was not sitting under it this time; he was walking, slowly, round and round the well-head, with his hands behind his back, and Matteo had to stand in the middle of the gravel and turn to keep facing him, like a horse on a lunge rein.\n\n\"Her niece. Lucia Zen. A lacemaker from Burano. Nineteen.\"\n\n\"Pretty?\"\n\n\"I've never seen her.\"\n\n\"Then you'll have that to look forward to.\" Dolfin completed a circuit. \"And the friar is cutting the seal. Our seal. On the Sensa. Up the Censori.\" He said each thing as though he were laying it on a table, to look at it. \"The boy Sanudo. You've been thorough, Matteo. I'd forgotten how thorough you were. It's a pity about the cards.\"\n\n\"Excellency.\" Matteo had not meant to speak. \"The girl. When you come to the Rialto with the Capi—\"\n\n\"The girl will be with her aunt and her aunt will be selling the Republic's property to a foreigner. What becomes of her depends on what her aunt does next.\" Dolfin stopped walking. \"You're not growing a conscience, I hope. At your age it's like growing teeth; it hurts, and nobody admires the result.\"\n\n\"No, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Good.\" Dolfin resumed his circuit. \"There's one thing more I want. When you have the girl in the Chancery with the book in her pillow, I want you to bring her out by the Censori. Not the Scala d'Oro, not the Giganti, not some other little rat-hole of your own. The Censori, and across the courtyard, and out by the Porta del Frumento to the Molo. I'll have a man in the courtyard to see her go. I don't want her taken. I want her watched, all the way to the Rialto, so that when I come in at the Fleming's door the book is on his table and her hands are still warm from it.\"\n\n\"As you say.\"\n\n\"As I say.\" Dolfin smiled at him across the well-head. \"And Matteo. That debt of yours. Two hundred and twelve, you said? I took the liberty of buying it. From the Greek, and from that other fellow at the Frari. I hold all of it now. It seemed tidier.\"\n\nMatteo stood very still on the gravel. Above him a sparrow came down onto the pergola and looked at the new leaves and decided against them and flew away.\n\n\"That's very kind, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Isn't it?\" said Dolfin. \"I thought so.\"","wordCount":439,"createdAt":1792361270221,"updatedAt":1792364155153},{"id":"09qt-tDfRqj-14-VFJMLJ","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"2ELvnobYYBoagGZKaAtCN","text":"She did not open it in the boat.\n\nShe sat in the bottom of Piero's sandolo all the way across the lagoon with the basket on her knees and her hand flat on the pillow, and she did not lift the pillow, and she did not look under the linen, and she did not let herself think the thing she was thinking, which was that it might not be there. That the girl might have lost her nerve in the Chancery, at the last, and dropped the bobbins and picked them up and walked out with the friar's book still in her basket and the real one still in the press. That she was carrying three days of an old man's forgery across the water like a relic. Piero rowed, and said nothing, and once, off San Michele, where the dead of the city lay behind their red wall, he crossed himself, and she did too.\n\nAt home she barred the water-door and the street door both and carried the basket into the workshop and set it on the marver and lifted the pillow off.\n\nIt was there. Of course it was there. Boards and calf, swollen at the lower edge, and one brass corner missing, the bottom left, exactly as the friar had made it. She opened it at the front and read the first page, in her husband's father's hand, a receipt for a green glass for bottles that nobody had made since she was a girl. She opened it at the back. And then she turned to the end, to the gutter, where Anselmo's false leaf had been sewn in so neatly that she had had to put her nail to the stitches to find them, and there was no leaf. There was a stub of cut paper no wider than a fingernail. There was Bortolo's knife-cut, slightly ragged at the bottom where his hand had slipped, the night he did it, in February, with her holding the candle.\n\nShe put her hand flat on the stub and stood like that for a long time.\n\nShe found the collar when she went to put the basket away. It was at the bottom, under the bobbins, folded into its square of linen exactly where Lucia had been told to keep it, in the work-basket she took everywhere; it had gone into the Palace of the Doges that morning, and up the Censori, and into the Secret Chancery of the Ten, and lain under her husband's book while forty bobbins rolled about the marble, and nobody had looked at it at all. Isotta sat down on the floor of the workshop with the little square of linen in her lap and laughed until she had to wipe her eyes on her sleeve.\n\nThen she wrapped it again in the oilcloth Orsato had brought it away in, which she had kept, and carried it to the great furnace, and lay down on her side on the cold floor, and reached into the ash pit under the grate as far as her arm would go, past the old ash and the grit and the bones of a mouse, to the ledge at the back where Bortolo's father had kept his money in the plague year, and pushed it in. When she stood up she was grey to the shoulder. She washed her arm in the bucket by the marver, and washed her face, and changed into the black wool and the good collar; and she put on, for the first time, the little collar of punto in aria that Lucia had made, with the vine and the pomegranates and the birds and the border of knots like a fence round a garden, and pinned it at her throat with a pin of Murano glass.\n\nThen she went to sell the Fleming nothing.","wordCount":641,"createdAt":1792362131877,"updatedAt":1792364142348},{"id":"0PomK10v08NWkB_g3Y0v2","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"X5cj4zTjmev3PwWKCfkEx","text":"It was half an hour, by the bells. They waited it out in the Fleming's room with the candles burning down, like people waiting for a birth or a death: Vos at the window with his back to them, Dolfin by the door with his hands folded inside his sleeves, the two fanti at either side of it with their swords grounded and their eyes on the gold, and the old man in the only good chair, with his eyes shut, so that Isotta thought he had gone to sleep until he said, without opening them:\n\n\"Lion.\"\n\nMatteo stepped forward. \"Excellency.\"\n\n\"I know you. You took a deposition for me in the matter of the Grimani wills. Nine years ago. You had a good voice. They struck you off.\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"For what?\"\n\n\"For selling a copy of a sealed deposition to the family it concerned, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Did you do it?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nContarini opened his eyes. \"Well. That's something.\" He held out his hand, palm up, without looking at it, as though he expected something to be put into it, as presumably all his life things had been. \"What have you got for me? You've got something. You've been standing there like a man with a stone in his shoe.\"\n\nMatteo looked at Isotta. She took the letter out of her sleeve, where it had been since the night of the glory-hole, and put it into his hand; and he held it out.\n\nIsotta watched Dolfin see it. He was very good. He had been very good all his life. He did not move; he only looked at the letter in the Chancery fold, with its plain red seal lifted cleanly and pressed back, going from Matteo's hand into the hand of the Capo of the Ten, and something went out of him, very quietly, like the air out of a bellows when the boy at the handle stops.\n\nContarini read it. He read it twice, holding it far from his face, the way old men read. Then he looked at Dolfin over the top of it.\n\n\"Your hand, Zuane.\"\n\n\"A forgery, Excellency. That man has been in my pay for four years as an informer. He's a known gambler; he owes money everywhere; he has every reason—\"\n\n\"He has been in your pay for four years,\" said Contarini, \"as an informer. That is very interesting. That was going to be the next thing I asked him.\" He folded the letter. \"And it's your hand. I've had your hand on my table every morning for eleven years. You make your g like a little fish.\"\n\nThen the bells of San Bartolomeo struck the half, and there were feet on the stair, and Orsato came back into the room carrying a book.\n\nIt was wrapped in nothing. He carried it in both hands against his chest, the way he had carried it out of Isotta's house on the day of the sirocco, and he crossed the room to the old man's chair and set it on his knees.\n\n\"From the middle shelf of the press, Excellency,\" he said. \"Sanudo opened it in front of me. It was locked, and the key was on his belt, and he swears the book has not left the Chancery except to be read this morning, in his sight, on the authority of a warrant he could not now find, and put back.\"\n\nContarini looked down at the book on his knees. Boards and calf. Swollen at the lower edge. One brass corner missing, the bottom left.\n\n\"So it was never taken,\" he said, and looked at Dolfin.\n\nAnd Zuane Dolfin, secretary of the Ten for eleven years, who had never once in Isotta's sight done anything he had not decided to do, crossed the room in three strides and took the book off the old man's knees, which nobody in the room had ever seen anyone do, and opened it at the end. Isotta saw his fingers go into the gutter for the stub. She saw them find, instead, a leaf, a whole leaf, sewn in so neatly that you had to put your nail to the stitches to find them; and on it, in the cramped brown hand of Bortolo Ferro, the word CALCEDONIO.\n\n\"There,\" said Dolfin. His voice had gone high and strange. \"There. You see. You see, Excellency? The leaf. The leaf was in it all the time. She put it back. She had it all along and she's put it back, here, it's the receipt, it's the calcedonio, it's what I promised the Fleming—\"\n\nHe heard it. Isotta saw him hear it, a moment after everyone else in the room.\n\nNobody said anything. Over by the window, Pieter Vos put his face in his hands.\n\n\"Take him,\" said Alvise Contarini to the fanti, quite gently. \"And the Fleming. Impound the gold.\" He held out his hand again, palm up, and Orsato took the book out of Dolfin's hands, not roughly, the way he had taken it from the shelf by San Donato, and put it back into the old man's. \"And give me that. It is, after all, the property of the Republic.\"\n\nWhen they had gone, the two fanti and the Fleming and the secretary between them, down the stair into the noise of the fair, the old man sat for a while with the book closed on his knees and looked at Isotta Ferro, and at the collar at her throat, the vine and the pomegranates and the birds.\n\n\"Very pretty lace,\" he said. \"Your niece's?\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Mm.\" He turned the book over in his hands, the false book, Anselmo's three nights' work, with every weight wrong. \"I'm told your son has the gaol fever.\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"That's the spring tides.\" The Capo of the Ten stood up, slowly, in stages, with one hand on Matteo Lion's arm, which Matteo gave him without being asked. \"It comes up through the floor.\"","wordCount":993,"createdAt":1792362131191,"updatedAt":1792364904292},{"id":"63aSJ5uf_55An5iymk4MZ","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"skVwObWMZbfNYo854ju6n","text":"On the third night Lucia carried the last of the weights across the city in her bodice, as her aunt had once carried the leaf, and found the friar's cell lit like a chapel.\n\nThere were candles everywhere. On the sill, on the floor, on the lid of the chest at the foot of the bed, stuck in their own wax on the corner of the board under the window, so many that the little room was warm as a kitchen and smelled of honey and hot tallow and, under that, of the ink, which Fra Anselmo made himself from oak-galls and copperas and something he would not name, and which came out of the pot brown, exactly the brown of her uncle's hand. The friar sat at the board in the middle of it all in his shirt, with his sleeves tied back and his spectacles on the end of his nose and his bad eye shut, writing.\n\nMatteo Lion was sitting on the bed with a pair of scales on his knees.\n\n\"Ah,\" said the friar without looking up. \"The weights. Good. Give them to the notary. He reads your aunt's hand better than I do. I think she writes it badly on purpose.\"\n\nLucia gave them. Matteo unfolded the paper and held it to the nearest candle, and she watched his lips move as he read, and saw him smile, once, at a line near the bottom.\n\n\"Twelve ounces of the zafre,\" he said. \"Twelve. In a batch that size. She means it to come out the colour of a bruise.\"\n\n\"She means it to crack in the lehr,\" said Lucia. \"She said a bruise was too good for them.\"\n\nOn the board in front of the friar lay the false ledger, open. She had not seen it before. She had imagined something rough, a thing made in a hurry, and it was not; it was her uncle's book. Not like it. It. The boards were the boards, swollen at the lower edge, because Fra Anselmo had soaked them in the canal and dried them in the bread-oven at the convent bakery. The calf was rubbed at the spine where a thumb would rub it. One of the brass corners was gone, the bottom left, and where it had been the leather was darker, the way leather is darker where it has been covered for twenty years. She put her hand out to touch it and stopped.\n\n\"Go on,\" said the friar. \"Touch it. If it doesn't fool you it won't fool the Ten.\"\n\nShe touched it. It was her uncle's book. She took her hand away quickly, as if it were hot.\n\n\"The first gathering is done,\" Anselmo said, writing. \"Your great-grandfather's green for bottles, and the cristallo, and the lattimo, all true, every grain, because a man who opens a book at the front reads the first page with care and nothing after it. The last gathering is done. The middle—\" he waved his pen \"—is accounts. Sand bought, ash bought, wages, the price of beech in the winter of the plague. Your aunt kept your uncle's day-books. I have copied two years of them. Nobody alive could read them without falling asleep.\" He blotted a line. \"And this is the last leaf.\"\n\nHe lifted it from under the horn: a single sheet of old rag paper, browned at the edges, with eleven lines of figures in the cramped brown hand, and at the top, underlined twice, CALCEDONIO.\n\n\"Your aunt's weights,\" he said, \"in your uncle's hand, on paper from the back of a missal printed in the year your uncle was born. It wants sewing in. To the stub. Where the knife went.\" He looked at her over the spectacles with his good eye. \"I can cut a seal. I can write any hand in the Republic. I cannot sew to save my soul.\"\n\nSo that was why she had been sent with the weights, and not Piero.\n\nShe sat on the stool he gave up to her and opened her own little case of needles and found the finest, the one she used for the picots on the Grimani's border, and a thread of unbleached linen she waxed with the end of a candle. The stub in the gutter of the false book was there, a sliver of cut paper no wider than a fingernail; the friar had cut it himself, with Bortolo's own knife, which her aunt had given him, so that it would be slightly ragged at the bottom where a hand had slipped. Lucia laid the leaf against it. She made her stitches through the old holes of the gathering, under the stub, so that from the outside they could not be seen at all, and drew the thread so gently that the paper did not pucker, and tied it off inside the fold with a knot so small it was only a thickening of the thread.\n\nWhen she sat back, the two men were watching her.\n\n\"Again,\" said Matteo softly. \"I didn't see where you began.\"\n\n\"That's the point,\" said Lucia.\n\nThey weighed the books after that, the false one on one pan and on the other a block of cut stone Fra Anselmo had brought up from the cloister, which Matteo said was the weight of the real book to within an ounce, because he had held the real book in his hands in the old days and a notary's hands remember weights the way a glassmaker's remember heat. The false book was lighter. The friar tore out a leaf of the accounts and Matteo shook his head; he put a thin sheet of lead under the endpaper, as they used to do in the Chancery to keep the big registers from blowing open, and the pans hung level and trembled and were still.\n\nIt was past midnight. The candles had burned down into puddles. Lucia packed her needles away and stood, and found that Matteo Lion had stood too, and was holding the door for her, which nobody had ever done.\n\n\"I'll walk you to the Fondamente,\" he said. \"The ferry for Burano goes at the first bell. There are men by the Misericordia at this hour who'd be glad of a girl alone.\"\n\nShe went out past him into the dark of the dormitory. Halfway down the corridor she stopped and turned round.\n\n\"Messer Lion. Why did you change?\"\n\nHe was a long time answering. In the light of the friar's door behind him she could see him put his hand in his pocket and turn something over and over, the way she turned the end of a rope, the way she turned a bobbin.\n\n\"Your cousin told me something,\" he said at last. \"In the wells. I'll tell you what it was one day, when it's finished. If it's finished well.\"\n\n\"And if it isn't?\"\n\n\"Then,\" said Matteo Lion, \"you were right not to trust me.\" He held out his arm. \"Come along. You have to sleep. On Thursday you have to drop forty bobbins on a marble floor, and I'm told it's harder than it sounds.\"","wordCount":1192,"createdAt":1792364201678,"updatedAt":1792364203183},{"id":"6FOkhKIVR5DOuoDoBH1Io","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"0YoSvhnQYY2tWtmUCGQdm","text":"\n\nThe sirocco had been blowing for three days, and everything in the house on the Rio dei Vetrai was damp to the touch: the bread, the linen, the ash in the furnace mouth, which no longer glowed. Isotta Ferro had let the fire die on the ninth day after the funeral because there was no one left to tend it but her, and a widow who keeps a furnace lit is a widow who means to work it, and the Guild had already sent a man to remind her what that would cost.\n\nShe was sweeping the cold floor when the boat came.\n\nShe heard it before she saw it: oars, and no singing. Murano boats sang. The ferrymen sang, the sand-barges sang, even the priest's boy sang when he rowed the Host across to the sick. A boat that came up the canal in silence was a boat from the Piazza, and she set the broom against the marver and wiped her hands on her apron and went to the water door to meet it, because there was no point in being found.\n\nThere were four of them. Three wore the grey of the Ten's fanti, swords on short belts. The fourth wore black, and a cap of black felt, and he climbed out onto the step with the care of a man who did not expect to be helped.\n\n\"Madonna Ferro.\"\n\n\"Messer Orsato.\" She knew him. Everyone on the island knew him. He had come for the Barovier boy's tools the winter before, and for old Seguso's moulds, and he would come, one day, for hers. \"You are early for a condolence.\"\n\n\"I am not here for a condolence.\" He did not smile; it was not a face that had been built for it. \"The Most Excellent Council of Ten instructs me to take into its keeping the book of receipts of the late master Bortolo Ferro, glassmaker of this island, which by the statutes of the Art is the property of the Republic upon the death of a master without a sworn heir.\"\n\n\"He has a sworn heir.\"\n\n\"He had a son who did not swear.\" Orsato looked past her into the workshop, at the cold glory-hole and the racked blowpipes and the shelf of clay crucibles cracked from the last firing. \"And the son is not, at present, in a position to swear.\"\n\nShe did not understand that, and she did not let him see that she did not understand it. She stood aside.\n\nThey were not rough. She had expected them to be rough, and it was worse that they were not. One of the fanti lifted the cloth from the cooling-oven as though there might be a child sleeping under it. Another opened the chest of oxides and closed it again without looking, because Orsato had not told him to look. Orsato himself went straight to the shelf above the bench, where the book had sat for twenty-two years between the jar of manganese and the image of San Donato, and took it down with both hands.\n\nIt was a thick book, bound in boards and calf, swollen with damp at the lower corners. Bortolo's father had begun it and Bortolo had filled it, and it held, in a cramped brown hand and a cipher of his own that was no cipher at all to anyone who had stood beside him at the pot, everything the Ferro family knew about making glass clear. The weights of the ash. The washing of the sand from the Ticino. How long to hold the batch at the first heat and how long at the second. The cristallo that had made the house rich and the lattimo, milk-white, that had made it famous. Two hundred and eleven leaves.\n\nOrsato turned them with his thumb, quickly, the way a man counts coin he has already been told is correct. He stopped near the end. He turned back. He looked at the stub of paper sewn into the gathering where the last leaf should have been, a sliver no wider than a fingernail, cut close with a sharp knife.\n\n\"There is a leaf missing.\"\n\n\"There are several.\" Isotta kept her voice tired. That was not difficult. \"He tore them out when a receipt failed. He would not keep a failure in the book. He said it would teach the book bad habits.\"\n\n\"This one was cut.\"\n\n\"Then he cut it.\"\n\nOrsato looked at her for a long moment. Behind him the sirocco shoved at the shutters, and somewhere down the canal a dog was barking at the wind, the high complaining bark of a dog that has been barking for three days.\n\n\"The Council will want to know what was on it,\" he said.\n\n\"The Council may ask him. He is in San Pietro Martire, under the third slab on the left. He was always a good listener in church.\"\n\nOne of the fanti laughed and turned it into a cough. Orsato did not laugh. He wrapped the book in an oilcloth he had brought for the purpose, which told her he had known it would be raining, or sweating, or whatever it was the air did in this wind, and he tucked it under his arm.\n\n\"You will be told,\" he said, \"when the Council wishes to speak to you.\" And then, because he was not a cruel man, only an exact one, he added: \"You should go to the Palace in any case. Before they send for you. Ask at the door of the Pozzi. It is better if a mother asks.\"\n\nHe was in the boat before she had found her voice, and by the time she reached the step the oars were moving, and the boat was going away down the Rio dei Vetrai in silence, past the Seguso works and the Barovier works and the little bridge where the children sat to watch the furnace-glow at night, though there was no glow now, there had been no glow for nine days.\n\nThe Pozzi. The wells. The cells under the Palace where the water came up through the floor at high tide.\n\nShe went back inside and stood in the cold workshop with her hands in her apron. She did not sit down; if she sat down she did not think she would get up. After a while she crossed to the rack of blowpipes and took down the fourth from the left, the oldest, the one with the brass ferrule Bortolo had never let anyone else use, and unscrewed the mouthpiece, and tipped it.\n\nA roll of paper slid out into her palm, tight as a spill for lighting candles, singed brown at one edge where the heat had come too close. She did not unroll it. She knew what was on it. She had watched him write it, and she had corrected the weights while he wrote, and he had let her, which was the nearest thing to an apology for twenty years that she had ever had from him.\n\nCalcedonio. The stone-glass. The streaked and clouded glass that looked like agate, like chalcedony, like something dug out of a mountain and not breathed into shape on a pipe. The Venetians had made it once, a hundred years ago, and lost it. Bortolo had found it again in the last winter of his life. Nobody else knew. Nobody but her.\n\nShe screwed the mouthpiece back on and put the roll inside her bodice, against her skin, where it would be warm, and went to find her cloak.","wordCount":1263,"createdAt":1792360485284,"updatedAt":1792360669007},{"id":"76y2hdupbDb1vVrOTDMrY","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"Hq8_8_xvRODxArlMBYfhb","text":"Outside it was evening, and warm, the first warm evening of the year, with the first mosquitoes coming up off the canals like smoke. The bridge was crowded with people going home and people going out, and the shutters of the shops along the Ruga were going up with a sound like a long roll of drums. Her aunt walked fast, and did not speak, and Lucia had almost to run to keep beside her.\n\nAt the top of the bridge her aunt stopped. Below them the Grand Canal was full of boats and the light was going pink on the water, and a man on a barge was singing something about a girl from Chioggia with a voice so bad it was almost beautiful.\n\n\"Well?\" said her aunt.\n\n\"He knew about the leaf.\"\n\n\"Nane told him.\"\n\n\"Nane told him there was a last thing. He didn't tell him what. Zia, he wasn't surprised. When you said you could get the book out of the Ten's own house, he wasn't surprised. He was pleased, he was greedy, but he wasn't surprised. People are surprised when you say a thing like that.\" Lucia gripped the warm stone of the parapet. \"And he turned the letter over. On the desk. When we came in.\"\n\n\"Merchants turn their letters over. They're all thieves; they think everyone else is.\"\n\n\"And he said the Sensa,\" Lucia said. \"You said the day after the Feast. You didn't say which Feast. There are three Feasts in May. And he said the Zeeridder sails the morning after the Sensa, as you wish.\"\n\nHer aunt was quiet. On the barge the man had come to the part of the song where the girl from Chioggia drowned herself, and was making the most of it.\n\n\"Everybody in Venice knows the Sensa is the only Feast that matters,\" Isotta said at last. \"He's been here a year. He'd have learned that much.\"\n\n\"Zia—\"\n\n\"Lucia.\" Her aunt turned to her, and put both hands on her shoulders, and Lucia saw in the pink light how tired she was, how the skin under her eyes had gone thin and dark like the skin of a bruised plum. \"I don't trust him. I don't trust Matteo Lion and I don't entirely trust Fra Anselmo, God forgive me, and the man I trust least in the world is Zuane Dolfin, who is the only one of them who has never lied to me. I don't have to trust them. I have to use them. That's all.\" She let go. \"And he's not getting the calcedonio. Whatever happens. He's getting two hundred and eleven leaves and a widow who keeps house and has a very bad memory for weights.\"\n\n\"And Nane?\"\n\n\"Nane will have a bench in Antwerp and hate it, and in five years he'll come home and the Ten will have forgotten him, and he'll make calcedonio on Murano, out of your collar, and be famous.\" Her aunt took her arm. \"Come on. Piero won't wait after the Ave Maria and I'm not rowing you back to Burano in the dark.\"\n\nThey went down off the bridge into the crowd. Lucia looked back once, up at the windows above the spice-merchant's, and there was a candle lit in one of them now; and for a moment a man stood in it, small and neat, looking down at the bridge where they had been. Then he drew the curtain.","wordCount":574,"createdAt":1792361421854,"updatedAt":1792361557506},{"id":"7q-D3WC4hIN0KeiqzlPde","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"VvxSkA1OXIEZRFdcspW_i","text":"They came down the Censori into the courtyard, and Orsato was there.\n\nHe was not pretending to be a guard any more. He was standing by the great bronze well-head in the middle of the courtyard in his black cap, with his hands behind his back, looking at nothing, the way the Capi had looked at nothing as they walked to the boat; and as Lucia came down the last steps with her basket he turned his head and looked at her, and she felt it go right through the pillow and the linen and the boards and the calf, and she thought, he knows. He can see it. He can see the book through the basket the way he saw me through the storeroom door.\n\n\"Don't stop,\" said Matteo pleasantly beside her. \"Smile at him. He's a friend of mine.\"\n\nShe did not smile. She walked. Across the courtyard, past the well-head, past Orsato, who did not move, through the Porta del Frumento, where the sun hit her in the face like a slap and the noise of the Molo came back all at once, trumpets, bells, the roar of the people as the Bucintoro, far out on the water, came about in a great slow curve towards the Lido.\n\n\"He's following,\" said Matteo. \"Good. Don't look.\"\n\nThey went along the Molo, through the crowd, past the column of San Todaro where the man in the grey coat had finished his fritter and was licking his fingers, past the Zecca, to the place at the end of the Molo where the boats were, hundreds of them, gondolas and sandoli and barges and the big painted peote of the guilds, all jostling and rocking at the steps waiting to follow the Doge to the sea. And in among them, very small, a sandolo from Murano with Piero at the oar and a woman in black sitting in the bottom of it, with a basket on her knee.\n\nIt was the same basket. Lucia had made sure of that; she had bought the second from the man by the fish-market at the Rialto on Monday, and rubbed it with ash and lard until it looked as old as hers, and sewn a strip of red cloth on its rim. And the pillow on top of it was the same, or near enough, with a half-made border pinned to it and bobbins hanging down.\n\n\"Zia!\" She waved. It was the most natural thing in the world. A girl from Burano, in the crowd on the Sensa, seeing her aunt in a boat. \"Zia, hold this, I want to go and see the Doge come back—\"\n\nShe went down the wet steps. Her aunt reached up. There was a moment, one, with a gondola bumping on one side and a fat man from the butchers' guild shouting on the other, when both baskets were in the air between them; and then Lucia was climbing back up the steps with a basket on her arm that weighed exactly what hers had weighed when she came out of the Censori, because Fra Anselmo had weighed them on his scales, and her aunt was sitting in the bottom of the boat with a basket on her knee and her hand flat on the pillow, the way she had put her hand flat on the false leaf at dawn.\n\nPiero pushed off. The sandolo slid out between the gondolas, and turned, and went away towards the Arsenal and the open water and Murano, small and black and unremarkable among a thousand boats.\n\nLucia did not watch it go. She turned round on the top step and looked straight across the Molo at Orsato, who had stopped ten paces off by a stall selling glass beads, and was holding one up to the light as though he were thinking of buying it.\n\nHe looked at her. He looked at the basket on her arm. He looked, for a long moment, out at the water where the Murano boat had gone.\n\nThen he put the bead down, and paid for it anyway, and walked away along the Molo towards the Rialto, without hurrying, to tell his master where the girl was going.","wordCount":703,"createdAt":1792361920733,"updatedAt":1792364116981},{"id":"9Sp-583MxzMMz2Y-9nWKW","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"OBzJf-CqpTS80cJKVT4cR","text":"The Servite brothers had their house on the far side of Cannaregio, where the city ran out of grandeur and began to smell of the tanneries, and Fra Anselmo had a cell at the end of the dormitory with a window on the orchard and a lock on the door that the Prior did not have the key to. Matteo had never asked how that had been arranged. In eight years he had learned that the friar's arrangements were like his seals: if you looked at them closely enough, you could see they were perfect, and that was how you knew they were false.\n\n\"Sit, sit,\" the friar said. \"On the bed. The stool is for the work.\"\n\nThe work was laid out on a board under the window: a block of pear-wood the size of a walnut, a row of little gravers on a strip of leather, a dish of lampblack, a candle stub, and, pinned flat under a sheet of horn so that nothing could breathe on it, a letter. Matteo knew the seal at the foot of it before he was close enough to see it properly. Everyone in Venice knew it. The lion in a circle, with the book open under his paw, and round the circle in tiny capitals the words that made men lose their sleep: CONSILIVM DECEM.\n\n\"Where did you get that?\"\n\n\"It came to the convent,\" said Fra Anselmo placidly, \"two years ago, to inform the Prior that a brother of ours was to be questioned about a pamphlet. The brother was questioned. The Prior was distressed. The letter was filed. I am the convent's archivist.\" He sat down at the stool and picked up a graver. \"I am very good at filing.\"\n\nIsotta Ferro had come with Matteo across the city in silence, and now she stood in the corner by the door with her arms folded, and looked at the seal, and then at the friar, and Matteo saw her understand for the first time what she was paying for.\n\n\"It is not enough to have the seal,\" Matteo said, for her benefit. \"A seal alone is nothing. Anyone can carry a seal. What opens a door in the Palace is a warrant: a sheet of the Chancery's own paper, in a Chancery hand, under a Chancery seal, naming the bearer and his business. The guards cannot read, most of them. But they know what a warrant looks like, the way a dog knows what its master's boots smell like.\"\n\n\"And the paper?\"\n\n\"I have the paper,\" said Matteo. \"I kept some. When they struck me off.\" He shrugged. \"Notaries are magpies.\"\n\n\"And the hand?\"\n\n\"That is mine,\" said the friar, and smiled with the side of his face that worked. \"I have the hands of forty-one secretaries of the Chancery in my files. Not all are alive. All of them are very easy.\"\n\nHe bent over the pear-wood. The graver made a sound like a mouse in a wall.\n\n\"Tell me the way,\" Isotta said to Matteo. \"From the beginning. As if I were a fool.\"\n\nSo he told her. He had drawn it the night before, on the back of a will, and he unfolded it now on the bed between them: the Palace, the courtyard, the Scala dei Giganti where the Doge was crowned, and beside it, much humbler, the Scala dei Censori that the clerks used, up three turns to the floor of the Secret Chancery. The guard at the stairhead. The long panelled passage. The doors: the Sala della Bussola on the left, where people waited to be accused, and on the right the door to the Chancery itself, and beyond that, through a smaller door that most people did not know was a door, the little room where the iron chests were kept.\n\n\"On the Sensa,\" he said, \"the Doge leaves the Palace at the third hour of the morning by the Porta della Carta, with the Signoria behind him. They walk to the Molo and go aboard the Bucintoro. Everyone who can be seen with them goes with them, and everyone who cannot be seen with them goes to watch. The Chancery is left with one secretary of the lowest grade and the guards. The secretaries take it in turns. This year it is a boy called Sanudo, who is twenty and has a mistress at San Polo and has been heard to complain about the duty.\"\n\n\"How do you know that?\"\n\n\"Because I asked him.\" Matteo smiled. \"In a malvasia, while I lost to him at primero. He is a very lucky boy.\"\n\n\"And the chest? The three keys?\"\n\n\"There,\" said Fra Anselmo without looking up, \"is where the warrant comes in.\"\n\nThey had worked it out between them the week before, he and Matteo, over the friar's terrible wine. A warrant under the Ten's seal, in the hand of a senior secretary, ordering the officer on duty to deliver to the bearer the book of receipts of the late Bortolo Ferro, to be carried to the Capi of the Ten on the Bucintoro for an urgent question of the Art. Sanudo would not have the three keys. But Sanudo would know that the Ferro book was not in the iron chests at all, because it had been brought in a month ago and not yet entered; it would be in the press of new matters, behind the Chancery door, under one lock, with one key, on Sanudo's own belt. Matteo had seen that press a hundred times when he was a notary. He had seen what went in it and how long it stayed.\n\n\"He'll look at the warrant,\" said Matteo, \"and he'll look at me, and he'll think: either this is real, and if I refuse it I will be explaining myself to the Capi tomorrow; or it is false, and the only person who could have forged it is a man I beat at primero, which is not possible. And then he'll open the press.\"\n\n\"And if he doesn't?\"\n\n\"Then we run,\" Matteo said. \"Very fast.\"\n\nIsotta looked at the map for a long time.\n\n\"Who carries it?\" she said. \"The warrant. Who goes in?\"\n\n\"I do.\" He had expected the question. \"They know my face on the Censori, from the old days. It's an advantage. A struck-off notary running errands for a secretary is a thing that happens. A widow from Murano is not.\"\n\n\"And who carries the book out?\"\n\nHe hesitated. That part he had not decided, because the answer he wanted to give was not one he could give her.\n\n\"Someone who could carry a book,\" said Fra Anselmo, into the pause, \"without anyone thinking it was a book.\" He held up the pear-wood to the light from the window. The lion was there now, half-born, his paw on the open page. \"Your niece, Madonna. The lacemaker. She carries a pillow and a basket everywhere, does she not? Every girl from Burano carries a pillow. Nobody in the world has ever looked inside one.\"","wordCount":1178,"createdAt":1792361270500,"updatedAt":1792361271959},{"id":"CrHX2suBUc27vtqPCn11N","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"lIFNCQBy7DVD0TeJ_LzrI","text":"He said yes, of course. He had known he would say yes from the moment she said Dolfin's name, and he had haggled only so that she would believe him: forty ducats now, which she did not have, and a hundred when the book was in her hands, which she would not have either, unless the Fleming paid. She had given him nine ducats and her mother's coral, and he had taken the coral, and felt it in his pocket all the way home like a stone.\n\nHe did not go home.\n\nHe went the long way, through the rain, by the Merceria and the Frezzeria, by the back of San Moisè where the whores sheltered in the porch and called to him by name, along the fondamenta to a door in a wall with a bell-pull of twisted iron. He did not pull it. He knocked, twice and once, and waited, and the door was opened by a servant who did not look at his face.\n\nZuane Dolfin's garden was small and very old, a square of wet gravel with a well-head in the middle and a pergola of vines along one wall that had not yet come into leaf. Dolfin sat under the pergola in a cloak, with a brazier at his feet and a book on his knee, as though rain were a thing that happened to other people.\n\n\"Matteo. You're wet.\"\n\n\"It's raining, Excellency.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" Dolfin closed his book on one finger. \"Sit, if you like. There's a stool.\"\n\nMatteo did not sit. He never sat, here. It was a superstition: as long as he stood, he could tell himself he was only passing through.\n\n\"The widow Ferro,\" he said, \"came to the malvasia this afternoon with Fra Anselmo of the Servi. She wants her husband's book out of the Secret Chancery. She means to take it on the Sensa while the Signoria is on the water, and sell it to a Fleming at the Rialto, and use the money to buy her son out of the Pozzi and carry him to Antwerp.\"\n\nDolfin smiled. It was the first time Matteo had ever seen him surprised, and he did not like it, because the surprise was so pleased.\n\n\"On the Sensa,\" he said. \"Of course. She is a sensible woman. I told her she was a plain woman and I was wrong; she is a sensible one, which is much rarer.\" He looked into the brazier. \"And the leaf?\"\n\n\"She didn't speak of the leaf. Only that you wanted it and she would not give it.\"\n\n\"She has it, then. She would have said there was no leaf, otherwise. People only refuse to give you things they have.\" Dolfin turned his book over in his hands. \"And you, Matteo. What did you tell her?\"\n\n\"That I'd do it. For forty ducats and a hundred after.\"\n\n\"Good. Do it.\" Dolfin said it as lightly as if he were telling a servant to bring the lamps. \"Help her. Find her a way in. Find the stair, find the hour, get Anselmo to cut whatever seals you need, I'm sure he's longing to. Let her have the book in her hand on the day of the Sensa.\" He opened his own book again, and found his place. \"And then let the Fleming have it in his. And then I will come to the Rialto with the Capi of the Ten and find a Murano widow selling the secrets of the Art to a foreigner, which is treason, with the Republic's own property in her hands, which is theft, with a Servite friar's forged seals, which is sacrilege, and a struck-off notary's help, which is merely sad.\" He looked up. \"Except that the notary will have been working for me all along, and will be thanked, and will owe me nothing any longer. Two hundred ducats, isn't it?\"\n\n\"Two hundred and twelve.\"\n\n\"Then nothing. And the widow, to save her neck, will give me the leaf. She'll give it to me on her knees.\" Dolfin sighed, contentedly, and drew his cloak closer. \"Go home, Matteo. You're dripping on my gravel.\"\n\nMatteo went. In the street he put his hand in his pocket and found the coral there, cold and knobbed like a little branch of bone, and he stood under the dripping eaves for a long time holding it, until a woman in the porch of San Moisè called out to ask if he had lost something, and he said no, and went home.","wordCount":756,"createdAt":1792361084025,"updatedAt":1792361178500},{"id":"GU1DJI2R8La0MCsoi8ncm","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"x4h1FfhvncsMXoADqZCTC","text":"The gold was on the table.\n\nVos had done it properly. He had taken the Turkish carpet off and laid a white cloth instead, and on the white cloth stood four leather bags, tied at the neck and sealed, and beside them a little brass balance with its pans level and a stack of Venetian sequins in a column to show the weight, catching the candlelight like the scales of a fish. It was the most money Isotta had ever seen in one place. It looked like a stage. It looked like the altar of a church in a town that did not believe in God.\n\n\"Madonna.\" Vos bowed. He was in black silk again, with a ruff, as though for a wedding. \"And the lacemaker. And—\" he looked at Matteo, in the doorway behind them, and something went across his face very fast and was gone \"—a notary. You've brought a notary. Very wise. One should always have a contract witnessed.\"\n\n\"Messer Lion is my friend,\" said Isotta. \"He carried my basket.\"\n\nLucia set the basket on the table, by the gold. She did it carefully, as if it were heavy. It was not heavy. Vos's eyes went to it and stayed.\n\n\"May I?\"\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nHe lifted the pillow off, gently, with both hands, the way a man lifts a sleeping cat. He folded back the linen underneath.\n\nThere was lace under it. Nothing but lace. Collars and cuffs and a border for an altar-cloth, a dozen pieces, Lucia's best, in a white drift that rose up a little as the linen came away, like breath.\n\nVos looked at it. He looked for a long time. Then he looked at Isotta, and she saw the merchant go out of his face and something else come into it, something smaller and older and more frightened.\n\n\"Where is the book?\"\n\n\"I couldn't get it,\" Isotta said. \"The press was locked. The clerk wouldn't open it. We tried, Messer Vos. I'm sorry. But I didn't want to come empty-handed, after you'd been so kind, so I've brought you the best lace in Burano at a very good price. For the ladies of Antwerp. They say the ladies of Antwerp have no lace worth the name.\"\n\nHe did not answer. He was listening; she saw him doing it. Listening past her, past the door, down the stair, to the Ruga below, where the evening crowd was loud with the Sensa fair, and where, over the noise, very faint, there was a sound of many feet on stone coming steadily nearer and not hurrying.\n\n\"You stupid woman,\" Pieter Vos said softly. \"What have you done?\"\n\n\"I?\" said Isotta Ferro. \"I've come to sell you lace.\"\n\nThe door opened.\n\nIt opened all the way, so hard it struck the wall, and Zuane Dolfin came into the room in a gown of black damask with a fur at the collar, and behind him two fanti with drawn swords, and behind them, slowly, because he was seventy and the stair was steep and he was not a man who had ever needed to hurry for anyone, an old man in the black of the Ten, with a gold chain on his chest and a face like a shut door. And behind him, last of all, in his black cap, Orsato.\n\n\"Excellency,\" Dolfin said, turning to the old man, and his voice was the pleasant tired voice of the little room at the Palace, a voice for small business between friends. \"Messer Contarini. Here is the widow Ferro of Murano. You see her with the Fleming, Vos, of Antwerp, who has been asking questions about the Art in every malvasia in the city since the spring. You see the gold. You see—\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\nHe had seen the lace.\n\nThere was a silence in the Fleming's room so complete that Isotta heard the candle flame move. Dolfin looked at the lace on the table, the white drift of it rising out of the girl's basket beside four bags of gold, and the pleasant tired face did not change at all, and that was how she knew. A man who has nothing to fear looks surprised. Zuane Dolfin looked at the lace the way a man looks at a letter he did not expect to see again, in a hand he knows is his own.\n\n\"Well, Dolfin?\" said the old man, Contarini. He had one hand cupped behind his ear. \"What do I see? I see lace.\"\n\n\"She has hidden it,\" said Dolfin. \"Excellency, the book was taken from the Chancery this morning while the Signoria was on the water. This girl carried it out. My man saw her. Orsato—\"\n\n\"I saw the girl,\" said Orsato, from the doorway, \"come down the Censori with a basket.\"\n\n\"There. You see. She has hidden it, she has given it to someone, the boat—Excellency, send to the Chancery. Send to the press. It will be empty.\"\n\nContarini looked at him for a moment out of his shut-door face. Then he turned his head, and said to Orsato, without raising his voice, \"Go to the Chancery. Wake the boy Sanudo if he's asleep, and have him open the press in front of you, and bring me whatever is on the middle shelf. Run.\"\n\nOrsato lifted his cap. He did not run. He was not, Isotta thought, a man who had ever run in his life. But he went.","wordCount":905,"createdAt":1792362131527,"updatedAt":1792362134544},{"id":"HRbT48mM0wsS6cxm9n2of","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"MsLhDkYbkLalyMtBs3Twg","text":"She did not go to her aunt.\n\nShe thought about it the whole way from the Rialto. She thought about it crossing the Strada Nova, and on the bridge at the Misericordia where the boys were fishing for eels with bent pins, and she knew exactly what would happen. Her aunt would listen with her hands folded. Her aunt would say thank you, Lucia. And then her aunt would say: it's over, we stop. And she would go to Dolfin and give him what he wanted, because there would be nothing else left to do, and Nane would come home, and the calcedonio would go into the Guild's chest and Zio Bortolo would be nobody, and her aunt would be nobody, and every time her aunt looked at a piece of glass for the rest of her life she would think: I gave it to them.\n\nOr else she would not stop. She would say: we go on, and we are cleverer. And she would ask Lucia to go into the Palace anyway, knowing.\n\nLucia did not know which was worse. So she went to the Servi.\n\nFra Anselmo was in the orchard behind the dormitory, in his shirt-sleeves, with a hoe, among rows of something green and feathery that turned out to be fennel. He did not seem surprised to see her. He did not seem anything, at first; he looked at her with his one good eye and went on hoeing until she had finished, and then he leaned on the hoe and looked at the fennel for a while.\n\n\"Orsato,\" he said. \"Of all people. He has a sister at the Celestia. He brings her oranges.\" He shook his head. \"Everyone is someone's brother.\"\n\n\"What do we do?\"\n\n\"We?\" He smiled with the working side of his face. \"You've come to me instead of your aunt. I take that as a great compliment, and also as a sign that you're frightened of what she'll do, which is sensible of you. She is a very brave woman. Brave people are the most dangerous ones to tell things to.\"\n\n\"Will you tell her?\"\n\n\"Yes. Not today.\" He set the hoe against the wall. \"Sit down, child. On the well. No, it won't fall in, it's been here since the Lombards.\" She sat. He sat beside her, slowly, the way old men sit, in stages. \"Tell me again who knows the day. The Sensa. Who knows we go on the Sensa?\"\n\nLucia counted on her fingers. \"Zia. You. Me. And the notary. Messer Lion.\"\n\n\"And who knows the girl from Burano will be the one to carry it?\"\n\n\"The same.\"\n\n\"Not Nane? Not your mother?\"\n\n\"Nobody else. Zia made me swear.\"\n\n\"Four, then.\" The friar looked at the orchard wall, where a fig tree had pushed its way out between the bricks and was hanging there in the sun as though it had been crucified. \"And the Fleming knew the day before your aunt told him. And Orsato and the Fleming were talking about the Capi coming in at the door, which means Dolfin knows not only the day but the end of the plan as well, which your aunt has told to nobody except the four of us.\" He was quiet a moment. \"So it is you, or your aunt, or the notary, or me.\"\n\n\"It isn't me.\"\n\n\"No. I don't think it is.\"\n\n\"And it isn't Zia.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nLucia looked at him. He was looking at the fig tree. The sun was on the milky side of his face and it made the bad eye look like a pearl.\n\n\"It might be you,\" she said.\n\n\"It might.\" He nodded, as if she had said something very sensible about fennel. \"You have only my word that it is not. That is the trouble with all of this. In the end, you have only someone's word.\" He stood up, in stages, and picked up the hoe. \"I have known Matteo Lion eight years. I like him very much. He is the best notary I ever met and the worst card player. And in eight years, child, I have never once asked him how a man who loses every Sunday at the Greek's goes on paying his rent.\" He began to hoe again. \"Go home. Say nothing. Make your aunt a collar. I'll come to Murano before the Feast.\"","wordCount":728,"createdAt":1792361557204,"updatedAt":1792361634470},{"id":"NCO3dXQHDoaDhtcWmUO8T","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"vEWRhlmys299MoaSvZJfB","text":"She had left her pattern-book under the Fleming's chair.\n\nShe did not know, afterwards, whether she had done it on purpose. She had taken it out of her basket when they sat down, because she always did, because her hands did not know what to do with themselves unless they were holding thread or something that told them about thread, and she had put it under the chair when Vos poured the wine, and she had not picked it up again when her aunt stood. She remembered seeing it there, a little red book on the Turkish carpet, as they went out of the door. She remembered thinking: I'll go back for it. And then thinking: no, don't. And then not thinking about it at all for three days, which was not like her.\n\nOn Sunday her mother went to Mass at San Martino and then to her sister's at Mazzorbo, and Lucia said she had a headache, and when the boat had gone she took the ferry to the Fondamente Nove and walked. She walked the whole way across the city to the Rialto, through Santi Apostoli and the Strada Nova and over the little bridges that went up and down like the stitches in a border, and when she came to the foot of the spice-merchant's stair she stood a long time sneezing in the pepper and telling herself she had come for her book.\n\nThe stair was dark. The shop below was shut for the Sunday. At the top the door of the Fleming's rooms was not closed: it stood open the width of a hand, and through the gap came voices, and Lucia, who had raised her knuckle to knock the way her aunt knocked, held it there in the air and did not.\n\n\"—real gold,\" said a voice she did not know. Then she did know it. She had heard it once, through the water-door of her aunt's house, on the day the men from the Ten came: flat, exact, unhurried. \"His Excellency is particular. It must be real gold and it must be on the table. In bags. Counted. When the Capi come through that door they must see a merchant buying and a widow selling, with the Republic's book between them. Nothing that a clever advocate could call a misunderstanding.\"\n\n\"I'm not a fool, Orsato.\" That was Vos. His good Venetian had gone thick and careful, the way people speak a language when they are angry in it. \"You may tell your master the gold will be there. It is my gold. I should like to know it will be returned.\"\n\n\"It will be impounded. And returned to you, with the thanks of the Signoria, for having assisted the Republic in the matter of a theft of its property. You will sail on the Zeeridder as you planned, a day or two later. Nobody will touch your cargo.\"\n\n\"And the widow?\"\n\n\"The widow will be taken with the book in her hands. What becomes of her is for the Ten.\"\n\nA pause. Lucia heard wine poured, and then not drunk.\n\n\"And the receipt,\" Vos said. \"The last leaf. His Excellency was quite clear with me, Orsato. The book I can buy in any case; the Guild will sell me a copy of it in a year, I know how these things go. What I came to Venice for is the leaf. His Excellency promised me a fair copy of the leaf, in his own hand, when the widow gives it up.\"\n\n\"His Excellency keeps his promises,\" said Orsato.\n\n\"I'm sure he does. I'm sure he keeps them very exactly.\"\n\nLucia's arm was aching from holding her hand in the air. She lowered it, slowly, and as she did so a board shifted under her foot, not loudly, a small dry sound like a knuckle cracking.\n\nThe voices stopped.\n\nShe did not think. Her body did it for her. She went down the stair not like a person but like water, three steps and three steps and the turn, her skirts gathered in both fists so they should not whisper on the treads, and at the bottom the door of the spice-merchant's storeroom was beside her, not the street door, and she went through it into a dark that was all pepper and cinnamon and the sour smell of sacking, and pulled it to behind her, and crouched against a sack of something hard and round, and did not breathe.\n\nFeet on the stair. Slow ones. They came down to the turn, and stopped.\n\nThrough the crack of the storeroom door she could see a slice of the stairwell: grey light, the dusty bottom step, and then a black shoe, and the hem of a black gown, and a hand on the rail with a ring on it, a plain gold ring, the hand of a man who had carried her uncle's book out of her aunt's house wrapped in oilcloth.\n\nOrsato stood at the bottom of the stair for a long time.\n\nThen he sneezed.\n\nIt was such an ordinary sound. He sneezed, and said something under his breath in the dialect of Chioggia that Lucia's father used when he hit his thumb, and wiped his nose, and went out through the street door into the Sunday afternoon, and the door banged behind him in the draught.\n\nShe stayed among the sacks until the bells rang None. When she came out the stair was empty and the Fleming's door, at the top, was shut. She did not go up for her pattern-book. She never saw it again.","wordCount":934,"createdAt":1792361557458,"updatedAt":1792361558900},{"id":"U69hb9ilmAuNUBo6dfT0J","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"Lli335bACODw0CxTUpeik","text":"They let him out on the second of June, a Thursday, in the morning, without any ceremony at all.\n\nThere was no sentence. There had been none on the Sensa either, Matteo told her afterwards; the Council had sat as it always sat after the Doge came back from the Lido, and the matter of the three subjects of the Republic taken trying to carry the Art abroad had been deferred, on the motion of the Capo Contarini, to a later sitting, on the grounds that one of the secretaries who had prepared the depositions had become unavailable. Unavailable was the word. Zuane Dolfin was unavailable in a room at the top of the Palace, under the leads, where the sun came through the roof in summer and turned the air to soup, and where, Matteo said, he had been visited every day by a different member of the Ten and had told each of them something slightly different. Pieter Vos had been put on the Zeeridder at Malamocco without his gold and without his cargo and told that if he were seen in the lagoon again he would be hanged between the columns. And at the later sitting, which nobody outside the room ever heard about, it had been decided that a boy of seventeen with the gaol fever, whose father had been a master of the Art and whose mother had that week rendered the Republic a service it preferred not to describe, might be returned to his family on Murano on the understanding that he would never again try to leave it, and that his mother would keep the Ferro furnace lit.\n\n\"Keep it lit,\" Isotta said. \"Those were the words?\"\n\n\"Those were the words. In the minute.\" Matteo smiled. \"I wrote the minute.\"\n\nHe had a new gown, and ink on his fingers that was new ink, and he went to the Palace every morning at the second hour and came home at dusk to a room in the Calle dei Fabbri that he did not have to lie to anyone about. The Capo Contarini had needed, he had said, a notary in the Avogaria who knew precisely how secretaries of the Ten were corrupted, and could not think of a better one. It was on probation. Matteo said everything in Venice was on probation; it was only that some people were told.\n\nOrsato brought the boy.\n\nHe came up the Rio dei Vetrai in a boat without singing, as he had come the first time, with two fanti at the oars, and in the bottom of the boat, wrapped in the same old woman's blanket, was Nane. He was thin. He was yellow under the eyes, and his hair had grown down over his collar, and when the boat touched the step and he stood up he had to put his hand on Orsato's shoulder to do it, and Orsato let him.\n\nIsotta did not go down the step. She found that she could not. She stood in the water-door with her hands in her apron, and behind her in the workshop Lucia and the friar and the notary stood too, not speaking, and her son came up the three wet steps one at a time and stopped in front of her.\n\n\"Mamma,\" he said. \"I kept the blanket dry.\"\n\nShe put her arms round him. He smelt of the Pozzi, of the sea and what the sea left behind. She did not care. Over his shoulder she saw Orsato, standing in the boat with his black cap in his hand, looking up at the house; and she remembered the day of the sirocco, and the cap lifted once on the Ponte della Paglia, and how long she had wondered whose side he was on.\n\n\"Messer Orsato,\" she said. \"Will you come in? There's wine.\"\n\n\"Thank you, Madonna. No.\" He put his cap on. \"I'm on duty.\" He looked at the boy, and at her, and something happened in his face that was not quite a smile, because it was not a face that had been built for it. \"It was a waste of good hands,\" he said. \"I told you. Good day.\"\n\nThe boat went away down the Rio dei Vetrai, past the Seguso works and the Barovier works and the little bridge where the children sat at night to watch the glow. And Nane put his hand into his shirt and brought out something small and held it out to her on his palm.\n\nA bead. White, round as a pea, with a single blue thread wound once about it.\n\n\"You sent it back to me,\" he said. \"With Messer Lion, on the Sensa night, when he came down to the wells with the Capo. He said it meant you were coming, and that I was to give it to you myself, at the door, because it was mine to give and not his.\" He looked past her at Matteo, and then at her again, and he was seven, holding out his burnt hand. \"Is it round enough?\"\n\nShe took it. She closed her hand on it, as she had at the marver with the glory-hole roaring.\n\n\"It'll do,\" she said.\n\nWhen the boy had gone in to the kitchen, where Lucia had bread and soup and a clean shirt waiting and was already scolding him for the state of his hair, Matteo Lion came and stood by her in the water-door, and put his hand in his pocket, and brought out the coral.\n\nIt lay on his palm in the June sun, the little red branch her mother had worn at her wedding, and her grandmother at hers.\n\n\"I owe you this,\" he said. \"I never earned it. You paid me nine ducats and a necklace to steal a book and I sold you to Zuane Dolfin the same afternoon.\"\n\n\"And then you stole it.\"\n\n\"The girl stole it. I copied twenty lines about lattimo in my best hand.\" He held it out. \"Take it back, Madonna. Please. I've been carrying it about for six weeks like a stone in my shoe, and I'd like to walk properly again.\"\n\nShe looked at the coral, and at his hand, the notary's hand with the new ink in the creases, and she thought of him standing at the marver with the glory-hole roaring and the heat on his face, thinking, actually thinking, before he answered her.\n\n\"Keep it,\" she said. \"It was your fee. You didn't take the forty ducats, and you'll never see the hundred, so keep the coral. Give it to a wife one day, if any woman in Venice is fool enough to marry a gambler.\"\n\n\"I've stopped gambling.\"\n\n\"Since when?\"\n\nHe considered. \"Since about the third of May,\" said Matteo Lion. \"When the odds became too good to be interesting.\"\n\nShe laughed. It surprised them both. It came up out of her like something out of the ash pit, grey and unexpected and whole, and it went on rather longer than the joke deserved, and in the kitchen behind them her son put his head round the door to see what was the matter with his mother.","wordCount":1197,"createdAt":1792362332522,"updatedAt":1792365094212},{"id":"Va5vg_eZGALBFYCCeUVrm","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"IVuihn9m2e4zyRuOA30jT","text":"The friar came on the Monday before the Feast, with Lucia, in the middle of the afternoon, when the light lay in long gold bars across the cold floor of the workshop and the dust stood up in them like smoke.\n\nIsotta was cleaning the crucibles. She had begun it on Saturday because she could not sit still, and it was work that wanted nothing from her head: a scraper, a bucket of water, a brush, and twenty pots crusted with the glass of the last firing, Bortolo's last firing, a green-gold skin of it that came away in flakes. She had done eleven. When she heard the boat she did not get up. When she saw Lucia's face in the water-door, she put down the scraper.\n\n\"Tell me,\" she said.\n\nThey told her. Lucia told the stair and the open door and Orsato's voice and the Fleming asking about the leaf. She told the storeroom and the pepper and the black shoe on the bottom step, and the ring. She told it well, Isotta thought; in order, without decoration, the way Matteo Lion would have liked a witness to tell it. Then the friar told the rest, sitting on the bench by the marver with his hands on his knees: the four people who knew the day; the fig tree; eight years and the rent.\n\nIsotta listened to all of it with her hands folded on her apron. When they had finished she stood up and went to the rack of blowpipes and took down the fourth from the left, the oldest, and stood holding it, as though it were the only thing in the room that weighed what it should.\n\n\"When did you know?\" she said to Lucia.\n\n\"Sunday. A week.\"\n\n\"A week.\"\n\n\"Zia, I was afraid you'd stop. I was afraid you'd go to Dolfin. Fra Anselmo said—\"\n\n\"I know what Fra Anselmo said. Fra Anselmo was right.\" Isotta turned the pipe in her hands. \"I would have stopped. On Sunday I would have gone to Dolfin in my good black with my mother's coral and given him your collar and thanked him for his kindness.\" She looked at the pipe. \"I haven't got my mother's coral. I gave it to the notary.\"\n\n\"The notary,\" said Fra Anselmo, \"is coming tonight.\"\n\n\"Yes. I asked him to come. To go over the way once more, he said.\" She set the pipe back in the rack, carefully, the fourth from the left. \"Brother. Can you copy a book?\"\n\nThe friar raised his eyebrows. The good one and the bad one went up together, and for a moment he looked like a startled owl.\n\n\"That depends very much on the book.\"\n\n\"Boards. Calf. Brass corners, one missing, the bottom left. Swollen at the lower edge from the damp, the way all our books swell. Two hundred and eleven leaves of rag paper, foxed. My husband's hand, which I can give you in forty letters he wrote me when he was in Florence and I was not, and which I have kept, God knows why, because they are mostly about money.\" She looked at him. \"Not all two hundred and eleven. Nobody could do that in three days. The first gathering and the last, so that a man who opened it at the front or at the back would see what he expected to see. The middle you can fill with anything. Old accounts. Prayers. Recipes for jam.\"\n\n\"And the last leaf? The one he's waiting for?\"\n\n\"Put one in,\" said Isotta Ferro. \"Sewn into the stub, where the cut one was. Calcedonio. In my husband's hand. With every weight wrong.\"\n\nLucia made a small sound. The friar did not; he only looked at her for a long time with his one good eye, and then, slowly, with the side of his face that worked, he smiled.\n\n\"Every weight?\"\n\n\"Not so wrong that a glassmaker would laugh. Wrong enough that it would make something the colour of mud and crack in the lehr. I'll write it out for you.\" She almost smiled herself. \"I know exactly how to make calcedonio go wrong. We did it forty times in February.\"","wordCount":698,"createdAt":1792361756157,"updatedAt":1792361758522},{"id":"VhrYi84l6s92nX2f4zwKg","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"rVNeaCiG5wuO3GSDlW_cq","text":"The great furnace took three days to come up to heat.\n\nThey lit it on the Sunday after Nane came home, with the whole of Murano watching from the bridge, it seemed, and from the windows of the Seguso works and the Barovier works opposite, where the masters stood at their own doors with their arms folded and pretended to be looking at the weather. The Guild sent a man. He stood in the yard with his hat in his hands and looked at the smoke coming out of the Ferro chimney, and Isotta went out to him and said good morning, Messer Briati, and showed him a paper with the lion of the Ten on it that said in a notary's handsome hand that the widow Isotta Ferro was licensed to work the furnace of her late husband until her son should come to his mastership, and Briati read it twice and put his hat on and went away.\n\nBy the Wednesday the pots were glowing white in the furnace mouth, and the batch was in, and the whole house ticked and sang with the heat the way it had when she was a bride and could not sleep for the noise of it. Lucia had come over from Burano with her pillow. Nane had slept for most of three days and eaten for the rest of them and was a better colour; he stood now at the bench in his shirt with a pipe in his hands, his father's pipe, the fourth from the left, with the brass ferrule, turning it, not yet putting it in, the way his father had stood, and the way she had.\n\nThe book was on the shelf. It had come out of the ash pit grey and smelling of old fire, and she had cleaned it with a dry brush, leaf by leaf, two hundred and ten of them, and put it back where it had sat for twenty-two years, between the jar of manganese and the image of San Donato. Nobody would come for it. As far as the Republic knew, the book of receipts of the late Bortolo Ferro was in a press in the Secret Chancery, where the Capo Contarini had seen it with his own eyes; and if the Guild, in a year or two, made a fair copy of it and gave it to some furnace on the island that had paid the Ten enough, that furnace would make cristallo the colour of a ditch, and lattimo that was grey, and calcedonio that cracked in the lehr. She found that she did not mind that at all.\n\n\"Read it to me,\" she said to Lucia.\n\nLucia unfolded the collar on the marver, away from the heat, and smoothed it flat. The vine and the pomegranates and the birds. The border of knots like a fence round a garden. She put her finger on the first leaf of the vine.\n\n\"Four pounds of ash of Levant,\" she read.\n\nNane looked at the collar. Then at his cousin. Then at his mother, and his mouth opened, and she watched him understand, all at once, the way you understand a piece of glass when it comes out of the lehr whole.\n\n\"That's it?\" he said. \"That's the last leaf? In the lace? All this time?\"\n\n\"All this time.\"\n\n\"Babbo would have hated it.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Isotta. \"He would have said lace is for nuns.\" She took the pipe out of his hands, and put it into the glory-hole to warm, and turned it, slowly, the way you do. \"Six of the sand, Lucia. Washed thrice.\"\n\nLucia read. Isotta weighed. Nane carried. The copper and the silver went in at the hour Lucia read out from the birds, and the furnace roared, and the light came out of the mouth of it white and gold and hurt to look at, and on the shelf San Donato looked down at the three of them with his painted saint's patience, and outside on the bridge the children sat in a row to watch the glow.\n\nAt the hour of Vespers she put the pipe into the pot and gathered.\n\nIt came out on the end of the iron like honey, like sun, a glowing gob of nothing-colour that swelled and sagged and that she turned, and turned, and blew into, once, gently, the way you blow on a child's burnt hand. And as it cooled, slowly, on the end of the pipe, in the air of the workshop, the colour came up out of it. Not all at once. In veins. A thread of brown, and then green, and then a blue like the lagoon in winter, running through the clear glass the way rivers run through a map, the way the grain runs through stone, until what she held on the end of her husband's pipe was not glass at all but a piece of agate, a piece of chalcedony, a thing dug out of a mountain and not breathed into shape on a pipe in a widow's workshop on Murano on a Wednesday in June.\n\n\"Not to stir,\" said Lucia softly, with her finger on the border of knots.\n\n\"No,\" said Isotta Ferro. \"Not to stir.\"\n\nShe held it up to the window, so they could all see the light come through.","wordCount":891,"createdAt":1792362332174,"updatedAt":1792362334993},{"id":"YYEseLrnxDDqpXw3BG6NO","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"FCcx9EVdPWwkA6032mhg0","text":"She lit the furnace for him.\n\nNot the great furnace; there was not wood enough on the island for that, and no reason. The small one, the glory-hole by the bench, where Bortolo had warmed the pieces between gathers; she lit it at dusk with the kindling and then the beech, and fed it until the mouth of it stood open on a round of white-orange light that hurt to look at, and the workshop, which had been cold for five weeks, began to tick and creak around her as the walls remembered what they were for.\n\nWhen Matteo Lion came up the step from his boat she was standing at the bench with a blowpipe in the glory-hole, turning it slowly, as though there were glass on the end of it. There was no glass. There was only the iron, going red, then orange.\n\n\"Madonna.\" He stopped in the water-door. The heat met him there; she saw it in his face. \"You've lit the fire.\"\n\n\"Come in, Messer Lion. Shut the door. The draught's bad for the glass.\"\n\nHe shut it. He came across the floor, and stopped at the end of the bench, where the light from the hole lay over him and made him look younger, or else older, it was hard to tell. He had a roll of paper under his arm. The way, she supposed. He laid it on the marver.\n\n\"The friar's not here?\" he said.\n\n\"The friar's in the lehr-room with my niece. They're listening.\" She turned the pipe. \"I wanted them to hear what you say. In case I have to tell it afterwards to someone, and they ask for witnesses.\"\n\nHe did not move. She watched him not move; she watched him understand, and add it up, and arrive at the figure. It did not take him long. He had always been quick.\n\n\"Orsato,\" he said.\n\n\"Orsato. At the Fleming's. On Sunday last. Talking about gold on a table and the Capi at the door, and a widow taken with the book in her hands.\" She drew the pipe out of the hole and held it up between them. The last hand's length of it was the colour of an apricot. \"Four people knew the day, Messer Lion. I didn't tell Dolfin. Lucia didn't tell Dolfin. Fra Anselmo didn't tell Dolfin, though he's the only one of you I can't prove it of.\"\n\n\"It wasn't the friar.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe pipe hissed faintly in the air between them. Neither of them looked at it.\n\n\"Four years,\" he said. \"Since they struck me off. He came to me the week after, at the Greek's, and bought me a drink, and paid my rent, and asked me to tell him now and then what I heard. In the malvasie. In the courts. Who was in debt, who was in love, who was leaving the city. I told myself it was nothing. I told myself I was a notary still, taking depositions. That's what I told myself.\" He looked at the glowing iron. \"I'd tell you I'm sorry, but you wouldn't believe me, and you'd be right.\"\n\n\"I don't want you to be sorry. I want to know what you're going to do.\"\n\nHe put his hand in his pocket, then, and she lifted the pipe an inch, and he stopped, and slowly drew the hand out and opened it on the marver, in the light.\n\nThere were three things on his palm. Her mother's coral, the little red branch. A letter, folded very small, in a way she had seen once, on Dolfin's table, with its seal lifted cleanly and pressed back. And a bead.\n\nShe knew the bead before she knew she knew it. White, lattimo, round as a pea, with a single thread of blue wound once about it. She had sent back eleven of them, the spring he was fourteen, because they were not round, or the blue had run, or the white was not white enough. The twelfth she had kept. Or she had thought she had. It was on the shelf by San Donato.\n\nNo. It was not. It was here.\n\n\"He said to tell you he's keeping the blanket dry,\" Matteo Lion said. \"He has the gaol fever. Not the worst kind, I think; I've seen the worst kind. And Dolfin told me on Thursday, at the foot of the Scala d'Oro, that when you've been taken at the Fleming's and given him the leaf, the Council will send your son to the galleys regardless, and Dolfin will speak very movingly for clemency, and lose.\" He pushed the letter towards her with one finger. \"That's to the Fleming. In Dolfin's own hand. He gave it me to carry and I didn't carry it. It promises Vos the receipt, a fair copy, when you've given it up. If the Capi saw it, they'd know what he is. A secretary of the Ten selling the secrets of the Art to Antwerp for gold.\"\n\nIsotta put the pipe back in the glory-hole. Her hand was not steady and she did not want him to see it.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\nHe thought about it. He actually thought, standing there with the heat on his face; she watched him do it, and somehow that was what she believed, in the end, more than anything he said.\n\n\"Because your son told me he'd kill me with the hot end of a pipe if I got you killed,\" he said. \"And I believed him. And I found, when I thought about it, that I'd very much rather he didn't have to.\"\n\nFrom the lehr-room door, behind her, there came a small sound, as of an old man trying not to laugh.\n\n\"Come out, Brother,\" said Isotta, without turning round. \"Lucia. Bring the lamp.\" She picked up the bead and closed her hand on it, so hard she felt the little thread of blue against her palm, and looked at the notary across the marver. \"Sit down, Messer Lion. You can sit down here. We're going to steal my husband's book on Thursday exactly as you planned it. Up the Censori, with the friar's warrant, and out in my niece's pillow. And when the boy Sanudo shuts the press behind you, there will still be a book in it.\"","wordCount":1053,"createdAt":1792361755844,"updatedAt":1792364201582},{"id":"ZcZKH9xjaza2xFfuBwpy4","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"QTNnUEyj1eh9MHQvCY99h","text":"It rained on Sunday the way it only rains in Venice in April, as if the sky had been saving it. The water came down straight and grey into the canals and the canals did not seem to notice, being water already, and only the people minded: the people ran under arches with their cloaks over their heads, and the people crowded into the malvasia behind San Zulian until the windows ran on the inside as well as the out.\n\nMatteo Lion had been there since Mass. He had not gone to Mass. He had gone, at the hour of Mass, to the malvasia, where the Greek kept a back room with a table and a lamp and a pack of Neapolitan cards that had been shuffled so often the court cards were faceless, and where Matteo had lost, by the time the bells of San Marco struck the end of the sung Mass, eleven lire and a pair of gloves.\n\nThey were good gloves. He minded the gloves.\n\n\"You are a man of no luck,\" said the Greek, who was also the dealer, and who said it every Sunday with the same warm sorrow, like a priest reading the same Gospel.\n\n\"I am a man of excellent luck,\" Matteo said. \"It is only that it is all bad.\" He pushed his last coins across. \"Deal.\"\n\nHe was forty, and had been for some time. He had a long face that women had once called distinguished and now called tired, and a notary's hands, ink in the creases that no washing reached, and a voice that could read a deposition aloud in the Avogaria so that the accused wept to hear his own crimes in it. He had been struck from the roll of notaries four years ago for selling a copy of a sealed deposition to the family it concerned, who had paid him well and then betrayed him at the first opportunity, as families do. Since then he had lived by writing letters for people who could not write them, and wills for people who could not afford a real notary, and once, memorably, a love sonnet for a gondolier who had then married the wrong girl.\n\nAnd by one other thing, which he did not think about on Sundays.\n\nHe lost the hand. He was reaching for his cloak when the curtain of the back room moved and Fra Anselmo came in out of the rain like a wet black heron, and behind him a woman Matteo did not know.\n\n\"Brother,\" Matteo said. \"I have no money and I have not been to Mass. Whichever of those you've come about, you're too late.\"\n\n\"I have come about neither.\" The friar shook his hood off. He was sixty, or seventy, or a hundred; he had one milky eye and one that missed nothing, and he could cut a seal in pear-wood that the Doge's own chancery could not tell from the brass. Matteo had met him over a disputed will eight years ago and they had been, ever since, the kind of friends who do not ask each other questions. \"This is Madonna Isotta, widow of the late master Bortolo Ferro of Murano. Madonna, this is the man I told you about. He is a gambler and a liar and he was once the best notary in the Republic.\"\n\n\"I still am,\" said Matteo. \"It's only that the Republic hasn't noticed.\" He looked at the woman properly, and stopped talking.\n\nShe was not beautiful. She was something more inconvenient than that: she was paying attention. She stood in the doorway of the Greek's back room in a wet black cloak with her hands folded in front of her, and she looked at him, at the faceless cards, at his ungloved hands, at the coins in front of the Greek, and he saw her add it all up and arrive at a figure.\n\n\"Fra Anselmo says you know the Palace,\" she said.\n\n\"Everybody knows the Palace. It's very large and you can see it from the sea.\"\n\n\"He says you know the stairs. The ones the clerks use. He says you know where the Ten keep what they take.\"\n\nThe Greek, who understood a great deal more Venetian than he admitted, got up and went out through the curtain with the cards in his hand. Matteo heard him tell the boy at the barrels to bring wine to the back room and then to stay away from it. Then it was just the three of them, and the lamp, and the rain.\n\n\"Sit down, Madonna,\" Matteo said. \"Please. And tell me what they took.\"\n\nShe told him. She told it the way he would have liked witnesses to tell things in the old days, in order and without decoration: the men from the Ten, the book, the secretary in the little room, the offer, her son at the grille. She did not say what was on the missing leaf. She said only that there was a leaf, and that Dolfin wanted it, and that she would not give it to him, and that if she did not her son would go to the galleys on the Feast of the Sensa.\n\n\"Dolfin,\" Matteo said.\n\n\"You know him?\"\n\n\"Everyone who has ever been in trouble in this city knows Zuane Dolfin.\" That was true, as far as it went. He drank some of the wine the boy had brought. It was bad. \"So. You won't give him the leaf. Then what do you want from me?\"\n\n\"I want the book back.\"\n\nMatteo put down his cup.\n\n\"It was my husband's,\" she said. \"And his father's. Two hundred and eleven leaves. Everything the Ferro ever learned about glass. If it stays in that chest, they will copy it out for the Guild, and the Guild will give it to whichever house pays the Ten most, and in ten years every bead-maker on the island will be making Ferro cristallo and selling it for half. And my son will have nothing. Not a furnace. Not a name. And Dolfin will still want the leaf.\"\n\n\"And with the book back?\"\n\n\"With the book back, I have something to sell.\" She looked at Fra Anselmo, and then at him. \"There is a Fleming at the Rialto who wanted my son. I think he will want the book more. Enough to carry two people to Antwerp and enough besides to buy the gaoler who holds the key of the third cell.\"\n\nIt was, Matteo thought, the most insane thing anyone had said to him in four years, and he had once been asked to forge a papal dispensation for a marriage between a man and his own aunt.\n\n\"You understand,\" he said, \"that the Secret Chancery is the best-guarded room in the Republic. That there are men in it night and day. That the chests are iron and the keys are held by the Capi of the Ten themselves, three keys to a chest, one each.\"\n\n\"Fra Anselmo says there is one day a year when the men in it are not there.\"\n\nHe looked at the friar. The friar looked at the lamp.\n\n\"The Sensa,\" Matteo said slowly. \"When the Doge goes out on the Bucintoro to marry the sea, the whole Signoria goes with him. The Capi. The secretaries. Every clerk who can get a place on a boat. The Palace is a shell for four hours.\" He heard himself say it and was appalled at how easily it came. \"That is the day they will sentence your son.\"\n\n\"Then we have until that day,\" Isotta Ferro said, \"and not one hour more.\"","wordCount":1279,"createdAt":1792361084227,"updatedAt":1792361085533},{"id":"b2z8MlPY17a6VRlqxJgvZ","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"WSf2mi1nOzUfiITFDgqUs","text":"Pieter Vos lodged above a spice-merchant's on the Ruga dei Spezieri, so that the stair up to his rooms smelled of pepper and cloves and something sweeter underneath that Lucia could not name, and by the time they reached his door her eyes were watering and she had sneezed twice. Her aunt did not sneeze. Her aunt went up the stair as though it were the stair of her own house, one hand on the rail and the other holding her skirts out of the dust, and knocked on the Fleming's door with the knuckle of her middle finger, twice, the way you knock on a pot to hear if it is cracked.\n\nLucia had never met a Fleming. She had expected something large and pale and bearded, a sort of walking cheese. Vos was small and neat and brown, with a short grey beard cut close and a doublet of black silk better than anything a Venetian merchant would have worn on a weekday, and he bowed over her aunt's hand like a gentleman, and then over Lucia's, and said in very good Venetian, \"The lacemaker. Your cousin spoke of you. He said you were the only one in the family with any sense.\"\n\n\"My cousin is in the Pozzi,\" said Lucia, \"because of you.\"\n\n\"Lucia,\" said her aunt.\n\n\"No, no. She is right.\" Vos straightened. \"He is in the Pozzi because of me, and because of the Ten, and because of the law, and because he is seventeen and in a hurry. I am sorry for all of those except the last, which he will grow out of if they let him.\" He gestured to the room behind him. \"Please. Come in. I have wine from the Rhine which is terrible and wine from Candia which is not, and I am too old a merchant to make you choose.\"\n\nThe room was full of light from two windows on the Grand Canal and full of things from everywhere: bolts of cloth, a Turkish carpet on the table instead of on the floor, a brass astrolabe, a little lute with a broken string, a stack of ledgers bound in vellum, a box of clear glass beakers in straw that Lucia saw her aunt see, and look away from. There was a writing-desk under one window with a letter half-written on it, and a stick of red wax, and a candle, and a seal. Vos moved between them and the desk as he poured, without seeming to, and turned the letter face down.\n\n\"So,\" he said when they were sitting. \"The widow Ferro. I had hoped to meet you in happier days. Your husband's cristallo is on the tables of four princes in the Low Countries. My masters in Antwerp have been trying to copy it for ten years and they make something very fine that looks like the water in a ditch. I take it you have not come to discuss the weather.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then what have you come to discuss?\"\n\nHer aunt drank a little of the Candia wine. Lucia, watching, saw her hands were perfectly steady, and knew that meant she was terrified.\n\n\"My son told you there was a book.\"\n\n\"He did.\"\n\n\"There is. The Ten have it. They took it from my house the day after he was taken.\" Her aunt set down the cup. \"I can get it back.\"\n\nVos did not move, but something in the room did; it was as though the light from the canal had shifted on the ceiling. Lucia had seen men at the lace-market do the same when a nun brought in a piece of real Venetian point and pretended not to know what it was worth.\n\n\"From the Ten,\" he said.\n\n\"From the Ten.\"\n\n\"May I ask how?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nHe laughed, then, delighted, and poured more wine. \"Good. Good, Madonna. Never tell a buyer how you came by the goods; it only makes him think he could have done it himself. And when you have the book?\"\n\n\"Four hundred ducats,\" said her aunt. \"In gold, not paper. Passage to Antwerp for two, on a ship that will sail the day after the Feast and not one day later. And a place for my son in your masters' glasshouse, as a journeyman, not a sweeper. A bench of his own, in three years.\"\n\n\"And for you?\"\n\n\"I'll keep house for him.\"\n\nVos studied her over his cup. \"The book has a missing leaf,\" he said. \"Your son told me that too. He said his father kept the last thing he made out of it, because he did not trust the book to keep it.\"\n\nLucia felt her hands go cold in her lap. She did not look at her aunt. She looked at the box of glass beakers in their straw, and counted the beakers, seven, and counted them again.\n\n\"My son,\" said Isotta Ferro, \"tells everyone everything. It's why he's in the Pozzi. Two hundred and eleven leaves, Messer Vos. Everything the Ferro made for three generations. Cristallo, lattimo, the aventurine, the blue of Saint Mark, the rose that my husband's father made for the Pope. You may have the whole of it, and if there is some leaf that is not in it, it is a leaf that cost my husband four crucibles and his peace of mind and was not worth it.\" She stood up. \"Four hundred, Messer Vos. Or I find another Fleming. There are a great many of you about.\"\n\nHe did not answer at once. Then he stood too, and bowed again, and when he came up he was smiling the way he had smiled at the door.\n\n\"Four hundred,\" he said. \"And passage on the Zeeridder, which sails from Malamocco on the morning after the Sensa, as you wish. I'll have the gold here, in this room, on the evening of the Feast. Bring me the book when the Doge has had his wedding.\" He took her hand again. \"I would like to have known your husband, Madonna.\"\n\n\"No,\" said her aunt. \"You wouldn't.\"","wordCount":1013,"createdAt":1792361422120,"updatedAt":1792361423471},{"id":"bNCk-Fxo9MVOIHN8eXbC6","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"zIrHkBr1jO8kZ1DDBUd9J","text":"Lucia came across from Burano at dusk, rowing herself, which she was not supposed to do.\n\nIsotta heard the oars from the kitchen and knew them, because they were bad. Lucia rowed the way she did everything that was not lace, which was impatiently and with too much shoulder, and the little sandolo slapped the water like a hand slapping a table. By the time Isotta reached the step, her niece was already tying up, crooked, to the ring that had been meant for the sand barges.\n\n\"You'll have your father after you.\"\n\n\"My father is in Chioggia until Sunday, and my mother thinks I am at the Ursulines learning the punto in aria from Suor Benedetta, who is ninety and asleep.\" Lucia came up the step two at a time. She was nineteen and small and her hands were never still; even standing on the step she was turning the end of the painter over and over in her fingers. \"Zia. Have they come?\"\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Don't. Don't do that, you do that to the Guild men. Have the Ten come?\"\n\n\"This morning.\" Isotta stood aside to let her in, as she had stood aside for Orsato. \"They took the book.\"\n\n\"Only the book?\"\n\n\"What else is there?\"\n\nLucia stopped in the passage. The lamp in the kitchen behind Isotta threw both their shadows up the wall, long and wavering, and Isotta saw in her niece's face that she had come to say something and had been saying it to herself all the way across the lagoon and now did not know how to begin.\n\n\"Sit down,\" Isotta said.\n\n\"I won't. Zia, it's Nane.\"\n\nThe kitchen was warm, at least. She had lit the small fire for the soup, and the sirocco, which made every other room in the house sweat, made the kitchen merely close. Lucia would not sit, so Isotta did, on the bench by the table, and folded her hands, and waited.\n\n\"Piero the boatman was at the Riva degli Schiavoni on Tuesday,\" Lucia said. \"With a load for the Arsenal. There was a Flemish ship at the moorings, the Sint Joris, out of Antwerp, loading oil and currants. And the fanti came along the Riva at first light, eight of them, with a secretary, and they went aboard, and they brought off a boy. A tall boy with a burnt hand. Piero says he did not fight. Piero says he said his name when they asked it, very clearly, as though he wanted it written down right.\"\n\nIsotta looked at her hands.\n\nThe left one had the same burn, the old shiny pucker across the base of the thumb where every glassmaker's child learned, once, that a pipe fresh out of the glory-hole is hot along its whole length and not only at the end. Nane had got his at seven. He had not cried. He had looked at it with great interest and then at her, and asked if it would make him a master.\n\n\"He was going to Antwerp,\" she said.\n\n\"Zia, it's the law. A glassmaker who leaves the Republic—\"\n\n\"I know the law.\"\n\n\"—they can send someone after him. They can kill him in Antwerp if they like and nobody will say a word. And if he is caught before he goes—\"\n\n\"I know the law, Lucia. I was married to it.\" She heard her own voice, flat as a marver, and was ashamed of it. Lucia was crying now, quietly, without stopping turning the rope's end in her fingers, which she had carried in from the boat without noticing. \"He never told me. He never said one word.\"\n\n\"He told me,\" Lucia said.\n\nIsotta looked up.\n\n\"Not when. Not the ship. Only that he would not stay and blow beads for the Guild while they kept his father's book in a chest. He said the Ten would take the book the moment Zio Bortolo died, because Zio Bortolo had found something, and the Guild knew he had found something, and the Ten always know what the Guild knows.\" She wiped her face with the back of her wrist. \"He said there was a Fleming who would pay. For the book. Or for him, if he had the book in his head. He said you'd be better off without him to feed.\"\n\n\"When did he say this?\"\n\n\"At the funeral. In the cloister, while the Guild men were talking to you.\"\n\nSo he had known, at the funeral, with his father's body still on the bier, that the Ten would come. He had known about the Fleming. He had known, or guessed, about the last winter's work, and he had said nothing to her and everything to his cousin, because his cousin was nineteen and made lace and could not stop him.\n\nIsotta put her hand flat on her bodice, where the paper was.\n\n\"Is he in the Pozzi?\"\n\n\"Piero thinks so. They took him to the Palace by the Ponte della Paglia, not to the Arsenal.\"\n\n\"Then tomorrow,\" Isotta said, \"I am going to the Palace.\"\n\n\"They won't let you see him.\"\n\n\"Orsato said a mother should ask.\" She stood. The soup had boiled down to a brown sediment at the bottom of the pot and she lifted it off the fire and set it on the stone and stood looking at it. \"Orsato knew this morning. He knew when he took the book. He said the son is not at present in a position to swear. I thought he meant Nane was a child.\"\n\nLucia said nothing.\n\n\"You'll stay tonight. I'll take you back at first light and you can tell your mother Suor Benedetta kept you for vespers.\"\n\n\"Suor Benedetta has not kept anyone for vespers since the war of Cyprus.\"\n\n\"Then tell her something better. You're the one with the clever hands.\" Isotta reached up and took the rope's end gently out of her niece's fingers and laid it on the table. \"And Lucia. Whatever Nane told you, about the Fleming and the book. You will not tell anyone else.\"\n\n\"Who would I tell?\"\n\n\"Anyone who asks,\" Isotta said. \"That is who people tell.\"\n\nOutside, the wind had dropped at last. In the sudden quiet she could hear the water lapping the step, and the dog down the canal had stopped barking, and somewhere over toward the Fondamenta dei Vetrai a furnace that was still lit was being stoked for the night shift: the deep exhalation of the bellows, and a man's voice calling for more wood, and the small sharp ring of glass touching glass.","wordCount":1098,"createdAt":1792360669055,"updatedAt":1792360971175},{"id":"lOM6X4GKo8qEivdFjkmvT","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"ZfvRJLOr4LOCgCSIL5URI","text":"On Burano the fog came in at night and stayed until noon, and in the mornings the island was a room with no walls. Lucia sat at the window of her mother's house with the pillow on her knees and could not see the canal six feet below her, only hear it, and hear the bells of San Martino ring the hour from somewhere that might have been the next street or the next world.\n\nShe liked the fog. Nobody came to the window in the fog. Nobody could see what she was making.\n\nHer aunt came anyway, at the hour of terce, in Piero's boat. Lucia heard the oars stop and the boat knock against the step, and then her mother's voice in the passage, high and over-polite, the voice she used for priests and for her husband's sister, who had married above them into glass. Then Zia Isotta was in the doorway in her black wool, wet with fog to the shoulders, and her mother was behind her saying she would bring wine, and Zia Isotta was saying no, thank you, Marina, nothing, I've only come to see what the child is making.\n\n\"I'm not a child,\" Lucia said when her mother had gone.\n\n\"No.\" Her aunt shut the door and stood with her back against it. \"I'm sorry. I needed her to go away.\" She looked at the pillow on Lucia's knees, at the bobbins and the pins and the little cloud of thread. \"What are you making?\"\n\n\"A collar. For the Grimani. Punto in aria.\" She turned the pillow so her aunt could see. \"Stitch in the air. There's no cloth under it, you see; it's all thread, all the way through. Suor Benedetta says the first woman who made it was a fisherman's girl whose lover sent her a piece of seaweed from the far side of the world, and she copied it in thread so it wouldn't rot.\"\n\n\"Is that true?\"\n\n\"No. Suor Benedetta tells everybody something different.\" Lucia set down the pillow. \"Zia, what's happened? Is it Nane?\"\n\nHer aunt came and sat on the end of the bed, which nobody but Lucia ever sat on, and took something out of her bodice, and held it out on her open palm.\n\nIt was a roll of paper the length of a finger, browned at one edge.\n\n\"Don't unroll it,\" Isotta said. \"Not yet. Listen first. This is the last leaf of your uncle's book. The one the Ten didn't get. There is a man at the Palace called Dolfin who would let Nane go tomorrow if I gave it to him, and he would give it to the Guild, and the Guild would give it to whoever they liked, and the Ferro would be nobody. And there is a man at the Rialto who would give me a great deal of money for it, and take it to Antwerp, and the Ferro would be nobody there either.\" She turned the little roll over with one finger. \"On this leaf is how to make calcedonio. Glass that looks like stone. Your uncle found it last winter. He found it because I made him try the copper with the silver, which he said was a woman's idea and stupid, and it was not stupid. It was the last thing we made together. And as long as this exists, on paper, anyone who can take it from me has it.\"\n\nLucia looked at the paper. She could see, through the thin outer turn, the ghost of figures written in brown ink, columns, little crosses.\n\n\"Then burn it,\" she said.\n\n\"If I burn it, it's only in my head. And my head is easy to take.\" Her aunt said it without any expression at all. \"If they put me where they've put Nane, Lucia, or worse, I don't know how long I would keep it. I'm not brave. Nobody is, in the end. Your uncle wasn't.\"\n\n\"Zia—\"\n\n\"So I want to put it somewhere nobody would look for a receipt for glass. Somewhere it looks like something else entirely.\" She held out the roll again. \"You count stitches. Every pattern you make is numbers. How many twists, how many crossings, how many pins before the picot. Could you make this into a pattern? Weights and times and heats, as stitches? So that it was there, all of it, if you knew how to read it, and to anyone else it was a collar?\"\n\nLucia took the roll. It was warm from her aunt's skin.\n\nShe unrolled it on the pillow, across the half-made collar, and smoothed it flat with her palm, and read it. She did not understand most of it. Pounds of ash. Ounces of something called zafre. The copper and the silver her aunt had spoken of. Hours at the first heat. A thing her uncle had written in the margin and underlined twice: NOT TO STIR. There were eleven lines, and some had four figures and some had six, and none of the figures was greater than forty.\n\nNothing greater than forty. She could do anything under forty. She had done the Grimani's border in seventy-two repeats.\n\n\"Yes,\" she said. \"Give me three days.\"","wordCount":870,"createdAt":1792361178464,"updatedAt":1792361179903},{"id":"lu52wS2RrQR1yc5f79ZwX","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"M5Y_AFQAZ3U49Fmor2WYH","text":"On the morning of the Sensa the whole city walked to the sea.\n\nLucia had seen it before, every year of her life, from a boat on the edge of the crowd with her father swearing at the other boats; she had never seen it from the inside. From the inside it was not a crowd. It was weather. It moved in gusts and eddies along the Riva and across the Piazzetta, it sang, it shouted, it sold things, it smelled of fried fish and orange-water and incense and the sweat of ten thousand people in their best clothes, and it carried her along the Molo with her basket on her arm and her pillow on top of the basket the way the tide carries a cork.\n\nThe basket was heavy. Nobody would have known. She carried it the way she carried it every day, on the crook of her left arm with her hand over the pillow, and the pillow on top had a half-made border pinned on it with the bobbins hanging down, and under the pillow, wrapped in a cloth of unbleached linen, was a book in boards and calf, swollen at the lower edge, with one brass corner missing, the bottom left.\n\nFra Anselmo had brought it to Murano at dawn. He had not slept, he said, for three nights, and looked it; his good eye was red and his bad eye was redder, and his fingers were black to the second joint. Her aunt had opened it at the front and read the first page and closed it and opened it at the back and read the last, and then turned to the end, where the stub was, and found the leaf sewn in there, in Zio Bortolo's cramped brown hand, CALCEDONIO, with the weights all wrong. She had put her hand flat on it for a moment. Then she had kissed the friar on both cheeks, which Lucia had never seen her do to anyone, not even Zio Bortolo, and he had gone pink to the edge of his tonsure.\n\nAt the third hour the bells began.\n\nAll of them. San Marco first, the great bell, the Marangona, so deep Lucia felt it in her teeth rather than heard it; and then San Zaccaria and San Giorgio across the water and the Pietà and San Moisè, and the ones further off, Santi Apostoli and the Frari and the Madonna dell'Orto, until the whole city was ringing like a struck glass. And out of the Porta della Carta, between the guards, came the Doge.\n\nLucia had never been so close. He was an old man in cloth of gold, with the horned cap on his head and a face like a walnut, and he walked very slowly because of the weight of the gold, between two men holding up the train, and behind him came the Signoria in crimson, and behind them the Capi of the Ten in black, three old men in a row looking at nothing. The crowd went down on its knees. Lucia went down on her knees with her basket in her arms, and through the legs of the people in front she watched the procession go along the Molo to the water, where the Bucintoro lay against the steps like a great golden animal, and all its oars stood up in the air at once in a salute, two hundred of them, white, with a sound like a flock of birds rising.\n\nWhen she stood up, Matteo Lion was beside her.\n\nShe had seen him only once, the night of the glory-hole, in the bad light of the furnace. In the morning sun he looked older and more respectable, in a notary's black gown with a roll of papers in his hand and a cap like a pudding, and he was smiling at the Bucintoro as though he had paid for it.\n\n\"You're very punctual,\" he said, without looking at her. \"Don't look round. There's a man by the column of San Todaro in a grey coat eating a fritter. He's been there since the second hour. And Orsato is at the Porta della Carta, behind the guard, pretending to be a guard.\"\n\nLucia did not look round.\n\n\"The Capi are on the boat,\" Matteo said. \"All three of them. And Dolfin isn't.\"\n\n\"Where is he?\"\n\n\"He sent his excuses this morning. A fever.\" Matteo smiled more widely at the Bucintoro, which was moving now, very slowly, out from the steps into the basin, with the trumpets going on the poop and the oars rising and falling. \"I expect he'll recover by noon. Have you got it?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Have you got the other thing?\"\n\nShe moved her right hand on the basket handle, and felt, in the pocket of her apron, the little bag of bobbins that she had wound the night before: forty of them, old ones, boxwood and bone, every one she could find in her mother's house.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then walk with me,\" said Matteo Lion, \"like a girl from Burano who's been asked to carry something for a notary and doesn't think much of it, and when we're on the stair, don't speak at all. Your accent is very charming and very Burano and the guard on the Censori would remember it for the rest of his life.\"","wordCount":891,"createdAt":1792361921455,"updatedAt":1792361923240},{"id":"mEQAJa9Q-RscrSrQi_alt","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"PUre3Tk4FJuGGL9QbD2OU","text":"He told himself he went down to the wells to count the guards.\n\nThat was true, as far as it went. On the Sensa they would come up the Censori, and a man who came up the Censori could also be made to go down it, and down was the Pozzi; it was worth knowing how many men were on the lower passage on a feast day and whether they could hear the Chancery from where they stood. He had a pass. He had kept it, as he had kept the paper. It said that Matteo Lion, notary of the Avogaria, was to be admitted to the prisons to take depositions in the matter of the Republic's business, and it was four years out of date, and the guard on the stool by the low arch could not read it, and let him through for two soldi.\n\nHe counted the guards. There were three, on the lower passage, and one of them was asleep.\n\nThen he went to the third cell, because he had known all along that he would.\n\nThe boy was lying down. That was the first thing. A boy of seventeen does not lie down in the middle of the day unless he has been told to or cannot help it, and nobody had told him to. He was on the plank with the blanket over him up to the chin, and when Matteo lifted the little lamp he had bought from the guard and held it to the bars, the face that turned towards the light was shining wet, and the eyes were too bright.\n\n\"Who are you?\"\n\n\"A friend of your mother's.\"\n\n\"My mother hasn't got any friends.\" The boy laughed and the laugh went into a cough that went on a long time. \"She's got my father's friends, and they all came to the funeral and looked at the furnace. Are you from the Guild?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"From Dolfin?\"\n\nMatteo did not answer quickly enough.\n\n\"Get out,\" the boy said. Then: \"No. Wait. Wait.\" He pushed himself up on one elbow. In the lamplight Matteo could see the hand, the burnt one, gripping the edge of the blanket, the scar shiny as glass. \"Is she all right? Has he done anything to her?\"\n\n\"She's all right.\"\n\n\"She came once. She said she'd come again.\" The boy lay back. \"That was three weeks ago. It's hard to count. They don't tell you the days down here. They give you bread twice and that's a day, but sometimes they forget and give it once, and then you don't know.\" He was quiet for a moment. \"There's a man in the next cell who's been here since the war of Cyprus. He talks to me through the wall. He says it gets easier. He says after the first year you stop hearing the water.\"\n\n\"Your mother's coming for you,\" Matteo said.\n\nHe had not meant to say it. It was the kind of thing one did not say, in the Pozzi, with three guards on the passage and one of them perhaps not asleep.\n\nThe boy turned his head and looked at him. It was Isotta's look. It was exactly her look, the one she had given him in the back room of the Greek's, adding him up.\n\n\"You're the notary,\" Nane said. \"Fra Anselmo's notary. She told me about you. Not your name. She said there was a man who knew the stairs, who was clever and unlucky.\" He was breathing hard. \"Are you going to get her killed?\"\n\nMatteo opened his mouth. Nothing came out of it.\n\n\"Because if you are,\" said Giovanni Ferro, seventeen, lying on a plank under the Palace of the Doges with the gaol fever in him, \"I'll kill you. When I get out. I'll come and find you and I'll do it with a pipe from the glory-hole, the hot end, and it will take a long time.\" He coughed again. \"That's a promise. My father always kept his promises. Ask anybody.\"\n\nThen, before Matteo could say anything, he fumbled under the blanket and brought out something small and held it through the bars.\n\n\"Here. Give her this. I made it before. Before the ship. I had it in my shoe and they didn't find it.\" His fingers opened. On the palm, among the calluses, lay a bead: a little sphere of white glass the size of a chickpea, milk-white, perfectly round, with a single thread of blue wound once about its middle like a ring on a finger. \"Lattimo. The first one I ever made that she didn't send back. Tell her I'm keeping the blanket dry.\"\n\nMatteo took it. It was warm from the boy's hand. He put it in his pocket, where the coral was, and they lay there together, the two small hard things, the white and the red.\n\n\"I'll tell her,\" he said.","wordCount":817,"createdAt":1792361634436,"updatedAt":1792361635941},{"id":"oFm3UH355FKwvApJ_fSSB","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"3Ca9TRPohYxSK0z4dLlo_","text":"It took her four.\n\nNot because the numbers were hard. The numbers were the easy part; she made a key on the first night, by candle, on the back of an old pricking: a twist for an ounce, a crossing for a pound, a pin for an hour, a picot for each heat, and the four-petalled flower Suor Benedetta called the Sant'Andrea for the turn of a line. It was the hiding that was hard. A pattern that was only numbers looked like numbers: it had a stiffness to it, a lack of grace, and anyone who had ever made lace would see at a glance that it had been made by someone who was thinking about something else. So she had to make it beautiful. She had to make the zafre into a vine and the copper into a pomegranate and NOT TO STIR into a little border of knots that ran round the whole thing like a fence round a garden, and she had to make it so that the vine and the pomegranate and the fence were the reason for the collar and the numbers were only how many stitches it happened to need.\n\nOn the fourth morning there was no fog. The sun came in flat and gold over the lagoon and lit up the window like a lamp, and she unpinned the collar from the pillow and laid it on the black cloth and sent the boy from next door to Murano for her aunt.\n\nZia Isotta looked at it a long time without touching it.\n\nIt was a small thing, a woman's collar, no wider than two hands. A vine ran round it, heavy with fruit. In the corners there were flowers. Along the edge there was a border of tiny knots, and here and there among the leaves a bird, because Lucia had needed somewhere to put the second heat and a bird had seemed right.\n\n\"Read it to me,\" her aunt said.\n\nSo Lucia read it. She put her finger on the first leaf of the vine and counted the twists aloud and said \"four pounds of ash of Levant,\" and moved along the vine and said \"six of the sand, washed thrice,\" and went round the collar as if it were a clock, through the copper and the silver and the hours and the heats, through the birds, until she came to the border of knots and said, \"and not to stir.\"\n\nWhen she looked up her aunt was crying. Not loudly. The way a pot weeps on the outside when it is very cold and the room is warm.\n\n\"He would have hated it,\" Isotta said. \"He would have said lace is for nuns.\" She wiped her face with the heel of her hand. \"Give me the leaf.\"\n\nThey burned it in the kitchen fire with Lucia's mother at Mass. Her aunt held it by one corner over the coals and it went brown, then black, then a curl of bright orange ran along the edge and for a moment, in the last of the paper, Lucia saw the numbers stand out white, every one of them, as if the fire were reading them too. Then it was ash, and her aunt crumbled the ash in her fingers and dropped it into the coals and dusted her hands, and that was the end of the last leaf of the Glass Ledger.\n\n\"Now,\" Isotta said. \"Now it's in two places. Your hands and mine. And nobody looks at a girl's collar.\"\n\n\"The Grimani will look at it.\"\n\n\"The Grimani will never see it. Make them another.\" Her aunt folded the collar into a square of linen and put it into Lucia's hands and closed Lucia's fingers over it. \"Keep it in your work-basket, under the bobbins, the way you'd keep anything you hadn't finished. Don't hide it. Hidden things get found.\"\n\n\"Zia.\" Lucia held the little bundle. It weighed nothing. \"What are you going to do? About Nane?\"\n\nHer aunt was quiet for a moment, looking at the fire.\n\n\"I'm going to steal your uncle's book back from the Council of Ten,\" she said, \"on the Feast of the Sensa, while the Doge is marrying the sea. With a notary who drinks and a friar who forges seals. I am going to sell it to a Fleming. And then I am going to buy your cousin out of the Pozzi and take him a very long way away.\"\n\nLucia laughed, because it was so absurd. Then she saw her aunt's face and stopped.\n\n\"Can I come?\" she said.","wordCount":767,"createdAt":1792361178161,"updatedAt":1792361270532},{"id":"qqQDZns_q_FUfeFNlxHTs","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"gnMam_XH4_nPQ3uIi2eK8","text":"The passage under the Palace smelled of the sea and of what the sea left behind. Orsato carried a lamp and went ahead of her and did not speak, and she was grateful, because she did not think she could have answered. The walls sweated. Somewhere water was running, not loud, a thread of it, and the floor under her shoes was soft with something she did not look at.\n\nThe third cell on the lower passage had a door of black oak with a square cut in it at the height of a man's face, and across the square three iron bars, and behind the bars, when Orsato lifted the lamp, her son.\n\nHe had grown. That was her first stupid thought. He had grown in the nineteen days since the funeral; or else she had never looked at him properly, or else the cell made him tall by being so low. His hair was flat with damp. He had a blanket round his shoulders like an old woman at a fire.\n\n\"Mamma.\"\n\n\"Don't,\" she said, because his voice had cracked on it, and if he cried she would, and Orsato was standing four paces off with his lamp and his patience. \"Don't. Are you hurt?\"\n\n\"No.\" He put his fingers through the bars, the burnt ones. She took them. They were cold as the canal. \"They haven't done anything. They ask me things. A man comes with a book and asks me what my father put in the batch for lattimo, and I tell him, because everyone in Murano knows what goes in lattimo, and he writes it down very slowly and goes away.\"\n\n\"Nane, why?\"\n\nHe looked at her, and for a moment he was seven again, holding out his blistered hand and asking if it would make him a master.\n\n\"Because they were going to take the book,\" he said. \"Because the Guild had already been to see you, I saw them, I saw old Briati in the yard with his hat in his hands looking at our furnace like a man looking at a widow. Because I'm not going to blow beads for them for thirty years while some secretary keeps Babbo's book in a chest and sells it a page at a time.\" He lowered his voice. \"There's a Fleming. Vos. He's at the Rialto. He said if I came to Antwerp with what was in my head he'd give me a furnace of my own. A furnace, Mamma. Not a bench in someone else's.\"\n\n\"And what was in your head?\"\n\n\"Everything I'd seen.\" His fingers tightened. \"Not the last thing. Babbo never let me see the last thing. But Vos didn't know that.\"\n\nSo he had meant to cheat the Fleming too. He was his father's son. She closed her eyes for a moment.\n\n\"Who else did you tell?\"\n\n\"Nobody. Lucia. Only Lucia.\" He tried to smile. \"She told me I was an idiot.\"\n\n\"She was right.\"\n\n\"Time,\" Orsato said, not unkindly.\n\nNane held on. \"Mamma, the man with the book said something. He said if you were sensible I'd be home for the Sensa. What does that mean? What does he want from you?\"\n\nShe looked at her son through the bars, at his flat wet hair and the old woman's blanket and the hand that had been burnt into the shape of a master's hand, and she thought of Dolfin saying pride is very expensive in the Pozzi.\n\n\"It means he is a liar,\" she said, \"like all of them. Eat what they give you. Keep the blanket dry. Say your prayers and say them out loud, so the guard hears you and thinks you're a good boy. I'll come back.\"\n\n\"When?\"\n\n\"When I can.\"\n\nOrsato was already lowering the lamp. In the last of its light she saw her son's face go back into the dark of the cell, not all at once but by parts, the eyes last, and then there was only the square in the door and the three bars and the sound of the water running somewhere it should not be.\n\nOn the stair, in the daylight again, she had to stop and put her hand against the wall. Orsato stopped too, a step above, and waited, and looked out over the courtyard where the light had moved on to the next drain.\n\n\"He is right,\" he said, to nobody. \"It is a waste of good hands.\"\n\nThen he took her back to the Ponte della Paglia, and handed her down into Piero's boat, and lifted his black cap to her, once, which she would remember a long time afterwards, when she had reason to wonder what side he was on.","wordCount":785,"createdAt":1792360970841,"updatedAt":1792364114019},{"id":"rFVBaDyFiMkbOiFarjtdK","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"eGefyjbO4ydqoB5RwpDOc","text":"The guard at the stairhead of the Censori was a Dalmatian with a broken nose who had been a guard at the stairhead of the Censori, Matteo was fairly sure, since the war of Cyprus. He did not read the warrant. He looked at the seal on it, the lion with his paw on the book, and at Matteo's gown and cap, and at the girl with the basket, and spat out of the window into the Rio di Palazzo, and waved them on.\n\nMatteo's legs had been fine on the stair. They went to water in the passage. It was the smell that did it: ink, and beeswax on the panelling, and the faint sharp smell of the sand they shook over wet signatures, which he had not smelled for four years and which went into him like a hook into a fish. The clerks' desks along the wall were empty. The pens lay on them in a row. Somewhere far off, through the Palace, he could hear the trumpets on the water, thin as a gnat.\n\nSanudo was in the Chancery with his feet on a desk, eating cherries out of his hat.\n\n\"Lion!\" He took his feet down. He was twenty, and plump, and had a downy beginning of a beard that he touched when he was nervous; he touched it now. \"What are you doing here? You're not supposed to be here. You're not anything any more, are you? They struck you off.\" He seemed pleased with himself for remembering. \"And who's she?\"\n\n\"My sister's girl. She carries my papers.\" Matteo held out the warrant. He did it the old way, the way he had once held out depositions to the Avogadori, with the seal uppermost and his thumb not quite touching it, so the eye went to the wax and stayed there. \"His Excellency Dolfin's compliments, Sanudo. He's sick, poor man, and he's sent me for something, and if you don't help me I shall have to go out to the Bucintoro in a sandolo and explain to the Capi in the middle of the Doge's wedding why the Ferro receipt isn't on their table, and I'll give them your name. Read it.\"\n\nSanudo read it. It took him some time. His lips moved. Matteo, who had written it, could have recited it: the officer on duty in the Secret Chancery to show the bearer the book of receipts of the late Bortolo Ferro, received the twenty-first of April, so that the bearer might copy from it the receipt for lattimo, of which the Capi had need on the Bucintoro for a question raised by the Most Serene Prince touching the making of the cups for the wedding feast. Signed, in a hand that had been dead for six years, by a secretary of the first grade; under it, the lion.\n\nIt was, Matteo thought, a deeply silly warrant. That was why he loved it. Nobody would forge anything so silly.\n\n\"Lattimo,\" said Sanudo.\n\n\"The Doge wants white cups. The Patriarch wants clear ones. There's been a quarrel. You know what they're like.\" Matteo shrugged. \"Twenty lines. I copy them, you lock it up again, I go out to the boat, you go back to your cherries. Or you send me away and tomorrow you explain.\"\n\nSanudo looked at the warrant, and at Matteo, and touched his beard; and Matteo watched him think exactly what he had told Isotta Ferro, in the friar's cell, that the boy would think. Either it was real; or the only man who could have forged it was a man he had beaten at primero.\n\nHe took the key off his belt.\n\nThe press stood against the wall of the little room beyond the Chancery door, the room most people did not know was a room: a cupboard of walnut, tall as a man, with a lock like a fist. Inside, on three shelves, were the new matters, the things that had come in and not yet been entered and sent to their proper chests. Bundles of letters tied with tape. A box of seized pamphlets. A dagger in a bag with a label on it. And on the middle shelf, by itself, a book in boards and calf, swollen at the lower edge, with one brass corner missing, the bottom left.\n\nMatteo heard the girl behind him breathe in, once.\n\n\"There,\" Sanudo said, and lifted it out, and put it on the Chancery desk, and opened it, and stood over it. \"Lattimo. Go on, then.\"\n\nMatteo sat. He took out his own paper and ink, and uncapped the ink, and turned the leaves, slowly, as a notary does, looking for the place, and the boy stood at his shoulder and watched every leaf turn.\n\nThat was the moment, and they had known it would be. Sanudo had to look away. They had argued about it for an hour in the friar's cell and for another hour at the marver with the glory-hole roaring, and in the end it was the girl who had said, quite calmly, turning a bobbin over in her fingers: Nobody can watch a book when a girl drops forty bobbins on a stone floor. Not even a priest. I've seen it.\n\nMatteo found the lattimo. He dipped his pen.\n\nAnd Lucia Zen, standing behind him with her basket, put her hand into her apron for her handkerchief, and brought out the little bag of bobbins instead, and somehow, as she did so, the drawstring came undone.\n\nThey went everywhere. Forty bobbins of boxwood and bone on a floor of red and white marble, bouncing and rolling, clattering like hail on a roof, under the desks, under the press, into the corners, a sound that went on and on as though it would never stop; and the girl gave a cry of real distress, and went down on her knees with her basket, and Sanudo, who was twenty and kind and had a mistress at San Polo and would be a good husband to someone one day, said \"Oh, God, wait, let me,\" and went down on his knees too.\n\nIt took them, afterwards, Matteo calculated, about as long as a Paternoster and two Aves to pick them all up. The girl kept finding more, and holding them up, and thanking him. Sanudo crawled under the press and came out with dust on his hat.\n\nWhen they stood up, Matteo was copying the receipt for lattimo, very neatly, in his best hand, and the book was open on the desk beside him at the right page, and the girl's basket was on her arm, and her pillow was on top of it, with the half-made border pinned to it, and the bobbins hanging down.\n\nIt was heavier on the left now, Matteo thought, watching her settle it; she had to brace her hip. It was not heavier. It was precisely the same weight. He had weighed them both, on the friar's scales, the night before.\n\nHe sanded his copy, and folded it, and stood.\n\n\"My thanks, Sanudo. I'll tell His Excellency you were very helpful.\"\n\n\"Will you?\" The boy looked absurdly pleased. He shut the book, and lifted it, and put it back on the middle shelf of the press, by itself, and locked the lock like a fist, and hung the key on his belt, and patted it. \"There. Safe as the Mint.\"\n\n\"Safer,\" said Matteo.","wordCount":1241,"createdAt":1792361921071,"updatedAt":1792361923241},{"id":"v-DK9a4KJO5YnZnFFkI-M","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"ElASJa2Vxm6oNK1C3eGhQ","text":"The bora had come down off the mountains in the night and swept the sirocco out to sea, and the morning was so clear that from the Fondamenta dei Vetrai Isotta could see snow on the Alps, a white rind laid along the top of the world. The lagoon was the colour of a knife. Piero the boatman rowed her across without singing either, out of respect, and set her down at the Ponte della Paglia among the fishwives and the clerks.\n\nShe had dressed for it. The black wool, the good collar, the coral her mother had left her; not so much that she looked like a woman who could pay, not so little that she looked like a woman who could not. She had thought about it for an hour in the cold kitchen before dawn, and then she had thought that thinking about it was what the Palace wanted from people, that they should come to the door already half on their knees, and she had nearly gone in her apron out of spite.\n\nThe door of the Pozzi was a low arch at the bottom of a stair, with a guard on a stool beside it eating bread. She told him her name, and her son's name, and he went on eating bread and looked at her as though she were weather.\n\n\"Ferro,\" he said at last. \"From Murano.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Wait.\"\n\nShe waited. She waited long enough to watch the light move across the courtyard flagstones from one drain to the next. People went up and down the stair past her: a priest with a basket, a notary with a sheaf of papers under his chin, two women who were plainly also mothers, from the way they did not look at each other. Then the guard came back with someone else, and it was not a gaoler. It was Orsato, in his black cap, and he did not take her down. He took her up.\n\nUp the Scala dei Censori, up again, through a door so plain she understood it was important, along a passage panelled in dark wood where the clerks' pens made a sound like rain, and into a room no larger than her kitchen, with one window on the Rio di Palazzo and one man at a table under it.\n\n\"Madonna Ferro. Please. Sit.\" He rose for her. Nobody in the Palace had risen for her. \"Zuane Dolfin. I have the honour to be one of the secretaries of the Most Excellent Council. You will forgive the room. The great rooms are for great business, and this, I hope, is a small business, between ourselves.\"\n\nHe was perhaps fifty, grey at the temples, with a pleasant tired face and very clean hands. On the table in front of him, unwrapped from Orsato's oilcloth, lay Bortolo's book.\n\nShe sat because her knees had decided it.\n\n\"Your son is well,\" Dolfin said. \"I asked, before you came. He is in the third cell on the lower passage, which is not the best cell, but it is not the worst; it is dry except at the spring tides. He is fed. He has not been put to the question and I see no reason he should be.\" He let that lie a moment, the way a man lets a coin lie on the table. \"You know, of course, what the law says.\"\n\n\"I know what it says.\"\n\n\"Then you know I have been generous already.\" He touched the book with one finger, on the boards, not the pages, as though it might be warm. \"I will be plain with you, Madonna, because you seem to me a plain woman, and plainness saves time. The Guild of the Art has, as you know, its ears. One of those ears belongs to a man who carried wood for your husband last winter. He tells us that the furnace was lit at hours when no orders were being filled. He tells us that master Bortolo burned through four crucibles in February making something he would not let the boys see. He tells us that on the second Sunday of Lent your husband went to San Donato and paid for a Mass of thanksgiving, which he had never done in his life.\"\n\nIsotta said nothing.\n\n\"And here,\" said Dolfin, and now he did open the book, at the end, where Orsato had opened it, and turned it round so she could see the sliver of cut paper in the gutter, \"here is a leaf that is not here.\"\n\n\"He cut out his failures.\"\n\n\"So Orsato told me you said. I think it is a good answer. I think it is the answer I would give.\" He closed the book. \"Calcedonio, Madonna. That is what the Guild thinks. Glass like stone. They have not made it on this island since my grandfather's time and I do not need to tell you what the Republic would give to make it again, or what the Emperor would give, or the King of France, or a certain house in Antwerp that your son was so eager to visit.\"\n\n\"My son knows nothing about it.\"\n\n\"Then he has nothing to sell, and he will go to the galleys for trying to sell it, which seems to me a waste of a good pair of hands.\" Dolfin folded his own. \"On the Feast of the Sensa the Most Excellent Council will sit after the Doge returns from the Lido, as it does every year, and it will pass sentence on those subjects of the Republic who were taken trying to carry the Art abroad. There are three this year. Your son is one. I would like him not to be. Bring me the leaf before the Sensa, and he walks out of the Pozzi on the Feast and goes home with you to Murano, and no one ever writes his name in a book again.\"\n\n\"And if there is no leaf?\"\n\n\"Then I am sorry for you.\" He meant it, she thought; that was the worst of him. He meant every word. \"Orsato will take you down now. You may have a little while at the grille. I would not tell him about our conversation. Boys of that age are proud, and pride is very expensive in the Pozzi.\"","wordCount":1053,"createdAt":1792360971141,"updatedAt":1792360972542},{"id":"v8O7TLwDZYbuOsvTmZdoQ","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"2rW2A0wi2wIlkCEMMTq6j","text":"It was nearly dark when Isotta came to Burano, and she did not knock. She came up the step from Piero's boat and through the water-door and into the little room at the back where her niece sat working by the last of the window, and stood in the doorway until Lucia looked up.\n\n\"The friar thinks you should carry it,\" she said. \"The book. Out of the Chancery, under your pillow, on the Sensa. With the notary beside you and half the Ten on the water and the other half, God knows, anywhere at all.\" She heard her own voice and did not like it; it was the voice of the Guild men in the yard. \"I said I would ask you. I'm asking you. You can say no. I'd think better of you, I think, if you said no.\"\n\nLucia put down her bobbins.\n\n\"Then you'd have to find another girl from Burano with a basket,\" she said, \"and teach her the stairs in two weeks, and trust her. And you don't trust anybody, Zia. You told me so on the bridge.\"\n\n\"I trust you.\"\n\n\"Then let me.\" Lucia got up and went to the corner by the bed, where her work-basket stood, the old one, the rush gone dark with the grease of her hands and patched at the rim with a strip of red cloth. She lifted it and turned it round, so that her aunt could see the patch. \"But not with one basket. With two.\"\n\n\"Two.\"\n\n\"Everybody knows my basket. Every boatman from here to the Fondamente. If they see it going into the Palace on the Sensa and coming out again, they'll remember it. So there should be two the same. I'll buy one at the Rialto, from the man by the fish-market, and rub it with ash and lard until it looks like this, and sew the same red cloth on the rim.\" She was talking quickly now, the way she did when a pattern came to her all at once and she had to get it down before it went. \"And when I come out of the Palace I'll give you mine, with the book in it, and you give me yours, with lace in it. In the crowd. On the Molo, at the boats, where everyone's handing things up and down. And whoever's watching me will follow a girl with a basket of lace all the way to wherever they like.\"\n\nIsotta looked at the basket for a long time. Then at her niece.\n\n\"Who taught you to think like that?\"\n\n\"Suor Benedetta,\" said Lucia. \"She says the first thing a lacemaker learns is that the pattern people look at is never the one that's holding the thing together.\"\n\n\"Is that true?\"\n\n\"No,\" said Lucia, and smiled for the first time in two weeks. \"She tells everybody something different.\"","wordCount":479,"createdAt":1792364155238,"updatedAt":1792364156579},{"id":"wnMEGk0dF0RHNecEGOuPt","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"Fydy7EaqZZr40hKtbAF0P","text":"He came up out of the wells into the courtyard and there was Dolfin, at the foot of the Scala d'Oro, in a gown of black damask with a fur at the collar though it was May, talking to two senators as though he had been waiting there all morning for nobody in particular.\n\nHe had not been waiting for nobody. He finished with the senators and turned and came across the flagstones, and Matteo felt the stones under his own feet go soft, the way the floor of the lower passage had been soft.\n\n\"Matteo. You've been visiting.\"\n\n\"I wanted to count the guards, Excellency. For the day.\"\n\n\"Of course you did.\" Dolfin fell in beside him, and they walked, slowly, along the arcade towards the Porta della Carta. Two men in black, talking business. Anyone watching would have thought Matteo had come up in the world. \"And how many are there?\"\n\n\"Three. On the lower passage.\"\n\n\"And how is the boy?\"\n\n\"He has the gaol fever.\"\n\n\"Ah.\" Dolfin nodded, regretfully. \"Yes. It's the spring tides. It comes up through the floor.\" They walked a few paces. \"Well, it hardly matters now, does it?\"\n\nMatteo stopped.\n\n\"Excellency?\"\n\n\"Walk, Matteo. People are looking at us.\" Dolfin took his elbow, quite gently, and walked him on. \"I said it hardly matters. The Capi have agreed, on my word, to come to the Rialto on the evening of the Feast. I have promised them a widow selling the secrets of the Art to a Fleming, and they are very excited, in the way that old men are excited, which is to say they have stopped listening to me and started composing their speeches. When she is taken, the widow will give me the leaf. And when she has given it to me, the Council will consider the matter of her son, who tried to carry the Art abroad, and whose mother has now been taken trying to do the same. And they will send him to the galleys, Matteo, because there will be no other possible thing for them to do. It will not be my decision. I shall argue for clemency. I shall be very moving.\" He smiled. \"I've always been told I'm very moving.\"\n\n\"You told her he'd walk out on the Sensa.\"\n\n\"I told her he would walk out if she brought me the leaf. She has not brought me the leaf. She has chosen to steal from the Republic instead. People make their choices.\" Dolfin patted his arm. \"You look ill. Is it the wells? They take some people that way. One breath and they're ill for a week.\"\n\nThey had come to the Porta della Carta. Beyond it, in the Piazzetta, the sun was blazing on the two columns and on the water, and men were already putting up the stands for the Sensa fair, a forest of poles and canvas and shouting.\n\n\"One thing more,\" Dolfin said, and took a letter from his sleeve. It was folded small, in the Chancery fold, and sealed with plain red wax and no device. \"For our friend at the Rialto. He's anxious, and anxious men talk. Take it to him tonight. He'll know who it's from. Don't let him keep you.\"\n\nMatteo took it. It went into his pocket with the bead and the coral.\n\n\"Excellency,\" he said. \"The boy. Supposing she did bring you the leaf. Supposing she came tomorrow.\"\n\n\"Then she'd be a fool,\" said Dolfin, \"to bring it to me before the Feast instead of after it, wouldn't she, when the price will be so much lower after.\" He looked at Matteo, curiously, the way he had looked at him across the well-head the day he bought his debts. \"Why do you ask?\"\n\n\"I like to know the odds, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Dolfin. \"That's always been your trouble.\" He lifted a hand, and went back across the courtyard to the Scala d'Oro, and up it, into the dark of the Palace, where the gold on the vault caught the light and held it as the rest of the stair went into shadow.\n\nMatteo did not go to the Rialto that night. He went home and sat at his table and took the three things out of his pocket and laid them in a row: the coral, and the bead, and the letter in the Chancery fold. He looked at them until the candle had burned down a finger's width.\n\nThen he took his penknife, and warmed the blade in the flame, and very carefully, the way he had done a hundred times in the old days when he was the best notary in the Republic and had not yet been caught, lifted the seal.","wordCount":788,"createdAt":1792361634143,"updatedAt":1792361756198}],"plotThreads":[],"motifs":[],"continuitySuppressions":[{"id":"4uc6VsYaZJ7DykoLKXXwe","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","issueId":"item-handoff-MJYhBoZeAPreO4Kr6mblb-txMWhAmoQ5MKX1y3i93KU-ovmu-1zRoQUHJcKMDmj3J-eGefyjbO4ydqoB5RwpDOc","note":"The ledger sat in a press in the Chancery, inside the Palace; Dolfin never handed it over."},{"id":"8iUBF1VMQzHcvPCVp_sA4","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","issueId":"prose-leak-1A9LiuphoOBJ5D9v0DnRG-ElASJa2Vxm6oNK1C3eGhQ","note":"\"collar\" here is Isotta's dress collar, not the lace."},{"id":"GVh8eqI-fDRW2YoEUBKr5","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","issueId":"prose-leak-1A9LiuphoOBJ5D9v0DnRG-zIrHkBr1jO8kZ1DDBUd9J","note":""},{"id":"SenWIylAElYf1ySe1pigV","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","issueId":"prose-leak-1A9LiuphoOBJ5D9v0DnRG-zIrHkBr1jO8kZ1DDBUd9J","note":"Planted on purpose: Lucia's cover story names the stitch."},{"id":"VrTyzGjRJ18zxFQDICSTf","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","issueId":"item-handoff-MJYhBoZeAPreO4Kr6mblb-txMWhAmoQ5MKX1y3i93KU-ovmu-1zRoQUHJcKMDmj3J-eGefyjbO4ydqoB5RwpDOc","note":""},{"id":"cBv2OL1yeWS6Tf0Ez3kIg","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","issueId":"prose-leak-1A9LiuphoOBJ5D9v0DnRG-ElASJa2Vxm6oNK1C3eGhQ","note":""}],"writingLogs":[{"id":"gfyKH1r4pLwKNf0R238N0","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","date":"2026-10-18","words":25173,"createdAt":1792360326666,"updatedAt":1792364238832}],"sceneRevisions":[{"id":"-ODaKIdmQ1OhruKvZQpa6","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"VvxSkA1OXIEZRFdcspW_i","text":"They came down the Censori into the courtyard, and Orsato was there.\n\nHe was not pretending to be a guard any more. He was standing by the great bronze well-head in the middle of the courtyard in his black cap, with his hands behind his back, looking at nothing, the way the Capi had looked at nothing as they walked to the boat; and as Lucia came down the last steps with her basket he turned his head and looked at her, and she felt it go right through the pillow and the linen and the boards and the calf, and she thought, he knows. He can see it. He can see the book through the basket the way he saw me through the storeroom door.\n\n\"Don't stop,\" said Matteo pleasantly beside her. \"Smile at him. He's a friend of mine.\"\n\nShe did not smile. She walked. Across the courtyard, past the well-head, past Orsato, who did not move, through the Porta del Frumento, where the sun hit her in the face like a slap and the noise of the Molo came back all at once, trumpets, bells, the roar of the people as the Bucintoro, far out on the water, came about in a great slow curve towards the Lido.\n\n\"He's following,\" said Matteo. \"Good. Don't look.\"\n\nThey went along the Molo, through the crowd, past the column of San Todaro where the man in the grey coat had finished his fritter and was licking his fingers, past the Zecca, to the place at the end of the Molo where the boats were, hundreds of them, gondolas and sandoli and barges and the big painted peote of the guilds, all jostling and rocking at the steps waiting to follow the Doge to the sea. And in among them, very small, a sandolo from Murano with Piero at the oar and a woman in black sitting in the bottom of it, with a basket on her knee.\n\nIt was the same basket. Lucia had made sure of that; she had bought two from the same man at the Rialto on Monday and rubbed the new one with ash and grease until it looked as old as hers. And the pillow on top of it was the same, or near enough, with a half-made border pinned to it and bobbins hanging down.\n\n\"Zia!\" She waved. It was the most natural thing in the world. A girl from Burano, in the crowd on the Sensa, seeing her aunt in a boat. \"Zia, hold this, I want to go and see the Doge come back—\"\n\nShe went down the wet steps. Her aunt reached up. There was a moment, one, with a gondola bumping on one side and a fat man from the butchers' guild shouting on the other, when both baskets were in the air between them; and then Lucia was climbing back up the steps with a basket on her arm that weighed exactly what hers had weighed when she came out of the Censori, because Fra Anselmo had weighed them on his scales, and her aunt was sitting in the bottom of the boat with a basket on her knee and her hand flat on the pillow, the way she had put her hand flat on the false leaf at dawn.\n\nPiero pushed off. The sandolo slid out between the gondolas, and turned, and went away towards the Arsenal and the open water and Murano, small and black and unremarkable among a thousand boats.\n\nLucia did not watch it go. She turned round on the top step and looked straight across the Molo at Orsato, who had stopped ten paces off by a stall selling glass beads, and was holding one up to the light as though he were thinking of buying it.\n\nHe looked at her. He looked at the basket on her arm. He looked, for a long moment, out at the water where the Murano boat had gone.\n\nThen he put the bead down, and paid for it anyway, and walked away along the Molo towards the Rialto, without hurrying, to tell his master where the girl was going.\n\n# Gold on the Table\n\n## The ash pit\n[\n  Place: #The Ferro furnace\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The Glass Ledger:#The Ferro furnace\n]\n\nShe did not open it in the boat.\n\nShe sat in the bottom of Piero's sandolo all the way across the lagoon with the basket on her knees and her hand flat on the pillow, and she did not lift the pillow, and she did not look under the linen, and she did not let herself think the thing she was thinking, which was that it might not be there. That the girl might have lost her nerve in the Chancery, at the last, and dropped the bobbins and picked them up and walked out with the friar's book still in her basket and the real one still in the press. That she was carrying three days of an old man's forgery across the water like a relic. Piero rowed, and said nothing, and once, off San Michele, where the dead of the city lay behind their red wall, he crossed himself, and she did too.\n\nAt home she barred the water-door and the street door both and carried the basket into the workshop and set it on the marver and lifted the pillow off.\n\nIt was there. Of course it was there. Boards and calf, swollen at the lower edge, and one brass corner missing, the bottom left, exactly as the friar had made it. She opened it at the front and read the first page, in her husband's father's hand, a receipt for a green glass for bottles that nobody had made since she was a girl. She opened it at the back. And then she turned to the end, to the gutter, where Anselmo's false leaf had been sewn in so neatly that she had had to put her nail to the stitches to find them, and there was no leaf. There was a stub of cut paper no wider than a fingernail. There was Bortolo's knife-cut, slightly ragged at the bottom where his hand had slipped, the night he did it, in February, with her holding the candle.\n\nShe put her hand flat on the stub and stood like that for a long time.\n\nThen she wrapped it again in the oilcloth Orsato had brought it away in, which she had kept, and carried it to the great furnace, and lay down on her side on the cold floor, and reached into the ash pit under the grate as far as her arm would go, past the old ash and the grit and the bones of a mouse, to the ledge at the back where Bortolo's father had kept his money in the plague year, and pushed it in. When she stood up she was grey to the shoulder. She washed her arm in the bucket by the marver, and washed her face, and changed into the black wool and the good collar; and she put on, for the first time, the little collar of punto in aria that Lucia had made, with the vine and the pomegranates and the birds and the border of knots like a fence round a garden, and pinned it at her throat with a pin of Murano glass.\n\nThen she went to sell the Fleming nothing.\n\n## Gold on the table\n[\n  Place: #The Fleming's lodging\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Pieter Vos @@Lucia Zen @@Matteo Lion\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?The Glass Ledger is back with Isotta:@Pieter Vos:before\n]\n\nThe gold was on the table.\n\nVos had done it properly. He had taken the Turkish carpet off and laid a white cloth instead, and on the white cloth stood four leather bags, tied at the neck and sealed, and beside them a little brass balance with its pans level and a stack of Venetian sequins in a column to show the weight, catching the candlelight like the scales of a fish. It was the most money Isotta had ever seen in one place. It looked like a stage. It looked like the altar of a church in a town that did not believe in God.\n\n\"Madonna.\" Vos bowed. He was in black silk again, with a ruff, as though for a wedding. \"And the lacemaker. And—\" he looked at Matteo, in the doorway behind them, and something went across his face very fast and was gone \"—a notary. You've brought a notary. Very wise. One should always have a contract witnessed.\"\n\n\"Messer Lion is my friend,\" said Isotta. \"He carried my basket.\"\n\nLucia set the basket on the table, by the gold. She did it carefully, as if it were heavy. It was not heavy. Vos's eyes went to it and stayed.\n\n\"May I?\"\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nHe lifted the pillow off, gently, with both hands, the way a man lifts a sleeping cat. He folded back the linen underneath.\n\nThere was lace under it. Nothing but lace. Collars and cuffs and a border for an altar-cloth, a dozen pieces, Lucia's best, in a white drift that rose up a little as the linen came away, like breath.\n\nVos looked at it. He looked for a long time. Then he looked at Isotta, and she saw the merchant go out of his face and something else come into it, something smaller and older and more frightened.\n\n\"Where is the book?\"\n\n\"I couldn't get it,\" Isotta said. \"The press was locked. The clerk wouldn't open it. We tried, Messer Vos. I'm sorry. But I didn't want to come empty-handed, after you'd been so kind, so I've brought you the best lace in Burano at a very good price. For the ladies of Antwerp. They say the ladies of Antwerp have no lace worth the name.\"\n\nHe did not answer. He was listening; she saw him doing it. Listening past her, past the door, down the stair, to the Ruga below, where the evening crowd was loud with the Sensa fair, and where, over the noise, very faint, there was a sound of many feet on stone coming steadily nearer and not hurrying.\n\n\"You stupid woman,\" Pieter Vos said softly. \"What have you done?\"\n\n\"I?\" said Isotta Ferro. \"I've come to sell you lace.\"\n\nThe door opened.\n\nIt opened all the way, so hard it struck the wall, and Zuane Dolfin came into the room in a gown of black damask with a fur at the collar, and behind him two fanti with drawn swords, and behind them, slowly, because he was seventy and the stair was steep and he was not a man who had ever needed to hurry for anyone, an old man in the black of the Ten, with a gold chain on his chest and a face like a shut door. And behind him, last of all, in his black cap, Orsato.\n\n\"Excellency,\" Dolfin said, turning to the old man, and his voice was the pleasant tired voice of the little room at the Palace, a voice for small business between friends. \"Messer Contarini. Here is the widow Ferro of Murano. You see her with the Fleming, Vos, of Antwerp, who has been asking questions about the Art in every malvasia in the city since the spring. You see the gold. You see—\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\nHe had seen the lace.\n\nThere was a silence in the Fleming's room so complete that Isotta heard the candle flame move. Dolfin looked at the lace on the table, the white drift of it rising out of the girl's basket beside four bags of gold, and the pleasant tired face did not change at all, and that was how she knew. A man who has nothing to fear looks surprised. Zuane Dolfin looked at the lace the way a man looks at a letter he did not expect to see again, in a hand he knows is his own.\n\n\"Well, Dolfin?\" said the old man, Contarini. He had one hand cupped behind his ear. \"What do I see? I see lace.\"\n\n\"She has hidden it,\" said Dolfin. \"Excellency, the book was taken from the Chancery this morning while the Signoria was on the water. This girl carried it out. My man saw her. Orsato—\"\n\n\"I saw the girl,\" said Orsato, from the doorway, \"come down the Censori with a basket.\"\n\n\"There. You see. She has hidden it, she has given it to someone, the boat—Excellency, send to the Chancery. Send to the press. It will be empty.\"\n\nContarini looked at him for a moment out of his shut-door face. Then he turned his head, and said to Orsato, without raising his voice, \"Go to the Chancery. Wake the boy Sanudo if he's asleep, and have him open the press in front of you, and bring me whatever is on the middle shelf. Run.\"\n\nOrsato lifted his cap. He did not run. He was not, Isotta thought, a man who had ever run in his life. But he went.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Fleming's lodging\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Pieter Vos @@Lucia Zen @@Matteo Lion @@Zuane Dolfin @@Alvise Contarini @@Orsato\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^Dolfin's letter to Vos:@Alvise Contarini ^The false ledger:@Zuane Dolfin\n  Facts: ?Dolfin promised Vos a copy of the calcedonio receipt:@Alvise Contarini ?Matteo informs for Dolfin:@Alvise Contarini ?Dolfin is arrested:@Isotta Ferro ?Dolfin is arrested:@Lucia Zen ?Dolfin is arrested:@Matteo Lion ?Dolfin is arrested:@Pieter Vos ?Dolfin is arrested:@Orsato ?Dolfin is arrested:@Alvise Contarini ?Dolfin is arrested:reader\n]\n\nIt was half an hour, by the bells. They waited it out in the Fleming's room with the candles burning down, like people waiting for a birth or a death: Vos at the window with his back to them, Dolfin by the door with his hands folded inside his sleeves, the two fanti at either side of it with their swords grounded and their eyes on the gold, and the old man in the only good chair, with his eyes shut, so that Isotta thought he had gone to sleep until he said, without opening them:\n\n\"Lion.\"\n\nMatteo stepped forward. \"Excellency.\"\n\n\"I know you. You took a deposition for me in the matter of the Grimani wills. Nine years ago. You had a good voice. They struck you off.\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"For what?\"\n\n\"For selling a copy of a sealed deposition to the family it concerned, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Did you do it?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nContarini opened his eyes. \"Well. That's something.\" He held out his hand, palm up, without looking at it, as though he expected something to be put into it, as presumably all his life things had been. \"What have you got for me? You've got something. You've been standing there like a man with a stone in his shoe.\"\n\nMatteo took the letter out of his gown.\n\nIsotta watched Dolfin see it. He was very good. He had been very good all his life. He did not move; he only looked at the letter in the Chancery fold, with its plain red seal lifted cleanly and pressed back, going from Matteo's hand into the hand of the Capo of the Ten, and something went out of him, very quietly, like the air out of a bellows when the boy at the handle stops.\n\nContarini read it. He read it twice, holding it far from his face, the way old men read. Then he looked at Dolfin over the top of it.\n\n\"Your hand, Zuane.\"\n\n\"A forgery, Excellency. That man has been in my pay for four years as an informer. He's a known gambler; he owes money everywhere; he has every reason—\"\n\n\"He has been in your pay for four years,\" said Contarini, \"as an informer. That is very interesting. That was going to be the next thing I asked him.\" He folded the letter. \"And it's your hand. I've had your hand on my table every morning for eleven years. You make your g like a little fish.\"\n\nThen the bells of San Bartolomeo struck the half, and there were feet on the stair, and Orsato came back into the room carrying a book.\n\nIt was wrapped in nothing. He carried it in both hands against his chest, the way he had carried it out of Isotta's house on the day of the sirocco, and he crossed the room to the old man's chair and set it on his knees.\n\n\"From the middle shelf of the press, Excellency,\" he said. \"Sanudo opened it in front of me. It was locked, and the key was on his belt, and he swears the book has not left the Chancery except to be read this morning, in his sight, on the authority of a warrant he could not now find, and put back.\"\n\nContarini looked down at the book on his knees. Boards and calf. Swollen at the lower edge. One brass corner missing, the bottom left.\n\n\"So it was never taken,\" he said, and looked at Dolfin.\n\nAnd Zuane Dolfin, secretary of the Ten for eleven years, who had never once in Isotta's sight done anything he had not decided to do, crossed the room in three strides and took the book off the old man's knees, which nobody in the room had ever seen anyone do, and opened it at the end. Isotta saw his fingers go into the gutter for the stub. She saw them find, instead, a leaf, a whole leaf, sewn in so neatly that you had to put your nail to the stitches to find them; and on it, in the cramped brown hand of Bortolo Ferro, the word CALCEDONIO.\n\n\"There,\" said Dolfin. His voice had gone high and strange. \"There. You see. You see, Excellency? The leaf. The leaf was in it all the time. She put it back. She had it all along and she's put it back, here, it's the receipt, it's the calcedonio, it's what I promised the Fleming—\"\n\nHe heard it. Isotta saw him hear it, a moment after everyone else in the room.\n\nNobody said anything. Over by the window, Pieter Vos put his face in his hands.\n\n\"Take him,\" said Alvise Contarini to the fanti, quite gently. \"And the Fleming. Impound the gold.\" He held out his hand again, palm up, and Orsato took the book out of Dolfin's hands, not roughly, the way he had taken it from the shelf by San Donato, and put it back into the old man's. \"And give me that. It is, after all, the property of the Republic.\"\n\nWhen they had gone, the two fanti and the Fleming and the secretary between them, down the stair into the noise of the fair, the old man sat for a while with the book closed on his knees and looked at Isotta Ferro, and at the collar at her throat, the vine and the pomegranates and the birds.\n\n\"Very pretty lace,\" he said. \"Your niece's?\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Mm.\" He turned the book over in his hands, the false book, Anselmo's three nights' work, with every weight wrong. \"I'm told your son has the gaol fever.\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"That's the spring tides.\" The Capo of the Ten stood up, slowly, in stages, with one hand on Matteo Lion's arm, which Matteo gave him without being asked. \"It comes up through the floor.\"","wordCount":3225,"createdAt":1792362131112},{"id":"-_ZD5vHrHda5FNrVUNQ6u","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"M5Y_AFQAZ3U49Fmor2WYH","text":"[\n  Place: #The Molo\n  Characters: @@Lucia Zen @@Matteo Lion @@Orsato\n  When: Thursday 19 May\n  Items: ^The false ledger:@Lucia Zen ^The forged warrant:@Matteo Lion\n]\n\nOn the morning of the Sensa the whole city walked to the sea.\n\nLucia had seen it before, every year of her life, from a boat on the edge of the crowd with her father swearing at the other boats; she had never seen it from the inside. From the inside it was not a crowd. It was weather. It moved in gusts and eddies along the Riva and across the Piazzetta, it sang, it shouted, it sold things, it smelled of fried fish and orange-water and incense and the sweat of ten thousand people in their best clothes, and it carried her along the Molo with her basket on her arm and her pillow on top of the basket the way the tide carries a cork.\n\nThe basket was heavy. Nobody would have known. She carried it the way she carried it every day, on the crook of her left arm with her hand over the pillow, and the pillow on top had a half-made border pinned on it with the bobbins hanging down, and under the pillow, wrapped in a cloth of unbleached linen, was a book in boards and calf, swollen at the lower edge, with one brass corner missing, the bottom left.\n\nFra Anselmo had brought it to Murano at dawn. He had not slept, he said, for three nights, and looked it; his good eye was red and his bad eye was redder, and his fingers were black to the second joint. Her aunt had opened it at the front and read the first page and closed it and opened it at the back and read the last, and then turned to the end, where the stub was, and found the leaf sewn in there, in Zio Bortolo's cramped brown hand, CALCEDONIO, with the weights all wrong. She had put her hand flat on it for a moment. Then she had kissed the friar on both cheeks, which Lucia had never seen her do to anyone, not even Zio Bortolo, and he had gone pink to the edge of his tonsure.\n\nAt the third hour the bells began.\n\nAll of them. San Marco first, the great bell, the Marangona, so deep Lucia felt it in her teeth rather than heard it; and then San Zaccaria and San Giorgio across the water and the Pietà and San Moisè, and the ones further off, Santi Apostoli and the Frari and the Madonna dell'Orto, until the whole city was ringing like a struck glass. And out of the Porta della Carta, between the guards, came the Doge.\n\nLucia had never been so close. He was an old man in cloth of gold, with the horned cap on his head and a face like a walnut, and he walked very slowly because of the weight of the gold, between two men holding up the train, and behind him came the Signoria in crimson, and behind them the Capi of the Ten in black, three old men in a row looking at nothing. The crowd went down on its knees. Lucia went down on her knees with her basket in her arms, and through the legs of the people in front she watched the procession go along the Molo to the water, where the Bucintoro lay against the steps like a great golden animal, and all its oars stood up in the air at once in a salute, two hundred of them, white, with a sound like a flock of birds rising.\n\nWhen she stood up, Matteo Lion was beside her.\n\nShe had seen him only once, the night of the glory-hole, in the bad light of the furnace. In the morning sun he looked older and more respectable, in a notary's black gown with a roll of papers in his hand and a cap like a pudding, and he was smiling at the Bucintoro as though he had paid for it.\n\n\"You're very punctual,\" he said, without looking at her. \"Don't look round. There's a man by the column of San Todaro in a grey coat eating a fritter. He's been there since the second hour. And Orsato is at the Porta della Carta, behind the guard, pretending to be a guard.\"\n\nLucia did not look round.\n\n\"The Capi are on the boat,\" Matteo said. \"All three of them. And Dolfin isn't.\"\n\n\"Where is he?\"\n\n\"He sent his excuses this morning. A fever.\" Matteo smiled more widely at the Bucintoro, which was moving now, very slowly, out from the steps into the basin, with the trumpets going on the poop and the oars rising and falling. \"I expect he'll recover by noon. Have you got it?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Have you got the other thing?\"\n\nShe moved her right hand on the basket handle, and felt, in the pocket of her apron, the little bag of bobbins that she had wound the night before: forty of them, old ones, boxwood and bone, every one she could find in her mother's house.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then walk with me,\" said Matteo Lion, \"like a girl from Burano who's been asked to carry something for a notary and doesn't think much of it, and when we're on the stair, don't speak at all. Your accent is very charming and very Burano and the guard on the Censori would remember it for the rest of his life.\"","wordCount":915,"createdAt":1792361923240},{"id":"2f7cvtVgHWEP9okX33_v6","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"VvxSkA1OXIEZRFdcspW_i","text":"[\n  Place: #The Molo\n  Characters: @@Lucia Zen @@Matteo Lion @@Isotta Ferro @@Orsato\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The Glass Ledger:@Isotta Ferro\n  Facts: ?The Glass Ledger is back with Isotta:@Isotta Ferro ?The Glass Ledger is back with Isotta:@Lucia Zen ?The Glass Ledger is back with Isotta:@Matteo Lion ?The Glass Ledger is back with Isotta:reader\n]\n\nThey came down the Censori into the courtyard, and Orsato was there.\n\nHe was not pretending to be a guard any more. He was standing by the great bronze well-head in the middle of the courtyard in his black cap, with his hands behind his back, looking at nothing, the way the Capi had looked at nothing as they walked to the boat; and as Lucia came down the last steps with her basket he turned his head and looked at her, and she felt it go right through the pillow and the linen and the boards and the calf, and she thought, he knows. He can see it. He can see the book through the basket the way he saw me through the storeroom door.\n\n\"Don't stop,\" said Matteo pleasantly beside her. \"Smile at him. He's a friend of mine.\"\n\nShe did not smile. She walked. Across the courtyard, past the well-head, past Orsato, who did not move, through the Porta del Frumento, where the sun hit her in the face like a slap and the noise of the Molo came back all at once, trumpets, bells, the roar of the people as the Bucintoro, far out on the water, came about in a great slow curve towards the Lido.\n\n\"He's following,\" said Matteo. \"Good. Don't look.\"\n\nThey went along the Molo, through the crowd, past the column of San Todaro where the man in the grey coat had finished his fritter and was licking his fingers, past the Zecca, to the place at the end of the Molo where the boats were, hundreds of them, gondolas and sandoli and barges and the big painted peote of the guilds, all jostling and rocking at the steps waiting to follow the Doge to the sea. And in among them, very small, a sandolo from Murano with Piero at the oar and a woman in black sitting in the bottom of it, with a basket on her knee.\n\nIt was the same basket. Lucia had made sure of that; she had bought two from the same man at the Rialto on Monday and rubbed the new one with ash and grease until it looked as old as hers. And the pillow on top of it was the same, or near enough, with a half-made border pinned to it and bobbins hanging down.\n\n\"Zia!\" She waved. It was the most natural thing in the world. A girl from Burano, in the crowd on the Sensa, seeing her aunt in a boat. \"Zia, hold this, I want to go and see the Doge come back—\"\n\nShe went down the wet steps. Her aunt reached up. There was a moment, one, with a gondola bumping on one side and a fat man from the butchers' guild shouting on the other, when both baskets were in the air between them; and then Lucia was climbing back up the steps with a basket on her arm that weighed exactly what hers had weighed when she came out of the Censori, because Fra Anselmo had weighed them on his scales, and her aunt was sitting in the bottom of the boat with a basket on her knee and her hand flat on the pillow, the way she had put her hand flat on the false leaf at dawn.\n\nPiero pushed off. The sandolo slid out between the gondolas, and turned, and went away towards the Arsenal and the open water and Murano, small and black and unremarkable among a thousand boats.\n\nLucia did not watch it go. She turned round on the top step and looked straight across the Molo at Orsato, who had stopped ten paces off by a stall selling glass beads, and was holding one up to the light as though he were thinking of buying it.\n\nHe looked at her. He looked at the basket on her arm. He looked, for a long moment, out at the water where the Murano boat had gone.\n\nThen he put the bead down, and paid for it anyway, and walked away along the Molo towards the Rialto, without hurrying, to tell his master where the girl was going.","wordCount":745,"createdAt":1792361923242},{"id":"2xP7_oTBRiwUNvLntwMpX","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"Hq8_8_xvRODxArlMBYfhb","text":"Outside it was evening, and warm, the first warm evening of the year, with the first mosquitoes coming up off the canals like smoke. The bridge was crowded with people going home and people going out, and the shutters of the shops along the Ruga were going up with a sound like a long roll of drums. Her aunt walked fast, and did not speak, and Lucia had almost to run to keep beside her.\n\nAt the top of the bridge her aunt stopped. Below them the Grand Canal was full of boats and the light was going pink on the water, and a man on a barge was singing something about a girl from Chioggia with a voice so bad it was almost beautiful.\n\n\"Well?\" said her aunt.\n\n\"He knew about the leaf.\"\n\n\"Nane told him.\"\n\n\"Nane told him there was a last thing. He didn't tell him what. Zia, he wasn't surprised. When you said you could get the book out of the Ten's own house, he wasn't surprised. He was pleased, he was greedy, but he wasn't surprised. People are surprised when you say a thing like that.\" Lucia gripped the warm stone of the parapet. \"And he turned the letter over. On the desk. When we came in.\"\n\n\"Merchants turn their letters over. They're all thieves; they think everyone else is.\"\n\n\"And he said the Sensa,\" Lucia said. \"You said the day after the Feast. You didn't say which Feast. There are three Feasts in May. And he said the Zeeridder sails the morning after the Sensa, as you wish.\"\n\nHer aunt was quiet. On the barge the man had come to the part of the song where the girl from Chioggia drowned herself, and was making the most of it.\n\n\"Everybody in Venice knows the Sensa is the only Feast that matters,\" Isotta said at last. \"He's been here a year. He'd have learned that much.\"\n\n\"Zia—\"\n\n\"Lucia.\" Her aunt turned to her, and put both hands on her shoulders, and Lucia saw in the pink light how tired she was, how the skin under her eyes had gone thin and dark like the skin of a bruised plum. \"I don't trust him. I don't trust Matteo Lion and I don't entirely trust Fra Anselmo, God forgive me, and the man I trust least in the world is Zuane Dolfin, who is the only one of them who has never lied to me. I don't have to trust them. I have to use them. That's all.\" She let go. \"And he's not getting the calcedonio. Whatever happens. He's getting two hundred and eleven leaves and a widow who keeps house and has a very bad memory for weights.\"\n\n\"And Nane?\"\n\n\"Nane will have a bench in Antwerp and hate it, and in five years he'll come home and the Ten will have forgotten him, and he'll make calcedonio on Murano, out of your collar, and be famous.\" Her aunt took her arm. \"Come on. Piero won't wait after the Ave Maria and I'm not rowing you back to Burano in the dark.\"\n\nThey went down off the bridge into the crowd. Lucia looked back once, up at the windows above the spice-merchant's, and there was a candle lit in one of them now; and for a moment a man stood in it, small and neat, looking down at the bridge where they had been. Then he drew the curtain.","wordCount":574,"createdAt":1792361556964},{"id":"3XQyC3kIU_Vr-Gn0VOWjM","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"X5cj4zTjmev3PwWKCfkEx","text":"It was half an hour, by the bells. They waited it out in the Fleming's room with the candles burning down, like people waiting for a birth or a death: Vos at the window with his back to them, Dolfin by the door with his hands folded inside his sleeves, the two fanti at either side of it with their swords grounded and their eyes on the gold, and the old man in the only good chair, with his eyes shut, so that Isotta thought he had gone to sleep until he said, without opening them:\n\n\"Lion.\"\n\nMatteo stepped forward. \"Excellency.\"\n\n\"I know you. You took a deposition for me in the matter of the Grimani wills. Nine years ago. You had a good voice. They struck you off.\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"For what?\"\n\n\"For selling a copy of a sealed deposition to the family it concerned, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Did you do it?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nContarini opened his eyes. \"Well. That's something.\" He held out his hand, palm up, without looking at it, as though he expected something to be put into it, as presumably all his life things had been. \"What have you got for me? You've got something. You've been standing there like a man with a stone in his shoe.\"\n\nMatteo took the letter out of his gown.\n\nIsotta watched Dolfin see it. He was very good. He had been very good all his life. He did not move; he only looked at the letter in the Chancery fold, with its plain red seal lifted cleanly and pressed back, going from Matteo's hand into the hand of the Capo of the Ten, and something went out of him, very quietly, like the air out of a bellows when the boy at the handle stops.\n\nContarini read it. He read it twice, holding it far from his face, the way old men read. Then he looked at Dolfin over the top of it.\n\n\"Your hand, Zuane.\"\n\n\"A forgery, Excellency. That man has been in my pay for four years as an informer. He's a known gambler; he owes money everywhere; he has every reason—\"\n\n\"He has been in your pay for four years,\" said Contarini, \"as an informer. That is very interesting. That was going to be the next thing I asked him.\" He folded the letter. \"And it's your hand. I've had your hand on my table every morning for eleven years. You make your g like a little fish.\"\n\nThen the bells of San Bartolomeo struck the half, and there were feet on the stair, and Orsato came back into the room carrying a book.\n\nIt was wrapped in nothing. He carried it in both hands against his chest, the way he had carried it out of Isotta's house on the day of the sirocco, and he crossed the room to the old man's chair and set it on his knees.\n\n\"From the middle shelf of the press, Excellency,\" he said. \"Sanudo opened it in front of me. It was locked, and the key was on his belt, and he swears the book has not left the Chancery except to be read this morning, in his sight, on the authority of a warrant he could not now find, and put back.\"\n\nContarini looked down at the book on his knees. Boards and calf. Swollen at the lower edge. One brass corner missing, the bottom left.\n\n\"So it was never taken,\" he said, and looked at Dolfin.\n\nAnd Zuane Dolfin, secretary of the Ten for eleven years, who had never once in Isotta's sight done anything he had not decided to do, crossed the room in three strides and took the book off the old man's knees, which nobody in the room had ever seen anyone do, and opened it at the end. Isotta saw his fingers go into the gutter for the stub. She saw them find, instead, a leaf, a whole leaf, sewn in so neatly that you had to put your nail to the stitches to find them; and on it, in the cramped brown hand of Bortolo Ferro, the word CALCEDONIO.\n\n\"There,\" said Dolfin. His voice had gone high and strange. \"There. You see. You see, Excellency? The leaf. The leaf was in it all the time. She put it back. She had it all along and she's put it back, here, it's the receipt, it's the calcedonio, it's what I promised the Fleming—\"\n\nHe heard it. Isotta saw him hear it, a moment after everyone else in the room.\n\nNobody said anything. Over by the window, Pieter Vos put his face in his hands.\n\n\"Take him,\" said Alvise Contarini to the fanti, quite gently. \"And the Fleming. Impound the gold.\" He held out his hand again, palm up, and Orsato took the book out of Dolfin's hands, not roughly, the way he had taken it from the shelf by San Donato, and put it back into the old man's. \"And give me that. It is, after all, the property of the Republic.\"\n\nWhen they had gone, the two fanti and the Fleming and the secretary between them, down the stair into the noise of the fair, the old man sat for a while with the book closed on his knees and looked at Isotta Ferro, and at the collar at her throat, the vine and the pomegranates and the birds.\n\n\"Very pretty lace,\" he said. \"Your niece's?\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Mm.\" He turned the book over in his hands, the false book, Anselmo's three nights' work, with every weight wrong. \"I'm told your son has the gaol fever.\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"That's the spring tides.\" The Capo of the Ten stood up, slowly, in stages, with one hand on Matteo Lion's arm, which Matteo gave him without being asked. \"It comes up through the floor.\"\n\n# Cristallo\n\n[\n  Place: #The Ferro furnace\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Nane Ferro @@Matteo Lion @@Fra Anselmo @@Lucia Zen @@Orsato\n  When: +2 weeks\n  Items: ^The lattimo bead:@Nane Ferro\n  Facts: ?Dolfin is arrested:@Nane Ferro ?Dolfin is arrested:@Fra Anselmo ?The calcedonio receipt is worked into Lucia's lace:@Nane Ferro\n]\n\nThey let him out on the second of June, a Thursday, in the morning, without any ceremony at all.\n\nThere was no sentence. There had been none on the Sensa either, Matteo told her afterwards; the Council had sat as it always sat after the Doge came back from the Lido, and the matter of the three subjects of the Republic taken trying to carry the Art abroad had been deferred, on the motion of the Capo Contarini, to a later sitting, on the grounds that one of the secretaries who had prepared the depositions had become unavailable. Unavailable was the word. Zuane Dolfin was unavailable in a room at the top of the Palace, under the leads, where the sun came through the roof in summer and turned the air to soup, and where, Matteo said, he had been visited every day by a different member of the Ten and had told each of them something slightly different. Pieter Vos had been put on the Zeeridder at Malamocco without his gold and without his cargo and told that if he were seen in the lagoon again he would be hanged between the columns. And at the later sitting, which nobody outside the room ever heard about, it had been decided that a boy of seventeen with the gaol fever, whose father had been a master of the Art and whose mother had that week rendered the Republic a service it preferred not to describe, might be returned to his family on Murano on the understanding that he would never again try to leave it, and that his mother would keep the Ferro furnace lit.\n\n\"Keep it lit,\" Isotta said. \"Those were the words?\"\n\n\"Those were the words. In the minute.\" Matteo smiled. \"I wrote the minute.\"\n\nHe had a new gown, and ink on his fingers that was new ink, and he went to the Palace every morning at the second hour and came home at dusk to a room in the Calle dei Fabbri that he did not have to lie to anyone about. The Capo Contarini had needed, he had said, a notary in the Avogaria who knew precisely how secretaries of the Ten were corrupted, and could not think of a better one. It was on probation. Matteo said everything in Venice was on probation; it was only that some people were told.\n\nOrsato brought the boy.\n\nHe came up the Rio dei Vetrai in a boat without singing, as he had come the first time, with two fanti at the oars, and in the bottom of the boat, wrapped in the same old woman's blanket, was Nane. He was thin. He was yellow under the eyes, and his hair had grown down over his collar, and when the boat touched the step and he stood up he had to put his hand on Orsato's shoulder to do it, and Orsato let him.\n\nIsotta did not go down the step. She found that she could not. She stood in the water-door with her hands in her apron, and behind her in the workshop Lucia and the friar and the notary stood too, not speaking, and her son came up the three wet steps one at a time and stopped in front of her.\n\n\"Mamma,\" he said. \"I kept the blanket dry.\"\n\nShe put her arms round him. He smelt of the Pozzi, of the sea and what the sea left behind. She did not care. Over his shoulder she saw Orsato, standing in the boat with his black cap in his hand, looking up at the house; and she remembered the day of the sirocco, and the cap lifted once on the Ponte della Paglia, and how long she had wondered whose side he was on.\n\n\"Messer Orsato,\" she said. \"Will you come in? There's wine.\"\n\n\"Thank you, Madonna. No.\" He put his cap on. \"I'm on duty.\" He looked at the boy, and at her, and something happened in his face that was not quite a smile, because it was not a face that had been built for it. \"It was a waste of good hands,\" he said. \"I told you. Good day.\"\n\nThe boat went away down the Rio dei Vetrai, past the Seguso works and the Barovier works and the little bridge where the children sat at night to watch the glow. And Nane put his hand into his shirt and brought out something small and held it out to her on his palm.\n\nA bead. White, round as a pea, with a single blue thread wound once about it.\n\n\"Messer Lion gave it back to me,\" he said. \"In the boat, on the Sensa night, when he came down to the wells with the Capo. He said it was mine to give you and not his.\" He looked past her at Matteo, and then at her again, and he was seven, holding out his burnt hand. \"Is it round enough?\"\n\nShe took it. She closed her hand on it, as she had at the marver with the glory-hole roaring.\n\n\"It'll do,\" she said.","wordCount":1865,"createdAt":1792362332435},{"id":"4UUivo42fWRDZbwPeiysW","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"0YoSvhnQYY2tWtmUCGQdm","text":"[\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Orsato\n  Place: #The Ferro furnace\n  When: 21 April 1605\n  Items: ^The Glass Ledger\n\n","wordCount":17,"createdAt":1792360466473},{"id":"73ZYacB_ye_GPOGwsXF9R","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"WfzecJCXcnkhuVLjye4PH","text":"\"A girl,\" Dolfin said. \"How delightful.\"\n\nThe vine on the pergola had put out its first leaves since Matteo's last visit, small and crumpled and very green, like the hands of a newborn. Dolfin was not sitting under it this time; he was walking, slowly, round and round the well-head, with his hands behind his back, and Matteo had to stand in the middle of the gravel and turn to keep facing him, like a horse on a lunge rein.\n\n\"Her niece. Lucia Zen. A lacemaker from Burano. Nineteen.\"\n\n\"Pretty?\"\n\n\"I've never seen her.\"\n\n\"Then you'll have that to look forward to.\" Dolfin completed a circuit. \"And the friar is cutting the seal. Our seal. On the Sensa. Up the Censori.\" He said each thing as though he were laying it on a table, to look at it. \"The boy Sanudo. You've been thorough, Matteo. I'd forgotten how thorough you were. It's a pity about the cards.\"\n\n\"Excellency.\" Matteo had not meant to speak. \"The girl. When you come to the Rialto with the Capi—\"\n\n\"The girl will be with her aunt and her aunt will be selling the Republic's property to a foreigner. What becomes of her depends on what her aunt does next.\" Dolfin stopped walking. \"You're not growing a conscience, I hope. At your age it's like growing teeth; it hurts, and nobody admires the result.\"\n\n\"No, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Good.\" Dolfin resumed his circuit. \"There's one thing more I want. When you have the girl in the Chancery with the book in her pillow, I want you to bring her out by the Censori. Not the Scala d'Oro, not the Giganti, not some other little rat-hole of your own. The Censori, and across the courtyard, and out by the Porta del Frumento to the Molo. I'll have a man in the courtyard to see her go. I don't want her taken. I want her watched, all the way to the Rialto, so that when I come in at the Fleming's door the book is on his table and her hands are still warm from it.\"\n\n\"As you say.\"\n\n\"As I say.\" Dolfin smiled at him across the well-head. \"And Matteo. That debt of yours. Two hundred and twelve, you said? I took the liberty of buying it. From the Greek, and from that other fellow at the Frari. I hold all of it now. It seemed tidier.\"\n\nMatteo stood very still on the gravel. Above him a sparrow came down onto the pergola and looked at the new leaves and decided against them and flew away.\n\n\"That's very kind, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Isn't it?\" said Dolfin. \"I thought so.\"\n\n# The Fleming\n\n## The lodging at the Rialto\n[\n  Place: #The Fleming's lodging\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Lucia Zen @@Pieter Vos\n  When: +3 days\n  Facts: ?Vos will pay four hundred ducats and passage for the ledger:@Isotta Ferro ?Vos will pay four hundred ducats and passage for the ledger:@Lucia Zen ?Vos will pay four hundred ducats and passage for the ledger:reader ?Isotta means to steal the ledger back:@Pieter Vos\n]\n\nPieter Vos lodged above a spice-merchant's on the Ruga dei Spezieri, so that the stair up to his rooms smelled of pepper and cloves and something sweeter underneath that Lucia could not name, and by the time they reached his door her eyes were watering and she had sneezed twice. Her aunt did not sneeze. Her aunt went up the stair as though it were the stair of her own house, one hand on the rail and the other holding her skirts out of the dust, and knocked on the Fleming's door with the knuckle of her middle finger, twice, the way you knock on a pot to hear if it is cracked.\n\nLucia had never met a Fleming. She had expected something large and pale and bearded, a sort of walking cheese. Vos was small and neat and brown, with a short grey beard cut close and a doublet of black silk better than anything a Venetian merchant would have worn on a weekday, and he bowed over her aunt's hand like a gentleman, and then over Lucia's, and said in very good Venetian, \"The lacemaker. Your cousin spoke of you. He said you were the only one in the family with any sense.\"\n\n\"My cousin is in the Pozzi,\" said Lucia, \"because of you.\"\n\n\"Lucia,\" said her aunt.\n\n\"No, no. She is right.\" Vos straightened. \"He is in the Pozzi because of me, and because of the Ten, and because of the law, and because he is seventeen and in a hurry. I am sorry for all of those except the last, which he will grow out of if they let him.\" He gestured to the room behind him. \"Please. Come in. I have wine from the Rhine which is terrible and wine from Candia which is not, and I am too old a merchant to make you choose.\"\n\nThe room was full of light from two windows on the Grand Canal and full of things from everywhere: bolts of cloth, a Turkish carpet on the table instead of on the floor, a brass astrolabe, a little lute with a broken string, a stack of ledgers bound in vellum, a box of clear glass beakers in straw that Lucia saw her aunt see, and look away from. There was a writing-desk under one window with a letter half-written on it, and a stick of red wax, and a candle, and a seal. Vos moved between them and the desk as he poured, without seeming to, and turned the letter face down.\n\n\"So,\" he said when they were sitting. \"The widow Ferro. I had hoped to meet you in happier days. Your husband's cristallo is on the tables of four princes in the Low Countries. My masters in Antwerp have been trying to copy it for ten years and they make something very fine that looks like the water in a ditch. I take it you have not come to discuss the weather.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then what have you come to discuss?\"\n\nHer aunt drank a little of the Candia wine. Lucia, watching, saw her hands were perfectly steady, and knew that meant she was terrified.\n\n\"My son told you there was a book.\"\n\n\"He did.\"\n\n\"There is. The Ten have it. They took it from my house the day after he was taken.\" Her aunt set down the cup. \"I can get it back.\"\n\nVos did not move, but something in the room did; it was as though the light from the canal had shifted on the ceiling. Lucia had seen men at the lace-market do the same when a nun brought in a piece of real Venetian point and pretended not to know what it was worth.\n\n\"From the Ten,\" he said.\n\n\"From the Ten.\"\n\n\"May I ask how?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nHe laughed, then, delighted, and poured more wine. \"Good. Good, Madonna. Never tell a buyer how you came by the goods; it only makes him think he could have done it himself. And when you have the book?\"\n\n\"Four hundred ducats,\" said her aunt. \"In gold, not paper. Passage to Antwerp for two, on a ship that will sail the day after the Feast and not one day later. And a place for my son in your masters' glasshouse, as a journeyman, not a sweeper. A bench of his own, in three years.\"\n\n\"And for you?\"\n\n\"I'll keep house for him.\"\n\nVos studied her over his cup. \"The book has a missing leaf,\" he said. \"Your son told me that too. He said his father kept the last thing he made out of it, because he did not trust the book to keep it.\"\n\nLucia felt her hands go cold in her lap. She did not look at her aunt. She looked at the box of glass beakers in their straw, and counted the beakers, seven, and counted them again.\n\n\"My son,\" said Isotta Ferro, \"tells everyone everything. It's why he's in the Pozzi. Two hundred and eleven leaves, Messer Vos. Everything the Ferro made for three generations. Cristallo, lattimo, the aventurine, the blue of Saint Mark, the rose that my husband's father made for the Pope. You may have the whole of it, and if there is some leaf that is not in it, it is a leaf that cost my husband four crucibles and his peace of mind and was not worth it.\" She stood up. \"Four hundred, Messer Vos. Or I find another Fleming. There are a great many of you about.\"\n\nHe did not answer at once. Then he stood too, and bowed again, and when he came up he was smiling the way he had smiled at the door.\n\n\"Four hundred,\" he said. \"And passage on the Zeeridder, which sails from Malamocco on the morning after the Sensa, as you wish. I'll have the gold here, in this room, on the evening of the Feast. Bring me the book when the Doge has had his wedding.\" He took her hand again. \"I would like to have known your husband, Madonna.\"\n\n\"No,\" said her aunt. \"You wouldn't.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Rialto\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Lucia Zen\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?Isotta will not give Vos the calcedonio:@Isotta Ferro ?Isotta will not give Vos the calcedonio:@Lucia Zen ?Isotta will not give Vos the calcedonio:reader\n]\n\nOutside it was evening, and warm, the first warm evening of the year, with the first mosquitoes coming up off the canals like smoke. The bridge was crowded with people going home and people going out, and the shutters of the shops along the Ruga were going up with a sound like a long roll of drums. Her aunt walked fast, and did not speak, and Lucia had almost to run to keep beside her.\n\nAt the top of the bridge her aunt stopped. Below them the Grand Canal was full of boats and the light was going pink on the water, and a man on a barge was singing something about a girl from Chioggia with a voice so bad it was almost beautiful.\n\n\"Well?\" said her aunt.\n\n\"He knew about the leaf.\"\n\n\"Nane told him.\"\n\n\"Nane told him there was a last thing. He didn't tell him what. Zia, he wasn't surprised. When you said you could get the book out of the Ten's own house, he wasn't surprised. He was pleased, he was greedy, but he wasn't surprised. People are surprised when you say a thing like that.\" Lucia gripped the warm stone of the parapet. \"And he turned the letter over. On the desk. When we came in.\"\n\n\"Merchants turn their letters over. They're all thieves; they think everyone else is.\"\n\n\"And he said the Sensa,\" Lucia said. \"You said the day after the Feast. You didn't say which Feast. There are three Feasts in May. And he said the Zeeridder sails the morning after the Sensa, as you wish.\"\n\nHer aunt was quiet. On the barge the man had come to the part of the song where the girl from Chioggia drowned herself, and was making the most of it.\n\n\"Everybody in Venice knows the Sensa is the only Feast that matters,\" Isotta said at last. \"He's been here a year. He'd have learned that much.\"\n\n\"Zia—\"\n\n\"Lucia.\" Her aunt turned to her, and put both hands on her shoulders, and Lucia saw in the pink light how tired she was, how the skin under her eyes had gone thin and dark like the skin of a bruised plum. \"I don't trust him. I don't trust Matteo Lion and I don't entirely trust Fra Anselmo, God forgive me, and the man I trust least in the world is Zuane Dolfin, who is the only one of them who has never lied to me. I don't have to trust them. I have to use them. That's all.\" She let go. \"And he's not getting the calcedonio. Whatever happens. He's getting two hundred and eleven leaves and a widow who keeps house and has a very bad memory for weights.\"\n\n\"And Nane?\"\n\n\"Nane will have a bench in Antwerp and hate it, and in five years he'll come home and the Ten will have forgotten him, and he'll make calcedonio on Murano, out of your collar, and be famous.\" Her aunt took her arm. \"Come on. Piero won't wait after the Ave Maria and I'm not rowing you back to Burano in the dark.\"\n\nThey went down off the bridge into the crowd. Lucia looked back once, up at the windows above the spice-merchant's, and there was a candle lit in one of them now; and for a moment a man stood in it, small and neat, looking down at the bridge where they had been. Then he drew the curtain.","wordCount":2132,"createdAt":1792361421798},{"id":"7XkTTnfBLTPyd2iBoViPD","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"3Ca9TRPohYxSK0z4dLlo_","text":"It took her four.\n\nNot because the numbers were hard. The numbers were the easy part; she made a key on the first night, by candle, on the back of an old pricking: a twist for an ounce, a crossing for a pound, a pin for an hour, a picot for each heat, and the four-petalled flower Suor Benedetta called the Sant'Andrea for the turn of a line. It was the hiding that was hard. A pattern that was only numbers looked like numbers: it had a stiffness to it, a lack of grace, and anyone who had ever made lace would see at a glance that it had been made by someone who was thinking about something else. So she had to make it beautiful. She had to make the zafre into a vine and the copper into a pomegranate and NOT TO STIR into a little border of knots that ran round the whole thing like a fence round a garden, and she had to make it so that the vine and the pomegranate and the fence were the reason for the collar and the numbers were only how many stitches it happened to need.\n\nOn the fourth morning there was no fog. The sun came in flat and gold over the lagoon and lit up the window like a lamp, and she unpinned the collar from the pillow and laid it on the black cloth and sent the boy from next door to Murano for her aunt.\n\nZia Isotta looked at it a long time without touching it.\n\nIt was a small thing, a woman's collar, no wider than two hands. A vine ran round it, heavy with fruit. In the corners there were flowers. Along the edge there was a border of tiny knots, and here and there among the leaves a bird, because Lucia had needed somewhere to put the second heat and a bird had seemed right.\n\n\"Read it to me,\" her aunt said.\n\nSo Lucia read it. She put her finger on the first leaf of the vine and counted the twists aloud and said \"four pounds of ash of Levant,\" and moved along the vine and said \"six of the sand, washed thrice,\" and went round the collar as if it were a clock, through the copper and the silver and the hours and the heats, through the birds, until she came to the border of knots and said, \"and not to stir.\"\n\nWhen she looked up her aunt was crying. Not loudly. The way a pot weeps on the outside when it is very cold and the room is warm.\n\n\"He would have hated it,\" Isotta said. \"He would have said lace is for nuns.\" She wiped her face with the heel of her hand. \"Give me the leaf.\"\n\nThey burned it in the kitchen fire with Lucia's mother at Mass. Her aunt held it by one corner over the coals and it went brown, then black, then a curl of bright orange ran along the edge and for a moment, in the last of the paper, Lucia saw the numbers stand out white, every one of them, as if the fire were reading them too. Then it was ash, and her aunt crumbled the ash in her fingers and dropped it into the coals and dusted her hands, and that was the end of the last leaf of the Glass Ledger.\n\n\"Now,\" Isotta said. \"Now it's in two places. Your hands and mine. And nobody looks at a girl's collar.\"\n\n\"The Grimani will look at it.\"\n\n\"The Grimani will never see it. Make them another.\" Her aunt folded the collar into a square of linen and put it into Lucia's hands and closed Lucia's fingers over it. \"Keep it in your work-basket, under the bobbins, the way you'd keep anything you hadn't finished. Don't hide it. Hidden things get found.\"\n\n\"Zia.\" Lucia held the little bundle. It weighed nothing. \"What are you going to do? About Nane?\"\n\nHer aunt was quiet for a moment, looking at the fire.\n\n\"I'm going to steal your uncle's book back from the Council of Ten,\" she said, \"on the Feast of the Sensa, while the Doge is marrying the sea. With a notary who drinks and a friar who forges seals. I am going to sell it to a Fleming. And then I am going to buy your cousin out of the Pozzi and take him a very long way away.\"\n\nLucia laughed, because it was so absurd. Then she saw her aunt's face and stopped.\n\n\"Can I come?\" she said.\n\n# Seals\n\n## Fra Anselmo's cell\n[\n  Place: #Santa Maria dei Servi\n  Characters: @@Matteo Lion @@Fra Anselmo @@Isotta Ferro\n  When: +2 days\n  Items: ^The forged warrant:@Fra Anselmo\n  Facts: ?The Chancery is entered by the Scala dei Censori:@Isotta Ferro ?The Chancery is entered by the Scala dei Censori:@Fra Anselmo ?The Chancery is entered by the Scala dei Censori:@Matteo Lion:before\n]\n\nThe Servite brothers had their house on the far side of Cannaregio, where the city ran out of grandeur and began to smell of the tanneries, and Fra Anselmo had a cell at the end of the dormitory with a window on the orchard and a lock on the door that the Prior did not have the key to. Matteo had never asked how that had been arranged. In eight years he had learned that the friar's arrangements were like his seals: if you looked at them closely enough, you could see they were perfect, and that was how you knew they were false.\n\n\"Sit, sit,\" the friar said. \"On the bed. The stool is for the work.\"\n\nThe work was laid out on a board under the window: a block of pear-wood the size of a walnut, a row of little gravers on a strip of leather, a dish of lampblack, a candle stub, and, pinned flat under a sheet of horn so that nothing could breathe on it, a letter. Matteo knew the seal at the foot of it before he was close enough to see it properly. Everyone in Venice knew it. The lion in a circle, with the book open under his paw, and round the circle in tiny capitals the words that made men lose their sleep: CONSILIVM DECEM.\n\n\"Where did you get that?\"\n\n\"It came to the convent,\" said Fra Anselmo placidly, \"two years ago, to inform the Prior that a brother of ours was to be questioned about a pamphlet. The brother was questioned. The Prior was distressed. The letter was filed. I am the convent's archivist.\" He sat down at the stool and picked up a graver. \"I am very good at filing.\"\n\nIsotta Ferro had come with Matteo across the city in silence, and now she stood in the corner by the door with her arms folded, and looked at the seal, and then at the friar, and Matteo saw her understand for the first time what she was paying for.\n\n\"It is not enough to have the seal,\" Matteo said, for her benefit. \"A seal alone is nothing. Anyone can carry a seal. What opens a door in the Palace is a warrant: a sheet of the Chancery's own paper, in a Chancery hand, under a Chancery seal, naming the bearer and his business. The guards cannot read, most of them. But they know what a warrant looks like, the way a dog knows what its master's boots smell like.\"\n\n\"And the paper?\"\n\n\"I have the paper,\" said Matteo. \"I kept some. When they struck me off.\" He shrugged. \"Notaries are magpies.\"\n\n\"And the hand?\"\n\n\"That is mine,\" said the friar, and smiled with the side of his face that worked. \"I have the hands of forty-one secretaries of the Chancery in my files. Not all are alive. All of them are very easy.\"\n\nHe bent over the pear-wood. The graver made a sound like a mouse in a wall.\n\n\"Tell me the way,\" Isotta said to Matteo. \"From the beginning. As if I were a fool.\"\n\nSo he told her. He had drawn it the night before, on the back of a will, and he unfolded it now on the bed between them: the Palace, the courtyard, the Scala dei Giganti where the Doge was crowned, and beside it, much humbler, the Scala dei Censori that the clerks used, up three turns to the floor of the Secret Chancery. The guard at the stairhead. The long panelled passage. The doors: the Sala della Bussola on the left, where people waited to be accused, and on the right the door to the Chancery itself, and beyond that, through a smaller door that most people did not know was a door, the little room where the iron chests were kept.\n\n\"On the Sensa,\" he said, \"the Doge leaves the Palace at the third hour of the morning by the Porta della Carta, with the Signoria behind him. They walk to the Molo and go aboard the Bucintoro. Everyone who can be seen with them goes with them, and everyone who cannot be seen with them goes to watch. The Chancery is left with one secretary of the lowest grade and the guards. The secretaries take it in turns. This year it is a boy called Sanudo, who is twenty and has a mistress at San Polo and has been heard to complain about the duty.\"\n\n\"How do you know that?\"\n\n\"Because I asked him.\" Matteo smiled. \"In a malvasia, while I lost to him at primero. He is a very lucky boy.\"\n\n\"And the chest? The three keys?\"\n\n\"There,\" said Fra Anselmo without looking up, \"is where the warrant comes in.\"\n\nThey had worked it out between them the week before, he and Matteo, over the friar's terrible wine. A warrant under the Ten's seal, in the hand of a senior secretary, ordering the officer on duty to deliver to the bearer the book of receipts of the late Bortolo Ferro, to be carried to the Capi of the Ten on the Bucintoro for an urgent question of the Art. Sanudo would not have the three keys. But Sanudo would know that the Ferro book was not in the iron chests at all, because it had been brought in a month ago and not yet entered; it would be in the press of new matters, behind the Chancery door, under one lock, with one key, on Sanudo's own belt. Matteo had seen that press a hundred times when he was a notary. He had seen what went in it and how long it stayed.\n\n\"He'll look at the warrant,\" said Matteo, \"and he'll look at me, and he'll think: either this is real, and if I refuse it I will be explaining myself to the Capi tomorrow; or it is false, and the only person who could have forged it is a man I beat at primero, which is not possible. And then he'll open the press.\"\n\n\"And if he doesn't?\"\n\n\"Then we run,\" Matteo said. \"Very fast.\"\n\nIsotta looked at the map for a long time.\n\n\"Who carries it?\" she said. \"The warrant. Who goes in?\"\n\n\"I do.\" He had expected the question. \"They know my face on the Censori, from the old days. It's an advantage. A struck-off notary running errands for a secretary is a thing that happens. A widow from Murano is not.\"\n\n\"And who carries the book out?\"\n\nHe hesitated. That part he had not decided, because the answer he wanted to give was not one he could give her.\n\n\"Someone who could carry a book,\" said Fra Anselmo, into the pause, \"without anyone thinking it was a book.\" He held up the pear-wood to the light from the window. The lion was there now, half-born, his paw on the open page. \"Your niece, Madonna. The lacemaker. She carries a pillow and a basket everywhere, does she not? Every girl from Burano carries a pillow. Nobody in the world has ever looked inside one.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #Dolfin's garden\n  Characters: @@Matteo Lion @@Zuane Dolfin\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?Lucia will carry the ledger out:@Matteo Lion ?Lucia will carry the ledger out:@Zuane Dolfin ?Lucia will carry the ledger out:reader\n]\n\n\"A girl,\" Dolfin said. \"How delightful.\"\n\nThe vine on the pergola had put out its first leaves since Matteo's last visit, small and crumpled and very green, like the hands of a newborn. Dolfin was not sitting under it this time; he was walking, slowly, round and round the well-head, with his hands behind his back, and Matteo had to stand in the middle of the gravel and turn to keep facing him, like a horse on a lunge rein.\n\n\"Her niece. Lucia Zen. A lacemaker from Burano. Nineteen.\"\n\n\"Pretty?\"\n\n\"I've never seen her.\"\n\n\"Then you'll have that to look forward to.\" Dolfin completed a circuit. \"And the friar is cutting the seal. Our seal. On the Sensa. Up the Censori.\" He said each thing as though he were laying it on a table, to look at it. \"The boy Sanudo. You've been thorough, Matteo. I'd forgotten how thorough you were. It's a pity about the cards.\"\n\n\"Excellency.\" Matteo had not meant to speak. \"The girl. When you come to the Rialto with the Capi—\"\n\n\"The girl will be with her aunt and her aunt will be selling the Republic's property to a foreigner. What becomes of her depends on what her aunt does next.\" Dolfin stopped walking. \"You're not growing a conscience, I hope. At your age it's like growing teeth; it hurts, and nobody admires the result.\"\n\n\"No, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Good.\" Dolfin resumed his circuit. \"There's one thing more I want. When you have the girl in the Chancery with the book in her pillow, I want you to bring her out by the Censori. Not the Scala d'Oro, not the Giganti, not some other little rat-hole of your own. The Censori, and across the courtyard, and out by the Porta del Frumento to the Molo. I'll have a man in the courtyard to see her go. I don't want her taken. I want her watched, all the way to the Rialto, so that when I come in at the Fleming's door the book is on his table and her hands are still warm from it.\"\n\n\"As you say.\"\n\n\"As I say.\" Dolfin smiled at him across the well-head. \"And Matteo. That debt of yours. Two hundred and twelve, you said? I took the liberty of buying it. From the Greek, and from that other fellow at the Frari. I hold all of it now. It seemed tidier.\"\n\nMatteo stood very still on the gravel. Above him a sparrow came down onto the pergola and looked at the new leaves and decided against them and flew away.\n\n\"That's very kind, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Isn't it?\" said Dolfin. \"I thought so.\"","wordCount":2477,"createdAt":1792361270168},{"id":"7_OHmuuYk3HRlx_ubo2Wo","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"Fydy7EaqZZr40hKtbAF0P","text":"[\n  Place: #The Doge's Palace\n  Characters: @@Matteo Lion @@Zuane Dolfin\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^Dolfin's letter to Vos:@Matteo Lion\n  Facts: ?Dolfin means to send Nane to the galleys regardless:@Matteo Lion ?Dolfin means to send Nane to the galleys regardless:reader ?Dolfin means to send Nane to the galleys regardless:@Zuane Dolfin\n]\n\nHe came up out of the wells into the courtyard and there was Dolfin, at the foot of the Scala d'Oro, in a gown of black damask with a fur at the collar though it was May, talking to two senators as though he had been waiting there all morning for nobody in particular.\n\nHe had not been waiting for nobody. He finished with the senators and turned and came across the flagstones, and Matteo felt the stones under his own feet go soft, the way the floor of the lower passage had been soft.\n\n\"Matteo. You've been visiting.\"\n\n\"I wanted to count the guards, Excellency. For the day.\"\n\n\"Of course you did.\" Dolfin fell in beside him, and they walked, slowly, along the arcade towards the Porta della Carta. Two men in black, talking business. Anyone watching would have thought Matteo had come up in the world. \"And how many are there?\"\n\n\"Three. On the lower passage.\"\n\n\"And how is the boy?\"\n\n\"He has the gaol fever.\"\n\n\"Ah.\" Dolfin nodded, regretfully. \"Yes. It's the spring tides. It comes up through the floor.\" They walked a few paces. \"Well, it hardly matters now, does it?\"\n\nMatteo stopped.\n\n\"Excellency?\"\n\n\"Walk, Matteo. People are looking at us.\" Dolfin took his elbow, quite gently, and walked him on. \"I said it hardly matters. The Capi have agreed, on my word, to come to the Rialto on the evening of the Feast. I have promised them a widow selling the secrets of the Art to a Fleming, and they are very excited, in the way that old men are excited, which is to say they have stopped listening to me and started composing their speeches. When she is taken, the widow will give me the leaf. And when she has given it to me, the Council will consider the matter of her son, who tried to carry the Art abroad, and whose mother has now been taken trying to do the same. And they will send him to the galleys, Matteo, because there will be no other possible thing for them to do. It will not be my decision. I shall argue for clemency. I shall be very moving.\" He smiled. \"I've always been told I'm very moving.\"\n\n\"You told her he'd walk out on the Sensa.\"\n\n\"I told her he would walk out if she brought me the leaf. She has not brought me the leaf. She has chosen to steal from the Republic instead. People make their choices.\" Dolfin patted his arm. \"You look ill. Is it the wells? They take some people that way. One breath and they're ill for a week.\"\n\nThey had come to the Porta della Carta. Beyond it, in the Piazzetta, the sun was blazing on the two columns and on the water, and men were already putting up the stands for the Sensa fair, a forest of poles and canvas and shouting.\n\n\"One thing more,\" Dolfin said, and took a letter from his sleeve. It was folded small, in the Chancery fold, and sealed with plain red wax and no device. \"For our friend at the Rialto. He's anxious, and anxious men talk. Take it to him tonight. He'll know who it's from. Don't let him keep you.\"\n\nMatteo took it. It went into his pocket with the bead and the coral.\n\n\"Excellency,\" he said. \"The boy. Supposing she did bring you the leaf. Supposing she came tomorrow.\"\n\n\"Then she'd be a fool,\" said Dolfin, \"to bring it to me before the Feast instead of after it, wouldn't she, when the price will be so much lower after.\" He looked at Matteo, curiously, the way he had looked at him across the well-head the day he bought his debts. \"Why do you ask?\"\n\n\"I like to know the odds, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Dolfin. \"That's always been your trouble.\" He lifted a hand, and went back across the courtyard to the Scala d'Oro, and up it, into the dark of the Palace, where the gold on the vault caught the light and held it as the rest of the stair went into shadow.\n\nMatteo did not go to the Rialto that night. He went home and sat at his table and took the three things out of his pocket and laid them in a row: the coral, and the bead, and the letter in the Chancery fold. He looked at them until the candle had burned down a finger's width.\n\nThen he took his penknife, and warmed the blade in the flame, and very carefully, the way he had done a hundred times in the old days when he was the best notary in the Republic and had not yet been caught, lifted the seal.","wordCount":838,"createdAt":1792361635942},{"id":"8udqUuJGFXCQRl7dg_Fow","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"Lli335bACODw0CxTUpeik","text":"They let him out on the second of June, a Thursday, in the morning, without any ceremony at all.\n\nThere was no sentence. There had been none on the Sensa either, Matteo told her afterwards; the Council had sat as it always sat after the Doge came back from the Lido, and the matter of the three subjects of the Republic taken trying to carry the Art abroad had been deferred, on the motion of the Capo Contarini, to a later sitting, on the grounds that one of the secretaries who had prepared the depositions had become unavailable. Unavailable was the word. Zuane Dolfin was unavailable in a room at the top of the Palace, under the leads, where the sun came through the roof in summer and turned the air to soup, and where, Matteo said, he had been visited every day by a different member of the Ten and had told each of them something slightly different. Pieter Vos had been put on the Zeeridder at Malamocco without his gold and without his cargo and told that if he were seen in the lagoon again he would be hanged between the columns. And at the later sitting, which nobody outside the room ever heard about, it had been decided that a boy of seventeen with the gaol fever, whose father had been a master of the Art and whose mother had that week rendered the Republic a service it preferred not to describe, might be returned to his family on Murano on the understanding that he would never again try to leave it, and that his mother would keep the Ferro furnace lit.\n\n\"Keep it lit,\" Isotta said. \"Those were the words?\"\n\n\"Those were the words. In the minute.\" Matteo smiled. \"I wrote the minute.\"\n\nHe had a new gown, and ink on his fingers that was new ink, and he went to the Palace every morning at the second hour and came home at dusk to a room in the Calle dei Fabbri that he did not have to lie to anyone about. The Capo Contarini had needed, he had said, a notary in the Avogaria who knew precisely how secretaries of the Ten were corrupted, and could not think of a better one. It was on probation. Matteo said everything in Venice was on probation; it was only that some people were told.\n\nOrsato brought the boy.\n\nHe came up the Rio dei Vetrai in a boat without singing, as he had come the first time, with two fanti at the oars, and in the bottom of the boat, wrapped in the same old woman's blanket, was Nane. He was thin. He was yellow under the eyes, and his hair had grown down over his collar, and when the boat touched the step and he stood up he had to put his hand on Orsato's shoulder to do it, and Orsato let him.\n\nIsotta did not go down the step. She found that she could not. She stood in the water-door with her hands in her apron, and behind her in the workshop Lucia and the friar and the notary stood too, not speaking, and her son came up the three wet steps one at a time and stopped in front of her.\n\n\"Mamma,\" he said. \"I kept the blanket dry.\"\n\nShe put her arms round him. He smelt of the Pozzi, of the sea and what the sea left behind. She did not care. Over his shoulder she saw Orsato, standing in the boat with his black cap in his hand, looking up at the house; and she remembered the day of the sirocco, and the cap lifted once on the Ponte della Paglia, and how long she had wondered whose side he was on.\n\n\"Messer Orsato,\" she said. \"Will you come in? There's wine.\"\n\n\"Thank you, Madonna. No.\" He put his cap on. \"I'm on duty.\" He looked at the boy, and at her, and something happened in his face that was not quite a smile, because it was not a face that had been built for it. \"It was a waste of good hands,\" he said. \"I told you. Good day.\"\n\nThe boat went away down the Rio dei Vetrai, past the Seguso works and the Barovier works and the little bridge where the children sat at night to watch the glow. And Nane put his hand into his shirt and brought out something small and held it out to her on his palm.\n\nA bead. White, round as a pea, with a single blue thread wound once about it.\n\n\"Messer Lion gave it back to me,\" he said. \"In the boat, on the Sensa night, when he came down to the wells with the Capo. He said it was mine to give you and not his.\" He looked past her at Matteo, and then at her again, and he was seven, holding out his burnt hand. \"Is it round enough?\"\n\nShe took it. She closed her hand on it, as she had at the marver with the glory-hole roaring.\n\n\"It'll do,\" she said.\n\nWhen the boy had gone in to the kitchen, where Lucia had bread and soup and a clean shirt waiting and was already scolding him for the state of his hair, Matteo Lion came and stood by her in the water-door, and put his hand in his pocket, and brought out the coral.\n\nIt lay on his palm in the June sun, the little red branch her mother had worn at her wedding, and her grandmother at hers.\n\n\"I owe you this,\" he said. \"I never earned it. You paid me nine ducats and a necklace to steal a book and I sold you to Zuane Dolfin the same afternoon.\"\n\n\"And then you stole it.\"\n\n\"The girl stole it. I copied twenty lines about lattimo in my best hand.\" He held it out. \"Take it back, Madonna. Please. I've been carrying it about for six weeks like a stone in my shoe, and I'd like to walk properly again.\"\n\nShe looked at the coral, and at his hand, the notary's hand with the new ink in the creases, and she thought of him standing at the marver with the glory-hole roaring and the heat on his face, thinking, actually thinking, before he answered her.\n\n\"Keep it,\" she said. \"It was your fee. You didn't take the forty ducats, and you'll never see the hundred, so keep the coral. Give it to a wife one day, if any woman in Venice is fool enough to marry a gambler.\"\n\n\"I've stopped gambling.\"\n\n\"Since when?\"\n\nHe considered. \"Since about the third of May,\" said Matteo Lion. \"When the odds became too good to be interesting.\"\n\nShe laughed. It surprised them both. It came up out of her like something out of the ash pit, grey and unexpected and whole, and it went on rather longer than the joke deserved, and in the kitchen behind them her son put his head round the door to see what was the matter with his mother.","wordCount":1180,"createdAt":1792365094212},{"id":"9S5RhhuxjJRDQfumBwdXz","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"skVwObWMZbfNYo854ju6n","text":"[\n  Place: #Santa Maria dei Servi\n  Characters: @@Lucia Zen @@Fra Anselmo @@Matteo Lion\n  When: +2 days\n  Items: ^The false ledger:@Fra Anselmo\n]\n\nOn the third night Lucia carried the last of the weights across the city in her bodice, as her aunt had once carried the leaf, and found the friar's cell lit like a chapel.\n\nThere were candles everywhere. On the sill, on the floor, on the lid of the chest at the foot of the bed, stuck in their own wax on the corner of the board under the window, so many that the little room was warm as a kitchen and smelled of honey and hot tallow and, under that, of the ink, which Fra Anselmo made himself from oak-galls and copperas and something he would not name, and which came out of the pot brown, exactly the brown of her uncle's hand. The friar sat at the board in the middle of it all in his shirt, with his sleeves tied back and his spectacles on the end of his nose and his bad eye shut, writing.\n\nMatteo Lion was sitting on the bed with a pair of scales on his knees.\n\n\"Ah,\" said the friar without looking up. \"The weights. Good. Give them to the notary. He reads your aunt's hand better than I do. I think she writes it badly on purpose.\"\n\nLucia gave them. Matteo unfolded the paper and held it to the nearest candle, and she watched his lips move as he read, and saw him smile, once, at a line near the bottom.\n\n\"Twelve ounces of the zafre,\" he said. \"Twelve. In a batch that size. She means it to come out the colour of a bruise.\"\n\n\"She means it to crack in the lehr,\" said Lucia. \"She said a bruise was too good for them.\"\n\nOn the board in front of the friar lay the false ledger, open. She had not seen it before. She had imagined something rough, a thing made in a hurry, and it was not; it was her uncle's book. Not like it. It. The boards were the boards, swollen at the lower edge, because Fra Anselmo had soaked them in the canal and dried them in the bread-oven at the convent bakery. The calf was rubbed at the spine where a thumb would rub it. One of the brass corners was gone, the bottom left, and where it had been the leather was darker, the way leather is darker where it has been covered for twenty years. She put her hand out to touch it and stopped.\n\n\"Go on,\" said the friar. \"Touch it. If it doesn't fool you it won't fool the Ten.\"\n\nShe touched it. It was her uncle's book. She took her hand away quickly, as if it were hot.\n\n\"The first gathering is done,\" Anselmo said, writing. \"Your great-grandfather's green for bottles, and the cristallo, and the lattimo, all true, every grain, because a man who opens a book at the front reads the first page with care and nothing after it. The last gathering is done. The middle—\" he waved his pen \"—is accounts. Sand bought, ash bought, wages, the price of beech in the winter of the plague. Your aunt kept your uncle's day-books. I have copied two years of them. Nobody alive could read them without falling asleep.\" He blotted a line. \"And this is the last leaf.\"\n\nHe lifted it from under the horn: a single sheet of old rag paper, browned at the edges, with eleven lines of figures in the cramped brown hand, and at the top, underlined twice, CALCEDONIO.\n\n\"Your aunt's weights,\" he said, \"in your uncle's hand, on paper from the back of a missal printed in the year your uncle was born. It wants sewing in. To the stub. Where the knife went.\" He looked at her over the spectacles with his good eye. \"I can cut a seal. I can write any hand in the Republic. I cannot sew to save my soul.\"\n\nSo that was why she had been sent with the weights, and not Piero.\n\nShe sat on the stool he gave up to her and opened her own little case of needles and found the finest, the one she used for the picots on the Grimani's border, and a thread of unbleached linen she waxed with the end of a candle. The stub in the gutter of the false book was there, a sliver of cut paper no wider than a fingernail; the friar had cut it himself, with Bortolo's own knife, which her aunt had given him, so that it would be slightly ragged at the bottom where a hand had slipped. Lucia laid the leaf against it. She made her stitches through the old holes of the gathering, under the stub, so that from the outside they could not be seen at all, and drew the thread so gently that the paper did not pucker, and tied it off inside the fold with a knot so small it was only a thickening of the thread.\n\nWhen she sat back, the two men were watching her.\n\n\"Again,\" said Matteo softly. \"I didn't see where you began.\"\n\n\"That's the point,\" said Lucia.\n\nThey weighed the books after that, the false one on one pan and on the other a block of cut stone Fra Anselmo had brought up from the cloister, which Matteo said was the weight of the real book to within an ounce, because he had held the real book in his hands in the old days and a notary's hands remember weights the way a glassmaker's remember heat. The false book was lighter. The friar tore out a leaf of the accounts and Matteo shook his head; he put a thin sheet of lead under the endpaper, as they used to do in the Chancery to keep the big registers from blowing open, and the pans hung level and trembled and were still.\n\nIt was past midnight. The candles had burned down into puddles. Lucia packed her needles away and stood, and found that Matteo Lion had stood too, and was holding the door for her, which nobody had ever done.\n\n\"I'll walk you to the Fondamente,\" he said. \"The ferry for Burano goes at the first bell. There are men by the Misericordia at this hour who'd be glad of a girl alone.\"\n\nShe went out past him into the dark of the dormitory. Halfway down the corridor she stopped and turned round.\n\n\"Messer Lion. Why did you change?\"\n\nHe was a long time answering. In the light of the friar's door behind him she could see him put his hand in his pocket and turn something over and over, the way she turned the end of a rope, the way she turned a bobbin.\n\n\"Your cousin told me something,\" he said at last. \"In the wells. I'll tell you what it was one day, when it's finished. If it's finished well.\"\n\n\"And if it isn't?\"\n\n\"Then,\" said Matteo Lion, \"you were right not to trust me.\" He held out his arm. \"Come along. You have to sleep. On Thursday you have to drop forty bobbins on a marble floor, and I'm told it's harder than it sounds.\"","wordCount":1214,"createdAt":1792364203183},{"id":"APQ5yEAqemg6Rmr8T2bXC","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"FCcx9EVdPWwkA6032mhg0","text":"[\n  Place: #The Ferro furnace\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Matteo Lion @@Fra Anselmo @@Lucia Zen\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The lattimo bead:@Isotta Ferro ^Dolfin's letter to Vos:@Isotta Ferro ^The false ledger:@Fra Anselmo\n  Facts: ?Matteo informs for Dolfin:@Isotta Ferro ?Matteo informs for Dolfin:@Lucia Zen ?Matteo informs for Dolfin:@Fra Anselmo ?Dolfin means to send Nane to the galleys regardless:@Isotta Ferro ?Matteo has turned against Dolfin:@Isotta Ferro ?Matteo has turned against Dolfin:reader ?The false ledger will be left in the press:@Isotta Ferro ?The false ledger will be left in the press:@Matteo Lion ?The false ledger will be left in the press:@Fra Anselmo ?The false ledger will be left in the press:@Lucia Zen ?The false ledger will be left in the press:reader\n]\n\nShe lit the furnace for him.\n\nNot the great furnace; there was not wood enough on the island for that, and no reason. The small one, the glory-hole by the bench, where Bortolo had warmed the pieces between gathers; she lit it at dusk with the kindling and then the beech, and fed it until the mouth of it stood open on a round of white-orange light that hurt to look at, and the workshop, which had been cold for five weeks, began to tick and creak around her as the walls remembered what they were for.\n\nWhen Matteo Lion came up the step from his boat she was standing at the bench with a blowpipe in the glory-hole, turning it slowly, as though there were glass on the end of it. There was no glass. There was only the iron, going red, then orange.\n\n\"Madonna.\" He stopped in the water-door. The heat met him there; she saw it in his face. \"You've lit the fire.\"\n\n\"Come in, Messer Lion. Shut the door. The draught's bad for the glass.\"\n\nHe shut it. He came across the floor, and stopped at the end of the bench, where the light from the hole lay over him and made him look younger, or else older, it was hard to tell. He had a roll of paper under his arm. The way, she supposed. He laid it on the marver.\n\n\"The friar's not here?\" he said.\n\n\"The friar's in the lehr-room with my niece. They're listening.\" She turned the pipe. \"I wanted them to hear what you say. In case I have to tell it afterwards to someone, and they ask for witnesses.\"\n\nHe did not move. She watched him not move; she watched him understand, and add it up, and arrive at the figure. It did not take him long. He had always been quick.\n\n\"Orsato,\" he said.\n\n\"Orsato. At the Fleming's. On Sunday last. Talking about gold on a table and the Capi at the door, and a widow taken with the book in her hands.\" She drew the pipe out of the hole and held it up between them. The last hand's length of it was the colour of an apricot. \"Four people knew the day, Messer Lion. I didn't tell Dolfin. Lucia didn't tell Dolfin. Fra Anselmo didn't tell Dolfin, though he's the only one of you I can't prove it of.\"\n\n\"It wasn't the friar.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe pipe hissed faintly in the air between them. Neither of them looked at it.\n\n\"Four years,\" he said. \"Since they struck me off. He came to me the week after, at the Greek's, and bought me a drink, and paid my rent, and asked me to tell him now and then what I heard. In the malvasie. In the courts. Who was in debt, who was in love, who was leaving the city. I told myself it was nothing. I told myself I was a notary still, taking depositions. That's what I told myself.\" He looked at the glowing iron. \"I'd tell you I'm sorry, but you wouldn't believe me, and you'd be right.\"\n\n\"I don't want you to be sorry. I want to know what you're going to do.\"\n\nHe put his hand in his pocket, then, and she lifted the pipe an inch, and he stopped, and slowly drew the hand out and opened it on the marver, in the light.\n\nThere were three things on his palm. Her mother's coral, the little red branch. A letter, folded very small, in a way she had seen once, on Dolfin's table, with its seal lifted cleanly and pressed back. And a bead.\n\nShe knew the bead before she knew she knew it. White, lattimo, round as a pea, with a single thread of blue wound once about it. She had sent back eleven of them, the spring he was fourteen, because they were not round, or the blue had run, or the white was not white enough. The twelfth she had kept. Or she had thought she had. It was on the shelf by San Donato.\n\nNo. It was not. It was here.\n\n\"He said to tell you he's keeping the blanket dry,\" Matteo Lion said. \"He has the gaol fever. Not the worst kind, I think; I've seen the worst kind. And Dolfin told me on Thursday, at the foot of the Scala d'Oro, that when you've been taken at the Fleming's and given him the leaf, the Council will send your son to the galleys regardless, and Dolfin will speak very movingly for clemency, and lose.\" He pushed the letter towards her with one finger. \"That's to the Fleming. In Dolfin's own hand. He gave it me to carry and I didn't carry it. It promises Vos the receipt, a fair copy, when you've given it up. If the Capi saw it, they'd know what he is. A secretary of the Ten selling the secrets of the Art to Antwerp for gold.\"\n\nIsotta put the pipe back in the glory-hole. Her hand was not steady and she did not want him to see it.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\nHe thought about it. He actually thought, standing there with the heat on his face; she watched him do it, and somehow that was what she believed, in the end, more than anything he said.\n\n\"Because your son told me he'd kill me with the hot end of a pipe if I got you killed,\" he said. \"And I believed him. And I found, when I thought about it, that I'd very much rather he didn't have to.\"\n\nFrom the lehr-room door, behind her, there came a small sound, as of an old man trying not to laugh.\n\n\"Come out, Brother,\" said Isotta, without turning round. \"Lucia. Bring the lamp.\" She picked up the bead and closed her hand on it, so hard she felt the little thread of blue against her palm, and looked at the notary across the marver. \"Sit down, Messer Lion. You can sit down here. We're going to steal my husband's book on Thursday exactly as you planned it. Up the Censori, with the friar's warrant, and out in my niece's pillow. And when the boy Sanudo shuts the press behind you, there will still be a book in it.\"","wordCount":1171,"createdAt":1792361758522},{"id":"By0i8_w9mpDV1GK-_mWXq","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"zIrHkBr1jO8kZ1DDBUd9J","text":"[\n  Place: #The Ferro furnace\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Lucia Zen\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?Nane was arrested aboard the Sint Joris:@Isotta Ferro ?Nane was arrested aboard the Sint Joris:@Lucia Zen:before ?Nane meant to sell the ledger to a Fleming:@Lucia Zen:before ?Nane meant to sell the ledger to a Fleming:@Isotta Ferro\n]\n\nLucia came across from Burano at dusk, rowing herself, which she was not supposed to do.\n\nIsotta heard the oars from the kitchen and knew them, because they were bad. Lucia rowed the way she did everything that was not lace, which was impatiently and with too much shoulder, and the little sandolo slapped the water like a hand slapping a table. By the time Isotta reached the step, her niece was already tying up, crooked, to the ring that had been meant for the sand barges.\n\n\"You'll have your father after you.\"\n\n\"My father is in Chioggia until Sunday, and my mother thinks I am at the Ursulines learning the punto in aria from Suor Benedetta, who is ninety and asleep.\" Lucia came up the step two at a time. She was nineteen and small and her hands were never still; even standing on the step she was turning the end of the painter over and over in her fingers. \"Zia. Have they come?\"\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Don't. Don't do that, you do that to the Guild men. Have the Ten come?\"\n\n\"This morning.\" Isotta stood aside to let her in, as she had stood aside for Orsato. \"They took the book.\"\n\n\"Only the book?\"\n\n\"What else is there?\"\n\nLucia stopped in the passage. The lamp in the kitchen behind Isotta threw both their shadows up the wall, long and wavering, and Isotta saw in her niece's face that she had come to say something and had been saying it to herself all the way across the lagoon and now did not know how to begin.\n\n\"Sit down,\" Isotta said.\n\n\"I won't. Zia, it's Nane.\"\n\nThe kitchen was warm, at least. She had lit the small fire for the soup, and the sirocco, which made every other room in the house sweat, made the kitchen merely close. Lucia would not sit, so Isotta did, on the bench by the table, and folded her hands, and waited.\n\n\"Piero the boatman was at the Riva degli Schiavoni on Tuesday,\" Lucia said. \"With a load for the Arsenal. There was a Flemish ship at the moorings, the Sint Joris, out of Antwerp, loading oil and currants. And the fanti came along the Riva at first light, eight of them, with a secretary, and they went aboard, and they brought off a boy. A tall boy with a burnt hand. Piero says he did not fight. Piero says he said his name when they asked it, very clearly, as though he wanted it written down right.\"\n\nIsotta looked at her hands.\n\nThe left one had the same burn, the old shiny pucker across the base of the thumb where every glassmaker's child learned, once, that a pipe fresh out of the glory-hole is hot along its whole length and not only at the end. Nane had got his at seven. He had not cried. He had looked at it with great interest and then at her, and asked if it would make him a master.\n\n\"He was going to Antwerp,\" she said.\n\n\"Zia, it's the law. A glassmaker who leaves the Republic—\"\n\n\"I know the law.\"\n\n\"—they can send someone after him. They can kill him in Antwerp if they like and nobody will say a word. And if he is caught before he goes—\"\n\n\"I know the law, Lucia. I was married to it.\" She heard her own voice, flat as a marver, and was ashamed of it. Lucia was crying now, quietly, without stopping turning the rope's end in her fingers, which she had carried in from the boat without noticing. \"He never told me. He never said one word.\"\n\n\"He told me,\" Lucia said.\n\nIsotta looked up.\n\n\"Not when. Not the ship. Only that he would not stay and blow beads for the Guild while they kept his father's book in a chest. He said the Ten would take the book the moment Zio Bortolo died, because Zio Bortolo had found something, and the Guild knew he had found something, and the Ten always know what the Guild knows.\" She wiped her face with the back of her wrist. \"He said there was a Fleming who would pay. For the book. Or for him, if he had the book in his head. He said you'd be better off without him to feed.\"\n\n\"When did he say this?\"\n\n\"At the funeral. In the cloister, while the Guild men were talking to you.\"\n\nSo he had known, at the funeral, with his father's body still on the bier, that the Ten would come. He had known about the Fleming. He had known, or guessed, about the last winter's work, and he had said nothing to her and everything to his cousin, because his cousin was nineteen and made lace and could not stop him.\n\nIsotta put her hand flat on her bodice, where the paper was.\n\n\"Is he in the Pozzi?\"\n\n\"Piero thinks so. They took him to the Palace by the Ponte della Paglia, not to the Arsenal.\"\n\n\"Then tomorrow,\" Isotta said, \"I am going to the Palace.\"\n\n\"They won't let you see him.\"\n\n\"Orsato said a mother should ask.\" She stood. The soup had boiled down to a brown sediment at the bottom of the pot and she lifted it off the fire and set it on the stone and stood looking at it. \"Orsato knew this morning. He knew when he took the book. He said the son is not at present in a position to swear. I thought he meant Nane was a child.\"\n\nLucia said nothing.\n\n\"You'll stay tonight. I'll take you back at first light and you can tell your mother Suor Benedetta kept you for vespers.\"\n\n\"Suor Benedetta has not kept anyone for vespers since the war of Cyprus.\"\n\n\"Then tell her something better. You're the one with the clever hands.\" Isotta reached up and took the rope's end gently out of her niece's fingers and laid it on the table. \"And Lucia. Whatever Nane told you, about the Fleming and the book. You will not tell anyone else.\"\n\n\"Who would I tell?\"\n\n\"Anyone who asks,\" Isotta said. \"That is who people tell.\"\n\nOutside, the wind had dropped at last. In the sudden quiet she could hear the water lapping the step, and the dog down the canal had stopped barking, and somewhere over toward the Fondamenta dei Vetrai a furnace that was still lit was being stoked for the night shift: the deep exhalation of the bellows, and a man's voice calling for more wood, and the small sharp ring of glass touching glass.","wordCount":1149,"createdAt":1792360670076},{"id":"Bylre-RTjF8KU5RJQu8ZP","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"X5cj4zTjmev3PwWKCfkEx","text":"It was half an hour, by the bells. They waited it out in the Fleming's room with the candles burning down, like people waiting for a birth or a death: Vos at the window with his back to them, Dolfin by the door with his hands folded inside his sleeves, the two fanti at either side of it with their swords grounded and their eyes on the gold, and the old man in the only good chair, with his eyes shut, so that Isotta thought he had gone to sleep until he said, without opening them:\n\n\"Lion.\"\n\nMatteo stepped forward. \"Excellency.\"\n\n\"I know you. You took a deposition for me in the matter of the Grimani wills. Nine years ago. You had a good voice. They struck you off.\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"For what?\"\n\n\"For selling a copy of a sealed deposition to the family it concerned, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Did you do it?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nContarini opened his eyes. \"Well. That's something.\" He held out his hand, palm up, without looking at it, as though he expected something to be put into it, as presumably all his life things had been. \"What have you got for me? You've got something. You've been standing there like a man with a stone in his shoe.\"\n\nMatteo took the letter out of his gown.\n\nIsotta watched Dolfin see it. He was very good. He had been very good all his life. He did not move; he only looked at the letter in the Chancery fold, with its plain red seal lifted cleanly and pressed back, going from Matteo's hand into the hand of the Capo of the Ten, and something went out of him, very quietly, like the air out of a bellows when the boy at the handle stops.\n\nContarini read it. He read it twice, holding it far from his face, the way old men read. Then he looked at Dolfin over the top of it.\n\n\"Your hand, Zuane.\"\n\n\"A forgery, Excellency. That man has been in my pay for four years as an informer. He's a known gambler; he owes money everywhere; he has every reason—\"\n\n\"He has been in your pay for four years,\" said Contarini, \"as an informer. That is very interesting. That was going to be the next thing I asked him.\" He folded the letter. \"And it's your hand. I've had your hand on my table every morning for eleven years. You make your g like a little fish.\"\n\nThen the bells of San Bartolomeo struck the half, and there were feet on the stair, and Orsato came back into the room carrying a book.\n\nIt was wrapped in nothing. He carried it in both hands against his chest, the way he had carried it out of Isotta's house on the day of the sirocco, and he crossed the room to the old man's chair and set it on his knees.\n\n\"From the middle shelf of the press, Excellency,\" he said. \"Sanudo opened it in front of me. It was locked, and the key was on his belt, and he swears the book has not left the Chancery except to be read this morning, in his sight, on the authority of a warrant he could not now find, and put back.\"\n\nContarini looked down at the book on his knees. Boards and calf. Swollen at the lower edge. One brass corner missing, the bottom left.\n\n\"So it was never taken,\" he said, and looked at Dolfin.\n\nAnd Zuane Dolfin, secretary of the Ten for eleven years, who had never once in Isotta's sight done anything he had not decided to do, crossed the room in three strides and took the book off the old man's knees, which nobody in the room had ever seen anyone do, and opened it at the end. Isotta saw his fingers go into the gutter for the stub. She saw them find, instead, a leaf, a whole leaf, sewn in so neatly that you had to put your nail to the stitches to find them; and on it, in the cramped brown hand of Bortolo Ferro, the word CALCEDONIO.\n\n\"There,\" said Dolfin. His voice had gone high and strange. \"There. You see. You see, Excellency? The leaf. The leaf was in it all the time. She put it back. She had it all along and she's put it back, here, it's the receipt, it's the calcedonio, it's what I promised the Fleming—\"\n\nHe heard it. Isotta saw him hear it, a moment after everyone else in the room.\n\nNobody said anything. Over by the window, Pieter Vos put his face in his hands.\n\n\"Take him,\" said Alvise Contarini to the fanti, quite gently. \"And the Fleming. Impound the gold.\" He held out his hand again, palm up, and Orsato took the book out of Dolfin's hands, not roughly, the way he had taken it from the shelf by San Donato, and put it back into the old man's. \"And give me that. It is, after all, the property of the Republic.\"\n\nWhen they had gone, the two fanti and the Fleming and the secretary between them, down the stair into the noise of the fair, the old man sat for a while with the book closed on his knees and looked at Isotta Ferro, and at the collar at her throat, the vine and the pomegranates and the birds.\n\n\"Very pretty lace,\" he said. \"Your niece's?\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Mm.\" He turned the book over in his hands, the false book, Anselmo's three nights' work, with every weight wrong. \"I'm told your son has the gaol fever.\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"That's the spring tides.\" The Capo of the Ten stood up, slowly, in stages, with one hand on Matteo Lion's arm, which Matteo gave him without being asked. \"It comes up through the floor.\"","wordCount":968,"createdAt":1792364904292},{"id":"C0JMsRdWAhfimsKvcTDdM","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"ZfvRJLOr4LOCgCSIL5URI","text":"[\n  Place: #The Zen house on Burano\n  Characters: @@Lucia Zen @@Isotta Ferro\n  When: +3 days\n  Items: ^The calcedonio leaf:@Lucia Zen ^The calcedonio lace:@Lucia Zen\n  Facts: ?Isotta has the calcedonio leaf:@Lucia Zen ?Bortolo found the lost calcedonio receipt:@Lucia Zen ?The calcedonio receipt is worked into Lucia's lace:@Lucia Zen ?The calcedonio receipt is worked into Lucia's lace:@Isotta Ferro ?The calcedonio receipt is worked into Lucia's lace:reader\n]\n\nOn Burano the fog came in at night and stayed until noon, and in the mornings the island was a room with no walls. Lucia sat at the window of her mother's house with the pillow on her knees and could not see the canal six feet below her, only hear it, and hear the bells of San Martino ring the hour from somewhere that might have been the next street or the next world.\n\nShe liked the fog. Nobody came to the window in the fog. Nobody could see what she was making.\n\nHer aunt came anyway, at the hour of terce, in Piero's boat. Lucia heard the oars stop and the boat knock against the step, and then her mother's voice in the passage, high and over-polite, the voice she used for priests and for her husband's sister, who had married above them into glass. Then Zia Isotta was in the doorway in her black wool, wet with fog to the shoulders, and her mother was behind her saying she would bring wine, and Zia Isotta was saying no, thank you, Marina, nothing, I've only come to see what the child is making.\n\n\"I'm not a child,\" Lucia said when her mother had gone.\n\n\"No.\" Her aunt shut the door and stood with her back against it. \"I'm sorry. I needed her to go away.\" She looked at the pillow on Lucia's knees, at the bobbins and the pins and the little cloud of thread. \"What are you making?\"\n\n\"A collar. For the Grimani. Punto in aria.\" She turned the pillow so her aunt could see. \"Stitch in the air. There's no cloth under it, you see; it's all thread, all the way through. Suor Benedetta says the first woman who made it was a fisherman's girl whose lover sent her a piece of seaweed from the far side of the world, and she copied it in thread so it wouldn't rot.\"\n\n\"Is that true?\"\n\n\"No. Suor Benedetta tells everybody something different.\" Lucia set down the pillow. \"Zia, what's happened? Is it Nane?\"\n\nHer aunt came and sat on the end of the bed, which nobody but Lucia ever sat on, and took something out of her bodice, and held it out on her open palm.\n\nIt was a roll of paper the length of a finger, browned at one edge.\n\n\"Don't unroll it,\" Isotta said. \"Not yet. Listen first. This is the last leaf of your uncle's book. The one the Ten didn't get. There is a man at the Palace called Dolfin who would let Nane go tomorrow if I gave it to him, and he would give it to the Guild, and the Guild would give it to whoever they liked, and the Ferro would be nobody. And there is a man at the Rialto who would give me a great deal of money for it, and take it to Antwerp, and the Ferro would be nobody there either.\" She turned the little roll over with one finger. \"On this leaf is how to make calcedonio. Glass that looks like stone. Your uncle found it last winter. He found it because I made him try the copper with the silver, which he said was a woman's idea and stupid, and it was not stupid. It was the last thing we made together. And as long as this exists, on paper, anyone who can take it from me has it.\"\n\nLucia looked at the paper. She could see, through the thin outer turn, the ghost of figures written in brown ink, columns, little crosses.\n\n\"Then burn it,\" she said.\n\n\"If I burn it, it's only in my head. And my head is easy to take.\" Her aunt said it without any expression at all. \"If they put me where they've put Nane, Lucia, or worse, I don't know how long I would keep it. I'm not brave. Nobody is, in the end. Your uncle wasn't.\"\n\n\"Zia—\"\n\n\"So I want to put it somewhere nobody would look for a receipt for glass. Somewhere it looks like something else entirely.\" She held out the roll again. \"You count stitches. Every pattern you make is numbers. How many twists, how many crossings, how many pins before the picot. Could you make this into a pattern? Weights and times and heats, as stitches? So that it was there, all of it, if you knew how to read it, and to anyone else it was a collar?\"\n\nLucia took the roll. It was warm from her aunt's skin.\n\nShe unrolled it on the pillow, across the half-made collar, and smoothed it flat with her palm, and read it. She did not understand most of it. Pounds of ash. Ounces of something called zafre. The copper and the silver her aunt had spoken of. Hours at the first heat. A thing her uncle had written in the margin and underlined twice: NOT TO STIR. There were eleven lines, and some had four figures and some had six, and none of the figures was greater than forty.\n\nNothing greater than forty. She could do anything under forty. She had done the Grimani's border in seventy-two repeats.\n\n\"Yes,\" she said. \"Give me three days.\"","wordCount":935,"createdAt":1792361179903},{"id":"D5WAtMw6JTrfWLsZevkI2","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"lIFNCQBy7DVD0TeJ_LzrI","text":"He said yes, of course. He had known he would say yes from the moment she said Dolfin's name, and he had haggled only so that she would believe him: forty ducats now, which she did not have, and a hundred when the book was in her hands, which she would not have either, unless the Fleming paid. She had given him nine ducats and her mother's coral, and he had taken the coral, and felt it in his pocket all the way home like a stone.\n\nHe did not go home.\n\nHe went the long way, through the rain, by the Merceria and the Frezzeria, by the back of San Moisè where the whores sheltered in the porch and called to him by name, along the fondamenta to a door in a wall with a bell-pull of twisted iron. He did not pull it. He knocked, twice and once, and waited, and the door was opened by a servant who did not look at his face.\n\nZuane Dolfin's garden was small and very old, a square of wet gravel with a well-head in the middle and a pergola of vines along one wall that had not yet come into leaf. Dolfin sat under the pergola in a cloak, with a brazier at his feet and a book on his knee, as though rain were a thing that happened to other people.\n\n\"Matteo. You're wet.\"\n\n\"It's raining, Excellency.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" Dolfin closed his book on one finger. \"Sit, if you like. There's a stool.\"\n\nMatteo did not sit. He never sat, here. It was a superstition: as long as he stood, he could tell himself he was only passing through.\n\n\"The widow Ferro,\" he said, \"came to the malvasia this afternoon with Fra Anselmo of the Servi. She wants her husband's book out of the Secret Chancery. She means to take it on the Sensa while the Signoria is on the water, and sell it to a Fleming at the Rialto, and use the money to buy her son out of the Pozzi and carry him to Antwerp.\"\n\nDolfin smiled. It was the first time Matteo had ever seen him surprised, and he did not like it, because the surprise was so pleased.\n\n\"On the Sensa,\" he said. \"Of course. She is a sensible woman. I told her she was a plain woman and I was wrong; she is a sensible one, which is much rarer.\" He looked into the brazier. \"And the leaf?\"\n\n\"She didn't speak of the leaf. Only that you wanted it and she would not give it.\"\n\n\"She has it, then. She would have said there was no leaf, otherwise. People only refuse to give you things they have.\" Dolfin turned his book over in his hands. \"And you, Matteo. What did you tell her?\"\n\n\"That I'd do it. For forty ducats and a hundred after.\"\n\n\"Good. Do it.\" Dolfin said it as lightly as if he were telling a servant to bring the lamps. \"Help her. Find her a way in. Find the stair, find the hour, get Anselmo to cut whatever seals you need, I'm sure he's longing to. Let her have the book in her hand on the day of the Sensa.\" He opened his own book again, and found his place. \"And then let the Fleming have it in his. And then I will come to the Rialto with the Capi of the Ten and find a Murano widow selling the secrets of the Art to a foreigner, which is treason, with the Republic's own property in her hands, which is theft, with a Servite friar's forged seals, which is sacrilege, and a struck-off notary's help, which is merely sad.\" He looked up. \"Except that the notary will have been working for me all along, and will be thanked, and will owe me nothing any longer. Two hundred ducats, isn't it?\"\n\n\"Two hundred and twelve.\"\n\n\"Then nothing. And the widow, to save her neck, will give me the leaf. She'll give it to me on her knees.\" Dolfin sighed, contentedly, and drew his cloak closer. \"Go home, Matteo. You're dripping on my gravel.\"\n\nMatteo went. In the street he put his hand in his pocket and found the coral there, cold and knobbed like a little branch of bone, and he stood under the dripping eaves for a long time holding it, until a woman in the porch of San Moisè called out to ask if he had lost something, and he said no, and went home.\n\n# Punto in Aria\n\n[\n  Place: #The Zen house on Burano\n  Characters: @@Lucia Zen @@Isotta Ferro\n  When: +3 days\n  Items: ^The calcedonio leaf:@Lucia Zen ^The calcedonio lace:@Lucia Zen\n  Facts: ?Isotta has the calcedonio leaf:@Lucia Zen ?Bortolo found the lost calcedonio receipt:@Lucia Zen ?The calcedonio receipt is worked into Lucia's lace:@Lucia Zen ?The calcedonio receipt is worked into Lucia's lace:@Isotta Ferro ?The calcedonio receipt is worked into Lucia's lace:reader\n]\n\nOn Burano the fog came in at night and stayed until noon, and in the mornings the island was a room with no walls. Lucia sat at the window of her mother's house with the pillow on her knees and could not see the canal six feet below her, only hear it, and hear the bells of San Martino ring the hour from somewhere that might have been the next street or the next world.\n\nShe liked the fog. Nobody came to the window in the fog. Nobody could see what she was making.\n\nHer aunt came anyway, at the hour of terce, in Piero's boat. Lucia heard the oars stop and the boat knock against the step, and then her mother's voice in the passage, high and over-polite, the voice she used for priests and for her husband's sister, who had married above them into glass. Then Zia Isotta was in the doorway in her black wool, wet with fog to the shoulders, and her mother was behind her saying she would bring wine, and Zia Isotta was saying no, thank you, Marina, nothing, I've only come to see what the child is making.\n\n\"I'm not a child,\" Lucia said when her mother had gone.\n\n\"No.\" Her aunt shut the door and stood with her back against it. \"I'm sorry. I needed her to go away.\" She looked at the pillow on Lucia's knees, at the bobbins and the pins and the little cloud of thread. \"What are you making?\"\n\n\"A collar. For the Grimani. Punto in aria.\" She turned the pillow so her aunt could see. \"Stitch in the air. There's no cloth under it, you see; it's all thread, all the way through. Suor Benedetta says the first woman who made it was a fisherman's girl whose lover sent her a piece of seaweed from the far side of the world, and she copied it in thread so it wouldn't rot.\"\n\n\"Is that true?\"\n\n\"No. Suor Benedetta tells everybody something different.\" Lucia set down the pillow. \"Zia, what's happened? Is it Nane?\"\n\nHer aunt came and sat on the end of the bed, which nobody but Lucia ever sat on, and took something out of her bodice, and held it out on her open palm.\n\nIt was a roll of paper the length of a finger, browned at one edge.\n\n\"Don't unroll it,\" Isotta said. \"Not yet. Listen first. This is the last leaf of your uncle's book. The one the Ten didn't get. There is a man at the Palace called Dolfin who would let Nane go tomorrow if I gave it to him, and he would give it to the Guild, and the Guild would give it to whoever they liked, and the Ferro would be nobody. And there is a man at the Rialto who would give me a great deal of money for it, and take it to Antwerp, and the Ferro would be nobody there either.\" She turned the little roll over with one finger. \"On this leaf is how to make calcedonio. Glass that looks like stone. Your uncle found it last winter. He found it because I made him try the copper with the silver, which he said was a woman's idea and stupid, and it was not stupid. It was the last thing we made together. And as long as this exists, on paper, anyone who can take it from me has it.\"\n\nLucia looked at the paper. She could see, through the thin outer turn, the ghost of figures written in brown ink, columns, little crosses.\n\n\"Then burn it,\" she said.\n\n\"If I burn it, it's only in my head. And my head is easy to take.\" Her aunt said it without any expression at all. \"If they put me where they've put Nane, Lucia, or worse, I don't know how long I would keep it. I'm not brave. Nobody is, in the end. Your uncle wasn't.\"\n\n\"Zia—\"\n\n\"So I want to put it somewhere nobody would look for a receipt for glass. Somewhere it looks like something else entirely.\" She held out the roll again. \"You count stitches. Every pattern you make is numbers. How many twists, how many crossings, how many pins before the picot. Could you make this into a pattern? Weights and times and heats, as stitches? So that it was there, all of it, if you knew how to read it, and to anyone else it was a collar?\"\n\nLucia took the roll. It was warm from her aunt's skin.\n\nShe unrolled it on the pillow, across the half-made collar, and smoothed it flat with her palm, and read it. She did not understand most of it. Pounds of ash. Ounces of something called zafre. The copper and the silver her aunt had spoken of. Hours at the first heat. A thing her uncle had written in the margin and underlined twice: NOT TO STIR. There were eleven lines, and some had four figures and some had six, and none of the figures was greater than forty.\n\nNothing greater than forty. She could do anything under forty. She had done the Grimani's border in seventy-two repeats.\n\n\"Yes,\" she said. \"Give me three days.\"","wordCount":1695,"createdAt":1792361178406},{"id":"G0Ce9e0-DVXxWiqrhLqJr","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"Lli335bACODw0CxTUpeik","text":"They let him out on the second of June, a Thursday, in the morning, without any ceremony at all.\n\nThere was no sentence. There had been none on the Sensa either, Matteo told her afterwards; the Council had sat as it always sat after the Doge came back from the Lido, and the matter of the three subjects of the Republic taken trying to carry the Art abroad had been deferred, on the motion of the Capo Contarini, to a later sitting, on the grounds that one of the secretaries who had prepared the depositions had become unavailable. Unavailable was the word. Zuane Dolfin was unavailable in a room at the top of the Palace, under the leads, where the sun came through the roof in summer and turned the air to soup, and where, Matteo said, he had been visited every day by a different member of the Ten and had told each of them something slightly different. Pieter Vos had been put on the Zeeridder at Malamocco without his gold and without his cargo and told that if he were seen in the lagoon again he would be hanged between the columns. And at the later sitting, which nobody outside the room ever heard about, it had been decided that a boy of seventeen with the gaol fever, whose father had been a master of the Art and whose mother had that week rendered the Republic a service it preferred not to describe, might be returned to his family on Murano on the understanding that he would never again try to leave it, and that his mother would keep the Ferro furnace lit.\n\n\"Keep it lit,\" Isotta said. \"Those were the words?\"\n\n\"Those were the words. In the minute.\" Matteo smiled. \"I wrote the minute.\"\n\nHe had a new gown, and ink on his fingers that was new ink, and he went to the Palace every morning at the second hour and came home at dusk to a room in the Calle dei Fabbri that he did not have to lie to anyone about. The Capo Contarini had needed, he had said, a notary in the Avogaria who knew precisely how secretaries of the Ten were corrupted, and could not think of a better one. It was on probation. Matteo said everything in Venice was on probation; it was only that some people were told.\n\nOrsato brought the boy.\n\nHe came up the Rio dei Vetrai in a boat without singing, as he had come the first time, with two fanti at the oars, and in the bottom of the boat, wrapped in the same old woman's blanket, was Nane. He was thin. He was yellow under the eyes, and his hair had grown down over his collar, and when the boat touched the step and he stood up he had to put his hand on Orsato's shoulder to do it, and Orsato let him.\n\nIsotta did not go down the step. She found that she could not. She stood in the water-door with her hands in her apron, and behind her in the workshop Lucia and the friar and the notary stood too, not speaking, and her son came up the three wet steps one at a time and stopped in front of her.\n\n\"Mamma,\" he said. \"I kept the blanket dry.\"\n\nShe put her arms round him. He smelt of the Pozzi, of the sea and what the sea left behind. She did not care. Over his shoulder she saw Orsato, standing in the boat with his black cap in his hand, looking up at the house; and she remembered the day of the sirocco, and the cap lifted once on the Ponte della Paglia, and how long she had wondered whose side he was on.\n\n\"Messer Orsato,\" she said. \"Will you come in? There's wine.\"\n\n\"Thank you, Madonna. No.\" He put his cap on. \"I'm on duty.\" He looked at the boy, and at her, and something happened in his face that was not quite a smile, because it was not a face that had been built for it. \"It was a waste of good hands,\" he said. \"I told you. Good day.\"\n\nThe boat went away down the Rio dei Vetrai, past the Seguso works and the Barovier works and the little bridge where the children sat at night to watch the glow. And Nane put his hand into his shirt and brought out something small and held it out to her on his palm.\n\nA bead. White, round as a pea, with a single blue thread wound once about it.\n\n\"Messer Lion gave it back to me,\" he said. \"In the boat, on the Sensa night, when he came down to the wells with the Capo. He said it was mine to give you and not his.\" He looked past her at Matteo, and then at her again, and he was seven, holding out his burnt hand. \"Is it round enough?\"\n\nShe took it. She closed her hand on it, as she had at the marver with the glory-hole roaring.\n\n\"It'll do,\" she said.","wordCount":851,"createdAt":1792364238832},{"id":"GwEhSDY7DUU2qXRLBwygR","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"FCcx9EVdPWwkA6032mhg0","text":"She lit the furnace for him.\n\nNot the great furnace; there was not wood enough on the island for that, and no reason. The small one, the glory-hole by the bench, where Bortolo had warmed the pieces between gathers; she lit it at dusk with the kindling and then the beech, and fed it until the mouth of it stood open on a round of white-orange light that hurt to look at, and the workshop, which had been cold for five weeks, began to tick and creak around her as the walls remembered what they were for.\n\nWhen Matteo Lion came up the step from his boat she was standing at the bench with a blowpipe in the glory-hole, turning it slowly, as though there were glass on the end of it. There was no glass. There was only the iron, going red, then orange.\n\n\"Madonna.\" He stopped in the water-door. The heat met him there; she saw it in his face. \"You've lit the fire.\"\n\n\"Come in, Messer Lion. Shut the door. The draught's bad for the glass.\"\n\nHe shut it. He came across the floor, and stopped at the end of the bench, where the light from the hole lay over him and made him look younger, or else older, it was hard to tell. He had a roll of paper under his arm. The way, she supposed. He laid it on the marver.\n\n\"The friar's not here?\" he said.\n\n\"The friar's in the lehr-room with my niece. They're listening.\" She turned the pipe. \"I wanted them to hear what you say. In case I have to tell it afterwards to someone, and they ask for witnesses.\"\n\nHe did not move. She watched him not move; she watched him understand, and add it up, and arrive at the figure. It did not take him long. He had always been quick.\n\n\"Orsato,\" he said.\n\n\"Orsato. At the Fleming's. On Sunday last. Talking about gold on a table and the Capi at the door, and a widow taken with the book in her hands.\" She drew the pipe out of the hole and held it up between them. The last hand's length of it was the colour of an apricot. \"Four people knew the day, Messer Lion. I didn't tell Dolfin. Lucia didn't tell Dolfin. Fra Anselmo didn't tell Dolfin, though he's the only one of you I can't prove it of.\"\n\n\"It wasn't the friar.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe pipe hissed faintly in the air between them. Neither of them looked at it.\n\n\"Four years,\" he said. \"Since they struck me off. He came to me the week after, at the Greek's, and bought me a drink, and paid my rent, and asked me to tell him now and then what I heard. In the malvasie. In the courts. Who was in debt, who was in love, who was leaving the city. I told myself it was nothing. I told myself I was a notary still, taking depositions. That's what I told myself.\" He looked at the glowing iron. \"I'd tell you I'm sorry, but you wouldn't believe me, and you'd be right.\"\n\n\"I don't want you to be sorry. I want to know what you're going to do.\"\n\nHe put his hand in his pocket, then, and she lifted the pipe an inch, and he stopped, and slowly drew the hand out and opened it on the marver, in the light.\n\nThere were three things on his palm. Her mother's coral, the little red branch. A letter, folded very small, in a way she had seen once, on Dolfin's table, with its seal lifted cleanly and pressed back. And a bead.\n\nShe knew the bead before she knew she knew it. White, lattimo, round as a pea, with a single thread of blue wound once about it. She had sent back eleven of them, the spring he was fourteen, because they were not round, or the blue had run, or the white was not white enough. The twelfth she had kept. Or she had thought she had. It was on the shelf by San Donato.\n\nNo. It was not. It was here.\n\n\"He said to tell you he's keeping the blanket dry,\" Matteo Lion said. \"He has the gaol fever. Not the worst kind, I think; I've seen the worst kind. And Dolfin told me on Thursday, at the foot of the Scala d'Oro, that when you've been taken at the Fleming's and given him the leaf, the Council will send your son to the galleys regardless, and Dolfin will speak very movingly for clemency, and lose.\" He pushed the letter towards her with one finger. \"That's to the Fleming. In Dolfin's own hand. He gave it me to carry and I didn't carry it. It promises Vos the receipt, a fair copy, when you've given it up. If the Capi saw it, they'd know what he is. A secretary of the Ten selling the secrets of the Art to Antwerp for gold.\"\n\nIsotta put the pipe back in the glory-hole. Her hand was not steady and she did not want him to see it.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\nHe thought about it. He actually thought, standing there with the heat on his face; she watched him do it, and somehow that was what she believed, in the end, more than anything he said.\n\n\"Because your son told me he'd kill me with the hot end of a pipe if I got you killed,\" he said. \"And I believed him. And I found, when I thought about it, that I'd very much rather he didn't have to.\"\n\nFrom the lehr-room door, behind her, there came a small sound, as of an old man trying not to laugh.\n\n\"Come out, Brother,\" said Isotta, without turning round. \"Lucia. Bring the lamp.\" She picked up the bead and closed her hand on it, so hard she felt the little thread of blue against her palm, and looked at the notary across the marver. \"Sit down, Messer Lion. You can sit down here. We're going to steal my husband's book on Thursday exactly as you planned it. Up the Censori, with the friar's warrant, and out in my niece's pillow. And when the boy Sanudo shuts the press behind you, there will still be a book in it.\"\n\n# The Marriage of the Sea\n\n## The Molo\n[\n  Place: #The Molo\n  Characters: @@Lucia Zen @@Matteo Lion @@Orsato\n  When: Thursday 19 May\n  Items: ^The false ledger:@Lucia Zen ^The forged warrant:@Matteo Lion\n]\n\nOn the morning of the Sensa the whole city walked to the sea.\n\nLucia had seen it before, every year of her life, from a boat on the edge of the crowd with her father swearing at the other boats; she had never seen it from the inside. From the inside it was not a crowd. It was weather. It moved in gusts and eddies along the Riva and across the Piazzetta, it sang, it shouted, it sold things, it smelled of fried fish and orange-water and incense and the sweat of ten thousand people in their best clothes, and it carried her along the Molo with her basket on her arm and her pillow on top of the basket the way the tide carries a cork.\n\nThe basket was heavy. Nobody would have known. She carried it the way she carried it every day, on the crook of her left arm with her hand over the pillow, and the pillow on top had a half-made border pinned on it with the bobbins hanging down, and under the pillow, wrapped in a cloth of unbleached linen, was a book in boards and calf, swollen at the lower edge, with one brass corner missing, the bottom left.\n\nFra Anselmo had brought it to Murano at dawn. He had not slept, he said, for three nights, and looked it; his good eye was red and his bad eye was redder, and his fingers were black to the second joint. Her aunt had opened it at the front and read the first page and closed it and opened it at the back and read the last, and then turned to the end, where the stub was, and found the leaf sewn in there, in Zio Bortolo's cramped brown hand, CALCEDONIO, with the weights all wrong. She had put her hand flat on it for a moment. Then she had kissed the friar on both cheeks, which Lucia had never seen her do to anyone, not even Zio Bortolo, and he had gone pink to the edge of his tonsure.\n\nAt the third hour the bells began.\n\nAll of them. San Marco first, the great bell, the Marangona, so deep Lucia felt it in her teeth rather than heard it; and then San Zaccaria and San Giorgio across the water and the Pietà and San Moisè, and the ones further off, Santi Apostoli and the Frari and the Madonna dell'Orto, until the whole city was ringing like a struck glass. And out of the Porta della Carta, between the guards, came the Doge.\n\nLucia had never been so close. He was an old man in cloth of gold, with the horned cap on his head and a face like a walnut, and he walked very slowly because of the weight of the gold, between two men holding up the train, and behind him came the Signoria in crimson, and behind them the Capi of the Ten in black, three old men in a row looking at nothing. The crowd went down on its knees. Lucia went down on her knees with her basket in her arms, and through the legs of the people in front she watched the procession go along the Molo to the water, where the Bucintoro lay against the steps like a great golden animal, and all its oars stood up in the air at once in a salute, two hundred of them, white, with a sound like a flock of birds rising.\n\nWhen she stood up, Matteo Lion was beside her.\n\nShe had seen him only once, the night of the glory-hole, in the bad light of the furnace. In the morning sun he looked older and more respectable, in a notary's black gown with a roll of papers in his hand and a cap like a pudding, and he was smiling at the Bucintoro as though he had paid for it.\n\n\"You're very punctual,\" he said, without looking at her. \"Don't look round. There's a man by the column of San Todaro in a grey coat eating a fritter. He's been there since the second hour. And Orsato is at the Porta della Carta, behind the guard, pretending to be a guard.\"\n\nLucia did not look round.\n\n\"The Capi are on the boat,\" Matteo said. \"All three of them. And Dolfin isn't.\"\n\n\"Where is he?\"\n\n\"He sent his excuses this morning. A fever.\" Matteo smiled more widely at the Bucintoro, which was moving now, very slowly, out from the steps into the basin, with the trumpets going on the poop and the oars rising and falling. \"I expect he'll recover by noon. Have you got it?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Have you got the other thing?\"\n\nShe moved her right hand on the basket handle, and felt, in the pocket of her apron, the little bag of bobbins that she had wound the night before: forty of them, old ones, boxwood and bone, every one she could find in her mother's house.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then walk with me,\" said Matteo Lion, \"like a girl from Burano who's been asked to carry something for a notary and doesn't think much of it, and when we're on the stair, don't speak at all. Your accent is very charming and very Burano and the guard on the Censori would remember it for the rest of his life.\"\n\n## The press\n[\n  Place: #The Secret Chancery\n  Characters: @@Matteo Lion @@Lucia Zen @@Sanudo\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The Glass Ledger:@Lucia Zen ^The false ledger:#The Secret Chancery ^The forged warrant:@Sanudo\n  Facts: ?The false ledger is in the press:@Matteo Lion ?The false ledger is in the press:@Lucia Zen ?The false ledger is in the press:reader\n]\n\nThe guard at the stairhead of the Censori was a Dalmatian with a broken nose who had been a guard at the stairhead of the Censori, Matteo was fairly sure, since the war of Cyprus. He did not read the warrant. He looked at the seal on it, the lion with his paw on the book, and at Matteo's gown and cap, and at the girl with the basket, and spat out of the window into the Rio di Palazzo, and waved them on.\n\nMatteo's legs had been fine on the stair. They went to water in the passage. It was the smell that did it: ink, and beeswax on the panelling, and the faint sharp smell of the sand they shook over wet signatures, which he had not smelled for four years and which went into him like a hook into a fish. The clerks' desks along the wall were empty. The pens lay on them in a row. Somewhere far off, through the Palace, he could hear the trumpets on the water, thin as a gnat.\n\nSanudo was in the Chancery with his feet on a desk, eating cherries out of his hat.\n\n\"Lion!\" He took his feet down. He was twenty, and plump, and had a downy beginning of a beard that he touched when he was nervous; he touched it now. \"What are you doing here? You're not supposed to be here. You're not anything any more, are you? They struck you off.\" He seemed pleased with himself for remembering. \"And who's she?\"\n\n\"My sister's girl. She carries my papers.\" Matteo held out the warrant. He did it the old way, the way he had once held out depositions to the Avogadori, with the seal uppermost and his thumb not quite touching it, so the eye went to the wax and stayed there. \"His Excellency Dolfin's compliments, Sanudo. He's sick, poor man, and he's sent me for something, and if you don't help me I shall have to go out to the Bucintoro in a sandolo and explain to the Capi in the middle of the Doge's wedding why the Ferro receipt isn't on their table, and I'll give them your name. Read it.\"\n\nSanudo read it. It took him some time. His lips moved. Matteo, who had written it, could have recited it: the officer on duty in the Secret Chancery to show the bearer the book of receipts of the late Bortolo Ferro, received the twenty-first of April, so that the bearer might copy from it the receipt for lattimo, of which the Capi had need on the Bucintoro for a question raised by the Most Serene Prince touching the making of the cups for the wedding feast. Signed, in a hand that had been dead for six years, by a secretary of the first grade; under it, the lion.\n\nIt was, Matteo thought, a deeply silly warrant. That was why he loved it. Nobody would forge anything so silly.\n\n\"Lattimo,\" said Sanudo.\n\n\"The Doge wants white cups. The Patriarch wants clear ones. There's been a quarrel. You know what they're like.\" Matteo shrugged. \"Twenty lines. I copy them, you lock it up again, I go out to the boat, you go back to your cherries. Or you send me away and tomorrow you explain.\"\n\nSanudo looked at the warrant, and at Matteo, and touched his beard; and Matteo watched him think exactly what he had told Isotta Ferro, in the friar's cell, that the boy would think. Either it was real; or the only man who could have forged it was a man he had beaten at primero.\n\nHe took the key off his belt.\n\nThe press stood against the wall of the little room beyond the Chancery door, the room most people did not know was a room: a cupboard of walnut, tall as a man, with a lock like a fist. Inside, on three shelves, were the new matters, the things that had come in and not yet been entered and sent to their proper chests. Bundles of letters tied with tape. A box of seized pamphlets. A dagger in a bag with a label on it. And on the middle shelf, by itself, a book in boards and calf, swollen at the lower edge, with one brass corner missing, the bottom left.\n\nMatteo heard the girl behind him breathe in, once.\n\n\"There,\" Sanudo said, and lifted it out, and put it on the Chancery desk, and opened it, and stood over it. \"Lattimo. Go on, then.\"\n\nMatteo sat. He took out his own paper and ink, and uncapped the ink, and turned the leaves, slowly, as a notary does, looking for the place, and the boy stood at his shoulder and watched every leaf turn.\n\nThat was the moment, and they had known it would be. Sanudo had to look away. They had argued about it for an hour in the friar's cell and for another hour at the marver with the glory-hole roaring, and in the end it was the girl who had said, quite calmly, turning a bobbin over in her fingers: Nobody can watch a book when a girl drops forty bobbins on a stone floor. Not even a priest. I've seen it.\n\nMatteo found the lattimo. He dipped his pen.\n\nAnd Lucia Zen, standing behind him with her basket, put her hand into her apron for her handkerchief, and brought out the little bag of bobbins instead, and somehow, as she did so, the drawstring came undone.\n\nThey went everywhere. Forty bobbins of boxwood and bone on a floor of red and white marble, bouncing and rolling, clattering like hail on a roof, under the desks, under the press, into the corners, a sound that went on and on as though it would never stop; and the girl gave a cry of real distress, and went down on her knees with her basket, and Sanudo, who was twenty and kind and had a mistress at San Polo and would be a good husband to someone one day, said \"Oh, God, wait, let me,\" and went down on his knees too.\n\nIt took them, afterwards, Matteo calculated, about as long as a Paternoster and two Aves to pick them all up. The girl kept finding more, and holding them up, and thanking him. Sanudo crawled under the press and came out with dust on his hat.\n\nWhen they stood up, Matteo was copying the receipt for lattimo, very neatly, in his best hand, and the book was open on the desk beside him at the right page, and the girl's basket was on her arm, and her pillow was on top of it, with the half-made border pinned to it, and the bobbins hanging down.\n\nIt was heavier on the left now, Matteo thought, watching her settle it; she had to brace her hip. It was not heavier. It was precisely the same weight. He had weighed them both, on the friar's scales, the night before.\n\nHe sanded his copy, and folded it, and stood.\n\n\"My thanks, Sanudo. I'll tell His Excellency you were very helpful.\"\n\n\"Will you?\" The boy looked absurdly pleased. He shut the book, and lifted it, and put it back on the middle shelf of the press, by itself, and locked the lock like a fist, and hung the key on his belt, and patted it. \"There. Safe as the Mint.\"\n\n\"Safer,\" said Matteo.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Molo\n  Characters: @@Lucia Zen @@Matteo Lion @@Isotta Ferro @@Orsato\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The Glass Ledger:@Isotta Ferro\n  Facts: ?The Glass Ledger is back with Isotta:@Isotta Ferro ?The Glass Ledger is back with Isotta:@Lucia Zen ?The Glass Ledger is back with Isotta:@Matteo Lion ?The Glass Ledger is back with Isotta:reader\n]\n\nThey came down the Censori into the courtyard, and Orsato was there.\n\nHe was not pretending to be a guard any more. He was standing by the great bronze well-head in the middle of the courtyard in his black cap, with his hands behind his back, looking at nothing, the way the Capi had looked at nothing as they walked to the boat; and as Lucia came down the last steps with her basket he turned his head and looked at her, and she felt it go right through the pillow and the linen and the boards and the calf, and she thought, he knows. He can see it. He can see the book through the basket the way he saw me through the storeroom door.\n\n\"Don't stop,\" said Matteo pleasantly beside her. \"Smile at him. He's a friend of mine.\"\n\nShe did not smile. She walked. Across the courtyard, past the well-head, past Orsato, who did not move, through the Porta del Frumento, where the sun hit her in the face like a slap and the noise of the Molo came back all at once, trumpets, bells, the roar of the people as the Bucintoro, far out on the water, came about in a great slow curve towards the Lido.\n\n\"He's following,\" said Matteo. \"Good. Don't look.\"\n\nThey went along the Molo, through the crowd, past the column of San Todaro where the man in the grey coat had finished his fritter and was licking his fingers, past the Zecca, to the place at the end of the Molo where the boats were, hundreds of them, gondolas and sandoli and barges and the big painted peote of the guilds, all jostling and rocking at the steps waiting to follow the Doge to the sea. And in among them, very small, a sandolo from Murano with Piero at the oar and a woman in black sitting in the bottom of it, with a basket on her knee.\n\nIt was the same basket. Lucia had made sure of that; she had bought two from the same man at the Rialto on Monday and rubbed the new one with ash and grease until it looked as old as hers. And the pillow on top of it was the same, or near enough, with a half-made border pinned to it and bobbins hanging down.\n\n\"Zia!\" She waved. It was the most natural thing in the world. A girl from Burano, in the crowd on the Sensa, seeing her aunt in a boat. \"Zia, hold this, I want to go and see the Doge come back—\"\n\nShe went down the wet steps. Her aunt reached up. There was a moment, one, with a gondola bumping on one side and a fat man from the butchers' guild shouting on the other, when both baskets were in the air between them; and then Lucia was climbing back up the steps with a basket on her arm that weighed exactly what hers had weighed when she came out of the Censori, because Fra Anselmo had weighed them on his scales, and her aunt was sitting in the bottom of the boat with a basket on her knee and her hand flat on the pillow, the way she had put her hand flat on the false leaf at dawn.\n\nPiero pushed off. The sandolo slid out between the gondolas, and turned, and went away towards the Arsenal and the open water and Murano, small and black and unremarkable among a thousand boats.\n\nLucia did not watch it go. She turned round on the top step and looked straight across the Molo at Orsato, who had stopped ten paces off by a stall selling glass beads, and was holding one up to the light as though he were thinking of buying it.\n\nHe looked at her. He looked at the basket on her arm. He looked, for a long moment, out at the water where the Murano boat had gone.\n\nThen he put the bead down, and paid for it anyway, and walked away along the Molo towards the Rialto, without hurrying, to tell his master where the girl was going.","wordCount":4018,"createdAt":1792361920628},{"id":"H9dBI2JbPGatS5xm5DzG3","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"zIrHkBr1jO8kZ1DDBUd9J","text":"Lucia came across from Burano at dusk, rowing herself, which she was not supposed to do.\n\nIsotta heard the oars from the kitchen and knew them, because they were bad. Lucia rowed the way she did everything that was not lace, which was impatiently and with too much shoulder, and the little sandolo slapped the water like a hand slapping a table. By the time Isotta reached the step, her niece was already tying up, crooked, to the ring that had been meant for the sand barges.\n\n\"You'll have your father after you.\"\n\n\"My father is in Chioggia until Sunday, and my mother thinks I am at the Ursulines learning the punto in aria from Suor Benedetta, who is ninety and asleep.\" Lucia came up the step two at a time. She was nineteen and small and her hands were never still; even standing on the step she was turning the end of the painter over and over in her fingers. \"Zia. Have they come?\"\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Don't. Don't do that, you do that to the Guild men. Have the Ten come?\"\n\n\"This morning.\" Isotta stood aside to let her in, as she had stood aside for Orsato. \"They took the book.\"\n\n\"Only the book?\"\n\n\"What else is there?\"\n\nLucia stopped in the passage. The lamp in the kitchen behind Isotta threw both their shadows up the wall, long and wavering, and Isotta saw in her niece's face that she had come to say something and had been saying it to herself all the way across the lagoon and now did not know how to begin.\n\n\"Sit down,\" Isotta said.\n\n\"I won't. Zia, it's Nane.\"\n\nThe kitchen was warm, at least. She had lit the small fire for the soup, and the sirocco, which made every other room in the house sweat, made the kitchen merely close. Lucia would not sit, so Isotta did, on the bench by the table, and folded her hands, and waited.\n\n\"Piero the boatman was at the Riva degli Schiavoni on Tuesday,\" Lucia said. \"With a load for the Arsenal. There was a Flemish ship at the moorings, the Sint Joris, out of Antwerp, loading oil and currants. And the fanti came along the Riva at first light, eight of them, with a secretary, and they went aboard, and they brought off a boy. A tall boy with a burnt hand. Piero says he did not fight. Piero says he said his name when they asked it, very clearly, as though he wanted it written down right.\"\n\nIsotta looked at her hands.\n\nThe left one had the same burn, the old shiny pucker across the base of the thumb where every glassmaker's child learned, once, that a pipe fresh out of the glory-hole is hot along its whole length and not only at the end. Nane had got his at seven. He had not cried. He had looked at it with great interest and then at her, and asked if it would make him a master.\n\n\"He was going to Antwerp,\" she said.\n\n\"Zia, it's the law. A glassmaker who leaves the Republic—\"\n\n\"I know the law.\"\n\n\"—they can send someone after him. They can kill him in Antwerp if they like and nobody will say a word. And if he is caught before he goes—\"\n\n\"I know the law, Lucia. I was married to it.\" She heard her own voice, flat as a marver, and was ashamed of it. Lucia was crying now, quietly, without stopping turning the rope's end in her fingers, which she had carried in from the boat without noticing. \"He never told me. He never said one word.\"\n\n\"He told me,\" Lucia said.\n\nIsotta looked up.\n\n\"Not when. Not the ship. Only that he would not stay and blow beads for the Guild while they kept his father's book in a chest. He said the Ten would take the book the moment Zio Bortolo died, because Zio Bortolo had found something, and the Guild knew he had found something, and the Ten always know what the Guild knows.\" She wiped her face with the back of her wrist. \"He said there was a Fleming who would pay. For the book. Or for him, if he had the book in his head. He said you'd be better off without him to feed.\"\n\n\"When did he say this?\"\n\n\"At the funeral. In the cloister, while the Guild men were talking to you.\"\n\nSo he had known, at the funeral, with his father's body still on the bier, that the Ten would come. He had known about the Fleming. He had known, or guessed, about the last winter's work, and he had said nothing to her and everything to his cousin, because his cousin was nineteen and made lace and could not stop him.\n\nIsotta put her hand flat on her bodice, where the paper was.\n\n\"Is he in the Pozzi?\"\n\n\"Piero thinks so. They took him to the Palace by the Ponte della Paglia, not to the Arsenal.\"\n\n\"Then tomorrow,\" Isotta said, \"I am going to the Palace.\"\n\n\"They won't let you see him.\"\n\n\"Orsato said a mother should ask.\" She stood. The soup had boiled down to a brown sediment at the bottom of the pot and she lifted it off the fire and set it on the stone and stood looking at it. \"Orsato knew this morning. He knew when he took the book. He said the son is not at present in a position to swear. I thought he meant Nane was a child.\"\n\nLucia said nothing.\n\n\"You'll stay tonight. I'll take you back at first light and you can tell your mother Suor Benedetta kept you for vespers.\"\n\n\"Suor Benedetta has not kept anyone for vespers since the war of Cyprus.\"\n\n\"Then tell her something better. You're the one with the clever hands.\" Isotta reached up and took the rope's end gently out of her niece's fingers and laid it on the table. \"And Lucia. Whatever Nane told you, about the Fleming and the book. You will not tell anyone else.\"\n\n\"Who would I tell?\"\n\n\"Anyone who asks,\" Isotta said. \"That is who people tell.\"\n\nOutside, the wind had dropped at last. In the sudden quiet she could hear the water lapping the step, and the dog down the canal had stopped barking, and somewhere over toward the Fondamenta dei Vetrai a furnace that was still lit was being stoked for the night shift: the deep exhalation of the bellows, and a man's voice calling for more wood, and the small sharp ring of glass touching glass.\n\n# The Secretary\n\n[\n  Place: #The Doge's Palace\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Zuane Dolfin @@Orsato\n  When: +1 day\n  Items: ^The Glass Ledger:@Zuane Dolfin\n  Facts: ?The last leaf of the ledger is missing:@Zuane Dolfin ?The last leaf of the ledger is missing:@Orsato ?Bortolo found the lost calcedonio receipt:@Zuane Dolfin ?Dolfin will trade Nane for the leaf:@Isotta Ferro ?Dolfin will trade Nane for the leaf:reader\n]\n\nThe bora had come down off the mountains in the night and swept the sirocco out to sea, and the morning was so clear that from the Fondamenta dei Vetrai Isotta could see snow on the Alps, a white rind laid along the top of the world. The lagoon was the colour of a knife. Piero the boatman rowed her across without singing either, out of respect, and set her down at the Ponte della Paglia among the fishwives and the clerks.\n\nShe had dressed for it. The black wool, the good collar, the coral her mother had left her; not so much that she looked like a woman who could pay, not so little that she looked like a woman who could not. She had thought about it for an hour in the cold kitchen before dawn, and then she had thought that thinking about it was what the Palace wanted from people, that they should come to the door already half on their knees, and she had nearly gone in her apron out of spite.\n\nThe door of the Pozzi was a low arch at the bottom of a stair, with a guard on a stool beside it eating bread. She told him her name, and her son's name, and he went on eating bread and looked at her as though she were weather.\n\n\"Ferro,\" he said at last. \"From Murano.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Wait.\"\n\nShe waited. She waited long enough to watch the light move across the courtyard flagstones from one drain to the next. People went up and down the stair past her: a priest with a basket, a notary with a sheaf of papers under his chin, two women who were plainly also mothers, from the way they did not look at each other. Then the guard came back with someone else, and it was not a gaoler. It was Orsato, in his black cap, and he did not take her down. He took her up.\n\nUp the Scala dei Censori, up again, through a door so plain she understood it was important, along a passage panelled in dark wood where the clerks' pens made a sound like rain, and into a room no larger than her kitchen, with one window on the Rio di Palazzo and one man at a table under it.\n\n\"Madonna Ferro. Please. Sit.\" He rose for her. Nobody in the Palace had risen for her. \"Zuane Dolfin. I have the honour to be one of the secretaries of the Most Excellent Council. You will forgive the room. The great rooms are for great business, and this, I hope, is a small business, between ourselves.\"\n\nHe was perhaps fifty, grey at the temples, with a pleasant tired face and very clean hands. On the table in front of him, unwrapped from Orsato's oilcloth, lay Bortolo's book.\n\nShe sat because her knees had decided it.\n\n\"Your son is well,\" Dolfin said. \"I asked, before you came. He is in the third cell on the lower passage, which is not the best cell, but it is not the worst; it is dry except at the spring tides. He is fed. He has not been put to the question and I see no reason he should be.\" He let that lie a moment, the way a man lets a coin lie on the table. \"You know, of course, what the law says.\"\n\n\"I know what it says.\"\n\n\"Then you know I have been generous already.\" He touched the book with one finger, on the boards, not the pages, as though it might be warm. \"I will be plain with you, Madonna, because you seem to me a plain woman, and plainness saves time. The Guild of the Art has, as you know, its ears. One of those ears belongs to a man who carried wood for your husband last winter. He tells us that the furnace was lit at hours when no orders were being filled. He tells us that master Bortolo burned through four crucibles in February making something he would not let the boys see. He tells us that on the second Sunday of Lent your husband went to San Donato and paid for a Mass of thanksgiving, which he had never done in his life.\"\n\nIsotta said nothing.\n\n\"And here,\" said Dolfin, and now he did open the book, at the end, where Orsato had opened it, and turned it round so she could see the sliver of cut paper in the gutter, \"here is a leaf that is not here.\"\n\n\"He cut out his failures.\"\n\n\"So Orsato told me you said. I think it is a good answer. I think it is the answer I would give.\" He closed the book. \"Calcedonio, Madonna. That is what the Guild thinks. Glass like stone. They have not made it on this island since my grandfather's time and I do not need to tell you what the Republic would give to make it again, or what the Emperor would give, or the King of France, or a certain house in Antwerp that your son was so eager to visit.\"\n\n\"My son knows nothing about it.\"\n\n\"Then he has nothing to sell, and he will go to the galleys for trying to sell it, which seems to me a waste of a good pair of hands.\" Dolfin folded his own. \"On the Feast of the Sensa the Most Excellent Council will sit after the Doge returns from the Lido, as it does every year, and it will pass sentence on those subjects of the Republic who were taken trying to carry the Art abroad. There are three this year. Your son is one. I would like him not to be. Bring me the leaf before the Sensa, and he walks out of the Pozzi on the Feast and goes home with you to Murano, and no one ever writes his name in a book again.\"\n\n\"And if there is no leaf?\"\n\n\"Then I am sorry for you.\" He meant it, she thought; that was the worst of him. He meant every word. \"Orsato will take you down now. You may have a little while at the grille. I would not tell him about our conversation. Boys of that age are proud, and pride is very expensive in the Pozzi.\"","wordCount":2214,"createdAt":1792360971066},{"id":"I-hO-ALJQbAOQnImEfz84","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"FCcx9EVdPWwkA6032mhg0","text":"She lit the furnace for him.\n\nNot the great furnace; there was not wood enough on the island for that, and no reason. The small one, the glory-hole by the bench, where Bortolo had warmed the pieces between gathers; she lit it at dusk with the kindling and then the beech, and fed it until the mouth of it stood open on a round of white-orange light that hurt to look at, and the workshop, which had been cold for five weeks, began to tick and creak around her as the walls remembered what they were for.\n\nWhen Matteo Lion came up the step from his boat she was standing at the bench with a blowpipe in the glory-hole, turning it slowly, as though there were glass on the end of it. There was no glass. There was only the iron, going red, then orange.\n\n\"Madonna.\" He stopped in the water-door. The heat met him there; she saw it in his face. \"You've lit the fire.\"\n\n\"Come in, Messer Lion. Shut the door. The draught's bad for the glass.\"\n\nHe shut it. He came across the floor, and stopped at the end of the bench, where the light from the hole lay over him and made him look younger, or else older, it was hard to tell. He had a roll of paper under his arm. The way, she supposed. He laid it on the marver.\n\n\"The friar's not here?\" he said.\n\n\"The friar's in the lehr-room with my niece. They're listening.\" She turned the pipe. \"I wanted them to hear what you say. In case I have to tell it afterwards to someone, and they ask for witnesses.\"\n\nHe did not move. She watched him not move; she watched him understand, and add it up, and arrive at the figure. It did not take him long. He had always been quick.\n\n\"Orsato,\" he said.\n\n\"Orsato. At the Fleming's. On Sunday last. Talking about gold on a table and the Capi at the door, and a widow taken with the book in her hands.\" She drew the pipe out of the hole and held it up between them. The last hand's length of it was the colour of an apricot. \"Four people knew the day, Messer Lion. I didn't tell Dolfin. Lucia didn't tell Dolfin. Fra Anselmo didn't tell Dolfin, though he's the only one of you I can't prove it of.\"\n\n\"It wasn't the friar.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe pipe hissed faintly in the air between them. Neither of them looked at it.\n\n\"Four years,\" he said. \"Since they struck me off. He came to me the week after, at the Greek's, and bought me a drink, and paid my rent, and asked me to tell him now and then what I heard. In the malvasie. In the courts. Who was in debt, who was in love, who was leaving the city. I told myself it was nothing. I told myself I was a notary still, taking depositions. That's what I told myself.\" He looked at the glowing iron. \"I'd tell you I'm sorry, but you wouldn't believe me, and you'd be right.\"\n\n\"I don't want you to be sorry. I want to know what you're going to do.\"\n\nHe put his hand in his pocket, then, and she lifted the pipe an inch, and he stopped, and slowly drew the hand out and opened it on the marver, in the light.\n\nThere were three things on his palm. Her mother's coral, the little red branch. A letter, folded very small, in a way she had seen once, on Dolfin's table, with its seal lifted cleanly and pressed back. And a bead.\n\nShe knew the bead before she knew she knew it. White, lattimo, round as a pea, with a single thread of blue wound once about it. She had sent back eleven of them, the spring he was fourteen, because they were not round, or the blue had run, or the white was not white enough. The twelfth she had kept. Or she had thought she had. It was on the shelf by San Donato.\n\nNo. It was not. It was here.\n\n\"He said to tell you he's keeping the blanket dry,\" Matteo Lion said. \"He has the gaol fever. Not the worst kind, I think; I've seen the worst kind. And Dolfin told me on Thursday, at the foot of the Scala d'Oro, that when you've been taken at the Fleming's and given him the leaf, the Council will send your son to the galleys regardless, and Dolfin will speak very movingly for clemency, and lose.\" He pushed the letter towards her with one finger. \"That's to the Fleming. In Dolfin's own hand. He gave it me to carry and I didn't carry it. It promises Vos the receipt, a fair copy, when you've given it up. If the Capi saw it, they'd know what he is. A secretary of the Ten selling the secrets of the Art to Antwerp for gold.\"\n\nIsotta put the pipe back in the glory-hole. Her hand was not steady and she did not want him to see it.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\nHe thought about it. He actually thought, standing there with the heat on his face; she watched him do it, and somehow that was what she believed, in the end, more than anything he said.\n\n\"Because your son told me he'd kill me with the hot end of a pipe if I got you killed,\" he said. \"And I believed him. And I found, when I thought about it, that I'd very much rather he didn't have to.\"\n\nFrom the lehr-room door, behind her, there came a small sound, as of an old man trying not to laugh.\n\n\"Come out, Brother,\" said Isotta, without turning round. \"Lucia. Bring the lamp.\" She picked up the bead and closed her hand on it, so hard she felt the little thread of blue against her palm, and looked at the notary across the marver. \"Sit down, Messer Lion. You can sit down here. We're going to steal my husband's book on Thursday exactly as you planned it. Up the Censori, with the friar's warrant, and out in my niece's pillow. And when the boy Sanudo shuts the press behind you, there will still be a book in it.\"","wordCount":1053,"createdAt":1792364201304},{"id":"MDsSPeHHuDKeMS-WrMNk6","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"Hq8_8_xvRODxArlMBYfhb","text":"[\n  Place: #The Rialto\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Lucia Zen\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?Isotta will not give Vos the calcedonio:@Isotta Ferro ?Isotta will not give Vos the calcedonio:@Lucia Zen ?Isotta will not give Vos the calcedonio:reader\n]\n\nOutside it was evening, and warm, the first warm evening of the year, with the first mosquitoes coming up off the canals like smoke. The bridge was crowded with people going home and people going out, and the shutters of the shops along the Ruga were going up with a sound like a long roll of drums. Her aunt walked fast, and did not speak, and Lucia had almost to run to keep beside her.\n\nAt the top of the bridge her aunt stopped. Below them the Grand Canal was full of boats and the light was going pink on the water, and a man on a barge was singing something about a girl from Chioggia with a voice so bad it was almost beautiful.\n\n\"Well?\" said her aunt.\n\n\"He knew about the leaf.\"\n\n\"Nane told him.\"\n\n\"Nane told him there was a last thing. He didn't tell him what. Zia, he wasn't surprised. When you said you could get the book out of the Ten's own house, he wasn't surprised. He was pleased, he was greedy, but he wasn't surprised. People are surprised when you say a thing like that.\" Lucia gripped the warm stone of the parapet. \"And he turned the letter over. On the desk. When we came in.\"\n\n\"Merchants turn their letters over. They're all thieves; they think everyone else is.\"\n\n\"And he said the Sensa,\" Lucia said. \"You said the day after the Feast. You didn't say which Feast. There are three Feasts in May. And he said the Zeeridder sails the morning after the Sensa, as you wish.\"\n\nHer aunt was quiet. On the barge the man had come to the part of the song where the girl from Chioggia drowned herself, and was making the most of it.\n\n\"Everybody in Venice knows the Sensa is the only Feast that matters,\" Isotta said at last. \"He's been here a year. He'd have learned that much.\"\n\n\"Zia—\"\n\n\"Lucia.\" Her aunt turned to her, and put both hands on her shoulders, and Lucia saw in the pink light how tired she was, how the skin under her eyes had gone thin and dark like the skin of a bruised plum. \"I don't trust him. I don't trust Matteo Lion and I don't entirely trust Fra Anselmo, God forgive me, and the man I trust least in the world is Zuane Dolfin, who is the only one of them who has never lied to me. I don't have to trust them. I have to use them. That's all.\" She let go. \"And he's not getting the calcedonio. Whatever happens. He's getting two hundred and eleven leaves and a widow who keeps house and has a very bad memory for weights.\"\n\n\"And Nane?\"\n\n\"Nane will have a bench in Antwerp and hate it, and in five years he'll come home and the Ten will have forgotten him, and he'll make calcedonio on Murano, out of your collar, and be famous.\" Her aunt took her arm. \"Come on. Piero won't wait after the Ave Maria and I'm not rowing you back to Burano in the dark.\"\n\nThey went down off the bridge into the crowd. Lucia looked back once, up at the windows above the spice-merchant's, and there was a candle lit in one of them now; and for a moment a man stood in it, small and neat, looking down at the bridge where they had been. Then he drew the curtain.","wordCount":611,"createdAt":1792361423471},{"id":"NV6jMiQWHdp4RsjiJh_FK","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"X5cj4zTjmev3PwWKCfkEx","text":"It was half an hour, by the bells. They waited it out in the Fleming's room with the candles burning down, like people waiting for a birth or a death: Vos at the window with his back to them, Dolfin by the door with his hands folded inside his sleeves, the two fanti at either side of it with their swords grounded and their eyes on the gold, and the old man in the only good chair, with his eyes shut, so that Isotta thought he had gone to sleep until he said, without opening them:\n\n\"Lion.\"\n\nMatteo stepped forward. \"Excellency.\"\n\n\"I know you. You took a deposition for me in the matter of the Grimani wills. Nine years ago. You had a good voice. They struck you off.\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"For what?\"\n\n\"For selling a copy of a sealed deposition to the family it concerned, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Did you do it?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nContarini opened his eyes. \"Well. That's something.\" He held out his hand, palm up, without looking at it, as though he expected something to be put into it, as presumably all his life things had been. \"What have you got for me? You've got something. You've been standing there like a man with a stone in his shoe.\"\n\nMatteo took the letter out of his gown.\n\nIsotta watched Dolfin see it. He was very good. He had been very good all his life. He did not move; he only looked at the letter in the Chancery fold, with its plain red seal lifted cleanly and pressed back, going from Matteo's hand into the hand of the Capo of the Ten, and something went out of him, very quietly, like the air out of a bellows when the boy at the handle stops.\n\nContarini read it. He read it twice, holding it far from his face, the way old men read. Then he looked at Dolfin over the top of it.\n\n\"Your hand, Zuane.\"\n\n\"A forgery, Excellency. That man has been in my pay for four years as an informer. He's a known gambler; he owes money everywhere; he has every reason—\"\n\n\"He has been in your pay for four years,\" said Contarini, \"as an informer. That is very interesting. That was going to be the next thing I asked him.\" He folded the letter. \"And it's your hand. I've had your hand on my table every morning for eleven years. You make your g like a little fish.\"\n\nThen the bells of San Bartolomeo struck the half, and there were feet on the stair, and Orsato came back into the room carrying a book.\n\nIt was wrapped in nothing. He carried it in both hands against his chest, the way he had carried it out of Isotta's house on the day of the sirocco, and he crossed the room to the old man's chair and set it on his knees.\n\n\"From the middle shelf of the press, Excellency,\" he said. \"Sanudo opened it in front of me. It was locked, and the key was on his belt, and he swears the book has not left the Chancery except to be read this morning, in his sight, on the authority of a warrant he could not now find, and put back.\"\n\nContarini looked down at the book on his knees. Boards and calf. Swollen at the lower edge. One brass corner missing, the bottom left.\n\n\"So it was never taken,\" he said, and looked at Dolfin.\n\nAnd Zuane Dolfin, secretary of the Ten for eleven years, who had never once in Isotta's sight done anything he had not decided to do, crossed the room in three strides and took the book off the old man's knees, which nobody in the room had ever seen anyone do, and opened it at the end. Isotta saw his fingers go into the gutter for the stub. She saw them find, instead, a leaf, a whole leaf, sewn in so neatly that you had to put your nail to the stitches to find them; and on it, in the cramped brown hand of Bortolo Ferro, the word CALCEDONIO.\n\n\"There,\" said Dolfin. His voice had gone high and strange. \"There. You see. You see, Excellency? The leaf. The leaf was in it all the time. She put it back. She had it all along and she's put it back, here, it's the receipt, it's the calcedonio, it's what I promised the Fleming—\"\n\nHe heard it. Isotta saw him hear it, a moment after everyone else in the room.\n\nNobody said anything. Over by the window, Pieter Vos put his face in his hands.\n\n\"Take him,\" said Alvise Contarini to the fanti, quite gently. \"And the Fleming. Impound the gold.\" He held out his hand again, palm up, and Orsato took the book out of Dolfin's hands, not roughly, the way he had taken it from the shelf by San Donato, and put it back into the old man's. \"And give me that. It is, after all, the property of the Republic.\"\n\nWhen they had gone, the two fanti and the Fleming and the secretary between them, down the stair into the noise of the fair, the old man sat for a while with the book closed on his knees and looked at Isotta Ferro, and at the collar at her throat, the vine and the pomegranates and the birds.\n\n\"Very pretty lace,\" he said. \"Your niece's?\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Mm.\" He turned the book over in his hands, the false book, Anselmo's three nights' work, with every weight wrong. \"I'm told your son has the gaol fever.\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"That's the spring tides.\" The Capo of the Ten stood up, slowly, in stages, with one hand on Matteo Lion's arm, which Matteo gave him without being asked. \"It comes up through the floor.\"\n\n# Cristallo\n\n## Nane comes home\n[\n  Place: #The Ferro furnace\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Nane Ferro @@Matteo Lion @@Fra Anselmo @@Lucia Zen @@Orsato\n  When: +2 weeks\n  Items: ^The lattimo bead:@Nane Ferro\n  Facts: ?Dolfin is arrested:@Nane Ferro ?Dolfin is arrested:@Fra Anselmo ?The calcedonio receipt is worked into Lucia's lace:@Nane Ferro\n]\n\nThey let him out on the second of June, a Thursday, in the morning, without any ceremony at all.\n\nThere was no sentence. There had been none on the Sensa either, Matteo told her afterwards; the Council had sat as it always sat after the Doge came back from the Lido, and the matter of the three subjects of the Republic taken trying to carry the Art abroad had been deferred, on the motion of the Capo Contarini, to a later sitting, on the grounds that one of the secretaries who had prepared the depositions had become unavailable. Unavailable was the word. Zuane Dolfin was unavailable in a room at the top of the Palace, under the leads, where the sun came through the roof in summer and turned the air to soup, and where, Matteo said, he had been visited every day by a different member of the Ten and had told each of them something slightly different. Pieter Vos had been put on the Zeeridder at Malamocco without his gold and without his cargo and told that if he were seen in the lagoon again he would be hanged between the columns. And at the later sitting, which nobody outside the room ever heard about, it had been decided that a boy of seventeen with the gaol fever, whose father had been a master of the Art and whose mother had that week rendered the Republic a service it preferred not to describe, might be returned to his family on Murano on the understanding that he would never again try to leave it, and that his mother would keep the Ferro furnace lit.\n\n\"Keep it lit,\" Isotta said. \"Those were the words?\"\n\n\"Those were the words. In the minute.\" Matteo smiled. \"I wrote the minute.\"\n\nHe had a new gown, and ink on his fingers that was new ink, and he went to the Palace every morning at the second hour and came home at dusk to a room in the Calle dei Fabbri that he did not have to lie to anyone about. The Capo Contarini had needed, he had said, a notary in the Avogaria who knew precisely how secretaries of the Ten were corrupted, and could not think of a better one. It was on probation. Matteo said everything in Venice was on probation; it was only that some people were told.\n\nOrsato brought the boy.\n\nHe came up the Rio dei Vetrai in a boat without singing, as he had come the first time, with two fanti at the oars, and in the bottom of the boat, wrapped in the same old woman's blanket, was Nane. He was thin. He was yellow under the eyes, and his hair had grown down over his collar, and when the boat touched the step and he stood up he had to put his hand on Orsato's shoulder to do it, and Orsato let him.\n\nIsotta did not go down the step. She found that she could not. She stood in the water-door with her hands in her apron, and behind her in the workshop Lucia and the friar and the notary stood too, not speaking, and her son came up the three wet steps one at a time and stopped in front of her.\n\n\"Mamma,\" he said. \"I kept the blanket dry.\"\n\nShe put her arms round him. He smelt of the Pozzi, of the sea and what the sea left behind. She did not care. Over his shoulder she saw Orsato, standing in the boat with his black cap in his hand, looking up at the house; and she remembered the day of the sirocco, and the cap lifted once on the Ponte della Paglia, and how long she had wondered whose side he was on.\n\n\"Messer Orsato,\" she said. \"Will you come in? There's wine.\"\n\n\"Thank you, Madonna. No.\" He put his cap on. \"I'm on duty.\" He looked at the boy, and at her, and something happened in his face that was not quite a smile, because it was not a face that had been built for it. \"It was a waste of good hands,\" he said. \"I told you. Good day.\"\n\nThe boat went away down the Rio dei Vetrai, past the Seguso works and the Barovier works and the little bridge where the children sat at night to watch the glow. And Nane put his hand into his shirt and brought out something small and held it out to her on his palm.\n\nA bead. White, round as a pea, with a single blue thread wound once about it.\n\n\"Messer Lion gave it back to me,\" he said. \"In the boat, on the Sensa night, when he came down to the wells with the Capo. He said it was mine to give you and not his.\" He looked past her at Matteo, and then at her again, and he was seven, holding out his burnt hand. \"Is it round enough?\"\n\nShe took it. She closed her hand on it, as she had at the marver with the glory-hole roaring.\n\n\"It'll do,\" she said.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Ferro furnace\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Nane Ferro @@Lucia Zen\n  When: +3 days\n  Items: ^The Glass Ledger:@Isotta Ferro ^The calcedonio lace:@Lucia Zen\n]\n\nThe great furnace took three days to come up to heat.\n\nThey lit it on the Sunday after Nane came home, with the whole of Murano watching from the bridge, it seemed, and from the windows of the Seguso works and the Barovier works opposite, where the masters stood at their own doors with their arms folded and pretended to be looking at the weather. The Guild sent a man. He stood in the yard with his hat in his hands and looked at the smoke coming out of the Ferro chimney, and Isotta went out to him and said good morning, Messer Briati, and showed him a paper with the lion of the Ten on it that said in a notary's handsome hand that the widow Isotta Ferro was licensed to work the furnace of her late husband until her son should come to his mastership, and Briati read it twice and put his hat on and went away.\n\nBy the Wednesday the pots were glowing white in the furnace mouth, and the batch was in, and the whole house ticked and sang with the heat the way it had when she was a bride and could not sleep for the noise of it. Lucia had come over from Burano with her pillow. Nane had slept for most of three days and eaten for the rest of them and was a better colour; he stood now at the bench in his shirt with a pipe in his hands, his father's pipe, the fourth from the left, with the brass ferrule, turning it, not yet putting it in, the way his father had stood, and the way she had.\n\nThe book was on the shelf. It had come out of the ash pit grey and smelling of old fire, and she had cleaned it with a dry brush, leaf by leaf, two hundred and ten of them, and put it back where it had sat for twenty-two years, between the jar of manganese and the image of San Donato. Nobody would come for it. As far as the Republic knew, the book of receipts of the late Bortolo Ferro was in a press in the Secret Chancery, where the Capo Contarini had seen it with his own eyes; and if the Guild, in a year or two, made a fair copy of it and gave it to some furnace on the island that had paid the Ten enough, that furnace would make cristallo the colour of a ditch, and lattimo that was grey, and calcedonio that cracked in the lehr. She found that she did not mind that at all.\n\n\"Read it to me,\" she said to Lucia.\n\nLucia unfolded the collar on the marver, away from the heat, and smoothed it flat. The vine and the pomegranates and the birds. The border of knots like a fence round a garden. She put her finger on the first leaf of the vine.\n\n\"Four pounds of ash of Levant,\" she read.\n\nNane looked at the collar. Then at his cousin. Then at his mother, and his mouth opened, and she watched him understand, all at once, the way you understand a piece of glass when it comes out of the lehr whole.\n\n\"That's it?\" he said. \"That's the last leaf? In the lace? All this time?\"\n\n\"All this time.\"\n\n\"Babbo would have hated it.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Isotta. \"He would have said lace is for nuns.\" She took the pipe out of his hands, and put it into the glory-hole to warm, and turned it, slowly, the way you do. \"Six of the sand, Lucia. Washed thrice.\"\n\nLucia read. Isotta weighed. Nane carried. The copper and the silver went in at the hour Lucia read out from the birds, and the furnace roared, and the light came out of the mouth of it white and gold and hurt to look at, and on the shelf San Donato looked down at the three of them with his painted saint's patience, and outside on the bridge the children sat in a row to watch the glow.\n\nAt the hour of Vespers she put the pipe into the pot and gathered.\n\nIt came out on the end of the iron like honey, like sun, a glowing gob of nothing-colour that swelled and sagged and that she turned, and turned, and blew into, once, gently, the way you blow on a child's burnt hand. And as it cooled, slowly, on the end of the pipe, in the air of the workshop, the colour came up out of it. Not all at once. In veins. A thread of brown, and then green, and then a blue like the lagoon in winter, running through the clear glass the way rivers run through a map, the way the grain runs through stone, until what she held on the end of her husband's pipe was not glass at all but a piece of agate, a piece of chalcedony, a thing dug out of a mountain and not breathed into shape on a pipe in a widow's workshop on Murano on a Wednesday in June.\n\n\"Not to stir,\" said Lucia softly, with her finger on the border of knots.\n\n\"No,\" said Isotta Ferro. \"Not to stir.\"\n\nShe held it up to the window, so they could all see the light come through.","wordCount":2785,"createdAt":1792362332074},{"id":"NbFx-6Xtz9pehTsxrbGth","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"WfzecJCXcnkhuVLjye4PH","text":"[\n  Place: #Dolfin's garden\n  Characters: @@Matteo Lion @@Zuane Dolfin\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?Lucia will carry the ledger out:@Matteo Lion ?Lucia will carry the ledger out:@Zuane Dolfin ?Lucia will carry the ledger out:reader\n]\n\n\"A girl,\" Dolfin said. \"How delightful.\"\n\nThe vine on the pergola had put out its first leaves since Matteo's last visit, small and crumpled and very green, like the hands of a newborn. Dolfin was not sitting under it this time; he was walking, slowly, round and round the well-head, with his hands behind his back, and Matteo had to stand in the middle of the gravel and turn to keep facing him, like a horse on a lunge rein.\n\n\"Her niece. Lucia Zen. A lacemaker from Burano. Nineteen.\"\n\n\"Pretty?\"\n\n\"I've never seen her.\"\n\n\"Then you'll have that to look forward to.\" Dolfin completed a circuit. \"And the friar is cutting the seal. Our seal. On the Sensa. Up the Censori.\" He said each thing as though he were laying it on a table, to look at it. \"The boy Sanudo. You've been thorough, Matteo. I'd forgotten how thorough you were. It's a pity about the cards.\"\n\n\"Excellency.\" Matteo had not meant to speak. \"The girl. When you come to the Rialto with the Capi—\"\n\n\"The girl will be with her aunt and her aunt will be selling the Republic's property to a foreigner. What becomes of her depends on what her aunt does next.\" Dolfin stopped walking. \"You're not growing a conscience, I hope. At your age it's like growing teeth; it hurts, and nobody admires the result.\"\n\n\"No, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Good.\" Dolfin resumed his circuit. \"There's one thing more I want. When you have the girl in the Chancery with the book in her pillow, I want you to bring her out by the Censori. Not the Scala d'Oro, not the Giganti, not some other little rat-hole of your own. The Censori, and across the courtyard, and out by the Porta del Frumento to the Molo. I'll have a man in the courtyard to see her go. I don't want her taken. I want her watched, all the way to the Rialto, so that when I come in at the Fleming's door the book is on his table and her hands are still warm from it.\"\n\n\"As you say.\"\n\n\"As I say.\" Dolfin smiled at him across the well-head. \"And Matteo. That debt of yours. Two hundred and twelve, you said? I took the liberty of buying it. From the Greek, and from that other fellow at the Frari. I hold all of it now. It seemed tidier.\"\n\nMatteo stood very still on the gravel. Above him a sparrow came down onto the pergola and looked at the new leaves and decided against them and flew away.\n\n\"That's very kind, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Isn't it?\" said Dolfin. \"I thought so.\"","wordCount":473,"createdAt":1792361271960},{"id":"Nv783PArJ4G5WQF-ANrxF","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"rVNeaCiG5wuO3GSDlW_cq","text":"[\n  Place: #The Ferro furnace\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Nane Ferro @@Lucia Zen\n  When: +3 days\n  Items: ^The Glass Ledger:@Isotta Ferro ^The calcedonio lace:@Lucia Zen\n]\n\nThe great furnace took three days to come up to heat.\n\nThey lit it on the Sunday after Nane came home, with the whole of Murano watching from the bridge, it seemed, and from the windows of the Seguso works and the Barovier works opposite, where the masters stood at their own doors with their arms folded and pretended to be looking at the weather. The Guild sent a man. He stood in the yard with his hat in his hands and looked at the smoke coming out of the Ferro chimney, and Isotta went out to him and said good morning, Messer Briati, and showed him a paper with the lion of the Ten on it that said in a notary's handsome hand that the widow Isotta Ferro was licensed to work the furnace of her late husband until her son should come to his mastership, and Briati read it twice and put his hat on and went away.\n\nBy the Wednesday the pots were glowing white in the furnace mouth, and the batch was in, and the whole house ticked and sang with the heat the way it had when she was a bride and could not sleep for the noise of it. Lucia had come over from Burano with her pillow. Nane had slept for most of three days and eaten for the rest of them and was a better colour; he stood now at the bench in his shirt with a pipe in his hands, his father's pipe, the fourth from the left, with the brass ferrule, turning it, not yet putting it in, the way his father had stood, and the way she had.\n\nThe book was on the shelf. It had come out of the ash pit grey and smelling of old fire, and she had cleaned it with a dry brush, leaf by leaf, two hundred and ten of them, and put it back where it had sat for twenty-two years, between the jar of manganese and the image of San Donato. Nobody would come for it. As far as the Republic knew, the book of receipts of the late Bortolo Ferro was in a press in the Secret Chancery, where the Capo Contarini had seen it with his own eyes; and if the Guild, in a year or two, made a fair copy of it and gave it to some furnace on the island that had paid the Ten enough, that furnace would make cristallo the colour of a ditch, and lattimo that was grey, and calcedonio that cracked in the lehr. She found that she did not mind that at all.\n\n\"Read it to me,\" she said to Lucia.\n\nLucia unfolded the collar on the marver, away from the heat, and smoothed it flat. The vine and the pomegranates and the birds. The border of knots like a fence round a garden. She put her finger on the first leaf of the vine.\n\n\"Four pounds of ash of Levant,\" she read.\n\nNane looked at the collar. Then at his cousin. Then at his mother, and his mouth opened, and she watched him understand, all at once, the way you understand a piece of glass when it comes out of the lehr whole.\n\n\"That's it?\" he said. \"That's the last leaf? In the lace? All this time?\"\n\n\"All this time.\"\n\n\"Babbo would have hated it.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Isotta. \"He would have said lace is for nuns.\" She took the pipe out of his hands, and put it into the glory-hole to warm, and turned it, slowly, the way you do. \"Six of the sand, Lucia. Washed thrice.\"\n\nLucia read. Isotta weighed. Nane carried. The copper and the silver went in at the hour Lucia read out from the birds, and the furnace roared, and the light came out of the mouth of it white and gold and hurt to look at, and on the shelf San Donato looked down at the three of them with his painted saint's patience, and outside on the bridge the children sat in a row to watch the glow.\n\nAt the hour of Vespers she put the pipe into the pot and gathered.\n\nIt came out on the end of the iron like honey, like sun, a glowing gob of nothing-colour that swelled and sagged and that she turned, and turned, and blew into, once, gently, the way you blow on a child's burnt hand. And as it cooled, slowly, on the end of the pipe, in the air of the workshop, the colour came up out of it. Not all at once. In veins. A thread of brown, and then green, and then a blue like the lagoon in winter, running through the clear glass the way rivers run through a map, the way the grain runs through stone, until what she held on the end of her husband's pipe was not glass at all but a piece of agate, a piece of chalcedony, a thing dug out of a mountain and not breathed into shape on a pipe in a widow's workshop on Murano on a Wednesday in June.\n\n\"Not to stir,\" said Lucia softly, with her finger on the border of knots.\n\n\"No,\" said Isotta Ferro. \"Not to stir.\"\n\nShe held it up to the window, so they could all see the light come through.","wordCount":916,"createdAt":1792362334993},{"id":"OYojSNrUR7QSGlJ26ZOOL","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"gnMam_XH4_nPQ3uIi2eK8","text":"The passage under the Palace smelled of the sea and of what the sea left behind. Orsato carried a lamp and went ahead of her and did not speak, and she was grateful, because she did not think she could have answered. The walls sweated. Somewhere water was running, not loud, a thread of it, and the floor under her shoes was soft with something she did not look at.\n\nThe third cell on the lower passage had a door of black oak with a square cut in it at the height of a man's face, and across the square three iron bars, and behind the bars, when Orsato lifted the lamp, her son.\n\nHe had grown. That was her first stupid thought. He had grown in the eleven days since the funeral; or else she had never looked at him properly, or else the cell made him tall by being so low. His hair was flat with damp. He had a blanket round his shoulders like an old woman at a fire.\n\n\"Mamma.\"\n\n\"Don't,\" she said, because his voice had cracked on it, and if he cried she would, and Orsato was standing four paces off with his lamp and his patience. \"Don't. Are you hurt?\"\n\n\"No.\" He put his fingers through the bars, the burnt ones. She took them. They were cold as the canal. \"They haven't done anything. They ask me things. A man comes with a book and asks me what my father put in the batch for lattimo, and I tell him, because everyone in Murano knows what goes in lattimo, and he writes it down very slowly and goes away.\"\n\n\"Nane, why?\"\n\nHe looked at her, and for a moment he was seven again, holding out his blistered hand and asking if it would make him a master.\n\n\"Because they were going to take the book,\" he said. \"Because the Guild had already been to see you, I saw them, I saw old Briati in the yard with his hat in his hands looking at our furnace like a man looking at a widow. Because I'm not going to blow beads for them for thirty years while some secretary keeps Babbo's book in a chest and sells it a page at a time.\" He lowered his voice. \"There's a Fleming. Vos. He's at the Rialto. He said if I came to Antwerp with what was in my head he'd give me a furnace of my own. A furnace, Mamma. Not a bench in someone else's.\"\n\n\"And what was in your head?\"\n\n\"Everything I'd seen.\" His fingers tightened. \"Not the last thing. Babbo never let me see the last thing. But Vos didn't know that.\"\n\nSo he had meant to cheat the Fleming too. He was his father's son. She closed her eyes for a moment.\n\n\"Who else did you tell?\"\n\n\"Nobody. Lucia. Only Lucia.\" He tried to smile. \"She told me I was an idiot.\"\n\n\"She was right.\"\n\n\"Time,\" Orsato said, not unkindly.\n\nNane held on. \"Mamma, the man with the book said something. He said if you were sensible I'd be home for the Sensa. What does that mean? What does he want from you?\"\n\nShe looked at her son through the bars, at his flat wet hair and the old woman's blanket and the hand that had been burnt into the shape of a master's hand, and she thought of Dolfin saying pride is very expensive in the Pozzi.\n\n\"It means he is a liar,\" she said, \"like all of them. Eat what they give you. Keep the blanket dry. Say your prayers and say them out loud, so the guard hears you and thinks you're a good boy. I'll come back.\"\n\n\"When?\"\n\n\"When I can.\"\n\nOrsato was already lowering the lamp. In the last of its light she saw her son's face go back into the dark of the cell, not all at once but by parts, the eyes last, and then there was only the square in the door and the three bars and the sound of the water running somewhere it should not be.\n\nOn the stair, in the daylight again, she had to stop and put her hand against the wall. Orsato stopped too, a step above, and waited, and looked out over the courtyard where the light had moved on to the next drain.\n\n\"He is right,\" he said, to nobody. \"It is a waste of good hands.\"\n\nThen he took her back to the Ponte della Paglia, and handed her down into Piero's boat, and lifted his black cap to her, once, which she would remember a long time afterwards, when she had reason to wonder what side he was on.\n\n# The Notary of Debts\n\n[\n  Place: #The malvasia at San Zulian\n  Characters: @@Matteo Lion @@Fra Anselmo @@Isotta Ferro\n  When: +2 days\n  Facts: ?Dolfin will trade Nane for the leaf:@Matteo Lion ?Isotta means to steal the ledger back:@Matteo Lion ?Isotta means to steal the ledger back:@Fra Anselmo ?Isotta means to steal the ledger back:reader\n]\n\nIt rained on Sunday the way it only rains in Venice in April, as if the sky had been saving it. The water came down straight and grey into the canals and the canals did not seem to notice, being water already, and only the people minded: the people ran under arches with their cloaks over their heads, and the people crowded into the malvasia behind San Zulian until the windows ran on the inside as well as the out.\n\nMatteo Lion had been there since Mass. He had not gone to Mass. He had gone, at the hour of Mass, to the malvasia, where the Greek kept a back room with a table and a lamp and a pack of Neapolitan cards that had been shuffled so often the court cards were faceless, and where Matteo had lost, by the time the bells of San Marco struck the end of the sung Mass, eleven lire and a pair of gloves.\n\nThey were good gloves. He minded the gloves.\n\n\"You are a man of no luck,\" said the Greek, who was also the dealer, and who said it every Sunday with the same warm sorrow, like a priest reading the same Gospel.\n\n\"I am a man of excellent luck,\" Matteo said. \"It is only that it is all bad.\" He pushed his last coins across. \"Deal.\"\n\nHe was forty, and had been for some time. He had a long face that women had once called distinguished and now called tired, and a notary's hands, ink in the creases that no washing reached, and a voice that could read a deposition aloud in the Avogaria so that the accused wept to hear his own crimes in it. He had been struck from the roll of notaries four years ago for selling a copy of a sealed deposition to the family it concerned, who had paid him well and then betrayed him at the first opportunity, as families do. Since then he had lived by writing letters for people who could not write them, and wills for people who could not afford a real notary, and once, memorably, a love sonnet for a gondolier who had then married the wrong girl.\n\nAnd by one other thing, which he did not think about on Sundays.\n\nHe lost the hand. He was reaching for his cloak when the curtain of the back room moved and Fra Anselmo came in out of the rain like a wet black heron, and behind him a woman Matteo did not know.\n\n\"Brother,\" Matteo said. \"I have no money and I have not been to Mass. Whichever of those you've come about, you're too late.\"\n\n\"I have come about neither.\" The friar shook his hood off. He was sixty, or seventy, or a hundred; he had one milky eye and one that missed nothing, and he could cut a seal in pear-wood that the Doge's own chancery could not tell from the brass. Matteo had met him over a disputed will eight years ago and they had been, ever since, the kind of friends who do not ask each other questions. \"This is Madonna Isotta, widow of the late master Bortolo Ferro of Murano. Madonna, this is the man I told you about. He is a gambler and a liar and he was once the best notary in the Republic.\"\n\n\"I still am,\" said Matteo. \"It's only that the Republic hasn't noticed.\" He looked at the woman properly, and stopped talking.\n\nShe was not beautiful. She was something more inconvenient than that: she was paying attention. She stood in the doorway of the Greek's back room in a wet black cloak with her hands folded in front of her, and she looked at him, at the faceless cards, at his ungloved hands, at the coins in front of the Greek, and he saw her add it all up and arrive at a figure.\n\n\"Fra Anselmo says you know the Palace,\" she said.\n\n\"Everybody knows the Palace. It's very large and you can see it from the sea.\"\n\n\"He says you know the stairs. The ones the clerks use. He says you know where the Ten keep what they take.\"\n\nThe Greek, who understood a great deal more Venetian than he admitted, got up and went out through the curtain with the cards in his hand. Matteo heard him tell the boy at the barrels to bring wine to the back room and then to stay away from it. Then it was just the three of them, and the lamp, and the rain.\n\n\"Sit down, Madonna,\" Matteo said. \"Please. And tell me what they took.\"\n\nShe told him. She told it the way he would have liked witnesses to tell things in the old days, in order and without decoration: the men from the Ten, the book, the secretary in the little room, the offer, her son at the grille. She did not say what was on the missing leaf. She said only that there was a leaf, and that Dolfin wanted it, and that she would not give it to him, and that if she did not her son would go to the galleys on the Feast of the Sensa.\n\n\"Dolfin,\" Matteo said.\n\n\"You know him?\"\n\n\"Everyone who has ever been in trouble in this city knows Zuane Dolfin.\" That was true, as far as it went. He drank some of the wine the boy had brought. It was bad. \"So. You won't give him the leaf. Then what do you want from me?\"\n\n\"I want the book back.\"\n\nMatteo put down his cup.\n\n\"It was my husband's,\" she said. \"And his father's. Two hundred and eleven leaves. Everything the Ferro ever learned about glass. If it stays in that chest, they will copy it out for the Guild, and the Guild will give it to whichever house pays the Ten most, and in ten years every bead-maker on the island will be making Ferro cristallo and selling it for half. And my son will have nothing. Not a furnace. Not a name. And Dolfin will still want the leaf.\"\n\n\"And with the book back?\"\n\n\"With the book back, I have something to sell.\" She looked at Fra Anselmo, and then at him. \"There is a Fleming at the Rialto who wanted my son. I think he will want the book more. Enough to carry two people to Antwerp and enough besides to buy the gaoler who holds the key of the third cell.\"\n\nIt was, Matteo thought, the most insane thing anyone had said to him in four years, and he had once been asked to forge a papal dispensation for a marriage between a man and his own aunt.\n\n\"You understand,\" he said, \"that the Secret Chancery is the best-guarded room in the Republic. That there are men in it night and day. That the chests are iron and the keys are held by the Capi of the Ten themselves, three keys to a chest, one each.\"\n\n\"Fra Anselmo says there is one day a year when the men in it are not there.\"\n\nHe looked at the friar. The friar looked at the lamp.\n\n\"The Sensa,\" Matteo said slowly. \"When the Doge goes out on the Bucintoro to marry the sea, the whole Signoria goes with him. The Capi. The secretaries. Every clerk who can get a place on a boat. The Palace is a shell for four hours.\" He heard himself say it and was appalled at how easily it came. \"That is the day they will sentence your son.\"\n\n\"Then we have until that day,\" Isotta Ferro said, \"and not one hour more.\"","wordCount":2119,"createdAt":1792361084188},{"id":"RbUHxE9c5NQem9pUQECx7","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"FCcx9EVdPWwkA6032mhg0","text":"She lit the furnace for him.\n\nNot the great furnace; there was not wood enough on the island for that, and no reason. The small one, the glory-hole by the bench, where Bortolo had warmed the pieces between gathers; she lit it at dusk with the kindling and then the beech, and fed it until the mouth of it stood open on a round of white-orange light that hurt to look at, and the workshop, which had been cold for five weeks, began to tick and creak around her as the walls remembered what they were for.\n\nWhen Matteo Lion came up the step from his boat she was standing at the bench with a blowpipe in the glory-hole, turning it slowly, as though there were glass on the end of it. There was no glass. There was only the iron, going red, then orange.\n\n\"Madonna.\" He stopped in the water-door. The heat met him there; she saw it in his face. \"You've lit the fire.\"\n\n\"Come in, Messer Lion. Shut the door. The draught's bad for the glass.\"\n\nHe shut it. He came across the floor, and stopped at the end of the bench, where the light from the hole lay over him and made him look younger, or else older, it was hard to tell. He had a roll of paper under his arm. The way, she supposed. He laid it on the marver.\n\n\"The friar's not here?\" he said.\n\n\"The friar's in the lehr-room with my niece. They're listening.\" She turned the pipe. \"I wanted them to hear what you say. In case I have to tell it afterwards to someone, and they ask for witnesses.\"\n\nHe did not move. She watched him not move; she watched him understand, and add it up, and arrive at the figure. It did not take him long. He had always been quick.\n\n\"Orsato,\" he said.\n\n\"Orsato. At the Fleming's. On Sunday last. Talking about gold on a table and the Capi at the door, and a widow taken with the book in her hands.\" She drew the pipe out of the hole and held it up between them. The last hand's length of it was the colour of an apricot. \"Four people knew the day, Messer Lion. I didn't tell Dolfin. Lucia didn't tell Dolfin. Fra Anselmo didn't tell Dolfin, though he's the only one of you I can't prove it of.\"\n\n\"It wasn't the friar.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe pipe hissed faintly in the air between them. Neither of them looked at it.\n\n\"Four years,\" he said. \"Since they struck me off. He came to me the week after, at the Greek's, and bought me a drink, and paid my rent, and asked me to tell him now and then what I heard. In the malvasie. In the courts. Who was in debt, who was in love, who was leaving the city. I told myself it was nothing. I told myself I was a notary still, taking depositions. That's what I told myself.\" He looked at the glowing iron. \"I'd tell you I'm sorry, but you wouldn't believe me, and you'd be right.\"\n\n\"I don't want you to be sorry. I want to know what you're going to do.\"\n\nHe put his hand in his pocket, then, and she lifted the pipe an inch, and he stopped, and slowly drew the hand out and opened it on the marver, in the light.\n\nThere were three things on his palm. Her mother's coral, the little red branch. A letter, folded very small, in a way she had seen once, on Dolfin's table, with its seal lifted cleanly and pressed back. And a bead.\n\nShe knew the bead before she knew she knew it. White, lattimo, round as a pea, with a single thread of blue wound once about it. She had sent back eleven of them, the spring he was fourteen, because they were not round, or the blue had run, or the white was not white enough. The twelfth she had kept. Or she had thought she had. It was on the shelf by San Donato.\n\nNo. It was not. It was here.\n\n\"He said to tell you he's keeping the blanket dry,\" Matteo Lion said. \"He has the gaol fever. Not the worst kind, I think; I've seen the worst kind. And Dolfin told me on Thursday, at the foot of the Scala d'Oro, that when you've been taken at the Fleming's and given him the leaf, the Council will send your son to the galleys regardless, and Dolfin will speak very movingly for clemency, and lose.\" He pushed the letter towards her with one finger. \"That's to the Fleming. In Dolfin's own hand. He gave it me to carry and I didn't carry it. It promises Vos the receipt, a fair copy, when you've given it up. If the Capi saw it, they'd know what he is. A secretary of the Ten selling the secrets of the Art to Antwerp for gold.\"\n\nIsotta put the pipe back in the glory-hole. Her hand was not steady and she did not want him to see it.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\nHe thought about it. He actually thought, standing there with the heat on his face; she watched him do it, and somehow that was what she believed, in the end, more than anything he said.\n\n\"Because your son told me he'd kill me with the hot end of a pipe if I got you killed,\" he said. \"And I believed him. And I found, when I thought about it, that I'd very much rather he didn't have to.\"\n\nFrom the lehr-room door, behind her, there came a small sound, as of an old man trying not to laugh.\n\n\"Come out, Brother,\" said Isotta, without turning round. \"Lucia. Bring the lamp.\" She picked up the bead and closed her hand on it, so hard she felt the little thread of blue against her palm, and looked at the notary across the marver. \"Sit down, Messer Lion. You can sit down here. We're going to steal my husband's book on Thursday exactly as you planned it. Up the Censori, with the friar's warrant, and out in my niece's pillow. And when the boy Sanudo shuts the press behind you, there will still be a book in it.\"","wordCount":1053,"createdAt":1792361920429},{"id":"T0v0-Bbu0VdyIpi3aoWIC","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"QTNnUEyj1eh9MHQvCY99h","text":"[\n  Place: #The malvasia at San Zulian\n  Characters: @@Matteo Lion @@Fra Anselmo @@Isotta Ferro\n  When: +2 days\n  Facts: ?Dolfin will trade Nane for the leaf:@Matteo Lion ?Isotta means to steal the ledger back:@Matteo Lion ?Isotta means to steal the ledger back:@Fra Anselmo ?Isotta means to steal the ledger back:reader\n]\n\nIt rained on Sunday the way it only rains in Venice in April, as if the sky had been saving it. The water came down straight and grey into the canals and the canals did not seem to notice, being water already, and only the people minded: the people ran under arches with their cloaks over their heads, and the people crowded into the malvasia behind San Zulian until the windows ran on the inside as well as the out.\n\nMatteo Lion had been there since Mass. He had not gone to Mass. He had gone, at the hour of Mass, to the malvasia, where the Greek kept a back room with a table and a lamp and a pack of Neapolitan cards that had been shuffled so often the court cards were faceless, and where Matteo had lost, by the time the bells of San Marco struck the end of the sung Mass, eleven lire and a pair of gloves.\n\nThey were good gloves. He minded the gloves.\n\n\"You are a man of no luck,\" said the Greek, who was also the dealer, and who said it every Sunday with the same warm sorrow, like a priest reading the same Gospel.\n\n\"I am a man of excellent luck,\" Matteo said. \"It is only that it is all bad.\" He pushed his last coins across. \"Deal.\"\n\nHe was forty, and had been for some time. He had a long face that women had once called distinguished and now called tired, and a notary's hands, ink in the creases that no washing reached, and a voice that could read a deposition aloud in the Avogaria so that the accused wept to hear his own crimes in it. He had been struck from the roll of notaries four years ago for selling a copy of a sealed deposition to the family it concerned, who had paid him well and then betrayed him at the first opportunity, as families do. Since then he had lived by writing letters for people who could not write them, and wills for people who could not afford a real notary, and once, memorably, a love sonnet for a gondolier who had then married the wrong girl.\n\nAnd by one other thing, which he did not think about on Sundays.\n\nHe lost the hand. He was reaching for his cloak when the curtain of the back room moved and Fra Anselmo came in out of the rain like a wet black heron, and behind him a woman Matteo did not know.\n\n\"Brother,\" Matteo said. \"I have no money and I have not been to Mass. Whichever of those you've come about, you're too late.\"\n\n\"I have come about neither.\" The friar shook his hood off. He was sixty, or seventy, or a hundred; he had one milky eye and one that missed nothing, and he could cut a seal in pear-wood that the Doge's own chancery could not tell from the brass. Matteo had met him over a disputed will eight years ago and they had been, ever since, the kind of friends who do not ask each other questions. \"This is Madonna Isotta, widow of the late master Bortolo Ferro of Murano. Madonna, this is the man I told you about. He is a gambler and a liar and he was once the best notary in the Republic.\"\n\n\"I still am,\" said Matteo. \"It's only that the Republic hasn't noticed.\" He looked at the woman properly, and stopped talking.\n\nShe was not beautiful. She was something more inconvenient than that: she was paying attention. She stood in the doorway of the Greek's back room in a wet black cloak with her hands folded in front of her, and she looked at him, at the faceless cards, at his ungloved hands, at the coins in front of the Greek, and he saw her add it all up and arrive at a figure.\n\n\"Fra Anselmo says you know the Palace,\" she said.\n\n\"Everybody knows the Palace. It's very large and you can see it from the sea.\"\n\n\"He says you know the stairs. The ones the clerks use. He says you know where the Ten keep what they take.\"\n\nThe Greek, who understood a great deal more Venetian than he admitted, got up and went out through the curtain with the cards in his hand. Matteo heard him tell the boy at the barrels to bring wine to the back room and then to stay away from it. Then it was just the three of them, and the lamp, and the rain.\n\n\"Sit down, Madonna,\" Matteo said. \"Please. And tell me what they took.\"\n\nShe told him. She told it the way he would have liked witnesses to tell things in the old days, in order and without decoration: the men from the Ten, the book, the secretary in the little room, the offer, her son at the grille. She did not say what was on the missing leaf. She said only that there was a leaf, and that Dolfin wanted it, and that she would not give it to him, and that if she did not her son would go to the galleys on the Feast of the Sensa.\n\n\"Dolfin,\" Matteo said.\n\n\"You know him?\"\n\n\"Everyone who has ever been in trouble in this city knows Zuane Dolfin.\" That was true, as far as it went. He drank some of the wine the boy had brought. It was bad. \"So. You won't give him the leaf. Then what do you want from me?\"\n\n\"I want the book back.\"\n\nMatteo put down his cup.\n\n\"It was my husband's,\" she said. \"And his father's. Two hundred and eleven leaves. Everything the Ferro ever learned about glass. If it stays in that chest, they will copy it out for the Guild, and the Guild will give it to whichever house pays the Ten most, and in ten years every bead-maker on the island will be making Ferro cristallo and selling it for half. And my son will have nothing. Not a furnace. Not a name. And Dolfin will still want the leaf.\"\n\n\"And with the book back?\"\n\n\"With the book back, I have something to sell.\" She looked at Fra Anselmo, and then at him. \"There is a Fleming at the Rialto who wanted my son. I think he will want the book more. Enough to carry two people to Antwerp and enough besides to buy the gaoler who holds the key of the third cell.\"\n\nIt was, Matteo thought, the most insane thing anyone had said to him in four years, and he had once been asked to forge a papal dispensation for a marriage between a man and his own aunt.\n\n\"You understand,\" he said, \"that the Secret Chancery is the best-guarded room in the Republic. That there are men in it night and day. That the chests are iron and the keys are held by the Capi of the Ten themselves, three keys to a chest, one each.\"\n\n\"Fra Anselmo says there is one day a year when the men in it are not there.\"\n\nHe looked at the friar. The friar looked at the lamp.\n\n\"The Sensa,\" Matteo said slowly. \"When the Doge goes out on the Bucintoro to marry the sea, the whole Signoria goes with him. The Capi. The secretaries. Every clerk who can get a place on a boat. The Palace is a shell for four hours.\" He heard himself say it and was appalled at how easily it came. \"That is the day they will sentence your son.\"\n\n\"Then we have until that day,\" Isotta Ferro said, \"and not one hour more.\"","wordCount":1329,"createdAt":1792361085533},{"id":"UJNr1NE7gQ4QwL6m4v6nH","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"Hq8_8_xvRODxArlMBYfhb","text":"Outside it was evening, and warm, the first warm evening of the year, with the first mosquitoes coming up off the canals like smoke. The bridge was crowded with people going home and people going out, and the shutters of the shops along the Ruga were going up with a sound like a long roll of drums. Her aunt walked fast, and did not speak, and Lucia had almost to run to keep beside her.\n\nAt the top of the bridge her aunt stopped. Below them the Grand Canal was full of boats and the light was going pink on the water, and a man on a barge was singing something about a girl from Chioggia with a voice so bad it was almost beautiful.\n\n\"Well?\" said her aunt.\n\n\"He knew about the leaf.\"\n\n\"Nane told him.\"\n\n\"Nane told him there was a last thing. He didn't tell him what. Zia, he wasn't surprised. When you said you could get the book out of the Ten's own house, he wasn't surprised. He was pleased, he was greedy, but he wasn't surprised. People are surprised when you say a thing like that.\" Lucia gripped the warm stone of the parapet. \"And he turned the letter over. On the desk. When we came in.\"\n\n\"Merchants turn their letters over. They're all thieves; they think everyone else is.\"\n\n\"And he said the Sensa,\" Lucia said. \"You said the day after the Feast. You didn't say which Feast. There are three Feasts in May. And he said the Zeeridder sails the morning after the Sensa, as you wish.\"\n\nHer aunt was quiet. On the barge the man had come to the part of the song where the girl from Chioggia drowned herself, and was making the most of it.\n\n\"Everybody in Venice knows the Sensa is the only Feast that matters,\" Isotta said at last. \"He's been here a year. He'd have learned that much.\"\n\n\"Zia—\"\n\n\"Lucia.\" Her aunt turned to her, and put both hands on her shoulders, and Lucia saw in the pink light how tired she was, how the skin under her eyes had gone thin and dark like the skin of a bruised plum. \"I don't trust him. I don't trust Matteo Lion and I don't entirely trust Fra Anselmo, God forgive me, and the man I trust least in the world is Zuane Dolfin, who is the only one of them who has never lied to me. I don't have to trust them. I have to use them. That's all.\" She let go. \"And he's not getting the calcedonio. Whatever happens. He's getting two hundred and eleven leaves and a widow who keeps house and has a very bad memory for weights.\"\n\n\"And Nane?\"\n\n\"Nane will have a bench in Antwerp and hate it, and in five years he'll come home and the Ten will have forgotten him, and he'll make calcedonio on Murano, out of your collar, and be famous.\" Her aunt took her arm. \"Come on. Piero won't wait after the Ave Maria and I'm not rowing you back to Burano in the dark.\"\n\nThey went down off the bridge into the crowd. Lucia looked back once, up at the windows above the spice-merchant's, and there was a candle lit in one of them now; and for a moment a man stood in it, small and neat, looking down at the bridge where they had been. Then he drew the curtain.\n\n# What Lucia Heard\n\n[\n  Place: #The Fleming's lodging\n  Characters: @@Lucia Zen @@Pieter Vos @@Orsato\n  When: +3 days\n  Facts: ?Vos is working with Dolfin:@Lucia Zen ?Vos is working with Dolfin:reader ?Vos is working with Dolfin:@Pieter Vos:before ?Vos is working with Dolfin:@Orsato:before ?Dolfin promised Vos a copy of the calcedonio receipt:@Lucia Zen ?Dolfin promised Vos a copy of the calcedonio receipt:reader\n]\n\nShe had left her pattern-book under the Fleming's chair.\n\nShe did not know, afterwards, whether she had done it on purpose. She had taken it out of her basket when they sat down, because she always did, because her hands did not know what to do with themselves unless they were holding thread or something that told them about thread, and she had put it under the chair when Vos poured the wine, and she had not picked it up again when her aunt stood. She remembered seeing it there, a little red book on the Turkish carpet, as they went out of the door. She remembered thinking: I'll go back for it. And then thinking: no, don't. And then not thinking about it at all for three days, which was not like her.\n\nOn Sunday her mother went to Mass at San Martino and then to her sister's at Mazzorbo, and Lucia said she had a headache, and when the boat had gone she took the ferry to the Fondamente Nove and walked. She walked the whole way across the city to the Rialto, through Santi Apostoli and the Strada Nova and over the little bridges that went up and down like the stitches in a border, and when she came to the foot of the spice-merchant's stair she stood a long time sneezing in the pepper and telling herself she had come for her book.\n\nThe stair was dark. The shop below was shut for the Sunday. At the top the door of the Fleming's rooms was not closed: it stood open the width of a hand, and through the gap came voices, and Lucia, who had raised her knuckle to knock the way her aunt knocked, held it there in the air and did not.\n\n\"—real gold,\" said a voice she did not know. Then she did know it. She had heard it once, through the water-door of her aunt's house, on the day the men from the Ten came: flat, exact, unhurried. \"His Excellency is particular. It must be real gold and it must be on the table. In bags. Counted. When the Capi come through that door they must see a merchant buying and a widow selling, with the Republic's book between them. Nothing that a clever advocate could call a misunderstanding.\"\n\n\"I'm not a fool, Orsato.\" That was Vos. His good Venetian had gone thick and careful, the way people speak a language when they are angry in it. \"You may tell your master the gold will be there. It is my gold. I should like to know it will be returned.\"\n\n\"It will be impounded. And returned to you, with the thanks of the Signoria, for having assisted the Republic in the matter of a theft of its property. You will sail on the Zeeridder as you planned, a day or two later. Nobody will touch your cargo.\"\n\n\"And the widow?\"\n\n\"The widow will be taken with the book in her hands. What becomes of her is for the Ten.\"\n\nA pause. Lucia heard wine poured, and then not drunk.\n\n\"And the receipt,\" Vos said. \"The last leaf. His Excellency was quite clear with me, Orsato. The book I can buy in any case; the Guild will sell me a copy of it in a year, I know how these things go. What I came to Venice for is the leaf. His Excellency promised me a fair copy of the leaf, in his own hand, when the widow gives it up.\"\n\n\"His Excellency keeps his promises,\" said Orsato.\n\n\"I'm sure he does. I'm sure he keeps them very exactly.\"\n\nLucia's arm was aching from holding her hand in the air. She lowered it, slowly, and as she did so a board shifted under her foot, not loudly, a small dry sound like a knuckle cracking.\n\nThe voices stopped.\n\nShe did not think. Her body did it for her. She went down the stair not like a person but like water, three steps and three steps and the turn, her skirts gathered in both fists so they should not whisper on the treads, and at the bottom the door of the spice-merchant's storeroom was beside her, not the street door, and she went through it into a dark that was all pepper and cinnamon and the sour smell of sacking, and pulled it to behind her, and crouched against a sack of something hard and round, and did not breathe.\n\nFeet on the stair. Slow ones. They came down to the turn, and stopped.\n\nThrough the crack of the storeroom door she could see a slice of the stairwell: grey light, the dusty bottom step, and then a black shoe, and the hem of a black gown, and a hand on the rail with a ring on it, a plain gold ring, the hand of a man who had carried her uncle's book out of her aunt's house wrapped in oilcloth.\n\nOrsato stood at the bottom of the stair for a long time.\n\nThen he sneezed.\n\nIt was such an ordinary sound. He sneezed, and said something under his breath in the dialect of Chioggia that Lucia's father used when he hit his thumb, and wiped his nose, and went out through the street door into the Sunday afternoon, and the door banged behind him in the draught.\n\nShe stayed among the sacks until the bells rang None. When she came out the stair was empty and the Fleming's door, at the top, was shut. She did not go up for her pattern-book. She never saw it again.","wordCount":1569,"createdAt":1792361557390},{"id":"UgMbkv1qFIk04g-LrHsPL","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"VvxSkA1OXIEZRFdcspW_i","text":"They came down the Censori into the courtyard, and Orsato was there.\n\nHe was not pretending to be a guard any more. He was standing by the great bronze well-head in the middle of the courtyard in his black cap, with his hands behind his back, looking at nothing, the way the Capi had looked at nothing as they walked to the boat; and as Lucia came down the last steps with her basket he turned his head and looked at her, and she felt it go right through the pillow and the linen and the boards and the calf, and she thought, he knows. He can see it. He can see the book through the basket the way he saw me through the storeroom door.\n\n\"Don't stop,\" said Matteo pleasantly beside her. \"Smile at him. He's a friend of mine.\"\n\nShe did not smile. She walked. Across the courtyard, past the well-head, past Orsato, who did not move, through the Porta del Frumento, where the sun hit her in the face like a slap and the noise of the Molo came back all at once, trumpets, bells, the roar of the people as the Bucintoro, far out on the water, came about in a great slow curve towards the Lido.\n\n\"He's following,\" said Matteo. \"Good. Don't look.\"\n\nThey went along the Molo, through the crowd, past the column of San Todaro where the man in the grey coat had finished his fritter and was licking his fingers, past the Zecca, to the place at the end of the Molo where the boats were, hundreds of them, gondolas and sandoli and barges and the big painted peote of the guilds, all jostling and rocking at the steps waiting to follow the Doge to the sea. And in among them, very small, a sandolo from Murano with Piero at the oar and a woman in black sitting in the bottom of it, with a basket on her knee.\n\nIt was the same basket. Lucia had made sure of that; she had bought two from the same man at the Rialto on Monday and rubbed the new one with ash and grease until it looked as old as hers. And the pillow on top of it was the same, or near enough, with a half-made border pinned to it and bobbins hanging down.\n\n\"Zia!\" She waved. It was the most natural thing in the world. A girl from Burano, in the crowd on the Sensa, seeing her aunt in a boat. \"Zia, hold this, I want to go and see the Doge come back—\"\n\nShe went down the wet steps. Her aunt reached up. There was a moment, one, with a gondola bumping on one side and a fat man from the butchers' guild shouting on the other, when both baskets were in the air between them; and then Lucia was climbing back up the steps with a basket on her arm that weighed exactly what hers had weighed when she came out of the Censori, because Fra Anselmo had weighed them on his scales, and her aunt was sitting in the bottom of the boat with a basket on her knee and her hand flat on the pillow, the way she had put her hand flat on the false leaf at dawn.\n\nPiero pushed off. The sandolo slid out between the gondolas, and turned, and went away towards the Arsenal and the open water and Murano, small and black and unremarkable among a thousand boats.\n\nLucia did not watch it go. She turned round on the top step and looked straight across the Molo at Orsato, who had stopped ten paces off by a stall selling glass beads, and was holding one up to the light as though he were thinking of buying it.\n\nHe looked at her. He looked at the basket on her arm. He looked, for a long moment, out at the water where the Murano boat had gone.\n\nThen he put the bead down, and paid for it anyway, and walked away along the Molo towards the Rialto, without hurrying, to tell his master where the girl was going.\n\n# Gold on the Table\n\n## The ash pit\n[\n  Place: #The Ferro furnace\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The Glass Ledger:#The Ferro furnace\n]\n\nShe did not open it in the boat.\n\nShe sat in the bottom of Piero's sandolo all the way across the lagoon with the basket on her knees and her hand flat on the pillow, and she did not lift the pillow, and she did not look under the linen, and she did not let herself think the thing she was thinking, which was that it might not be there. That the girl might have lost her nerve in the Chancery, at the last, and dropped the bobbins and picked them up and walked out with the friar's book still in her basket and the real one still in the press. That she was carrying three days of an old man's forgery across the water like a relic. Piero rowed, and said nothing, and once, off San Michele, where the dead of the city lay behind their red wall, he crossed himself, and she did too.\n\nAt home she barred the water-door and the street door both and carried the basket into the workshop and set it on the marver and lifted the pillow off.\n\nIt was there. Of course it was there. Boards and calf, swollen at the lower edge, and one brass corner missing, the bottom left, exactly as the friar had made it. She opened it at the front and read the first page, in her husband's father's hand, a receipt for a green glass for bottles that nobody had made since she was a girl. She opened it at the back. And then she turned to the end, to the gutter, where Anselmo's false leaf had been sewn in so neatly that she had had to put her nail to the stitches to find them, and there was no leaf. There was a stub of cut paper no wider than a fingernail. There was Bortolo's knife-cut, slightly ragged at the bottom where his hand had slipped, the night he did it, in February, with her holding the candle.\n\nShe put her hand flat on the stub and stood like that for a long time.\n\nThen she wrapped it again in the oilcloth Orsato had brought it away in, which she had kept, and carried it to the great furnace, and lay down on her side on the cold floor, and reached into the ash pit under the grate as far as her arm would go, past the old ash and the grit and the bones of a mouse, to the ledge at the back where Bortolo's father had kept his money in the plague year, and pushed it in. When she stood up she was grey to the shoulder. She washed her arm in the bucket by the marver, and washed her face, and changed into the black wool and the good collar; and she put on, for the first time, the little collar of punto in aria that Lucia had made, with the vine and the pomegranates and the birds and the border of knots like a fence round a garden, and pinned it at her throat with a pin of Murano glass.\n\nThen she went to sell the Fleming nothing.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Fleming's lodging\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Pieter Vos @@Lucia Zen @@Matteo Lion\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?The Glass Ledger is back with Isotta:@Pieter Vos:before\n]\n\nThe gold was on the table.\n\nVos had done it properly. He had taken the Turkish carpet off and laid a white cloth instead, and on the white cloth stood four leather bags, tied at the neck and sealed, and beside them a little brass balance with its pans level and a stack of Venetian sequins in a column to show the weight, catching the candlelight like the scales of a fish. It was the most money Isotta had ever seen in one place. It looked like a stage. It looked like the altar of a church in a town that did not believe in God.\n\n\"Madonna.\" Vos bowed. He was in black silk again, with a ruff, as though for a wedding. \"And the lacemaker. And—\" he looked at Matteo, in the doorway behind them, and something went across his face very fast and was gone \"—a notary. You've brought a notary. Very wise. One should always have a contract witnessed.\"\n\n\"Messer Lion is my friend,\" said Isotta. \"He carried my basket.\"\n\nLucia set the basket on the table, by the gold. She did it carefully, as if it were heavy. It was not heavy. Vos's eyes went to it and stayed.\n\n\"May I?\"\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nHe lifted the pillow off, gently, with both hands, the way a man lifts a sleeping cat. He folded back the linen underneath.\n\nThere was lace under it. Nothing but lace. Collars and cuffs and a border for an altar-cloth, a dozen pieces, Lucia's best, in a white drift that rose up a little as the linen came away, like breath.\n\nVos looked at it. He looked for a long time. Then he looked at Isotta, and she saw the merchant go out of his face and something else come into it, something smaller and older and more frightened.\n\n\"Where is the book?\"\n\n\"I couldn't get it,\" Isotta said. \"The press was locked. The clerk wouldn't open it. We tried, Messer Vos. I'm sorry. But I didn't want to come empty-handed, after you'd been so kind, so I've brought you the best lace in Burano at a very good price. For the ladies of Antwerp. They say the ladies of Antwerp have no lace worth the name.\"\n\nHe did not answer. He was listening; she saw him doing it. Listening past her, past the door, down the stair, to the Ruga below, where the evening crowd was loud with the Sensa fair, and where, over the noise, very faint, there was a sound of many feet on stone coming steadily nearer and not hurrying.\n\n\"You stupid woman,\" Pieter Vos said softly. \"What have you done?\"\n\n\"I?\" said Isotta Ferro. \"I've come to sell you lace.\"\n\nThe door opened.\n\nIt opened all the way, so hard it struck the wall, and Zuane Dolfin came into the room in a gown of black damask with a fur at the collar, and behind him two fanti with drawn swords, and behind them, slowly, because he was seventy and the stair was steep and he was not a man who had ever needed to hurry for anyone, an old man in the black of the Ten, with a gold chain on his chest and a face like a shut door. And behind him, last of all, in his black cap, Orsato.\n\n\"Excellency,\" Dolfin said, turning to the old man, and his voice was the pleasant tired voice of the little room at the Palace, a voice for small business between friends. \"Messer Contarini. Here is the widow Ferro of Murano. You see her with the Fleming, Vos, of Antwerp, who has been asking questions about the Art in every malvasia in the city since the spring. You see the gold. You see—\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\nHe had seen the lace.\n\nThere was a silence in the Fleming's room so complete that Isotta heard the candle flame move. Dolfin looked at the lace on the table, the white drift of it rising out of the girl's basket beside four bags of gold, and the pleasant tired face did not change at all, and that was how she knew. A man who has nothing to fear looks surprised. Zuane Dolfin looked at the lace the way a man looks at a letter he did not expect to see again, in a hand he knows is his own.\n\n\"Well, Dolfin?\" said the old man, Contarini. He had one hand cupped behind his ear. \"What do I see? I see lace.\"\n\n\"She has hidden it,\" said Dolfin. \"Excellency, the book was taken from the Chancery this morning while the Signoria was on the water. This girl carried it out. My man saw her. Orsato—\"\n\n\"I saw the girl,\" said Orsato, from the doorway, \"come down the Censori with a basket.\"\n\n\"There. You see. She has hidden it, she has given it to someone, the boat—Excellency, send to the Chancery. Send to the press. It will be empty.\"\n\nContarini looked at him for a moment out of his shut-door face. Then he turned his head, and said to Orsato, without raising his voice, \"Go to the Chancery. Wake the boy Sanudo if he's asleep, and have him open the press in front of you, and bring me whatever is on the middle shelf. Run.\"\n\nOrsato lifted his cap. He did not run. He was not, Isotta thought, a man who had ever run in his life. But he went.","wordCount":2177,"createdAt":1792362131438},{"id":"UlzRY3FCk61ZgQROQCOiW","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"X5cj4zTjmev3PwWKCfkEx","text":"It was half an hour, by the bells. They waited it out in the Fleming's room with the candles burning down, like people waiting for a birth or a death: Vos at the window with his back to them, Dolfin by the door with his hands folded inside his sleeves, the two fanti at either side of it with their swords grounded and their eyes on the gold, and the old man in the only good chair, with his eyes shut, so that Isotta thought he had gone to sleep until he said, without opening them:\n\n\"Lion.\"\n\nMatteo stepped forward. \"Excellency.\"\n\n\"I know you. You took a deposition for me in the matter of the Grimani wills. Nine years ago. You had a good voice. They struck you off.\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"For what?\"\n\n\"For selling a copy of a sealed deposition to the family it concerned, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Did you do it?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nContarini opened his eyes. \"Well. That's something.\" He held out his hand, palm up, without looking at it, as though he expected something to be put into it, as presumably all his life things had been. \"What have you got for me? You've got something. You've been standing there like a man with a stone in his shoe.\"\n\nMatteo took the letter out of his gown.\n\nIsotta watched Dolfin see it. He was very good. He had been very good all his life. He did not move; he only looked at the letter in the Chancery fold, with its plain red seal lifted cleanly and pressed back, going from Matteo's hand into the hand of the Capo of the Ten, and something went out of him, very quietly, like the air out of a bellows when the boy at the handle stops.\n\nContarini read it. He read it twice, holding it far from his face, the way old men read. Then he looked at Dolfin over the top of it.\n\n\"Your hand, Zuane.\"\n\n\"A forgery, Excellency. That man has been in my pay for four years as an informer. He's a known gambler; he owes money everywhere; he has every reason—\"\n\n\"He has been in your pay for four years,\" said Contarini, \"as an informer. That is very interesting. That was going to be the next thing I asked him.\" He folded the letter. \"And it's your hand. I've had your hand on my table every morning for eleven years. You make your g like a little fish.\"\n\nThen the bells of San Bartolomeo struck the half, and there were feet on the stair, and Orsato came back into the room carrying a book.\n\nIt was wrapped in nothing. He carried it in both hands against his chest, the way he had carried it out of Isotta's house on the day of the sirocco, and he crossed the room to the old man's chair and set it on his knees.\n\n\"From the middle shelf of the press, Excellency,\" he said. \"Sanudo opened it in front of me. It was locked, and the key was on his belt, and he swears the book has not left the Chancery except to be read this morning, in his sight, on the authority of a warrant he could not now find, and put back.\"\n\nContarini looked down at the book on his knees. Boards and calf. Swollen at the lower edge. One brass corner missing, the bottom left.\n\n\"So it was never taken,\" he said, and looked at Dolfin.\n\nAnd Zuane Dolfin, secretary of the Ten for eleven years, who had never once in Isotta's sight done anything he had not decided to do, crossed the room in three strides and took the book off the old man's knees, which nobody in the room had ever seen anyone do, and opened it at the end. Isotta saw his fingers go into the gutter for the stub. She saw them find, instead, a leaf, a whole leaf, sewn in so neatly that you had to put your nail to the stitches to find them; and on it, in the cramped brown hand of Bortolo Ferro, the word CALCEDONIO.\n\n\"There,\" said Dolfin. His voice had gone high and strange. \"There. You see. You see, Excellency? The leaf. The leaf was in it all the time. She put it back. She had it all along and she's put it back, here, it's the receipt, it's the calcedonio, it's what I promised the Fleming—\"\n\nHe heard it. Isotta saw him hear it, a moment after everyone else in the room.\n\nNobody said anything. Over by the window, Pieter Vos put his face in his hands.\n\n\"Take him,\" said Alvise Contarini to the fanti, quite gently. \"And the Fleming. Impound the gold.\" He held out his hand again, palm up, and Orsato took the book out of Dolfin's hands, not roughly, the way he had taken it from the shelf by San Donato, and put it back into the old man's. \"And give me that. It is, after all, the property of the Republic.\"\n\nWhen they had gone, the two fanti and the Fleming and the secretary between them, down the stair into the noise of the fair, the old man sat for a while with the book closed on his knees and looked at Isotta Ferro, and at the collar at her throat, the vine and the pomegranates and the birds.\n\n\"Very pretty lace,\" he said. \"Your niece's?\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Mm.\" He turned the book over in his hands, the false book, Anselmo's three nights' work, with every weight wrong. \"I'm told your son has the gaol fever.\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"That's the spring tides.\" The Capo of the Ten stood up, slowly, in stages, with one hand on Matteo Lion's arm, which Matteo gave him without being asked. \"It comes up through the floor.\"","wordCount":968,"createdAt":1792362331803},{"id":"VLrAfiwcn723CGqcbmEyr","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"VvxSkA1OXIEZRFdcspW_i","text":"They came down the Censori into the courtyard, and Orsato was there.\n\nHe was not pretending to be a guard any more. He was standing by the great bronze well-head in the middle of the courtyard in his black cap, with his hands behind his back, looking at nothing, the way the Capi had looked at nothing as they walked to the boat; and as Lucia came down the last steps with her basket he turned his head and looked at her, and she felt it go right through the pillow and the linen and the boards and the calf, and she thought, he knows. He can see it. He can see the book through the basket the way he saw me through the storeroom door.\n\n\"Don't stop,\" said Matteo pleasantly beside her. \"Smile at him. He's a friend of mine.\"\n\nShe did not smile. She walked. Across the courtyard, past the well-head, past Orsato, who did not move, through the Porta del Frumento, where the sun hit her in the face like a slap and the noise of the Molo came back all at once, trumpets, bells, the roar of the people as the Bucintoro, far out on the water, came about in a great slow curve towards the Lido.\n\n\"He's following,\" said Matteo. \"Good. Don't look.\"\n\nThey went along the Molo, through the crowd, past the column of San Todaro where the man in the grey coat had finished his fritter and was licking his fingers, past the Zecca, to the place at the end of the Molo where the boats were, hundreds of them, gondolas and sandoli and barges and the big painted peote of the guilds, all jostling and rocking at the steps waiting to follow the Doge to the sea. And in among them, very small, a sandolo from Murano with Piero at the oar and a woman in black sitting in the bottom of it, with a basket on her knee.\n\nIt was the same basket. Lucia had made sure of that; she had bought two from the same man at the Rialto on Monday and rubbed the new one with ash and grease until it looked as old as hers. And the pillow on top of it was the same, or near enough, with a half-made border pinned to it and bobbins hanging down.\n\n\"Zia!\" She waved. It was the most natural thing in the world. A girl from Burano, in the crowd on the Sensa, seeing her aunt in a boat. \"Zia, hold this, I want to go and see the Doge come back—\"\n\nShe went down the wet steps. Her aunt reached up. There was a moment, one, with a gondola bumping on one side and a fat man from the butchers' guild shouting on the other, when both baskets were in the air between them; and then Lucia was climbing back up the steps with a basket on her arm that weighed exactly what hers had weighed when she came out of the Censori, because Fra Anselmo had weighed them on his scales, and her aunt was sitting in the bottom of the boat with a basket on her knee and her hand flat on the pillow, the way she had put her hand flat on the false leaf at dawn.\n\nPiero pushed off. The sandolo slid out between the gondolas, and turned, and went away towards the Arsenal and the open water and Murano, small and black and unremarkable among a thousand boats.\n\nLucia did not watch it go. She turned round on the top step and looked straight across the Molo at Orsato, who had stopped ten paces off by a stall selling glass beads, and was holding one up to the light as though he were thinking of buying it.\n\nHe looked at her. He looked at the basket on her arm. He looked, for a long moment, out at the water where the Murano boat had gone.\n\nThen he put the bead down, and paid for it anyway, and walked away along the Molo towards the Rialto, without hurrying, to tell his master where the girl was going.","wordCount":692,"createdAt":1792362130875},{"id":"VWiv3ad_EAhm2m3IEYrqR","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"FCcx9EVdPWwkA6032mhg0","text":"She lit the furnace for him.\n\nNot the great furnace; there was not wood enough on the island for that, and no reason. The small one, the glory-hole by the bench, where Bortolo had warmed the pieces between gathers; she lit it at dusk with the kindling and then the beech, and fed it until the mouth of it stood open on a round of white-orange light that hurt to look at, and the workshop, which had been cold for five weeks, began to tick and creak around her as the walls remembered what they were for.\n\nWhen Matteo Lion came up the step from his boat she was standing at the bench with a blowpipe in the glory-hole, turning it slowly, as though there were glass on the end of it. There was no glass. There was only the iron, going red, then orange.\n\n\"Madonna.\" He stopped in the water-door. The heat met him there; she saw it in his face. \"You've lit the fire.\"\n\n\"Come in, Messer Lion. Shut the door. The draught's bad for the glass.\"\n\nHe shut it. He came across the floor, and stopped at the end of the bench, where the light from the hole lay over him and made him look younger, or else older, it was hard to tell. He had a roll of paper under his arm. The way, she supposed. He laid it on the marver.\n\n\"The friar's not here?\" he said.\n\n\"The friar's in the lehr-room with my niece. They're listening.\" She turned the pipe. \"I wanted them to hear what you say. In case I have to tell it afterwards to someone, and they ask for witnesses.\"\n\nHe did not move. She watched him not move; she watched him understand, and add it up, and arrive at the figure. It did not take him long. He had always been quick.\n\n\"Orsato,\" he said.\n\n\"Orsato. At the Fleming's. On Sunday last. Talking about gold on a table and the Capi at the door, and a widow taken with the book in her hands.\" She drew the pipe out of the hole and held it up between them. The last hand's length of it was the colour of an apricot. \"Four people knew the day, Messer Lion. I didn't tell Dolfin. Lucia didn't tell Dolfin. Fra Anselmo didn't tell Dolfin, though he's the only one of you I can't prove it of.\"\n\n\"It wasn't the friar.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe pipe hissed faintly in the air between them. Neither of them looked at it.\n\n\"Four years,\" he said. \"Since they struck me off. He came to me the week after, at the Greek's, and bought me a drink, and paid my rent, and asked me to tell him now and then what I heard. In the malvasie. In the courts. Who was in debt, who was in love, who was leaving the city. I told myself it was nothing. I told myself I was a notary still, taking depositions. That's what I told myself.\" He looked at the glowing iron. \"I'd tell you I'm sorry, but you wouldn't believe me, and you'd be right.\"\n\n\"I don't want you to be sorry. I want to know what you're going to do.\"\n\nHe put his hand in his pocket, then, and she lifted the pipe an inch, and he stopped, and slowly drew the hand out and opened it on the marver, in the light.\n\nThere were three things on his palm. Her mother's coral, the little red branch. A letter, folded very small, in a way she had seen once, on Dolfin's table, with its seal lifted cleanly and pressed back. And a bead.\n\nShe knew the bead before she knew she knew it. White, lattimo, round as a pea, with a single thread of blue wound once about it. She had sent back eleven of them, the spring he was fourteen, because they were not round, or the blue had run, or the white was not white enough. The twelfth she had kept. Or she had thought she had. It was on the shelf by San Donato.\n\nNo. It was not. It was here.\n\n\"He said to tell you he's keeping the blanket dry,\" Matteo Lion said. \"He has the gaol fever. Not the worst kind, I think; I've seen the worst kind. And Dolfin told me on Thursday, at the foot of the Scala d'Oro, that when you've been taken at the Fleming's and given him the leaf, the Council will send your son to the galleys regardless, and Dolfin will speak very movingly for clemency, and lose.\" He pushed the letter towards her with one finger. \"That's to the Fleming. In Dolfin's own hand. He gave it me to carry and I didn't carry it. It promises Vos the receipt, a fair copy, when you've given it up. If the Capi saw it, they'd know what he is. A secretary of the Ten selling the secrets of the Art to Antwerp for gold.\"\n\nIsotta put the pipe back in the glory-hole. Her hand was not steady and she did not want him to see it.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\nHe thought about it. He actually thought, standing there with the heat on his face; she watched him do it, and somehow that was what she believed, in the end, more than anything he said.\n\n\"Because your son told me he'd kill me with the hot end of a pipe if I got you killed,\" he said. \"And I believed him. And I found, when I thought about it, that I'd very much rather he didn't have to.\"\n\nFrom the lehr-room door, behind her, there came a small sound, as of an old man trying not to laugh.\n\n\"Come out, Brother,\" said Isotta, without turning round. \"Lucia. Bring the lamp.\" She picked up the bead and closed her hand on it, so hard she felt the little thread of blue against her palm, and looked at the notary across the marver. \"Sit down, Messer Lion. You can sit down here. We're going to steal my husband's book on Thursday exactly as you planned it. Up the Censori, with the friar's warrant, and out in my niece's pillow. And when the boy Sanudo shuts the press behind you, there will still be a book in it.\"\n\n# The Marriage of the Sea\n\n## The Molo\n[\n  Place: #The Molo\n  Characters: @@Lucia Zen @@Matteo Lion @@Orsato\n  When: Thursday 19 May\n  Items: ^The false ledger:@Lucia Zen ^The forged warrant:@Matteo Lion\n]\n\nOn the morning of the Sensa the whole city walked to the sea.\n\nLucia had seen it before, every year of her life, from a boat on the edge of the crowd with her father swearing at the other boats; she had never seen it from the inside. From the inside it was not a crowd. It was weather. It moved in gusts and eddies along the Riva and across the Piazzetta, it sang, it shouted, it sold things, it smelled of fried fish and orange-water and incense and the sweat of ten thousand people in their best clothes, and it carried her along the Molo with her basket on her arm and her pillow on top of the basket the way the tide carries a cork.\n\nThe basket was heavy. Nobody would have known. She carried it the way she carried it every day, on the crook of her left arm with her hand over the pillow, and the pillow on top had a half-made border pinned on it with the bobbins hanging down, and under the pillow, wrapped in a cloth of unbleached linen, was a book in boards and calf, swollen at the lower edge, with one brass corner missing, the bottom left.\n\nFra Anselmo had brought it to Murano at dawn. He had not slept, he said, for three nights, and looked it; his good eye was red and his bad eye was redder, and his fingers were black to the second joint. Her aunt had opened it at the front and read the first page and closed it and opened it at the back and read the last, and then turned to the end, where the stub was, and found the leaf sewn in there, in Zio Bortolo's cramped brown hand, CALCEDONIO, with the weights all wrong. She had put her hand flat on it for a moment. Then she had kissed the friar on both cheeks, which Lucia had never seen her do to anyone, not even Zio Bortolo, and he had gone pink to the edge of his tonsure.\n\nAt the third hour the bells began.\n\nAll of them. San Marco first, the great bell, the Marangona, so deep Lucia felt it in her teeth rather than heard it; and then San Zaccaria and San Giorgio across the water and the Pietà and San Moisè, and the ones further off, Santi Apostoli and the Frari and the Madonna dell'Orto, until the whole city was ringing like a struck glass. And out of the Porta della Carta, between the guards, came the Doge.\n\nLucia had never been so close. He was an old man in cloth of gold, with the horned cap on his head and a face like a walnut, and he walked very slowly because of the weight of the gold, between two men holding up the train, and behind him came the Signoria in crimson, and behind them the Capi of the Ten in black, three old men in a row looking at nothing. The crowd went down on its knees. Lucia went down on her knees with her basket in her arms, and through the legs of the people in front she watched the procession go along the Molo to the water, where the Bucintoro lay against the steps like a great golden animal, and all its oars stood up in the air at once in a salute, two hundred of them, white, with a sound like a flock of birds rising.\n\nWhen she stood up, Matteo Lion was beside her.\n\nShe had seen him only once, the night of the glory-hole, in the bad light of the furnace. In the morning sun he looked older and more respectable, in a notary's black gown with a roll of papers in his hand and a cap like a pudding, and he was smiling at the Bucintoro as though he had paid for it.\n\n\"You're very punctual,\" he said, without looking at her. \"Don't look round. There's a man by the column of San Todaro in a grey coat eating a fritter. He's been there since the second hour. And Orsato is at the Porta della Carta, behind the guard, pretending to be a guard.\"\n\nLucia did not look round.\n\n\"The Capi are on the boat,\" Matteo said. \"All three of them. And Dolfin isn't.\"\n\n\"Where is he?\"\n\n\"He sent his excuses this morning. A fever.\" Matteo smiled more widely at the Bucintoro, which was moving now, very slowly, out from the steps into the basin, with the trumpets going on the poop and the oars rising and falling. \"I expect he'll recover by noon. Have you got it?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Have you got the other thing?\"\n\nShe moved her right hand on the basket handle, and felt, in the pocket of her apron, the little bag of bobbins that she had wound the night before: forty of them, old ones, boxwood and bone, every one she could find in her mother's house.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then walk with me,\" said Matteo Lion, \"like a girl from Burano who's been asked to carry something for a notary and doesn't think much of it, and when we're on the stair, don't speak at all. Your accent is very charming and very Burano and the guard on the Censori would remember it for the rest of his life.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Secret Chancery\n  Characters: @@Matteo Lion @@Lucia Zen @@Sanudo\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The Glass Ledger:@Lucia Zen ^The false ledger:#The Secret Chancery ^The forged warrant:@Sanudo\n  Facts: ?The false ledger is in the press:@Matteo Lion ?The false ledger is in the press:@Lucia Zen ?The false ledger is in the press:reader\n]\n\nThe guard at the stairhead of the Censori was a Dalmatian with a broken nose who had been a guard at the stairhead of the Censori, Matteo was fairly sure, since the war of Cyprus. He did not read the warrant. He looked at the seal on it, the lion with his paw on the book, and at Matteo's gown and cap, and at the girl with the basket, and spat out of the window into the Rio di Palazzo, and waved them on.\n\nMatteo's legs had been fine on the stair. They went to water in the passage. It was the smell that did it: ink, and beeswax on the panelling, and the faint sharp smell of the sand they shook over wet signatures, which he had not smelled for four years and which went into him like a hook into a fish. The clerks' desks along the wall were empty. The pens lay on them in a row. Somewhere far off, through the Palace, he could hear the trumpets on the water, thin as a gnat.\n\nSanudo was in the Chancery with his feet on a desk, eating cherries out of his hat.\n\n\"Lion!\" He took his feet down. He was twenty, and plump, and had a downy beginning of a beard that he touched when he was nervous; he touched it now. \"What are you doing here? You're not supposed to be here. You're not anything any more, are you? They struck you off.\" He seemed pleased with himself for remembering. \"And who's she?\"\n\n\"My sister's girl. She carries my papers.\" Matteo held out the warrant. He did it the old way, the way he had once held out depositions to the Avogadori, with the seal uppermost and his thumb not quite touching it, so the eye went to the wax and stayed there. \"His Excellency Dolfin's compliments, Sanudo. He's sick, poor man, and he's sent me for something, and if you don't help me I shall have to go out to the Bucintoro in a sandolo and explain to the Capi in the middle of the Doge's wedding why the Ferro receipt isn't on their table, and I'll give them your name. Read it.\"\n\nSanudo read it. It took him some time. His lips moved. Matteo, who had written it, could have recited it: the officer on duty in the Secret Chancery to show the bearer the book of receipts of the late Bortolo Ferro, received the twenty-first of April, so that the bearer might copy from it the receipt for lattimo, of which the Capi had need on the Bucintoro for a question raised by the Most Serene Prince touching the making of the cups for the wedding feast. Signed, in a hand that had been dead for six years, by a secretary of the first grade; under it, the lion.\n\nIt was, Matteo thought, a deeply silly warrant. That was why he loved it. Nobody would forge anything so silly.\n\n\"Lattimo,\" said Sanudo.\n\n\"The Doge wants white cups. The Patriarch wants clear ones. There's been a quarrel. You know what they're like.\" Matteo shrugged. \"Twenty lines. I copy them, you lock it up again, I go out to the boat, you go back to your cherries. Or you send me away and tomorrow you explain.\"\n\nSanudo looked at the warrant, and at Matteo, and touched his beard; and Matteo watched him think exactly what he had told Isotta Ferro, in the friar's cell, that the boy would think. Either it was real; or the only man who could have forged it was a man he had beaten at primero.\n\nHe took the key off his belt.\n\nThe press stood against the wall of the little room beyond the Chancery door, the room most people did not know was a room: a cupboard of walnut, tall as a man, with a lock like a fist. Inside, on three shelves, were the new matters, the things that had come in and not yet been entered and sent to their proper chests. Bundles of letters tied with tape. A box of seized pamphlets. A dagger in a bag with a label on it. And on the middle shelf, by itself, a book in boards and calf, swollen at the lower edge, with one brass corner missing, the bottom left.\n\nMatteo heard the girl behind him breathe in, once.\n\n\"There,\" Sanudo said, and lifted it out, and put it on the Chancery desk, and opened it, and stood over it. \"Lattimo. Go on, then.\"\n\nMatteo sat. He took out his own paper and ink, and uncapped the ink, and turned the leaves, slowly, as a notary does, looking for the place, and the boy stood at his shoulder and watched every leaf turn.\n\nThat was the moment, and they had known it would be. Sanudo had to look away. They had argued about it for an hour in the friar's cell and for another hour at the marver with the glory-hole roaring, and in the end it was the girl who had said, quite calmly, turning a bobbin over in her fingers: Nobody can watch a book when a girl drops forty bobbins on a stone floor. Not even a priest. I've seen it.\n\nMatteo found the lattimo. He dipped his pen.\n\nAnd Lucia Zen, standing behind him with her basket, put her hand into her apron for her handkerchief, and brought out the little bag of bobbins instead, and somehow, as she did so, the drawstring came undone.\n\nThey went everywhere. Forty bobbins of boxwood and bone on a floor of red and white marble, bouncing and rolling, clattering like hail on a roof, under the desks, under the press, into the corners, a sound that went on and on as though it would never stop; and the girl gave a cry of real distress, and went down on her knees with her basket, and Sanudo, who was twenty and kind and had a mistress at San Polo and would be a good husband to someone one day, said \"Oh, God, wait, let me,\" and went down on his knees too.\n\nIt took them, afterwards, Matteo calculated, about as long as a Paternoster and two Aves to pick them all up. The girl kept finding more, and holding them up, and thanking him. Sanudo crawled under the press and came out with dust on his hat.\n\nWhen they stood up, Matteo was copying the receipt for lattimo, very neatly, in his best hand, and the book was open on the desk beside him at the right page, and the girl's basket was on her arm, and her pillow was on top of it, with the half-made border pinned to it, and the bobbins hanging down.\n\nIt was heavier on the left now, Matteo thought, watching her settle it; she had to brace her hip. It was not heavier. It was precisely the same weight. He had weighed them both, on the friar's scales, the night before.\n\nHe sanded his copy, and folded it, and stood.\n\n\"My thanks, Sanudo. I'll tell His Excellency you were very helpful.\"\n\n\"Will you?\" The boy looked absurdly pleased. He shut the book, and lifted it, and put it back on the middle shelf of the press, by itself, and locked the lock like a fist, and hung the key on his belt, and patted it. \"There. Safe as the Mint.\"\n\n\"Safer,\" said Matteo.","wordCount":3270,"createdAt":1792361920977},{"id":"VY-lSVaPwfZN5Va6ypmql","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"Lli335bACODw0CxTUpeik","text":"[\n  Place: #The Ferro furnace\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Nane Ferro @@Matteo Lion @@Fra Anselmo @@Lucia Zen @@Orsato\n  When: +2 weeks\n  Items: ^The lattimo bead:@Nane Ferro\n  Facts: ?Dolfin is arrested:@Nane Ferro ?Dolfin is arrested:@Fra Anselmo ?The calcedonio receipt is worked into Lucia's lace:@Nane Ferro\n]\n\nThey let him out on the second of June, a Thursday, in the morning, without any ceremony at all.\n\nThere was no sentence. There had been none on the Sensa either, Matteo told her afterwards; the Council had sat as it always sat after the Doge came back from the Lido, and the matter of the three subjects of the Republic taken trying to carry the Art abroad had been deferred, on the motion of the Capo Contarini, to a later sitting, on the grounds that one of the secretaries who had prepared the depositions had become unavailable. Unavailable was the word. Zuane Dolfin was unavailable in a room at the top of the Palace, under the leads, where the sun came through the roof in summer and turned the air to soup, and where, Matteo said, he had been visited every day by a different member of the Ten and had told each of them something slightly different. Pieter Vos had been put on the Zeeridder at Malamocco without his gold and without his cargo and told that if he were seen in the lagoon again he would be hanged between the columns. And at the later sitting, which nobody outside the room ever heard about, it had been decided that a boy of seventeen with the gaol fever, whose father had been a master of the Art and whose mother had that week rendered the Republic a service it preferred not to describe, might be returned to his family on Murano on the understanding that he would never again try to leave it, and that his mother would keep the Ferro furnace lit.\n\n\"Keep it lit,\" Isotta said. \"Those were the words?\"\n\n\"Those were the words. In the minute.\" Matteo smiled. \"I wrote the minute.\"\n\nHe had a new gown, and ink on his fingers that was new ink, and he went to the Palace every morning at the second hour and came home at dusk to a room in the Calle dei Fabbri that he did not have to lie to anyone about. The Capo Contarini had needed, he had said, a notary in the Avogaria who knew precisely how secretaries of the Ten were corrupted, and could not think of a better one. It was on probation. Matteo said everything in Venice was on probation; it was only that some people were told.\n\nOrsato brought the boy.\n\nHe came up the Rio dei Vetrai in a boat without singing, as he had come the first time, with two fanti at the oars, and in the bottom of the boat, wrapped in the same old woman's blanket, was Nane. He was thin. He was yellow under the eyes, and his hair had grown down over his collar, and when the boat touched the step and he stood up he had to put his hand on Orsato's shoulder to do it, and Orsato let him.\n\nIsotta did not go down the step. She found that she could not. She stood in the water-door with her hands in her apron, and behind her in the workshop Lucia and the friar and the notary stood too, not speaking, and her son came up the three wet steps one at a time and stopped in front of her.\n\n\"Mamma,\" he said. \"I kept the blanket dry.\"\n\nShe put her arms round him. He smelt of the Pozzi, of the sea and what the sea left behind. She did not care. Over his shoulder she saw Orsato, standing in the boat with his black cap in his hand, looking up at the house; and she remembered the day of the sirocco, and the cap lifted once on the Ponte della Paglia, and how long she had wondered whose side he was on.\n\n\"Messer Orsato,\" she said. \"Will you come in? There's wine.\"\n\n\"Thank you, Madonna. No.\" He put his cap on. \"I'm on duty.\" He looked at the boy, and at her, and something happened in his face that was not quite a smile, because it was not a face that had been built for it. \"It was a waste of good hands,\" he said. \"I told you. Good day.\"\n\nThe boat went away down the Rio dei Vetrai, past the Seguso works and the Barovier works and the little bridge where the children sat at night to watch the glow. And Nane put his hand into his shirt and brought out something small and held it out to her on his palm.\n\nA bead. White, round as a pea, with a single blue thread wound once about it.\n\n\"Messer Lion gave it back to me,\" he said. \"In the boat, on the Sensa night, when he came down to the wells with the Capo. He said it was mine to give you and not his.\" He looked past her at Matteo, and then at her again, and he was seven, holding out his burnt hand. \"Is it round enough?\"\n\nShe took it. She closed her hand on it, as she had at the marver with the glory-hole roaring.\n\n\"It'll do,\" she said.","wordCount":895,"createdAt":1792362334993},{"id":"W8-M6XcCaRfmmQ3_w7I7a","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"x4h1FfhvncsMXoADqZCTC","text":"[\n  Place: #The Fleming's lodging\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Pieter Vos @@Lucia Zen @@Matteo Lion\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?The Glass Ledger is back with Isotta:@Pieter Vos:before\n]\n\nThe gold was on the table.\n\nVos had done it properly. He had taken the Turkish carpet off and laid a white cloth instead, and on the white cloth stood four leather bags, tied at the neck and sealed, and beside them a little brass balance with its pans level and a stack of Venetian sequins in a column to show the weight, catching the candlelight like the scales of a fish. It was the most money Isotta had ever seen in one place. It looked like a stage. It looked like the altar of a church in a town that did not believe in God.\n\n\"Madonna.\" Vos bowed. He was in black silk again, with a ruff, as though for a wedding. \"And the lacemaker. And—\" he looked at Matteo, in the doorway behind them, and something went across his face very fast and was gone \"—a notary. You've brought a notary. Very wise. One should always have a contract witnessed.\"\n\n\"Messer Lion is my friend,\" said Isotta. \"He carried my basket.\"\n\nLucia set the basket on the table, by the gold. She did it carefully, as if it were heavy. It was not heavy. Vos's eyes went to it and stayed.\n\n\"May I?\"\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nHe lifted the pillow off, gently, with both hands, the way a man lifts a sleeping cat. He folded back the linen underneath.\n\nThere was lace under it. Nothing but lace. Collars and cuffs and a border for an altar-cloth, a dozen pieces, Lucia's best, in a white drift that rose up a little as the linen came away, like breath.\n\nVos looked at it. He looked for a long time. Then he looked at Isotta, and she saw the merchant go out of his face and something else come into it, something smaller and older and more frightened.\n\n\"Where is the book?\"\n\n\"I couldn't get it,\" Isotta said. \"The press was locked. The clerk wouldn't open it. We tried, Messer Vos. I'm sorry. But I didn't want to come empty-handed, after you'd been so kind, so I've brought you the best lace in Burano at a very good price. For the ladies of Antwerp. They say the ladies of Antwerp have no lace worth the name.\"\n\nHe did not answer. He was listening; she saw him doing it. Listening past her, past the door, down the stair, to the Ruga below, where the evening crowd was loud with the Sensa fair, and where, over the noise, very faint, there was a sound of many feet on stone coming steadily nearer and not hurrying.\n\n\"You stupid woman,\" Pieter Vos said softly. \"What have you done?\"\n\n\"I?\" said Isotta Ferro. \"I've come to sell you lace.\"\n\nThe door opened.\n\nIt opened all the way, so hard it struck the wall, and Zuane Dolfin came into the room in a gown of black damask with a fur at the collar, and behind him two fanti with drawn swords, and behind them, slowly, because he was seventy and the stair was steep and he was not a man who had ever needed to hurry for anyone, an old man in the black of the Ten, with a gold chain on his chest and a face like a shut door. And behind him, last of all, in his black cap, Orsato.\n\n\"Excellency,\" Dolfin said, turning to the old man, and his voice was the pleasant tired voice of the little room at the Palace, a voice for small business between friends. \"Messer Contarini. Here is the widow Ferro of Murano. You see her with the Fleming, Vos, of Antwerp, who has been asking questions about the Art in every malvasia in the city since the spring. You see the gold. You see—\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\nHe had seen the lace.\n\nThere was a silence in the Fleming's room so complete that Isotta heard the candle flame move. Dolfin looked at the lace on the table, the white drift of it rising out of the girl's basket beside four bags of gold, and the pleasant tired face did not change at all, and that was how she knew. A man who has nothing to fear looks surprised. Zuane Dolfin looked at the lace the way a man looks at a letter he did not expect to see again, in a hand he knows is his own.\n\n\"Well, Dolfin?\" said the old man, Contarini. He had one hand cupped behind his ear. \"What do I see? I see lace.\"\n\n\"She has hidden it,\" said Dolfin. \"Excellency, the book was taken from the Chancery this morning while the Signoria was on the water. This girl carried it out. My man saw her. Orsato—\"\n\n\"I saw the girl,\" said Orsato, from the doorway, \"come down the Censori with a basket.\"\n\n\"There. You see. She has hidden it, she has given it to someone, the boat—Excellency, send to the Chancery. Send to the press. It will be empty.\"\n\nContarini looked at him for a moment out of his shut-door face. Then he turned his head, and said to Orsato, without raising his voice, \"Go to the Chancery. Wake the boy Sanudo if he's asleep, and have him open the press in front of you, and bring me whatever is on the middle shelf. Run.\"\n\nOrsato lifted his cap. He did not run. He was not, Isotta thought, a man who had ever run in his life. But he went.","wordCount":932,"createdAt":1792362134544},{"id":"WsZXvddGqtR9qTFciWq-2","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"gnMam_XH4_nPQ3uIi2eK8","text":"The passage under the Palace smelled of the sea and of what the sea left behind. Orsato carried a lamp and went ahead of her and did not speak, and she was grateful, because she did not think she could have answered. The walls sweated. Somewhere water was running, not loud, a thread of it, and the floor under her shoes was soft with something she did not look at.\n\nThe third cell on the lower passage had a door of black oak with a square cut in it at the height of a man's face, and across the square three iron bars, and behind the bars, when Orsato lifted the lamp, her son.\n\nHe had grown. That was her first stupid thought. He had grown in the eleven days since the funeral; or else she had never looked at him properly, or else the cell made him tall by being so low. His hair was flat with damp. He had a blanket round his shoulders like an old woman at a fire.\n\n\"Mamma.\"\n\n\"Don't,\" she said, because his voice had cracked on it, and if he cried she would, and Orsato was standing four paces off with his lamp and his patience. \"Don't. Are you hurt?\"\n\n\"No.\" He put his fingers through the bars, the burnt ones. She took them. They were cold as the canal. \"They haven't done anything. They ask me things. A man comes with a book and asks me what my father put in the batch for lattimo, and I tell him, because everyone in Murano knows what goes in lattimo, and he writes it down very slowly and goes away.\"\n\n\"Nane, why?\"\n\nHe looked at her, and for a moment he was seven again, holding out his blistered hand and asking if it would make him a master.\n\n\"Because they were going to take the book,\" he said. \"Because the Guild had already been to see you, I saw them, I saw old Briati in the yard with his hat in his hands looking at our furnace like a man looking at a widow. Because I'm not going to blow beads for them for thirty years while some secretary keeps Babbo's book in a chest and sells it a page at a time.\" He lowered his voice. \"There's a Fleming. Vos. He's at the Rialto. He said if I came to Antwerp with what was in my head he'd give me a furnace of my own. A furnace, Mamma. Not a bench in someone else's.\"\n\n\"And what was in your head?\"\n\n\"Everything I'd seen.\" His fingers tightened. \"Not the last thing. Babbo never let me see the last thing. But Vos didn't know that.\"\n\nSo he had meant to cheat the Fleming too. He was his father's son. She closed her eyes for a moment.\n\n\"Who else did you tell?\"\n\n\"Nobody. Lucia. Only Lucia.\" He tried to smile. \"She told me I was an idiot.\"\n\n\"She was right.\"\n\n\"Time,\" Orsato said, not unkindly.\n\nNane held on. \"Mamma, the man with the book said something. He said if you were sensible I'd be home for the Sensa. What does that mean? What does he want from you?\"\n\nShe looked at her son through the bars, at his flat wet hair and the old woman's blanket and the hand that had been burnt into the shape of a master's hand, and she thought of Dolfin saying pride is very expensive in the Pozzi.\n\n\"It means he is a liar,\" she said, \"like all of them. Eat what they give you. Keep the blanket dry. Say your prayers and say them out loud, so the guard hears you and thinks you're a good boy. I'll come back.\"\n\n\"When?\"\n\n\"When I can.\"\n\nOrsato was already lowering the lamp. In the last of its light she saw her son's face go back into the dark of the cell, not all at once but by parts, the eyes last, and then there was only the square in the door and the three bars and the sound of the water running somewhere it should not be.\n\nOn the stair, in the daylight again, she had to stop and put her hand against the wall. Orsato stopped too, a step above, and waited, and looked out over the courtyard where the light had moved on to the next drain.\n\n\"He is right,\" he said, to nobody. \"It is a waste of good hands.\"\n\nThen he took her back to the Ponte della Paglia, and handed her down into Piero's boat, and lifted his black cap to her, once, which she would remember a long time afterwards, when she had reason to wonder what side he was on.","wordCount":785,"createdAt":1792364114019},{"id":"YWzqth21flcenvPGQx6U7","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"0YoSvhnQYY2tWtmUCGQdm","text":"\n\nThe sirocco had been blowing for three days, and everything in the house on the Rio dei Vetrai was damp to the touch: the bread, the linen, the ash in the furnace mouth, which no longer glowed. Isotta Ferro had let the fire die on the ninth day after the funeral because there was no one left to tend it but her, and a widow who keeps a furnace lit is a widow who means to work it, and the Guild had already sent a man to remind her what that would cost.\n\nShe was sweeping the cold floor when the boat came.\n\nShe heard it before she saw it: oars, and no singing. Murano boats sang. The ferrymen sang, the sand-barges sang, even the priest's boy sang when he rowed the Host across to the sick. A boat that came up the canal in silence was a boat from the Piazza, and she set the broom against the marver and wiped her hands on her apron and went to the water door to meet it, because there was no point in being found.\n\nThere were four of them. Three wore the grey of the Ten's fanti, swords on short belts. The fourth wore black, and a cap of black felt, and he climbed out onto the step with the care of a man who did not expect to be helped.\n\n\"Madonna Ferro.\"\n\n\"Messer Orsato.\" She knew him. Everyone on the island knew him. He had come for the Barovier boy's tools the winter before, and for old Seguso's moulds, and he would come, one day, for hers. \"You are early for a condolence.\"\n\n\"I am not here for a condolence.\" He did not smile; it was not a face that had been built for it. \"The Most Excellent Council of Ten instructs me to take into its keeping the book of receipts of the late master Bortolo Ferro, glassmaker of this island, which by the statutes of the Art is the property of the Republic upon the death of a master without a sworn heir.\"\n\n\"He has a sworn heir.\"\n\n\"He had a son who did not swear.\" Orsato looked past her into the workshop, at the cold glory-hole and the racked blowpipes and the shelf of clay crucibles cracked from the last firing. \"And the son is not, at present, in a position to swear.\"\n\nShe did not understand that, and she did not let him see that she did not understand it. She stood aside.\n\nThey were not rough. She had expected them to be rough, and it was worse that they were not. One of the fanti lifted the cloth from the cooling-oven as though there might be a child sleeping under it. Another opened the chest of oxides and closed it again without looking, because Orsato had not told him to look. Orsato himself went straight to the shelf above the bench, where the book had sat for twenty-two years between the jar of manganese and the image of San Donato, and took it down with both hands.\n\nIt was a thick book, bound in boards and calf, swollen with damp at the lower corners. Bortolo's father had begun it and Bortolo had filled it, and it held, in a cramped brown hand and a cipher of his own that was no cipher at all to anyone who had stood beside him at the pot, everything the Ferro family knew about making glass clear. The weights of the ash. The washing of the sand from the Ticino. How long to hold the batch at the first heat and how long at the second. The cristallo that had made the house rich and the lattimo, milk-white, that had made it famous. Two hundred and eleven leaves.\n\nOrsato turned them with his thumb, quickly, the way a man counts coin he has already been told is correct. He stopped near the end. He turned back. He looked at the stub of paper sewn into the gathering where the last leaf should have been, a sliver no wider than a fingernail, cut close with a sharp knife.\n\n\"There is a leaf missing.\"\n\n\"There are several.\" Isotta kept her voice tired. That was not difficult. \"He tore them out when a receipt failed. He would not keep a failure in the book. He said it would teach the book bad habits.\"\n\n\"This one was cut.\"\n\n\"Then he cut it.\"\n\nOrsato looked at her for a long moment. Behind him the sirocco shoved at the shutters, and somewhere down the canal a dog was barking at the wind, the high complaining bark of a dog that has been barking for three days.\n\n\"The Council will want to know what was on it,\" he said.\n\n\"The Council may ask him. He is in San Pietro Martire, under the third slab on the left. He was always a good listener in church.\"\n\nOne of the fanti laughed and turned it into a cough. Orsato did not laugh. He wrapped the book in an oilcloth he had brought for the purpose, which told her he had known it would be raining, or sweating, or whatever it was the air did in this wind, and he tucked it under his arm.\n\n\"You will be told,\" he said, \"when the Council wishes to speak to you.\" And then, because he was not a cruel man, only an exact one, he added: \"You should go to the Palace in any case. Before they send for you. Ask at the door of the Pozzi. It is better if a mother asks.\"\n\nHe was in the boat before she had found her voice, and by the time she reached the step the oars were moving, and the boat was going away down the Rio dei Vetrai in silence, past the Seguso works and the Barovier works and the little bridge where the children sat to watch the furnace-glow at night, though there was no glow now, there had been no glow for nine days.\n\nThe Pozzi. The wells. The cells under the Palace where the water came up through the floor at high tide.\n\nShe went back inside and stood in the cold workshop with her hands in her apron. She did not sit down; if she sat down she did not think she would get up. After a while she crossed to the rack of blowpipes and took down the fourth from the left, the oldest, the one with the brass ferrule Bortolo had never let anyone else use, and unscrewed the mouthpiece, and tipped it.\n\nA roll of paper slid out into her palm, tight as a spill for lighting candles, singed brown at one edge where the heat had come too close. She did not unroll it. She knew what was on it. She had watched him write it, and she had corrected the weights while he wrote, and he had let her, which was the nearest thing to an apology for twenty years that she had ever had from him.\n\nCalcedonio. The stone-glass. The streaked and clouded glass that looked like agate, like chalcedony, like something dug out of a mountain and not breathed into shape on a pipe. The Venetians had made it once, a hundred years ago, and lost it. Bortolo had found it again in the last winter of his life. Nobody else knew. Nobody but her.\n\nShe screwed the mouthpiece back on and put the roll inside her bodice, against her skin, where it would be warm, and went to find her cloak.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Ferro furnace\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Lucia Zen\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?Nane was arrested aboard the Sint Joris:@Isotta Ferro ?Nane was arrested aboard the Sint Joris:@Lucia Zen:before ?Nane meant to sell the ledger to a Fleming:@Lucia Zen:before ?Nane meant to sell the ledger to a Fleming:@Isotta Ferro\n]\n\nLucia came across from Burano at dusk, rowing herself, which she was not supposed to do.\n\nIsotta heard the oars from the kitchen and knew them, because they were bad. Lucia rowed the way she did everything that was not lace, which was impatiently and with too much shoulder, and the little sandolo slapped the water like a hand slapping a table. By the time Isotta reached the step, her niece was already tying up, crooked, to the ring that had been meant for the sand barges.\n\n\"You'll have your father after you.\"\n\n\"My father is in Chioggia until Sunday, and my mother thinks I am at the Ursulines learning the punto in aria from Suor Benedetta, who is ninety and asleep.\" Lucia came up the step two at a time. She was nineteen and small and her hands were never still; even standing on the step she was turning the end of the painter over and over in her fingers. \"Zia. Have they come?\"\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Don't. Don't do that, you do that to the Guild men. Have the Ten come?\"\n\n\"This morning.\" Isotta stood aside to let her in, as she had stood aside for Orsato. \"They took the book.\"\n\n\"Only the book?\"\n\n\"What else is there?\"\n\nLucia stopped in the passage. The lamp in the kitchen behind Isotta threw both their shadows up the wall, long and wavering, and Isotta saw in her niece's face that she had come to say something and had been saying it to herself all the way across the lagoon and now did not know how to begin.\n\n\"Sit down,\" Isotta said.\n\n\"I won't. Zia, it's Nane.\"\n\nThe kitchen was warm, at least. She had lit the small fire for the soup, and the sirocco, which made every other room in the house sweat, made the kitchen merely close. Lucia would not sit, so Isotta did, on the bench by the table, and folded her hands, and waited.\n\n\"Piero the boatman was at the Riva degli Schiavoni on Tuesday,\" Lucia said. \"With a load for the Arsenal. There was a Flemish ship at the moorings, the Sint Joris, out of Antwerp, loading oil and currants. And the fanti came along the Riva at first light, eight of them, with a secretary, and they went aboard, and they brought off a boy. A tall boy with a burnt hand. Piero says he did not fight. Piero says he said his name when they asked it, very clearly, as though he wanted it written down right.\"\n\nIsotta looked at her hands.\n\nThe left one had the same burn, the old shiny pucker across the base of the thumb where every glassmaker's child learned, once, that a pipe fresh out of the glory-hole is hot along its whole length and not only at the end. Nane had got his at seven. He had not cried. He had looked at it with great interest and then at her, and asked if it would make him a master.\n\n\"He was going to Antwerp,\" she said.\n\n\"Zia, it's the law. A glassmaker who leaves the Republic—\"\n\n\"I know the law.\"\n\n\"—they can send someone after him. They can kill him in Antwerp if they like and nobody will say a word. And if he is caught before he goes—\"\n\n\"I know the law, Lucia. I was married to it.\" She heard her own voice, flat as a marver, and was ashamed of it. Lucia was crying now, quietly, without stopping turning the rope's end in her fingers, which she had carried in from the boat without noticing. \"He never told me. He never said one word.\"\n\n\"He told me,\" Lucia said.\n\nIsotta looked up.\n\n\"Not when. Not the ship. Only that he would not stay and blow beads for the Guild while they kept his father's book in a chest. He said the Ten would take the book the moment Zio Bortolo died, because Zio Bortolo had found something, and the Guild knew he had found something, and the Ten always know what the Guild knows.\" She wiped her face with the back of her wrist. \"He said there was a Fleming who would pay. For the book. Or for him, if he had the book in his head. He said you'd be better off without him to feed.\"\n\n\"When did he say this?\"\n\n\"At the funeral. In the cloister, while the Guild men were talking to you.\"\n\nSo he had known, at the funeral, with his father's body still on the bier, that the Ten would come. He had known about the Fleming. He had known, or guessed, about the last winter's work, and he had said nothing to her and everything to his cousin, because his cousin was nineteen and made lace and could not stop him.\n\nIsotta put her hand flat on her bodice, where the paper was.\n\n\"Is he in the Pozzi?\"\n\n\"Piero thinks so. They took him to the Palace by the Ponte della Paglia, not to the Arsenal.\"\n\n\"Then tomorrow,\" Isotta said, \"I am going to the Palace.\"\n\n\"They won't let you see him.\"\n\n\"Orsato said a mother should ask.\" She stood. The soup had boiled down to a brown sediment at the bottom of the pot and she lifted it off the fire and set it on the stone and stood looking at it. \"Orsato knew this morning. He knew when he took the book. He said the son is not at present in a position to swear. I thought he meant Nane was a child.\"\n\nLucia said nothing.\n\n\"You'll stay tonight. I'll take you back at first light and you can tell your mother Suor Benedetta kept you for vespers.\"\n\n\"Suor Benedetta has not kept anyone for vespers since the war of Cyprus.\"\n\n\"Then tell her something better. You're the one with the clever hands.\" Isotta reached up and took the rope's end gently out of her niece's fingers and laid it on the table. \"And Lucia. Whatever Nane told you, about the Fleming and the book. You will not tell anyone else.\"\n\n\"Who would I tell?\"\n\n\"Anyone who asks,\" Isotta said. \"That is who people tell.\"\n\nOutside, the wind had dropped at last. In the sudden quiet she could hear the water lapping the step, and the dog down the canal had stopped barking, and somewhere over toward the Fondamenta dei Vetrai a furnace that was still lit was being stoked for the night shift: the deep exhalation of the bellows, and a man's voice calling for more wood, and the small sharp ring of glass touching glass.","wordCount":2412,"createdAt":1792360669007},{"id":"YqbUCFJgwGDWCf8jil7mZ","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"VvxSkA1OXIEZRFdcspW_i","text":"They came down the Censori into the courtyard, and Orsato was there.\n\nHe was not pretending to be a guard any more. He was standing by the great bronze well-head in the middle of the courtyard in his black cap, with his hands behind his back, looking at nothing, the way the Capi had looked at nothing as they walked to the boat; and as Lucia came down the last steps with her basket he turned his head and looked at her, and she felt it go right through the pillow and the linen and the boards and the calf, and she thought, he knows. He can see it. He can see the book through the basket the way he saw me through the storeroom door.\n\n\"Don't stop,\" said Matteo pleasantly beside her. \"Smile at him. He's a friend of mine.\"\n\nShe did not smile. She walked. Across the courtyard, past the well-head, past Orsato, who did not move, through the Porta del Frumento, where the sun hit her in the face like a slap and the noise of the Molo came back all at once, trumpets, bells, the roar of the people as the Bucintoro, far out on the water, came about in a great slow curve towards the Lido.\n\n\"He's following,\" said Matteo. \"Good. Don't look.\"\n\nThey went along the Molo, through the crowd, past the column of San Todaro where the man in the grey coat had finished his fritter and was licking his fingers, past the Zecca, to the place at the end of the Molo where the boats were, hundreds of them, gondolas and sandoli and barges and the big painted peote of the guilds, all jostling and rocking at the steps waiting to follow the Doge to the sea. And in among them, very small, a sandolo from Murano with Piero at the oar and a woman in black sitting in the bottom of it, with a basket on her knee.\n\nIt was the same basket. Lucia had made sure of that; she had bought two from the same man at the Rialto on Monday and rubbed the new one with ash and grease until it looked as old as hers. And the pillow on top of it was the same, or near enough, with a half-made border pinned to it and bobbins hanging down.\n\n\"Zia!\" She waved. It was the most natural thing in the world. A girl from Burano, in the crowd on the Sensa, seeing her aunt in a boat. \"Zia, hold this, I want to go and see the Doge come back—\"\n\nShe went down the wet steps. Her aunt reached up. There was a moment, one, with a gondola bumping on one side and a fat man from the butchers' guild shouting on the other, when both baskets were in the air between them; and then Lucia was climbing back up the steps with a basket on her arm that weighed exactly what hers had weighed when she came out of the Censori, because Fra Anselmo had weighed them on his scales, and her aunt was sitting in the bottom of the boat with a basket on her knee and her hand flat on the pillow, the way she had put her hand flat on the false leaf at dawn.\n\nPiero pushed off. The sandolo slid out between the gondolas, and turned, and went away towards the Arsenal and the open water and Murano, small and black and unremarkable among a thousand boats.\n\nLucia did not watch it go. She turned round on the top step and looked straight across the Molo at Orsato, who had stopped ten paces off by a stall selling glass beads, and was holding one up to the light as though he were thinking of buying it.\n\nHe looked at her. He looked at the basket on her arm. He looked, for a long moment, out at the water where the Murano boat had gone.\n\nThen he put the bead down, and paid for it anyway, and walked away along the Molo towards the Rialto, without hurrying, to tell his master where the girl was going.\n\n# Gold on the Table\n\n[\n  Place: #The Ferro furnace\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The Glass Ledger:#The Ferro furnace\n]\n\nShe did not open it in the boat.\n\nShe sat in the bottom of Piero's sandolo all the way across the lagoon with the basket on her knees and her hand flat on the pillow, and she did not lift the pillow, and she did not look under the linen, and she did not let herself think the thing she was thinking, which was that it might not be there. That the girl might have lost her nerve in the Chancery, at the last, and dropped the bobbins and picked them up and walked out with the friar's book still in her basket and the real one still in the press. That she was carrying three days of an old man's forgery across the water like a relic. Piero rowed, and said nothing, and once, off San Michele, where the dead of the city lay behind their red wall, he crossed himself, and she did too.\n\nAt home she barred the water-door and the street door both and carried the basket into the workshop and set it on the marver and lifted the pillow off.\n\nIt was there. Of course it was there. Boards and calf, swollen at the lower edge, and one brass corner missing, the bottom left, exactly as the friar had made it. She opened it at the front and read the first page, in her husband's father's hand, a receipt for a green glass for bottles that nobody had made since she was a girl. She opened it at the back. And then she turned to the end, to the gutter, where Anselmo's false leaf had been sewn in so neatly that she had had to put her nail to the stitches to find them, and there was no leaf. There was a stub of cut paper no wider than a fingernail. There was Bortolo's knife-cut, slightly ragged at the bottom where his hand had slipped, the night he did it, in February, with her holding the candle.\n\nShe put her hand flat on the stub and stood like that for a long time.\n\nThen she wrapped it again in the oilcloth Orsato had brought it away in, which she had kept, and carried it to the great furnace, and lay down on her side on the cold floor, and reached into the ash pit under the grate as far as her arm would go, past the old ash and the grit and the bones of a mouse, to the ledge at the back where Bortolo's father had kept his money in the plague year, and pushed it in. When she stood up she was grey to the shoulder. She washed her arm in the bucket by the marver, and washed her face, and changed into the black wool and the good collar; and she put on, for the first time, the little collar of punto in aria that Lucia had made, with the vine and the pomegranates and the birds and the border of knots like a fence round a garden, and pinned it at her throat with a pin of Murano glass.\n\nThen she went to sell the Fleming nothing.","wordCount":1241,"createdAt":1792362131779},{"id":"Z4E7dC_wuJygiwPp6Nasj","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"PUre3Tk4FJuGGL9QbD2OU","text":"[\n  Place: #The Pozzi\n  Characters: @@Matteo Lion @@Nane Ferro\n  When: +4 days\n  Items: ^The lattimo bead:@Matteo Lion\n  Facts: ?Nane has the gaol fever:@Matteo Lion ?Nane has the gaol fever:reader\n]\n\nHe told himself he went down to the wells to count the guards.\n\nThat was true, as far as it went. On the Sensa they would come up the Censori, and a man who came up the Censori could also be made to go down it, and down was the Pozzi; it was worth knowing how many men were on the lower passage on a feast day and whether they could hear the Chancery from where they stood. He had a pass. He had kept it, as he had kept the paper. It said that Matteo Lion, notary of the Avogaria, was to be admitted to the prisons to take depositions in the matter of the Republic's business, and it was four years out of date, and the guard on the stool by the low arch could not read it, and let him through for two soldi.\n\nHe counted the guards. There were three, on the lower passage, and one of them was asleep.\n\nThen he went to the third cell, because he had known all along that he would.\n\nThe boy was lying down. That was the first thing. A boy of seventeen does not lie down in the middle of the day unless he has been told to or cannot help it, and nobody had told him to. He was on the plank with the blanket over him up to the chin, and when Matteo lifted the little lamp he had bought from the guard and held it to the bars, the face that turned towards the light was shining wet, and the eyes were too bright.\n\n\"Who are you?\"\n\n\"A friend of your mother's.\"\n\n\"My mother hasn't got any friends.\" The boy laughed and the laugh went into a cough that went on a long time. \"She's got my father's friends, and they all came to the funeral and looked at the furnace. Are you from the Guild?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"From Dolfin?\"\n\nMatteo did not answer quickly enough.\n\n\"Get out,\" the boy said. Then: \"No. Wait. Wait.\" He pushed himself up on one elbow. In the lamplight Matteo could see the hand, the burnt one, gripping the edge of the blanket, the scar shiny as glass. \"Is she all right? Has he done anything to her?\"\n\n\"She's all right.\"\n\n\"She came once. She said she'd come again.\" The boy lay back. \"That was three weeks ago. It's hard to count. They don't tell you the days down here. They give you bread twice and that's a day, but sometimes they forget and give it once, and then you don't know.\" He was quiet for a moment. \"There's a man in the next cell who's been here since the war of Cyprus. He talks to me through the wall. He says it gets easier. He says after the first year you stop hearing the water.\"\n\n\"Your mother's coming for you,\" Matteo said.\n\nHe had not meant to say it. It was the kind of thing one did not say, in the Pozzi, with three guards on the passage and one of them perhaps not asleep.\n\nThe boy turned his head and looked at him. It was Isotta's look. It was exactly her look, the one she had given him in the back room of the Greek's, adding him up.\n\n\"You're the notary,\" Nane said. \"Fra Anselmo's notary. She told me about you. Not your name. She said there was a man who knew the stairs, who was clever and unlucky.\" He was breathing hard. \"Are you going to get her killed?\"\n\nMatteo opened his mouth. Nothing came out of it.\n\n\"Because if you are,\" said Giovanni Ferro, seventeen, lying on a plank under the Palace of the Doges with the gaol fever in him, \"I'll kill you. When I get out. I'll come and find you and I'll do it with a pipe from the glory-hole, the hot end, and it will take a long time.\" He coughed again. \"That's a promise. My father always kept his promises. Ask anybody.\"\n\nThen, before Matteo could say anything, he fumbled under the blanket and brought out something small and held it through the bars.\n\n\"Here. Give her this. I made it before. Before the ship. I had it in my shoe and they didn't find it.\" His fingers opened. On the palm, among the calluses, lay a bead: a little sphere of white glass the size of a chickpea, milk-white, perfectly round, with a single thread of blue wound once about its middle like a ring on a finger. \"Lattimo. The first one I ever made that she didn't send back. Tell her I'm keeping the blanket dry.\"\n\nMatteo took it. It was warm from the boy's hand. He put it in his pocket, where the coral was, and they lay there together, the two small hard things, the white and the red.\n\n\"I'll tell her,\" he said.","wordCount":847,"createdAt":1792361635941},{"id":"ZT616wxvDEryweP5go0d8","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"2ELvnobYYBoagGZKaAtCN","text":"[\n  Place: #The Ferro furnace\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The Glass Ledger:#The Ferro furnace\n]\n\nShe did not open it in the boat.\n\nShe sat in the bottom of Piero's sandolo all the way across the lagoon with the basket on her knees and her hand flat on the pillow, and she did not lift the pillow, and she did not look under the linen, and she did not let herself think the thing she was thinking, which was that it might not be there. That the girl might have lost her nerve in the Chancery, at the last, and dropped the bobbins and picked them up and walked out with the friar's book still in her basket and the real one still in the press. That she was carrying three days of an old man's forgery across the water like a relic. Piero rowed, and said nothing, and once, off San Michele, where the dead of the city lay behind their red wall, he crossed himself, and she did too.\n\nAt home she barred the water-door and the street door both and carried the basket into the workshop and set it on the marver and lifted the pillow off.\n\nIt was there. Of course it was there. Boards and calf, swollen at the lower edge, and one brass corner missing, the bottom left, exactly as the friar had made it. She opened it at the front and read the first page, in her husband's father's hand, a receipt for a green glass for bottles that nobody had made since she was a girl. She opened it at the back. And then she turned to the end, to the gutter, where Anselmo's false leaf had been sewn in so neatly that she had had to put her nail to the stitches to find them, and there was no leaf. There was a stub of cut paper no wider than a fingernail. There was Bortolo's knife-cut, slightly ragged at the bottom where his hand had slipped, the night he did it, in February, with her holding the candle.\n\nShe put her hand flat on the stub and stood like that for a long time.\n\nThen she wrapped it again in the oilcloth Orsato had brought it away in, which she had kept, and carried it to the great furnace, and lay down on her side on the cold floor, and reached into the ash pit under the grate as far as her arm would go, past the old ash and the grit and the bones of a mouse, to the ledge at the back where Bortolo's father had kept his money in the plague year, and pushed it in. When she stood up she was grey to the shoulder. She washed her arm in the bucket by the marver, and washed her face, and changed into the black wool and the good collar; and she put on, for the first time, the little collar of punto in aria that Lucia had made, with the vine and the pomegranates and the birds and the border of knots like a fence round a garden, and pinned it at her throat with a pin of Murano glass.\n\nThen she went to sell the Fleming nothing.","wordCount":544,"createdAt":1792362134543},{"id":"ZnHiw_FxAnjaiJmL3UbWa","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"X5cj4zTjmev3PwWKCfkEx","text":"[\n  Place: #The Fleming's lodging\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Pieter Vos @@Lucia Zen @@Matteo Lion @@Zuane Dolfin @@Alvise Contarini @@Orsato\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^Dolfin's letter to Vos:@Alvise Contarini ^The false ledger:@Zuane Dolfin\n  Facts: ?Dolfin promised Vos a copy of the calcedonio receipt:@Alvise Contarini ?Matteo informs for Dolfin:@Alvise Contarini ?Dolfin is arrested:@Isotta Ferro ?Dolfin is arrested:@Lucia Zen ?Dolfin is arrested:@Matteo Lion ?Dolfin is arrested:@Pieter Vos ?Dolfin is arrested:@Orsato ?Dolfin is arrested:@Alvise Contarini ?Dolfin is arrested:reader\n]\n\nIt was half an hour, by the bells. They waited it out in the Fleming's room with the candles burning down, like people waiting for a birth or a death: Vos at the window with his back to them, Dolfin by the door with his hands folded inside his sleeves, the two fanti at either side of it with their swords grounded and their eyes on the gold, and the old man in the only good chair, with his eyes shut, so that Isotta thought he had gone to sleep until he said, without opening them:\n\n\"Lion.\"\n\nMatteo stepped forward. \"Excellency.\"\n\n\"I know you. You took a deposition for me in the matter of the Grimani wills. Nine years ago. You had a good voice. They struck you off.\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"For what?\"\n\n\"For selling a copy of a sealed deposition to the family it concerned, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Did you do it?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nContarini opened his eyes. \"Well. That's something.\" He held out his hand, palm up, without looking at it, as though he expected something to be put into it, as presumably all his life things had been. \"What have you got for me? You've got something. You've been standing there like a man with a stone in his shoe.\"\n\nMatteo took the letter out of his gown.\n\nIsotta watched Dolfin see it. He was very good. He had been very good all his life. He did not move; he only looked at the letter in the Chancery fold, with its plain red seal lifted cleanly and pressed back, going from Matteo's hand into the hand of the Capo of the Ten, and something went out of him, very quietly, like the air out of a bellows when the boy at the handle stops.\n\nContarini read it. He read it twice, holding it far from his face, the way old men read. Then he looked at Dolfin over the top of it.\n\n\"Your hand, Zuane.\"\n\n\"A forgery, Excellency. That man has been in my pay for four years as an informer. He's a known gambler; he owes money everywhere; he has every reason—\"\n\n\"He has been in your pay for four years,\" said Contarini, \"as an informer. That is very interesting. That was going to be the next thing I asked him.\" He folded the letter. \"And it's your hand. I've had your hand on my table every morning for eleven years. You make your g like a little fish.\"\n\nThen the bells of San Bartolomeo struck the half, and there were feet on the stair, and Orsato came back into the room carrying a book.\n\nIt was wrapped in nothing. He carried it in both hands against his chest, the way he had carried it out of Isotta's house on the day of the sirocco, and he crossed the room to the old man's chair and set it on his knees.\n\n\"From the middle shelf of the press, Excellency,\" he said. \"Sanudo opened it in front of me. It was locked, and the key was on his belt, and he swears the book has not left the Chancery except to be read this morning, in his sight, on the authority of a warrant he could not now find, and put back.\"\n\nContarini looked down at the book on his knees. Boards and calf. Swollen at the lower edge. One brass corner missing, the bottom left.\n\n\"So it was never taken,\" he said, and looked at Dolfin.\n\nAnd Zuane Dolfin, secretary of the Ten for eleven years, who had never once in Isotta's sight done anything he had not decided to do, crossed the room in three strides and took the book off the old man's knees, which nobody in the room had ever seen anyone do, and opened it at the end. Isotta saw his fingers go into the gutter for the stub. She saw them find, instead, a leaf, a whole leaf, sewn in so neatly that you had to put your nail to the stitches to find them; and on it, in the cramped brown hand of Bortolo Ferro, the word CALCEDONIO.\n\n\"There,\" said Dolfin. His voice had gone high and strange. \"There. You see. You see, Excellency? The leaf. The leaf was in it all the time. She put it back. She had it all along and she's put it back, here, it's the receipt, it's the calcedonio, it's what I promised the Fleming—\"\n\nHe heard it. Isotta saw him hear it, a moment after everyone else in the room.\n\nNobody said anything. Over by the window, Pieter Vos put his face in his hands.\n\n\"Take him,\" said Alvise Contarini to the fanti, quite gently. \"And the Fleming. Impound the gold.\" He held out his hand again, palm up, and Orsato took the book out of Dolfin's hands, not roughly, the way he had taken it from the shelf by San Donato, and put it back into the old man's. \"And give me that. It is, after all, the property of the Republic.\"\n\nWhen they had gone, the two fanti and the Fleming and the secretary between them, down the stair into the noise of the fair, the old man sat for a while with the book closed on his knees and looked at Isotta Ferro, and at the collar at her throat, the vine and the pomegranates and the birds.\n\n\"Very pretty lace,\" he said. \"Your niece's?\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Mm.\" He turned the book over in his hands, the false book, Anselmo's three nights' work, with every weight wrong. \"I'm told your son has the gaol fever.\"\n\n\"Yes, Excellency.\"\n\n\"That's the spring tides.\" The Capo of the Ten stood up, slowly, in stages, with one hand on Matteo Lion's arm, which Matteo gave him without being asked. \"It comes up through the floor.\"","wordCount":1043,"createdAt":1792362134544},{"id":"_UvwWlfw1CJdwyXbJTkE7","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"3Ca9TRPohYxSK0z4dLlo_","text":"It took her four.\n\nNot because the numbers were hard. The numbers were the easy part; she made a key on the first night, by candle, on the back of an old pricking: a twist for an ounce, a crossing for a pound, a pin for an hour, a picot for each heat, and the four-petalled flower Suor Benedetta called the Sant'Andrea for the turn of a line. It was the hiding that was hard. A pattern that was only numbers looked like numbers: it had a stiffness to it, a lack of grace, and anyone who had ever made lace would see at a glance that it had been made by someone who was thinking about something else. So she had to make it beautiful. She had to make the zafre into a vine and the copper into a pomegranate and NOT TO STIR into a little border of knots that ran round the whole thing like a fence round a garden, and she had to make it so that the vine and the pomegranate and the fence were the reason for the collar and the numbers were only how many stitches it happened to need.\n\nOn the fourth morning there was no fog. The sun came in flat and gold over the lagoon and lit up the window like a lamp, and she unpinned the collar from the pillow and laid it on the black cloth and sent the boy from next door to Murano for her aunt.\n\nZia Isotta looked at it a long time without touching it.\n\nIt was a small thing, a woman's collar, no wider than two hands. A vine ran round it, heavy with fruit. In the corners there were flowers. Along the edge there was a border of tiny knots, and here and there among the leaves a bird, because Lucia had needed somewhere to put the second heat and a bird had seemed right.\n\n\"Read it to me,\" her aunt said.\n\nSo Lucia read it. She put her finger on the first leaf of the vine and counted the twists aloud and said \"four pounds of ash of Levant,\" and moved along the vine and said \"six of the sand, washed thrice,\" and went round the collar as if it were a clock, through the copper and the silver and the hours and the heats, through the birds, until she came to the border of knots and said, \"and not to stir.\"\n\nWhen she looked up her aunt was crying. Not loudly. The way a pot weeps on the outside when it is very cold and the room is warm.\n\n\"He would have hated it,\" Isotta said. \"He would have said lace is for nuns.\" She wiped her face with the heel of her hand. \"Give me the leaf.\"\n\nThey burned it in the kitchen fire with Lucia's mother at Mass. Her aunt held it by one corner over the coals and it went brown, then black, then a curl of bright orange ran along the edge and for a moment, in the last of the paper, Lucia saw the numbers stand out white, every one of them, as if the fire were reading them too. Then it was ash, and her aunt crumbled the ash in her fingers and dropped it into the coals and dusted her hands, and that was the end of the last leaf of the Glass Ledger.\n\n\"Now,\" Isotta said. \"Now it's in two places. Your hands and mine. And nobody looks at a girl's collar.\"\n\n\"The Grimani will look at it.\"\n\n\"The Grimani will never see it. Make them another.\" Her aunt folded the collar into a square of linen and put it into Lucia's hands and closed Lucia's fingers over it. \"Keep it in your work-basket, under the bobbins, the way you'd keep anything you hadn't finished. Don't hide it. Hidden things get found.\"\n\n\"Zia.\" Lucia held the little bundle. It weighed nothing. \"What are you going to do? About Nane?\"\n\nHer aunt was quiet for a moment, looking at the fire.\n\n\"I'm going to steal your uncle's book back from the Council of Ten,\" she said, \"on the Feast of the Sensa, while the Doge is marrying the sea. With a notary who drinks and a friar who forges seals. I am going to sell it to a Fleming. And then I am going to buy your cousin out of the Pozzi and take him a very long way away.\"\n\nLucia laughed, because it was so absurd. Then she saw her aunt's face and stopped.\n\n\"Can I come?\" she said.\n\n# Seals\n\n[\n  Place: #Santa Maria dei Servi\n  Characters: @@Matteo Lion @@Fra Anselmo @@Isotta Ferro\n  When: +2 days\n  Items: ^The forged warrant:@Fra Anselmo\n  Facts: ?The Chancery is entered by the Scala dei Censori:@Isotta Ferro ?The Chancery is entered by the Scala dei Censori:@Fra Anselmo ?The Chancery is entered by the Scala dei Censori:@Matteo Lion:before\n]\n\nThe Servite brothers had their house on the far side of Cannaregio, where the city ran out of grandeur and began to smell of the tanneries, and Fra Anselmo had a cell at the end of the dormitory with a window on the orchard and a lock on the door that the Prior did not have the key to. Matteo had never asked how that had been arranged. In eight years he had learned that the friar's arrangements were like his seals: if you looked at them closely enough, you could see they were perfect, and that was how you knew they were false.\n\n\"Sit, sit,\" the friar said. \"On the bed. The stool is for the work.\"\n\nThe work was laid out on a board under the window: a block of pear-wood the size of a walnut, a row of little gravers on a strip of leather, a dish of lampblack, a candle stub, and, pinned flat under a sheet of horn so that nothing could breathe on it, a letter. Matteo knew the seal at the foot of it before he was close enough to see it properly. Everyone in Venice knew it. The lion in a circle, with the book open under his paw, and round the circle in tiny capitals the words that made men lose their sleep: CONSILIVM DECEM.\n\n\"Where did you get that?\"\n\n\"It came to the convent,\" said Fra Anselmo placidly, \"two years ago, to inform the Prior that a brother of ours was to be questioned about a pamphlet. The brother was questioned. The Prior was distressed. The letter was filed. I am the convent's archivist.\" He sat down at the stool and picked up a graver. \"I am very good at filing.\"\n\nIsotta Ferro had come with Matteo across the city in silence, and now she stood in the corner by the door with her arms folded, and looked at the seal, and then at the friar, and Matteo saw her understand for the first time what she was paying for.\n\n\"It is not enough to have the seal,\" Matteo said, for her benefit. \"A seal alone is nothing. Anyone can carry a seal. What opens a door in the Palace is a warrant: a sheet of the Chancery's own paper, in a Chancery hand, under a Chancery seal, naming the bearer and his business. The guards cannot read, most of them. But they know what a warrant looks like, the way a dog knows what its master's boots smell like.\"\n\n\"And the paper?\"\n\n\"I have the paper,\" said Matteo. \"I kept some. When they struck me off.\" He shrugged. \"Notaries are magpies.\"\n\n\"And the hand?\"\n\n\"That is mine,\" said the friar, and smiled with the side of his face that worked. \"I have the hands of forty-one secretaries of the Chancery in my files. Not all are alive. All of them are very easy.\"\n\nHe bent over the pear-wood. The graver made a sound like a mouse in a wall.\n\n\"Tell me the way,\" Isotta said to Matteo. \"From the beginning. As if I were a fool.\"\n\nSo he told her. He had drawn it the night before, on the back of a will, and he unfolded it now on the bed between them: the Palace, the courtyard, the Scala dei Giganti where the Doge was crowned, and beside it, much humbler, the Scala dei Censori that the clerks used, up three turns to the floor of the Secret Chancery. The guard at the stairhead. The long panelled passage. The doors: the Sala della Bussola on the left, where people waited to be accused, and on the right the door to the Chancery itself, and beyond that, through a smaller door that most people did not know was a door, the little room where the iron chests were kept.\n\n\"On the Sensa,\" he said, \"the Doge leaves the Palace at the third hour of the morning by the Porta della Carta, with the Signoria behind him. They walk to the Molo and go aboard the Bucintoro. Everyone who can be seen with them goes with them, and everyone who cannot be seen with them goes to watch. The Chancery is left with one secretary of the lowest grade and the guards. The secretaries take it in turns. This year it is a boy called Sanudo, who is twenty and has a mistress at San Polo and has been heard to complain about the duty.\"\n\n\"How do you know that?\"\n\n\"Because I asked him.\" Matteo smiled. \"In a malvasia, while I lost to him at primero. He is a very lucky boy.\"\n\n\"And the chest? The three keys?\"\n\n\"There,\" said Fra Anselmo without looking up, \"is where the warrant comes in.\"\n\nThey had worked it out between them the week before, he and Matteo, over the friar's terrible wine. A warrant under the Ten's seal, in the hand of a senior secretary, ordering the officer on duty to deliver to the bearer the book of receipts of the late Bortolo Ferro, to be carried to the Capi of the Ten on the Bucintoro for an urgent question of the Art. Sanudo would not have the three keys. But Sanudo would know that the Ferro book was not in the iron chests at all, because it had been brought in a month ago and not yet entered; it would be in the press of new matters, behind the Chancery door, under one lock, with one key, on Sanudo's own belt. Matteo had seen that press a hundred times when he was a notary. He had seen what went in it and how long it stayed.\n\n\"He'll look at the warrant,\" said Matteo, \"and he'll look at me, and he'll think: either this is real, and if I refuse it I will be explaining myself to the Capi tomorrow; or it is false, and the only person who could have forged it is a man I beat at primero, which is not possible. And then he'll open the press.\"\n\n\"And if he doesn't?\"\n\n\"Then we run,\" Matteo said. \"Very fast.\"\n\nIsotta looked at the map for a long time.\n\n\"Who carries it?\" she said. \"The warrant. Who goes in?\"\n\n\"I do.\" He had expected the question. \"They know my face on the Censori, from the old days. It's an advantage. A struck-off notary running errands for a secretary is a thing that happens. A widow from Murano is not.\"\n\n\"And who carries the book out?\"\n\nHe hesitated. That part he had not decided, because the answer he wanted to give was not one he could give her.\n\n\"Someone who could carry a book,\" said Fra Anselmo, into the pause, \"without anyone thinking it was a book.\" He held up the pear-wood to the light from the window. The lion was there now, half-born, his paw on the open page. \"Your niece, Madonna. The lacemaker. She carries a pillow and a basket everywhere, does she not? Every girl from Burano carries a pillow. Nobody in the world has ever looked inside one.\"","wordCount":2000,"createdAt":1792361270438},{"id":"atndYYONj6xLVSrXj0hiR","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"vEWRhlmys299MoaSvZJfB","text":"[\n  Place: #The Fleming's lodging\n  Characters: @@Lucia Zen @@Pieter Vos @@Orsato\n  When: +3 days\n  Facts: ?Vos is working with Dolfin:@Lucia Zen ?Vos is working with Dolfin:reader ?Vos is working with Dolfin:@Pieter Vos:before ?Vos is working with Dolfin:@Orsato:before ?Dolfin promised Vos a copy of the calcedonio receipt:@Lucia Zen ?Dolfin promised Vos a copy of the calcedonio receipt:reader\n]\n\nShe had left her pattern-book under the Fleming's chair.\n\nShe did not know, afterwards, whether she had done it on purpose. She had taken it out of her basket when they sat down, because she always did, because her hands did not know what to do with themselves unless they were holding thread or something that told them about thread, and she had put it under the chair when Vos poured the wine, and she had not picked it up again when her aunt stood. She remembered seeing it there, a little red book on the Turkish carpet, as they went out of the door. She remembered thinking: I'll go back for it. And then thinking: no, don't. And then not thinking about it at all for three days, which was not like her.\n\nOn Sunday her mother went to Mass at San Martino and then to her sister's at Mazzorbo, and Lucia said she had a headache, and when the boat had gone she took the ferry to the Fondamente Nove and walked. She walked the whole way across the city to the Rialto, through Santi Apostoli and the Strada Nova and over the little bridges that went up and down like the stitches in a border, and when she came to the foot of the spice-merchant's stair she stood a long time sneezing in the pepper and telling herself she had come for her book.\n\nThe stair was dark. The shop below was shut for the Sunday. At the top the door of the Fleming's rooms was not closed: it stood open the width of a hand, and through the gap came voices, and Lucia, who had raised her knuckle to knock the way her aunt knocked, held it there in the air and did not.\n\n\"—real gold,\" said a voice she did not know. Then she did know it. She had heard it once, through the water-door of her aunt's house, on the day the men from the Ten came: flat, exact, unhurried. \"His Excellency is particular. It must be real gold and it must be on the table. In bags. Counted. When the Capi come through that door they must see a merchant buying and a widow selling, with the Republic's book between them. Nothing that a clever advocate could call a misunderstanding.\"\n\n\"I'm not a fool, Orsato.\" That was Vos. His good Venetian had gone thick and careful, the way people speak a language when they are angry in it. \"You may tell your master the gold will be there. It is my gold. I should like to know it will be returned.\"\n\n\"It will be impounded. And returned to you, with the thanks of the Signoria, for having assisted the Republic in the matter of a theft of its property. You will sail on the Zeeridder as you planned, a day or two later. Nobody will touch your cargo.\"\n\n\"And the widow?\"\n\n\"The widow will be taken with the book in her hands. What becomes of her is for the Ten.\"\n\nA pause. Lucia heard wine poured, and then not drunk.\n\n\"And the receipt,\" Vos said. \"The last leaf. His Excellency was quite clear with me, Orsato. The book I can buy in any case; the Guild will sell me a copy of it in a year, I know how these things go. What I came to Venice for is the leaf. His Excellency promised me a fair copy of the leaf, in his own hand, when the widow gives it up.\"\n\n\"His Excellency keeps his promises,\" said Orsato.\n\n\"I'm sure he does. I'm sure he keeps them very exactly.\"\n\nLucia's arm was aching from holding her hand in the air. She lowered it, slowly, and as she did so a board shifted under her foot, not loudly, a small dry sound like a knuckle cracking.\n\nThe voices stopped.\n\nShe did not think. Her body did it for her. She went down the stair not like a person but like water, three steps and three steps and the turn, her skirts gathered in both fists so they should not whisper on the treads, and at the bottom the door of the spice-merchant's storeroom was beside her, not the street door, and she went through it into a dark that was all pepper and cinnamon and the sour smell of sacking, and pulled it to behind her, and crouched against a sack of something hard and round, and did not breathe.\n\nFeet on the stair. Slow ones. They came down to the turn, and stopped.\n\nThrough the crack of the storeroom door she could see a slice of the stairwell: grey light, the dusty bottom step, and then a black shoe, and the hem of a black gown, and a hand on the rail with a ring on it, a plain gold ring, the hand of a man who had carried her uncle's book out of her aunt's house wrapped in oilcloth.\n\nOrsato stood at the bottom of the stair for a long time.\n\nThen he sneezed.\n\nIt was such an ordinary sound. He sneezed, and said something under his breath in the dialect of Chioggia that Lucia's father used when he hit his thumb, and wiped his nose, and went out through the street door into the Sunday afternoon, and the door banged behind him in the draught.\n\nShe stayed among the sacks until the bells rang None. When she came out the stair was empty and the Fleming's door, at the top, was shut. She did not go up for her pattern-book. She never saw it again.","wordCount":991,"createdAt":1792361558900},{"id":"b91rZgPefAwL6HMozqnmf","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"IVuihn9m2e4zyRuOA30jT","text":"[\n  Place: #The Ferro furnace\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Fra Anselmo @@Lucia Zen\n  When: +4 days\n  Facts: ?Vos is working with Dolfin:@Isotta Ferro ?Dolfin promised Vos a copy of the calcedonio receipt:@Isotta Ferro ?Only four people knew the day of the theft:@Isotta Ferro\n]\n\nThe friar came on the Monday before the Feast, with Lucia, in the middle of the afternoon, when the light lay in long gold bars across the cold floor of the workshop and the dust stood up in them like smoke.\n\nIsotta was cleaning the crucibles. She had begun it on Saturday because she could not sit still, and it was work that wanted nothing from her head: a scraper, a bucket of water, a brush, and twenty pots crusted with the glass of the last firing, Bortolo's last firing, a green-gold skin of it that came away in flakes. She had done eleven. When she heard the boat she did not get up. When she saw Lucia's face in the water-door, she put down the scraper.\n\n\"Tell me,\" she said.\n\nThey told her. Lucia told the stair and the open door and Orsato's voice and the Fleming asking about the leaf. She told the storeroom and the pepper and the black shoe on the bottom step, and the ring. She told it well, Isotta thought; in order, without decoration, the way Matteo Lion would have liked a witness to tell it. Then the friar told the rest, sitting on the bench by the marver with his hands on his knees: the four people who knew the day; the fig tree; eight years and the rent.\n\nIsotta listened to all of it with her hands folded on her apron. When they had finished she stood up and went to the rack of blowpipes and took down the fourth from the left, the oldest, and stood holding it, as though it were the only thing in the room that weighed what it should.\n\n\"When did you know?\" she said to Lucia.\n\n\"Sunday. A week.\"\n\n\"A week.\"\n\n\"Zia, I was afraid you'd stop. I was afraid you'd go to Dolfin. Fra Anselmo said—\"\n\n\"I know what Fra Anselmo said. Fra Anselmo was right.\" Isotta turned the pipe in her hands. \"I would have stopped. On Sunday I would have gone to Dolfin in my good black with my mother's coral and given him your collar and thanked him for his kindness.\" She looked at the pipe. \"I haven't got my mother's coral. I gave it to the notary.\"\n\n\"The notary,\" said Fra Anselmo, \"is coming tonight.\"\n\n\"Yes. I asked him to come. To go over the way once more, he said.\" She set the pipe back in the rack, carefully, the fourth from the left. \"Brother. Can you copy a book?\"\n\nThe friar raised his eyebrows. The good one and the bad one went up together, and for a moment he looked like a startled owl.\n\n\"That depends very much on the book.\"\n\n\"Boards. Calf. Brass corners, one missing, the bottom left. Swollen at the lower edge from the damp, the way all our books swell. Two hundred and eleven leaves of rag paper, foxed. My husband's hand, which I can give you in forty letters he wrote me when he was in Florence and I was not, and which I have kept, God knows why, because they are mostly about money.\" She looked at him. \"Not all two hundred and eleven. Nobody could do that in three days. The first gathering and the last, so that a man who opened it at the front or at the back would see what he expected to see. The middle you can fill with anything. Old accounts. Prayers. Recipes for jam.\"\n\n\"And the last leaf? The one he's waiting for?\"\n\n\"Put one in,\" said Isotta Ferro. \"Sewn into the stub, where the cut one was. Calcedonio. In my husband's hand. With every weight wrong.\"\n\nLucia made a small sound. The friar did not; he only looked at her for a long time with his one good eye, and then, slowly, with the side of his face that worked, he smiled.\n\n\"Every weight?\"\n\n\"Not so wrong that a glassmaker would laugh. Wrong enough that it would make something the colour of mud and crack in the lehr. I'll write it out for you.\" She almost smiled herself. \"I know exactly how to make calcedonio go wrong. We did it forty times in February.\"","wordCount":741,"createdAt":1792361758522},{"id":"bJU7Q-73dVmqejf06bI4O","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"lIFNCQBy7DVD0TeJ_LzrI","text":"[\n  Place: #Dolfin's garden\n  Characters: @@Matteo Lion @@Zuane Dolfin\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?Matteo informs for Dolfin:@Matteo Lion:before ?Matteo informs for Dolfin:@Zuane Dolfin:before ?Matteo informs for Dolfin:reader ?Isotta means to steal the ledger back:@Zuane Dolfin\n]\n\nHe said yes, of course. He had known he would say yes from the moment she said Dolfin's name, and he had haggled only so that she would believe him: forty ducats now, which she did not have, and a hundred when the book was in her hands, which she would not have either, unless the Fleming paid. She had given him nine ducats and her mother's coral, and he had taken the coral, and felt it in his pocket all the way home like a stone.\n\nHe did not go home.\n\nHe went the long way, through the rain, by the Merceria and the Frezzeria, by the back of San Moisè where the whores sheltered in the porch and called to him by name, along the fondamenta to a door in a wall with a bell-pull of twisted iron. He did not pull it. He knocked, twice and once, and waited, and the door was opened by a servant who did not look at his face.\n\nZuane Dolfin's garden was small and very old, a square of wet gravel with a well-head in the middle and a pergola of vines along one wall that had not yet come into leaf. Dolfin sat under the pergola in a cloak, with a brazier at his feet and a book on his knee, as though rain were a thing that happened to other people.\n\n\"Matteo. You're wet.\"\n\n\"It's raining, Excellency.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" Dolfin closed his book on one finger. \"Sit, if you like. There's a stool.\"\n\nMatteo did not sit. He never sat, here. It was a superstition: as long as he stood, he could tell himself he was only passing through.\n\n\"The widow Ferro,\" he said, \"came to the malvasia this afternoon with Fra Anselmo of the Servi. She wants her husband's book out of the Secret Chancery. She means to take it on the Sensa while the Signoria is on the water, and sell it to a Fleming at the Rialto, and use the money to buy her son out of the Pozzi and carry him to Antwerp.\"\n\nDolfin smiled. It was the first time Matteo had ever seen him surprised, and he did not like it, because the surprise was so pleased.\n\n\"On the Sensa,\" he said. \"Of course. She is a sensible woman. I told her she was a plain woman and I was wrong; she is a sensible one, which is much rarer.\" He looked into the brazier. \"And the leaf?\"\n\n\"She didn't speak of the leaf. Only that you wanted it and she would not give it.\"\n\n\"She has it, then. She would have said there was no leaf, otherwise. People only refuse to give you things they have.\" Dolfin turned his book over in his hands. \"And you, Matteo. What did you tell her?\"\n\n\"That I'd do it. For forty ducats and a hundred after.\"\n\n\"Good. Do it.\" Dolfin said it as lightly as if he were telling a servant to bring the lamps. \"Help her. Find her a way in. Find the stair, find the hour, get Anselmo to cut whatever seals you need, I'm sure he's longing to. Let her have the book in her hand on the day of the Sensa.\" He opened his own book again, and found his place. \"And then let the Fleming have it in his. And then I will come to the Rialto with the Capi of the Ten and find a Murano widow selling the secrets of the Art to a foreigner, which is treason, with the Republic's own property in her hands, which is theft, with a Servite friar's forged seals, which is sacrilege, and a struck-off notary's help, which is merely sad.\" He looked up. \"Except that the notary will have been working for me all along, and will be thanked, and will owe me nothing any longer. Two hundred ducats, isn't it?\"\n\n\"Two hundred and twelve.\"\n\n\"Then nothing. And the widow, to save her neck, will give me the leaf. She'll give it to me on her knees.\" Dolfin sighed, contentedly, and drew his cloak closer. \"Go home, Matteo. You're dripping on my gravel.\"\n\nMatteo went. In the street he put his hand in his pocket and found the coral there, cold and knobbed like a little branch of bone, and he stood under the dripping eaves for a long time holding it, until a woman in the porch of San Moisè called out to ask if he had lost something, and he said no, and went home.","wordCount":792,"createdAt":1792361085534},{"id":"cbmAi0G9N2_v6MMDXwmCH","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"MsLhDkYbkLalyMtBs3Twg","text":"She did not go to her aunt.\n\nShe thought about it the whole way from the Rialto. She thought about it crossing the Strada Nova, and on the bridge at the Misericordia where the boys were fishing for eels with bent pins, and she knew exactly what would happen. Her aunt would listen with her hands folded. Her aunt would say thank you, Lucia. And then her aunt would say: it's over, we stop. And she would go to Dolfin and give him what he wanted, because there would be nothing else left to do, and Nane would come home, and the calcedonio would go into the Guild's chest and Zio Bortolo would be nobody, and her aunt would be nobody, and every time her aunt looked at a piece of glass for the rest of her life she would think: I gave it to them.\n\nOr else she would not stop. She would say: we go on, and we are cleverer. And she would ask Lucia to go into the Palace anyway, knowing.\n\nLucia did not know which was worse. So she went to the Servi.\n\nFra Anselmo was in the orchard behind the dormitory, in his shirt-sleeves, with a hoe, among rows of something green and feathery that turned out to be fennel. He did not seem surprised to see her. He did not seem anything, at first; he looked at her with his one good eye and went on hoeing until she had finished, and then he leaned on the hoe and looked at the fennel for a while.\n\n\"Orsato,\" he said. \"Of all people. He has a sister at the Celestia. He brings her oranges.\" He shook his head. \"Everyone is someone's brother.\"\n\n\"What do we do?\"\n\n\"We?\" He smiled with the working side of his face. \"You've come to me instead of your aunt. I take that as a great compliment, and also as a sign that you're frightened of what she'll do, which is sensible of you. She is a very brave woman. Brave people are the most dangerous ones to tell things to.\"\n\n\"Will you tell her?\"\n\n\"Yes. Not today.\" He set the hoe against the wall. \"Sit down, child. On the well. No, it won't fall in, it's been here since the Lombards.\" She sat. He sat beside her, slowly, the way old men sit, in stages. \"Tell me again who knows the day. The Sensa. Who knows we go on the Sensa?\"\n\nLucia counted on her fingers. \"Zia. You. Me. And the notary. Messer Lion.\"\n\n\"And who knows the girl from Burano will be the one to carry it?\"\n\n\"The same.\"\n\n\"Not Nane? Not your mother?\"\n\n\"Nobody else. Zia made me swear.\"\n\n\"Four, then.\" The friar looked at the orchard wall, where a fig tree had pushed its way out between the bricks and was hanging there in the sun as though it had been crucified. \"And the Fleming knew the day before your aunt told him. And Orsato and the Fleming were talking about the Capi coming in at the door, which means Dolfin knows not only the day but the end of the plan as well, which your aunt has told to nobody except the four of us.\" He was quiet a moment. \"So it is you, or your aunt, or the notary, or me.\"\n\n\"It isn't me.\"\n\n\"No. I don't think it is.\"\n\n\"And it isn't Zia.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nLucia looked at him. He was looking at the fig tree. The sun was on the milky side of his face and it made the bad eye look like a pearl.\n\n\"It might be you,\" she said.\n\n\"It might.\" He nodded, as if she had said something very sensible about fennel. \"You have only my word that it is not. That is the trouble with all of this. In the end, you have only someone's word.\" He stood up, in stages, and picked up the hoe. \"I have known Matteo Lion eight years. I like him very much. He is the best notary I ever met and the worst card player. And in eight years, child, I have never once asked him how a man who loses every Sunday at the Greek's goes on paying his rent.\" He began to hoe again. \"Go home. Say nothing. Make your aunt a collar. I'll come to Murano before the Feast.\"\n\n# The Wells\n\n## The third cell\n[\n  Place: #The Pozzi\n  Characters: @@Matteo Lion @@Nane Ferro\n  When: +4 days\n  Items: ^The lattimo bead:@Matteo Lion\n  Facts: ?Nane has the gaol fever:@Matteo Lion ?Nane has the gaol fever:reader\n]\n\nHe told himself he went down to the wells to count the guards.\n\nThat was true, as far as it went. On the Sensa they would come up the Censori, and a man who came up the Censori could also be made to go down it, and down was the Pozzi; it was worth knowing how many men were on the lower passage on a feast day and whether they could hear the Chancery from where they stood. He had a pass. He had kept it, as he had kept the paper. It said that Matteo Lion, notary of the Avogaria, was to be admitted to the prisons to take depositions in the matter of the Republic's business, and it was four years out of date, and the guard on the stool by the low arch could not read it, and let him through for two soldi.\n\nHe counted the guards. There were three, on the lower passage, and one of them was asleep.\n\nThen he went to the third cell, because he had known all along that he would.\n\nThe boy was lying down. That was the first thing. A boy of seventeen does not lie down in the middle of the day unless he has been told to or cannot help it, and nobody had told him to. He was on the plank with the blanket over him up to the chin, and when Matteo lifted the little lamp he had bought from the guard and held it to the bars, the face that turned towards the light was shining wet, and the eyes were too bright.\n\n\"Who are you?\"\n\n\"A friend of your mother's.\"\n\n\"My mother hasn't got any friends.\" The boy laughed and the laugh went into a cough that went on a long time. \"She's got my father's friends, and they all came to the funeral and looked at the furnace. Are you from the Guild?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"From Dolfin?\"\n\nMatteo did not answer quickly enough.\n\n\"Get out,\" the boy said. Then: \"No. Wait. Wait.\" He pushed himself up on one elbow. In the lamplight Matteo could see the hand, the burnt one, gripping the edge of the blanket, the scar shiny as glass. \"Is she all right? Has he done anything to her?\"\n\n\"She's all right.\"\n\n\"She came once. She said she'd come again.\" The boy lay back. \"That was three weeks ago. It's hard to count. They don't tell you the days down here. They give you bread twice and that's a day, but sometimes they forget and give it once, and then you don't know.\" He was quiet for a moment. \"There's a man in the next cell who's been here since the war of Cyprus. He talks to me through the wall. He says it gets easier. He says after the first year you stop hearing the water.\"\n\n\"Your mother's coming for you,\" Matteo said.\n\nHe had not meant to say it. It was the kind of thing one did not say, in the Pozzi, with three guards on the passage and one of them perhaps not asleep.\n\nThe boy turned his head and looked at him. It was Isotta's look. It was exactly her look, the one she had given him in the back room of the Greek's, adding him up.\n\n\"You're the notary,\" Nane said. \"Fra Anselmo's notary. She told me about you. Not your name. She said there was a man who knew the stairs, who was clever and unlucky.\" He was breathing hard. \"Are you going to get her killed?\"\n\nMatteo opened his mouth. Nothing came out of it.\n\n\"Because if you are,\" said Giovanni Ferro, seventeen, lying on a plank under the Palace of the Doges with the gaol fever in him, \"I'll kill you. When I get out. I'll come and find you and I'll do it with a pipe from the glory-hole, the hot end, and it will take a long time.\" He coughed again. \"That's a promise. My father always kept his promises. Ask anybody.\"\n\nThen, before Matteo could say anything, he fumbled under the blanket and brought out something small and held it through the bars.\n\n\"Here. Give her this. I made it before. Before the ship. I had it in my shoe and they didn't find it.\" His fingers opened. On the palm, among the calluses, lay a bead: a little sphere of white glass the size of a chickpea, milk-white, perfectly round, with a single thread of blue wound once about its middle like a ring on a finger. \"Lattimo. The first one I ever made that she didn't send back. Tell her I'm keeping the blanket dry.\"\n\nMatteo took it. It was warm from the boy's hand. He put it in his pocket, where the coral was, and they lay there together, the two small hard things, the white and the red.\n\n\"I'll tell her,\" he said.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Doge's Palace\n  Characters: @@Matteo Lion @@Zuane Dolfin\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^Dolfin's letter to Vos:@Matteo Lion\n  Facts: ?Dolfin means to send Nane to the galleys regardless:@Matteo Lion ?Dolfin means to send Nane to the galleys regardless:reader ?Dolfin means to send Nane to the galleys regardless:@Zuane Dolfin\n]\n\nHe came up out of the wells into the courtyard and there was Dolfin, at the foot of the Scala d'Oro, in a gown of black damask with a fur at the collar though it was May, talking to two senators as though he had been waiting there all morning for nobody in particular.\n\nHe had not been waiting for nobody. He finished with the senators and turned and came across the flagstones, and Matteo felt the stones under his own feet go soft, the way the floor of the lower passage had been soft.\n\n\"Matteo. You've been visiting.\"\n\n\"I wanted to count the guards, Excellency. For the day.\"\n\n\"Of course you did.\" Dolfin fell in beside him, and they walked, slowly, along the arcade towards the Porta della Carta. Two men in black, talking business. Anyone watching would have thought Matteo had come up in the world. \"And how many are there?\"\n\n\"Three. On the lower passage.\"\n\n\"And how is the boy?\"\n\n\"He has the gaol fever.\"\n\n\"Ah.\" Dolfin nodded, regretfully. \"Yes. It's the spring tides. It comes up through the floor.\" They walked a few paces. \"Well, it hardly matters now, does it?\"\n\nMatteo stopped.\n\n\"Excellency?\"\n\n\"Walk, Matteo. People are looking at us.\" Dolfin took his elbow, quite gently, and walked him on. \"I said it hardly matters. The Capi have agreed, on my word, to come to the Rialto on the evening of the Feast. I have promised them a widow selling the secrets of the Art to a Fleming, and they are very excited, in the way that old men are excited, which is to say they have stopped listening to me and started composing their speeches. When she is taken, the widow will give me the leaf. And when she has given it to me, the Council will consider the matter of her son, who tried to carry the Art abroad, and whose mother has now been taken trying to do the same. And they will send him to the galleys, Matteo, because there will be no other possible thing for them to do. It will not be my decision. I shall argue for clemency. I shall be very moving.\" He smiled. \"I've always been told I'm very moving.\"\n\n\"You told her he'd walk out on the Sensa.\"\n\n\"I told her he would walk out if she brought me the leaf. She has not brought me the leaf. She has chosen to steal from the Republic instead. People make their choices.\" Dolfin patted his arm. \"You look ill. Is it the wells? They take some people that way. One breath and they're ill for a week.\"\n\nThey had come to the Porta della Carta. Beyond it, in the Piazzetta, the sun was blazing on the two columns and on the water, and men were already putting up the stands for the Sensa fair, a forest of poles and canvas and shouting.\n\n\"One thing more,\" Dolfin said, and took a letter from his sleeve. It was folded small, in the Chancery fold, and sealed with plain red wax and no device. \"For our friend at the Rialto. He's anxious, and anxious men talk. Take it to him tonight. He'll know who it's from. Don't let him keep you.\"\n\nMatteo took it. It went into his pocket with the bead and the coral.\n\n\"Excellency,\" he said. \"The boy. Supposing she did bring you the leaf. Supposing she came tomorrow.\"\n\n\"Then she'd be a fool,\" said Dolfin, \"to bring it to me before the Feast instead of after it, wouldn't she, when the price will be so much lower after.\" He looked at Matteo, curiously, the way he had looked at him across the well-head the day he bought his debts. \"Why do you ask?\"\n\n\"I like to know the odds, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Dolfin. \"That's always been your trouble.\" He lifted a hand, and went back across the courtyard to the Scala d'Oro, and up it, into the dark of the Palace, where the gold on the vault caught the light and held it as the rest of the stair went into shadow.\n\nMatteo did not go to the Rialto that night. He went home and sat at his table and took the three things out of his pocket and laid them in a row: the coral, and the bead, and the letter in the Chancery fold. He looked at them until the candle had burned down a finger's width.\n\nThen he took his penknife, and warmed the blade in the flame, and very carefully, the way he had done a hundred times in the old days when he was the best notary in the Republic and had not yet been caught, lifted the seal.","wordCount":2420,"createdAt":1792361634098},{"id":"ch_OeybsHR81-WL10zUYX","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"WSf2mi1nOzUfiITFDgqUs","text":"[\n  Place: #The Fleming's lodging\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Lucia Zen @@Pieter Vos\n  When: +3 days\n  Facts: ?Vos will pay four hundred ducats and passage for the ledger:@Isotta Ferro ?Vos will pay four hundred ducats and passage for the ledger:@Lucia Zen ?Vos will pay four hundred ducats and passage for the ledger:reader ?Isotta means to steal the ledger back:@Pieter Vos\n]\n\nPieter Vos lodged above a spice-merchant's on the Ruga dei Spezieri, so that the stair up to his rooms smelled of pepper and cloves and something sweeter underneath that Lucia could not name, and by the time they reached his door her eyes were watering and she had sneezed twice. Her aunt did not sneeze. Her aunt went up the stair as though it were the stair of her own house, one hand on the rail and the other holding her skirts out of the dust, and knocked on the Fleming's door with the knuckle of her middle finger, twice, the way you knock on a pot to hear if it is cracked.\n\nLucia had never met a Fleming. She had expected something large and pale and bearded, a sort of walking cheese. Vos was small and neat and brown, with a short grey beard cut close and a doublet of black silk better than anything a Venetian merchant would have worn on a weekday, and he bowed over her aunt's hand like a gentleman, and then over Lucia's, and said in very good Venetian, \"The lacemaker. Your cousin spoke of you. He said you were the only one in the family with any sense.\"\n\n\"My cousin is in the Pozzi,\" said Lucia, \"because of you.\"\n\n\"Lucia,\" said her aunt.\n\n\"No, no. She is right.\" Vos straightened. \"He is in the Pozzi because of me, and because of the Ten, and because of the law, and because he is seventeen and in a hurry. I am sorry for all of those except the last, which he will grow out of if they let him.\" He gestured to the room behind him. \"Please. Come in. I have wine from the Rhine which is terrible and wine from Candia which is not, and I am too old a merchant to make you choose.\"\n\nThe room was full of light from two windows on the Grand Canal and full of things from everywhere: bolts of cloth, a Turkish carpet on the table instead of on the floor, a brass astrolabe, a little lute with a broken string, a stack of ledgers bound in vellum, a box of clear glass beakers in straw that Lucia saw her aunt see, and look away from. There was a writing-desk under one window with a letter half-written on it, and a stick of red wax, and a candle, and a seal. Vos moved between them and the desk as he poured, without seeming to, and turned the letter face down.\n\n\"So,\" he said when they were sitting. \"The widow Ferro. I had hoped to meet you in happier days. Your husband's cristallo is on the tables of four princes in the Low Countries. My masters in Antwerp have been trying to copy it for ten years and they make something very fine that looks like the water in a ditch. I take it you have not come to discuss the weather.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then what have you come to discuss?\"\n\nHer aunt drank a little of the Candia wine. Lucia, watching, saw her hands were perfectly steady, and knew that meant she was terrified.\n\n\"My son told you there was a book.\"\n\n\"He did.\"\n\n\"There is. The Ten have it. They took it from my house the day after he was taken.\" Her aunt set down the cup. \"I can get it back.\"\n\nVos did not move, but something in the room did; it was as though the light from the canal had shifted on the ceiling. Lucia had seen men at the lace-market do the same when a nun brought in a piece of real Venetian point and pretended not to know what it was worth.\n\n\"From the Ten,\" he said.\n\n\"From the Ten.\"\n\n\"May I ask how?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nHe laughed, then, delighted, and poured more wine. \"Good. Good, Madonna. Never tell a buyer how you came by the goods; it only makes him think he could have done it himself. And when you have the book?\"\n\n\"Four hundred ducats,\" said her aunt. \"In gold, not paper. Passage to Antwerp for two, on a ship that will sail the day after the Feast and not one day later. And a place for my son in your masters' glasshouse, as a journeyman, not a sweeper. A bench of his own, in three years.\"\n\n\"And for you?\"\n\n\"I'll keep house for him.\"\n\nVos studied her over his cup. \"The book has a missing leaf,\" he said. \"Your son told me that too. He said his father kept the last thing he made out of it, because he did not trust the book to keep it.\"\n\nLucia felt her hands go cold in her lap. She did not look at her aunt. She looked at the box of glass beakers in their straw, and counted the beakers, seven, and counted them again.\n\n\"My son,\" said Isotta Ferro, \"tells everyone everything. It's why he's in the Pozzi. Two hundred and eleven leaves, Messer Vos. Everything the Ferro made for three generations. Cristallo, lattimo, the aventurine, the blue of Saint Mark, the rose that my husband's father made for the Pope. You may have the whole of it, and if there is some leaf that is not in it, it is a leaf that cost my husband four crucibles and his peace of mind and was not worth it.\" She stood up. \"Four hundred, Messer Vos. Or I find another Fleming. There are a great many of you about.\"\n\nHe did not answer at once. Then he stood too, and bowed again, and when he came up he was smiling the way he had smiled at the door.\n\n\"Four hundred,\" he said. \"And passage on the Zeeridder, which sails from Malamocco on the morning after the Sensa, as you wish. I'll have the gold here, in this room, on the evening of the Feast. Bring me the book when the Doge has had his wedding.\" He took her hand again. \"I would like to have known your husband, Madonna.\"\n\n\"No,\" said her aunt. \"You wouldn't.\"","wordCount":1073,"createdAt":1792361423471},{"id":"ciqxjD3Un-I5-hwF2O8wM","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"WfzecJCXcnkhuVLjye4PH","text":"\"A girl,\" Dolfin said. \"How delightful.\"\n\nThe vine on the pergola had put out its first leaves since Matteo's last visit, small and crumpled and very green, like the hands of a newborn. Dolfin was not sitting under it this time; he was walking, slowly, round and round the well-head, with his hands behind his back, and Matteo had to stand in the middle of the gravel and turn to keep facing him, like a horse on a lunge rein.\n\n\"Her niece. Lucia Zen. A lacemaker from Burano. Nineteen.\"\n\n\"Pretty?\"\n\n\"I've never seen her.\"\n\n\"Then you'll have that to look forward to.\" Dolfin completed a circuit. \"And the friar is cutting the seal. Our seal. On the Sensa. Up the Censori.\" He said each thing as though he were laying it on a table, to look at it. \"The boy Sanudo. You've been thorough, Matteo. I'd forgotten how thorough you were. It's a pity about the cards.\"\n\n\"Excellency.\" Matteo had not meant to speak. \"The girl. When you come to the Rialto with the Capi—\"\n\n\"The girl will be with her aunt and her aunt will be selling the Republic's property to a foreigner. What becomes of her depends on what her aunt does next.\" Dolfin stopped walking. \"You're not growing a conscience, I hope. At your age it's like growing teeth; it hurts, and nobody admires the result.\"\n\n\"No, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Good.\" Dolfin resumed his circuit. \"There's one thing more I want. When you have the girl in the Chancery with the book in her pillow, I want you to bring her out by the Censori. Not the Scala d'Oro, not the Giganti, not some other little rat-hole of your own. The Censori, and across the courtyard, and out by the Porta del Frumento to the Molo. I'll have a man in the courtyard to see her go. I don't want her taken. I want her watched, all the way to the Rialto, so that when I come in at the Fleming's door the book is on his table and her hands are still warm from it.\"\n\n\"As you say.\"\n\n\"As I say.\" Dolfin smiled at him across the well-head. \"And Matteo. That debt of yours. Two hundred and twelve, you said? I took the liberty of buying it. From the Greek, and from that other fellow at the Frari. I hold all of it now. It seemed tidier.\"\n\nMatteo stood very still on the gravel. Above him a sparrow came down onto the pergola and looked at the new leaves and decided against them and flew away.\n\n\"That's very kind, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Isn't it?\" said Dolfin. \"I thought so.\"","wordCount":439,"createdAt":1792364154941},{"id":"cuK4m_hSpyY3fvTcs0IOP","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"lIFNCQBy7DVD0TeJ_LzrI","text":"He said yes, of course. He had known he would say yes from the moment she said Dolfin's name, and he had haggled only so that she would believe him: forty ducats now, which she did not have, and a hundred when the book was in her hands, which she would not have either, unless the Fleming paid. She had given him nine ducats and her mother's coral, and he had taken the coral, and felt it in his pocket all the way home like a stone.\n\nHe did not go home.\n\nHe went the long way, through the rain, by the Merceria and the Frezzeria, by the back of San Moisè where the whores sheltered in the porch and called to him by name, along the fondamenta to a door in a wall with a bell-pull of twisted iron. He did not pull it. He knocked, twice and once, and waited, and the door was opened by a servant who did not look at his face.\n\nZuane Dolfin's garden was small and very old, a square of wet gravel with a well-head in the middle and a pergola of vines along one wall that had not yet come into leaf. Dolfin sat under the pergola in a cloak, with a brazier at his feet and a book on his knee, as though rain were a thing that happened to other people.\n\n\"Matteo. You're wet.\"\n\n\"It's raining, Excellency.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" Dolfin closed his book on one finger. \"Sit, if you like. There's a stool.\"\n\nMatteo did not sit. He never sat, here. It was a superstition: as long as he stood, he could tell himself he was only passing through.\n\n\"The widow Ferro,\" he said, \"came to the malvasia this afternoon with Fra Anselmo of the Servi. She wants her husband's book out of the Secret Chancery. She means to take it on the Sensa while the Signoria is on the water, and sell it to a Fleming at the Rialto, and use the money to buy her son out of the Pozzi and carry him to Antwerp.\"\n\nDolfin smiled. It was the first time Matteo had ever seen him surprised, and he did not like it, because the surprise was so pleased.\n\n\"On the Sensa,\" he said. \"Of course. She is a sensible woman. I told her she was a plain woman and I was wrong; she is a sensible one, which is much rarer.\" He looked into the brazier. \"And the leaf?\"\n\n\"She didn't speak of the leaf. Only that you wanted it and she would not give it.\"\n\n\"She has it, then. She would have said there was no leaf, otherwise. People only refuse to give you things they have.\" Dolfin turned his book over in his hands. \"And you, Matteo. What did you tell her?\"\n\n\"That I'd do it. For forty ducats and a hundred after.\"\n\n\"Good. Do it.\" Dolfin said it as lightly as if he were telling a servant to bring the lamps. \"Help her. Find her a way in. Find the stair, find the hour, get Anselmo to cut whatever seals you need, I'm sure he's longing to. Let her have the book in her hand on the day of the Sensa.\" He opened his own book again, and found his place. \"And then let the Fleming have it in his. And then I will come to the Rialto with the Capi of the Ten and find a Murano widow selling the secrets of the Art to a foreigner, which is treason, with the Republic's own property in her hands, which is theft, with a Servite friar's forged seals, which is sacrilege, and a struck-off notary's help, which is merely sad.\" He looked up. \"Except that the notary will have been working for me all along, and will be thanked, and will owe me nothing any longer. Two hundred ducats, isn't it?\"\n\n\"Two hundred and twelve.\"\n\n\"Then nothing. And the widow, to save her neck, will give me the leaf. She'll give it to me on her knees.\" Dolfin sighed, contentedly, and drew his cloak closer. \"Go home, Matteo. You're dripping on my gravel.\"\n\nMatteo went. In the street he put his hand in his pocket and found the coral there, cold and knobbed like a little branch of bone, and he stood under the dripping eaves for a long time holding it, until a woman in the porch of San Moisè called out to ask if he had lost something, and he said no, and went home.\n\n# Punto in Aria\n\n## Lace\n[\n  Place: #The Zen house on Burano\n  Characters: @@Lucia Zen @@Isotta Ferro\n  When: +3 days\n  Items: ^The calcedonio leaf:@Lucia Zen ^The calcedonio lace:@Lucia Zen\n  Facts: ?Isotta has the calcedonio leaf:@Lucia Zen ?Bortolo found the lost calcedonio receipt:@Lucia Zen ?The calcedonio receipt is worked into Lucia's lace:@Lucia Zen ?The calcedonio receipt is worked into Lucia's lace:@Isotta Ferro ?The calcedonio receipt is worked into Lucia's lace:reader\n]\n\nOn Burano the fog came in at night and stayed until noon, and in the mornings the island was a room with no walls. Lucia sat at the window of her mother's house with the pillow on her knees and could not see the canal six feet below her, only hear it, and hear the bells of San Martino ring the hour from somewhere that might have been the next street or the next world.\n\nShe liked the fog. Nobody came to the window in the fog. Nobody could see what she was making.\n\nHer aunt came anyway, at the hour of terce, in Piero's boat. Lucia heard the oars stop and the boat knock against the step, and then her mother's voice in the passage, high and over-polite, the voice she used for priests and for her husband's sister, who had married above them into glass. Then Zia Isotta was in the doorway in her black wool, wet with fog to the shoulders, and her mother was behind her saying she would bring wine, and Zia Isotta was saying no, thank you, Marina, nothing, I've only come to see what the child is making.\n\n\"I'm not a child,\" Lucia said when her mother had gone.\n\n\"No.\" Her aunt shut the door and stood with her back against it. \"I'm sorry. I needed her to go away.\" She looked at the pillow on Lucia's knees, at the bobbins and the pins and the little cloud of thread. \"What are you making?\"\n\n\"A collar. For the Grimani. Punto in aria.\" She turned the pillow so her aunt could see. \"Stitch in the air. There's no cloth under it, you see; it's all thread, all the way through. Suor Benedetta says the first woman who made it was a fisherman's girl whose lover sent her a piece of seaweed from the far side of the world, and she copied it in thread so it wouldn't rot.\"\n\n\"Is that true?\"\n\n\"No. Suor Benedetta tells everybody something different.\" Lucia set down the pillow. \"Zia, what's happened? Is it Nane?\"\n\nHer aunt came and sat on the end of the bed, which nobody but Lucia ever sat on, and took something out of her bodice, and held it out on her open palm.\n\nIt was a roll of paper the length of a finger, browned at one edge.\n\n\"Don't unroll it,\" Isotta said. \"Not yet. Listen first. This is the last leaf of your uncle's book. The one the Ten didn't get. There is a man at the Palace called Dolfin who would let Nane go tomorrow if I gave it to him, and he would give it to the Guild, and the Guild would give it to whoever they liked, and the Ferro would be nobody. And there is a man at the Rialto who would give me a great deal of money for it, and take it to Antwerp, and the Ferro would be nobody there either.\" She turned the little roll over with one finger. \"On this leaf is how to make calcedonio. Glass that looks like stone. Your uncle found it last winter. He found it because I made him try the copper with the silver, which he said was a woman's idea and stupid, and it was not stupid. It was the last thing we made together. And as long as this exists, on paper, anyone who can take it from me has it.\"\n\nLucia looked at the paper. She could see, through the thin outer turn, the ghost of figures written in brown ink, columns, little crosses.\n\n\"Then burn it,\" she said.\n\n\"If I burn it, it's only in my head. And my head is easy to take.\" Her aunt said it without any expression at all. \"If they put me where they've put Nane, Lucia, or worse, I don't know how long I would keep it. I'm not brave. Nobody is, in the end. Your uncle wasn't.\"\n\n\"Zia—\"\n\n\"So I want to put it somewhere nobody would look for a receipt for glass. Somewhere it looks like something else entirely.\" She held out the roll again. \"You count stitches. Every pattern you make is numbers. How many twists, how many crossings, how many pins before the picot. Could you make this into a pattern? Weights and times and heats, as stitches? So that it was there, all of it, if you knew how to read it, and to anyone else it was a collar?\"\n\nLucia took the roll. It was warm from her aunt's skin.\n\nShe unrolled it on the pillow, across the half-made collar, and smoothed it flat with her palm, and read it. She did not understand most of it. Pounds of ash. Ounces of something called zafre. The copper and the silver her aunt had spoken of. Hours at the first heat. A thing her uncle had written in the margin and underlined twice: NOT TO STIR. There were eleven lines, and some had four figures and some had six, and none of the figures was greater than forty.\n\nNothing greater than forty. She could do anything under forty. She had done the Grimani's border in seventy-two repeats.\n\n\"Yes,\" she said. \"Give me three days.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Zen house on Burano\n  Characters: @@Lucia Zen @@Isotta Ferro\n  When: +3 days\n  Items: ^The calcedonio leaf:destroyed ^The calcedonio lace:@Lucia Zen\n  Facts: ?The calcedonio leaf has been burned:@Isotta Ferro ?The calcedonio leaf has been burned:@Lucia Zen\n]\n\nIt took her four.\n\nNot because the numbers were hard. The numbers were the easy part; she made a key on the first night, by candle, on the back of an old pricking: a twist for an ounce, a crossing for a pound, a pin for an hour, a picot for each heat, and the four-petalled flower Suor Benedetta called the Sant'Andrea for the turn of a line. It was the hiding that was hard. A pattern that was only numbers looked like numbers: it had a stiffness to it, a lack of grace, and anyone who had ever made lace would see at a glance that it had been made by someone who was thinking about something else. So she had to make it beautiful. She had to make the zafre into a vine and the copper into a pomegranate and NOT TO STIR into a little border of knots that ran round the whole thing like a fence round a garden, and she had to make it so that the vine and the pomegranate and the fence were the reason for the collar and the numbers were only how many stitches it happened to need.\n\nOn the fourth morning there was no fog. The sun came in flat and gold over the lagoon and lit up the window like a lamp, and she unpinned the collar from the pillow and laid it on the black cloth and sent the boy from next door to Murano for her aunt.\n\nZia Isotta looked at it a long time without touching it.\n\nIt was a small thing, a woman's collar, no wider than two hands. A vine ran round it, heavy with fruit. In the corners there were flowers. Along the edge there was a border of tiny knots, and here and there among the leaves a bird, because Lucia had needed somewhere to put the second heat and a bird had seemed right.\n\n\"Read it to me,\" her aunt said.\n\nSo Lucia read it. She put her finger on the first leaf of the vine and counted the twists aloud and said \"four pounds of ash of Levant,\" and moved along the vine and said \"six of the sand, washed thrice,\" and went round the collar as if it were a clock, through the copper and the silver and the hours and the heats, through the birds, until she came to the border of knots and said, \"and not to stir.\"\n\nWhen she looked up her aunt was crying. Not loudly. The way a pot weeps on the outside when it is very cold and the room is warm.\n\n\"He would have hated it,\" Isotta said. \"He would have said lace is for nuns.\" She wiped her face with the heel of her hand. \"Give me the leaf.\"\n\nThey burned it in the kitchen fire with Lucia's mother at Mass. Her aunt held it by one corner over the coals and it went brown, then black, then a curl of bright orange ran along the edge and for a moment, in the last of the paper, Lucia saw the numbers stand out white, every one of them, as if the fire were reading them too. Then it was ash, and her aunt crumbled the ash in her fingers and dropped it into the coals and dusted her hands, and that was the end of the last leaf of the Glass Ledger.\n\n\"Now,\" Isotta said. \"Now it's in two places. Your hands and mine. And nobody looks at a girl's collar.\"\n\n\"The Grimani will look at it.\"\n\n\"The Grimani will never see it. Make them another.\" Her aunt folded the collar into a square of linen and put it into Lucia's hands and closed Lucia's fingers over it. \"Keep it in your work-basket, under the bobbins, the way you'd keep anything you hadn't finished. Don't hide it. Hidden things get found.\"\n\n\"Zia.\" Lucia held the little bundle. It weighed nothing. \"What are you going to do? About Nane?\"\n\nHer aunt was quiet for a moment, looking at the fire.\n\n\"I'm going to steal your uncle's book back from the Council of Ten,\" she said, \"on the Feast of the Sensa, while the Doge is marrying the sea. With a notary who drinks and a friar who forges seals. I am going to sell it to a Fleming. And then I am going to buy your cousin out of the Pozzi and take him a very long way away.\"\n\nLucia laughed, because it was so absurd. Then she saw her aunt's face and stopped.\n\n\"Can I come?\" she said.","wordCount":2503,"createdAt":1792361178093},{"id":"dwVUKfUyi7Jhyd8yYluW6","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"Hq8_8_xvRODxArlMBYfhb","text":"Outside it was evening, and warm, the first warm evening of the year, with the first mosquitoes coming up off the canals like smoke. The bridge was crowded with people going home and people going out, and the shutters of the shops along the Ruga were going up with a sound like a long roll of drums. Her aunt walked fast, and did not speak, and Lucia had almost to run to keep beside her.\n\nAt the top of the bridge her aunt stopped. Below them the Grand Canal was full of boats and the light was going pink on the water, and a man on a barge was singing something about a girl from Chioggia with a voice so bad it was almost beautiful.\n\n\"Well?\" said her aunt.\n\n\"He knew about the leaf.\"\n\n\"Nane told him.\"\n\n\"Nane told him there was a last thing. He didn't tell him what. Zia, he wasn't surprised. When you said you could get the book out of the Ten's own house, he wasn't surprised. He was pleased, he was greedy, but he wasn't surprised. People are surprised when you say a thing like that.\" Lucia gripped the warm stone of the parapet. \"And he turned the letter over. On the desk. When we came in.\"\n\n\"Merchants turn their letters over. They're all thieves; they think everyone else is.\"\n\n\"And he said the Sensa,\" Lucia said. \"You said the day after the Feast. You didn't say which Feast. There are three Feasts in May. And he said the Zeeridder sails the morning after the Sensa, as you wish.\"\n\nHer aunt was quiet. On the barge the man had come to the part of the song where the girl from Chioggia drowned herself, and was making the most of it.\n\n\"Everybody in Venice knows the Sensa is the only Feast that matters,\" Isotta said at last. \"He's been here a year. He'd have learned that much.\"\n\n\"Zia—\"\n\n\"Lucia.\" Her aunt turned to her, and put both hands on her shoulders, and Lucia saw in the pink light how tired she was, how the skin under her eyes had gone thin and dark like the skin of a bruised plum. \"I don't trust him. I don't trust Matteo Lion and I don't entirely trust Fra Anselmo, God forgive me, and the man I trust least in the world is Zuane Dolfin, who is the only one of them who has never lied to me. I don't have to trust them. I have to use them. That's all.\" She let go. \"And he's not getting the calcedonio. Whatever happens. He's getting two hundred and eleven leaves and a widow who keeps house and has a very bad memory for weights.\"\n\n\"And Nane?\"\n\n\"Nane will have a bench in Antwerp and hate it, and in five years he'll come home and the Ten will have forgotten him, and he'll make calcedonio on Murano, out of your collar, and be famous.\" Her aunt took her arm. \"Come on. Piero won't wait after the Ave Maria and I'm not rowing you back to Burano in the dark.\"\n\nThey went down off the bridge into the crowd. Lucia looked back once, up at the windows above the spice-merchant's, and there was a candle lit in one of them now; and for a moment a man stood in it, small and neat, looking down at the bridge where they had been. Then he drew the curtain.\n\n# What Lucia Heard\n\n## The pattern-book\n[\n  Place: #The Fleming's lodging\n  Characters: @@Lucia Zen @@Pieter Vos @@Orsato\n  When: +3 days\n  Facts: ?Vos is working with Dolfin:@Lucia Zen ?Vos is working with Dolfin:reader ?Vos is working with Dolfin:@Pieter Vos:before ?Vos is working with Dolfin:@Orsato:before ?Dolfin promised Vos a copy of the calcedonio receipt:@Lucia Zen ?Dolfin promised Vos a copy of the calcedonio receipt:reader\n]\n\nShe had left her pattern-book under the Fleming's chair.\n\nShe did not know, afterwards, whether she had done it on purpose. She had taken it out of her basket when they sat down, because she always did, because her hands did not know what to do with themselves unless they were holding thread or something that told them about thread, and she had put it under the chair when Vos poured the wine, and she had not picked it up again when her aunt stood. She remembered seeing it there, a little red book on the Turkish carpet, as they went out of the door. She remembered thinking: I'll go back for it. And then thinking: no, don't. And then not thinking about it at all for three days, which was not like her.\n\nOn Sunday her mother went to Mass at San Martino and then to her sister's at Mazzorbo, and Lucia said she had a headache, and when the boat had gone she took the ferry to the Fondamente Nove and walked. She walked the whole way across the city to the Rialto, through Santi Apostoli and the Strada Nova and over the little bridges that went up and down like the stitches in a border, and when she came to the foot of the spice-merchant's stair she stood a long time sneezing in the pepper and telling herself she had come for her book.\n\nThe stair was dark. The shop below was shut for the Sunday. At the top the door of the Fleming's rooms was not closed: it stood open the width of a hand, and through the gap came voices, and Lucia, who had raised her knuckle to knock the way her aunt knocked, held it there in the air and did not.\n\n\"—real gold,\" said a voice she did not know. Then she did know it. She had heard it once, through the water-door of her aunt's house, on the day the men from the Ten came: flat, exact, unhurried. \"His Excellency is particular. It must be real gold and it must be on the table. In bags. Counted. When the Capi come through that door they must see a merchant buying and a widow selling, with the Republic's book between them. Nothing that a clever advocate could call a misunderstanding.\"\n\n\"I'm not a fool, Orsato.\" That was Vos. His good Venetian had gone thick and careful, the way people speak a language when they are angry in it. \"You may tell your master the gold will be there. It is my gold. I should like to know it will be returned.\"\n\n\"It will be impounded. And returned to you, with the thanks of the Signoria, for having assisted the Republic in the matter of a theft of its property. You will sail on the Zeeridder as you planned, a day or two later. Nobody will touch your cargo.\"\n\n\"And the widow?\"\n\n\"The widow will be taken with the book in her hands. What becomes of her is for the Ten.\"\n\nA pause. Lucia heard wine poured, and then not drunk.\n\n\"And the receipt,\" Vos said. \"The last leaf. His Excellency was quite clear with me, Orsato. The book I can buy in any case; the Guild will sell me a copy of it in a year, I know how these things go. What I came to Venice for is the leaf. His Excellency promised me a fair copy of the leaf, in his own hand, when the widow gives it up.\"\n\n\"His Excellency keeps his promises,\" said Orsato.\n\n\"I'm sure he does. I'm sure he keeps them very exactly.\"\n\nLucia's arm was aching from holding her hand in the air. She lowered it, slowly, and as she did so a board shifted under her foot, not loudly, a small dry sound like a knuckle cracking.\n\nThe voices stopped.\n\nShe did not think. Her body did it for her. She went down the stair not like a person but like water, three steps and three steps and the turn, her skirts gathered in both fists so they should not whisper on the treads, and at the bottom the door of the spice-merchant's storeroom was beside her, not the street door, and she went through it into a dark that was all pepper and cinnamon and the sour smell of sacking, and pulled it to behind her, and crouched against a sack of something hard and round, and did not breathe.\n\nFeet on the stair. Slow ones. They came down to the turn, and stopped.\n\nThrough the crack of the storeroom door she could see a slice of the stairwell: grey light, the dusty bottom step, and then a black shoe, and the hem of a black gown, and a hand on the rail with a ring on it, a plain gold ring, the hand of a man who had carried her uncle's book out of her aunt's house wrapped in oilcloth.\n\nOrsato stood at the bottom of the stair for a long time.\n\nThen he sneezed.\n\nIt was such an ordinary sound. He sneezed, and said something under his breath in the dialect of Chioggia that Lucia's father used when he hit his thumb, and wiped his nose, and went out through the street door into the Sunday afternoon, and the door banged behind him in the draught.\n\nShe stayed among the sacks until the bells rang None. When she came out the stair was empty and the Fleming's door, at the top, was shut. She did not go up for her pattern-book. She never saw it again.\n\n[\n  Place: #Santa Maria dei Servi\n  Characters: @@Lucia Zen @@Fra Anselmo\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?Vos is working with Dolfin:@Fra Anselmo ?Dolfin promised Vos a copy of the calcedonio receipt:@Fra Anselmo ?Only four people knew the day of the theft:@Fra Anselmo ?Only four people knew the day of the theft:@Lucia Zen ?Only four people knew the day of the theft:reader\n]\n\nShe did not go to her aunt.\n\nShe thought about it the whole way from the Rialto. She thought about it crossing the Strada Nova, and on the bridge at the Misericordia where the boys were fishing for eels with bent pins, and she knew exactly what would happen. Her aunt would listen with her hands folded. Her aunt would say thank you, Lucia. And then her aunt would say: it's over, we stop. And she would go to Dolfin and give him what he wanted, because there would be nothing else left to do, and Nane would come home, and the calcedonio would go into the Guild's chest and Zio Bortolo would be nobody, and her aunt would be nobody, and every time her aunt looked at a piece of glass for the rest of her life she would think: I gave it to them.\n\nOr else she would not stop. She would say: we go on, and we are cleverer. And she would ask Lucia to go into the Palace anyway, knowing.\n\nLucia did not know which was worse. So she went to the Servi.\n\nFra Anselmo was in the orchard behind the dormitory, in his shirt-sleeves, with a hoe, among rows of something green and feathery that turned out to be fennel. He did not seem surprised to see her. He did not seem anything, at first; he looked at her with his one good eye and went on hoeing until she had finished, and then he leaned on the hoe and looked at the fennel for a while.\n\n\"Orsato,\" he said. \"Of all people. He has a sister at the Celestia. He brings her oranges.\" He shook his head. \"Everyone is someone's brother.\"\n\n\"What do we do?\"\n\n\"We?\" He smiled with the working side of his face. \"You've come to me instead of your aunt. I take that as a great compliment, and also as a sign that you're frightened of what she'll do, which is sensible of you. She is a very brave woman. Brave people are the most dangerous ones to tell things to.\"\n\n\"Will you tell her?\"\n\n\"Yes. Not today.\" He set the hoe against the wall. \"Sit down, child. On the well. No, it won't fall in, it's been here since the Lombards.\" She sat. He sat beside her, slowly, the way old men sit, in stages. \"Tell me again who knows the day. The Sensa. Who knows we go on the Sensa?\"\n\nLucia counted on her fingers. \"Zia. You. Me. And the notary. Messer Lion.\"\n\n\"And who knows the girl from Burano will be the one to carry it?\"\n\n\"The same.\"\n\n\"Not Nane? Not your mother?\"\n\n\"Nobody else. Zia made me swear.\"\n\n\"Four, then.\" The friar looked at the orchard wall, where a fig tree had pushed its way out between the bricks and was hanging there in the sun as though it had been crucified. \"And the Fleming knew the day before your aunt told him. And Orsato and the Fleming were talking about the Capi coming in at the door, which means Dolfin knows not only the day but the end of the plan as well, which your aunt has told to nobody except the four of us.\" He was quiet a moment. \"So it is you, or your aunt, or the notary, or me.\"\n\n\"It isn't me.\"\n\n\"No. I don't think it is.\"\n\n\"And it isn't Zia.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nLucia looked at him. He was looking at the fig tree. The sun was on the milky side of his face and it made the bad eye look like a pearl.\n\n\"It might be you,\" she said.\n\n\"It might.\" He nodded, as if she had said something very sensible about fennel. \"You have only my word that it is not. That is the trouble with all of this. In the end, you have only someone's word.\" He stood up, in stages, and picked up the hoe. \"I have known Matteo Lion eight years. I like him very much. He is the best notary I ever met and the worst card player. And in eight years, child, I have never once asked him how a man who loses every Sunday at the Greek's goes on paying his rent.\" He began to hoe again. \"Go home. Say nothing. Make your aunt a collar. I'll come to Murano before the Feast.\"","wordCount":2361,"createdAt":1792361557121},{"id":"fyG3YnkxJra3bRIVV7vi2","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"zIrHkBr1jO8kZ1DDBUd9J","text":"Lucia came across from Burano at dusk, rowing herself, which she was not supposed to do.\n\nIsotta heard the oars from the kitchen and knew them, because they were bad. Lucia rowed the way she did everything that was not lace, which was impatiently and with too much shoulder, and the little sandolo slapped the water like a hand slapping a table. By the time Isotta reached the step, her niece was already tying up, crooked, to the ring that had been meant for the sand barges.\n\n\"You'll have your father after you.\"\n\n\"My father is in Chioggia until Sunday, and my mother thinks I am at the Ursulines learning the punto in aria from Suor Benedetta, who is ninety and asleep.\" Lucia came up the step two at a time. She was nineteen and small and her hands were never still; even standing on the step she was turning the end of the painter over and over in her fingers. \"Zia. Have they come?\"\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Don't. Don't do that, you do that to the Guild men. Have the Ten come?\"\n\n\"This morning.\" Isotta stood aside to let her in, as she had stood aside for Orsato. \"They took the book.\"\n\n\"Only the book?\"\n\n\"What else is there?\"\n\nLucia stopped in the passage. The lamp in the kitchen behind Isotta threw both their shadows up the wall, long and wavering, and Isotta saw in her niece's face that she had come to say something and had been saying it to herself all the way across the lagoon and now did not know how to begin.\n\n\"Sit down,\" Isotta said.\n\n\"I won't. Zia, it's Nane.\"\n\nThe kitchen was warm, at least. She had lit the small fire for the soup, and the sirocco, which made every other room in the house sweat, made the kitchen merely close. Lucia would not sit, so Isotta did, on the bench by the table, and folded her hands, and waited.\n\n\"Piero the boatman was at the Riva degli Schiavoni on Tuesday,\" Lucia said. \"With a load for the Arsenal. There was a Flemish ship at the moorings, the Sint Joris, out of Antwerp, loading oil and currants. And the fanti came along the Riva at first light, eight of them, with a secretary, and they went aboard, and they brought off a boy. A tall boy with a burnt hand. Piero says he did not fight. Piero says he said his name when they asked it, very clearly, as though he wanted it written down right.\"\n\nIsotta looked at her hands.\n\nThe left one had the same burn, the old shiny pucker across the base of the thumb where every glassmaker's child learned, once, that a pipe fresh out of the glory-hole is hot along its whole length and not only at the end. Nane had got his at seven. He had not cried. He had looked at it with great interest and then at her, and asked if it would make him a master.\n\n\"He was going to Antwerp,\" she said.\n\n\"Zia, it's the law. A glassmaker who leaves the Republic—\"\n\n\"I know the law.\"\n\n\"—they can send someone after him. They can kill him in Antwerp if they like and nobody will say a word. And if he is caught before he goes—\"\n\n\"I know the law, Lucia. I was married to it.\" She heard her own voice, flat as a marver, and was ashamed of it. Lucia was crying now, quietly, without stopping turning the rope's end in her fingers, which she had carried in from the boat without noticing. \"He never told me. He never said one word.\"\n\n\"He told me,\" Lucia said.\n\nIsotta looked up.\n\n\"Not when. Not the ship. Only that he would not stay and blow beads for the Guild while they kept his father's book in a chest. He said the Ten would take the book the moment Zio Bortolo died, because Zio Bortolo had found something, and the Guild knew he had found something, and the Ten always know what the Guild knows.\" She wiped her face with the back of her wrist. \"He said there was a Fleming who would pay. For the book. Or for him, if he had the book in his head. He said you'd be better off without him to feed.\"\n\n\"When did he say this?\"\n\n\"At the funeral. In the cloister, while the Guild men were talking to you.\"\n\nSo he had known, at the funeral, with his father's body still on the bier, that the Ten would come. He had known about the Fleming. He had known, or guessed, about the last winter's work, and he had said nothing to her and everything to his cousin, because his cousin was nineteen and made lace and could not stop him.\n\nIsotta put her hand flat on her bodice, where the paper was.\n\n\"Is he in the Pozzi?\"\n\n\"Piero thinks so. They took him to the Palace by the Ponte della Paglia, not to the Arsenal.\"\n\n\"Then tomorrow,\" Isotta said, \"I am going to the Palace.\"\n\n\"They won't let you see him.\"\n\n\"Orsato said a mother should ask.\" She stood. The soup had boiled down to a brown sediment at the bottom of the pot and she lifted it off the fire and set it on the stone and stood looking at it. \"Orsato knew this morning. He knew when he took the book. He said the son is not at present in a position to swear. I thought he meant Nane was a child.\"\n\nLucia said nothing.\n\n\"You'll stay tonight. I'll take you back at first light and you can tell your mother Suor Benedetta kept you for vespers.\"\n\n\"Suor Benedetta has not kept anyone for vespers since the war of Cyprus.\"\n\n\"Then tell her something better. You're the one with the clever hands.\" Isotta reached up and took the rope's end gently out of her niece's fingers and laid it on the table. \"And Lucia. Whatever Nane told you, about the Fleming and the book. You will not tell anyone else.\"\n\n\"Who would I tell?\"\n\n\"Anyone who asks,\" Isotta said. \"That is who people tell.\"\n\nOutside, the wind had dropped at last. In the sudden quiet she could hear the water lapping the step, and the dog down the canal had stopped barking, and somewhere over toward the Fondamenta dei Vetrai a furnace that was still lit was being stoked for the night shift: the deep exhalation of the bellows, and a man's voice calling for more wood, and the small sharp ring of glass touching glass.","wordCount":1098,"createdAt":1792360970648},{"id":"gp2Y4mxfGNnxZKyezM9C8","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"gnMam_XH4_nPQ3uIi2eK8","text":"The passage under the Palace smelled of the sea and of what the sea left behind. Orsato carried a lamp and went ahead of her and did not speak, and she was grateful, because she did not think she could have answered. The walls sweated. Somewhere water was running, not loud, a thread of it, and the floor under her shoes was soft with something she did not look at.\n\nThe third cell on the lower passage had a door of black oak with a square cut in it at the height of a man's face, and across the square three iron bars, and behind the bars, when Orsato lifted the lamp, her son.\n\nHe had grown. That was her first stupid thought. He had grown in the eleven days since the funeral; or else she had never looked at him properly, or else the cell made him tall by being so low. His hair was flat with damp. He had a blanket round his shoulders like an old woman at a fire.\n\n\"Mamma.\"\n\n\"Don't,\" she said, because his voice had cracked on it, and if he cried she would, and Orsato was standing four paces off with his lamp and his patience. \"Don't. Are you hurt?\"\n\n\"No.\" He put his fingers through the bars, the burnt ones. She took them. They were cold as the canal. \"They haven't done anything. They ask me things. A man comes with a book and asks me what my father put in the batch for lattimo, and I tell him, because everyone in Murano knows what goes in lattimo, and he writes it down very slowly and goes away.\"\n\n\"Nane, why?\"\n\nHe looked at her, and for a moment he was seven again, holding out his blistered hand and asking if it would make him a master.\n\n\"Because they were going to take the book,\" he said. \"Because the Guild had already been to see you, I saw them, I saw old Briati in the yard with his hat in his hands looking at our furnace like a man looking at a widow. Because I'm not going to blow beads for them for thirty years while some secretary keeps Babbo's book in a chest and sells it a page at a time.\" He lowered his voice. \"There's a Fleming. Vos. He's at the Rialto. He said if I came to Antwerp with what was in my head he'd give me a furnace of my own. A furnace, Mamma. Not a bench in someone else's.\"\n\n\"And what was in your head?\"\n\n\"Everything I'd seen.\" His fingers tightened. \"Not the last thing. Babbo never let me see the last thing. But Vos didn't know that.\"\n\nSo he had meant to cheat the Fleming too. He was his father's son. She closed her eyes for a moment.\n\n\"Who else did you tell?\"\n\n\"Nobody. Lucia. Only Lucia.\" He tried to smile. \"She told me I was an idiot.\"\n\n\"She was right.\"\n\n\"Time,\" Orsato said, not unkindly.\n\nNane held on. \"Mamma, the man with the book said something. He said if you were sensible I'd be home for the Sensa. What does that mean? What does he want from you?\"\n\nShe looked at her son through the bars, at his flat wet hair and the old woman's blanket and the hand that had been burnt into the shape of a master's hand, and she thought of Dolfin saying pride is very expensive in the Pozzi.\n\n\"It means he is a liar,\" she said, \"like all of them. Eat what they give you. Keep the blanket dry. Say your prayers and say them out loud, so the guard hears you and thinks you're a good boy. I'll come back.\"\n\n\"When?\"\n\n\"When I can.\"\n\nOrsato was already lowering the lamp. In the last of its light she saw her son's face go back into the dark of the cell, not all at once but by parts, the eyes last, and then there was only the square in the door and the three bars and the sound of the water running somewhere it should not be.\n\nOn the stair, in the daylight again, she had to stop and put her hand against the wall. Orsato stopped too, a step above, and waited, and looked out over the courtyard where the light had moved on to the next drain.\n\n\"He is right,\" he said, to nobody. \"It is a waste of good hands.\"\n\nThen he took her back to the Ponte della Paglia, and handed her down into Piero's boat, and lifted his black cap to her, once, which she would remember a long time afterwards, when she had reason to wonder what side he was on.\n\n# The Notary of Debts\n\n## The malvasia at San Zulian\n[\n  Place: #The malvasia at San Zulian\n  Characters: @@Matteo Lion @@Fra Anselmo @@Isotta Ferro\n  When: +2 days\n  Facts: ?Dolfin will trade Nane for the leaf:@Matteo Lion ?Isotta means to steal the ledger back:@Matteo Lion ?Isotta means to steal the ledger back:@Fra Anselmo ?Isotta means to steal the ledger back:reader\n]\n\nIt rained on Sunday the way it only rains in Venice in April, as if the sky had been saving it. The water came down straight and grey into the canals and the canals did not seem to notice, being water already, and only the people minded: the people ran under arches with their cloaks over their heads, and the people crowded into the malvasia behind San Zulian until the windows ran on the inside as well as the out.\n\nMatteo Lion had been there since Mass. He had not gone to Mass. He had gone, at the hour of Mass, to the malvasia, where the Greek kept a back room with a table and a lamp and a pack of Neapolitan cards that had been shuffled so often the court cards were faceless, and where Matteo had lost, by the time the bells of San Marco struck the end of the sung Mass, eleven lire and a pair of gloves.\n\nThey were good gloves. He minded the gloves.\n\n\"You are a man of no luck,\" said the Greek, who was also the dealer, and who said it every Sunday with the same warm sorrow, like a priest reading the same Gospel.\n\n\"I am a man of excellent luck,\" Matteo said. \"It is only that it is all bad.\" He pushed his last coins across. \"Deal.\"\n\nHe was forty, and had been for some time. He had a long face that women had once called distinguished and now called tired, and a notary's hands, ink in the creases that no washing reached, and a voice that could read a deposition aloud in the Avogaria so that the accused wept to hear his own crimes in it. He had been struck from the roll of notaries four years ago for selling a copy of a sealed deposition to the family it concerned, who had paid him well and then betrayed him at the first opportunity, as families do. Since then he had lived by writing letters for people who could not write them, and wills for people who could not afford a real notary, and once, memorably, a love sonnet for a gondolier who had then married the wrong girl.\n\nAnd by one other thing, which he did not think about on Sundays.\n\nHe lost the hand. He was reaching for his cloak when the curtain of the back room moved and Fra Anselmo came in out of the rain like a wet black heron, and behind him a woman Matteo did not know.\n\n\"Brother,\" Matteo said. \"I have no money and I have not been to Mass. Whichever of those you've come about, you're too late.\"\n\n\"I have come about neither.\" The friar shook his hood off. He was sixty, or seventy, or a hundred; he had one milky eye and one that missed nothing, and he could cut a seal in pear-wood that the Doge's own chancery could not tell from the brass. Matteo had met him over a disputed will eight years ago and they had been, ever since, the kind of friends who do not ask each other questions. \"This is Madonna Isotta, widow of the late master Bortolo Ferro of Murano. Madonna, this is the man I told you about. He is a gambler and a liar and he was once the best notary in the Republic.\"\n\n\"I still am,\" said Matteo. \"It's only that the Republic hasn't noticed.\" He looked at the woman properly, and stopped talking.\n\nShe was not beautiful. She was something more inconvenient than that: she was paying attention. She stood in the doorway of the Greek's back room in a wet black cloak with her hands folded in front of her, and she looked at him, at the faceless cards, at his ungloved hands, at the coins in front of the Greek, and he saw her add it all up and arrive at a figure.\n\n\"Fra Anselmo says you know the Palace,\" she said.\n\n\"Everybody knows the Palace. It's very large and you can see it from the sea.\"\n\n\"He says you know the stairs. The ones the clerks use. He says you know where the Ten keep what they take.\"\n\nThe Greek, who understood a great deal more Venetian than he admitted, got up and went out through the curtain with the cards in his hand. Matteo heard him tell the boy at the barrels to bring wine to the back room and then to stay away from it. Then it was just the three of them, and the lamp, and the rain.\n\n\"Sit down, Madonna,\" Matteo said. \"Please. And tell me what they took.\"\n\nShe told him. She told it the way he would have liked witnesses to tell things in the old days, in order and without decoration: the men from the Ten, the book, the secretary in the little room, the offer, her son at the grille. She did not say what was on the missing leaf. She said only that there was a leaf, and that Dolfin wanted it, and that she would not give it to him, and that if she did not her son would go to the galleys on the Feast of the Sensa.\n\n\"Dolfin,\" Matteo said.\n\n\"You know him?\"\n\n\"Everyone who has ever been in trouble in this city knows Zuane Dolfin.\" That was true, as far as it went. He drank some of the wine the boy had brought. It was bad. \"So. You won't give him the leaf. Then what do you want from me?\"\n\n\"I want the book back.\"\n\nMatteo put down his cup.\n\n\"It was my husband's,\" she said. \"And his father's. Two hundred and eleven leaves. Everything the Ferro ever learned about glass. If it stays in that chest, they will copy it out for the Guild, and the Guild will give it to whichever house pays the Ten most, and in ten years every bead-maker on the island will be making Ferro cristallo and selling it for half. And my son will have nothing. Not a furnace. Not a name. And Dolfin will still want the leaf.\"\n\n\"And with the book back?\"\n\n\"With the book back, I have something to sell.\" She looked at Fra Anselmo, and then at him. \"There is a Fleming at the Rialto who wanted my son. I think he will want the book more. Enough to carry two people to Antwerp and enough besides to buy the gaoler who holds the key of the third cell.\"\n\nIt was, Matteo thought, the most insane thing anyone had said to him in four years, and he had once been asked to forge a papal dispensation for a marriage between a man and his own aunt.\n\n\"You understand,\" he said, \"that the Secret Chancery is the best-guarded room in the Republic. That there are men in it night and day. That the chests are iron and the keys are held by the Capi of the Ten themselves, three keys to a chest, one each.\"\n\n\"Fra Anselmo says there is one day a year when the men in it are not there.\"\n\nHe looked at the friar. The friar looked at the lamp.\n\n\"The Sensa,\" Matteo said slowly. \"When the Doge goes out on the Bucintoro to marry the sea, the whole Signoria goes with him. The Capi. The secretaries. Every clerk who can get a place on a boat. The Palace is a shell for four hours.\" He heard himself say it and was appalled at how easily it came. \"That is the day they will sentence your son.\"\n\n\"Then we have until that day,\" Isotta Ferro said, \"and not one hour more.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #Dolfin's garden\n  Characters: @@Matteo Lion @@Zuane Dolfin\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?Matteo informs for Dolfin:@Matteo Lion:before ?Matteo informs for Dolfin:@Zuane Dolfin:before ?Matteo informs for Dolfin:reader ?Isotta means to steal the ledger back:@Zuane Dolfin\n]\n\nHe said yes, of course. He had known he would say yes from the moment she said Dolfin's name, and he had haggled only so that she would believe him: forty ducats now, which she did not have, and a hundred when the book was in her hands, which she would not have either, unless the Fleming paid. She had given him nine ducats and her mother's coral, and he had taken the coral, and felt it in his pocket all the way home like a stone.\n\nHe did not go home.\n\nHe went the long way, through the rain, by the Merceria and the Frezzeria, by the back of San Moisè where the whores sheltered in the porch and called to him by name, along the fondamenta to a door in a wall with a bell-pull of twisted iron. He did not pull it. He knocked, twice and once, and waited, and the door was opened by a servant who did not look at his face.\n\nZuane Dolfin's garden was small and very old, a square of wet gravel with a well-head in the middle and a pergola of vines along one wall that had not yet come into leaf. Dolfin sat under the pergola in a cloak, with a brazier at his feet and a book on his knee, as though rain were a thing that happened to other people.\n\n\"Matteo. You're wet.\"\n\n\"It's raining, Excellency.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" Dolfin closed his book on one finger. \"Sit, if you like. There's a stool.\"\n\nMatteo did not sit. He never sat, here. It was a superstition: as long as he stood, he could tell himself he was only passing through.\n\n\"The widow Ferro,\" he said, \"came to the malvasia this afternoon with Fra Anselmo of the Servi. She wants her husband's book out of the Secret Chancery. She means to take it on the Sensa while the Signoria is on the water, and sell it to a Fleming at the Rialto, and use the money to buy her son out of the Pozzi and carry him to Antwerp.\"\n\nDolfin smiled. It was the first time Matteo had ever seen him surprised, and he did not like it, because the surprise was so pleased.\n\n\"On the Sensa,\" he said. \"Of course. She is a sensible woman. I told her she was a plain woman and I was wrong; she is a sensible one, which is much rarer.\" He looked into the brazier. \"And the leaf?\"\n\n\"She didn't speak of the leaf. Only that you wanted it and she would not give it.\"\n\n\"She has it, then. She would have said there was no leaf, otherwise. People only refuse to give you things they have.\" Dolfin turned his book over in his hands. \"And you, Matteo. What did you tell her?\"\n\n\"That I'd do it. For forty ducats and a hundred after.\"\n\n\"Good. Do it.\" Dolfin said it as lightly as if he were telling a servant to bring the lamps. \"Help her. Find her a way in. Find the stair, find the hour, get Anselmo to cut whatever seals you need, I'm sure he's longing to. Let her have the book in her hand on the day of the Sensa.\" He opened his own book again, and found his place. \"And then let the Fleming have it in his. And then I will come to the Rialto with the Capi of the Ten and find a Murano widow selling the secrets of the Art to a foreigner, which is treason, with the Republic's own property in her hands, which is theft, with a Servite friar's forged seals, which is sacrilege, and a struck-off notary's help, which is merely sad.\" He looked up. \"Except that the notary will have been working for me all along, and will be thanked, and will owe me nothing any longer. Two hundred ducats, isn't it?\"\n\n\"Two hundred and twelve.\"\n\n\"Then nothing. And the widow, to save her neck, will give me the leaf. She'll give it to me on her knees.\" Dolfin sighed, contentedly, and drew his cloak closer. \"Go home, Matteo. You're dripping on my gravel.\"\n\nMatteo went. In the street he put his hand in his pocket and found the coral there, cold and knobbed like a little branch of bone, and he stood under the dripping eaves for a long time holding it, until a woman in the porch of San Moisè called out to ask if he had lost something, and he said no, and went home.","wordCount":2917,"createdAt":1792361083979},{"id":"lRhal1tbjKsweuEDJhRm8","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"WfzecJCXcnkhuVLjye4PH","text":"\"A girl,\" Dolfin said. \"How delightful.\"\n\nThe vine on the pergola had put out its first leaves since Matteo's last visit, small and crumpled and very green, like the hands of a newborn. Dolfin was not sitting under it this time; he was walking, slowly, round and round the well-head, with his hands behind his back, and Matteo had to stand in the middle of the gravel and turn to keep facing him, like a horse on a lunge rein.\n\n\"Her niece. Lucia Zen. A lacemaker from Burano. Nineteen.\"\n\n\"Pretty?\"\n\n\"I've never seen her.\"\n\n\"Then you'll have that to look forward to.\" Dolfin completed a circuit. \"And the friar is cutting the seal. Our seal. On the Sensa. Up the Censori.\" He said each thing as though he were laying it on a table, to look at it. \"The boy Sanudo. You've been thorough, Matteo. I'd forgotten how thorough you were. It's a pity about the cards.\"\n\n\"Excellency.\" Matteo had not meant to speak. \"The girl. When you come to the Rialto with the Capi—\"\n\n\"The girl will be with her aunt and her aunt will be selling the Republic's property to a foreigner. What becomes of her depends on what her aunt does next.\" Dolfin stopped walking. \"You're not growing a conscience, I hope. At your age it's like growing teeth; it hurts, and nobody admires the result.\"\n\n\"No, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Good.\" Dolfin resumed his circuit. \"There's one thing more I want. When you have the girl in the Chancery with the book in her pillow, I want you to bring her out by the Censori. Not the Scala d'Oro, not the Giganti, not some other little rat-hole of your own. The Censori, and across the courtyard, and out by the Porta del Frumento to the Molo. I'll have a man in the courtyard to see her go. I don't want her taken. I want her watched, all the way to the Rialto, so that when I come in at the Fleming's door the book is on his table and her hands are still warm from it.\"\n\n\"As you say.\"\n\n\"As I say.\" Dolfin smiled at him across the well-head. \"And Matteo. That debt of yours. Two hundred and twelve, you said? I took the liberty of buying it. From the Greek, and from that other fellow at the Frari. I hold all of it now. It seemed tidier.\"\n\nMatteo stood very still on the gravel. Above him a sparrow came down onto the pergola and looked at the new leaves and decided against them and flew away.\n\n\"That's very kind, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Isn't it?\" said Dolfin. \"I thought so.\"","wordCount":439,"createdAt":1792361421637},{"id":"lZks07Pasn-IpmOgOR7f2","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"Fydy7EaqZZr40hKtbAF0P","text":"He came up out of the wells into the courtyard and there was Dolfin, at the foot of the Scala d'Oro, in a gown of black damask with a fur at the collar though it was May, talking to two senators as though he had been waiting there all morning for nobody in particular.\n\nHe had not been waiting for nobody. He finished with the senators and turned and came across the flagstones, and Matteo felt the stones under his own feet go soft, the way the floor of the lower passage had been soft.\n\n\"Matteo. You've been visiting.\"\n\n\"I wanted to count the guards, Excellency. For the day.\"\n\n\"Of course you did.\" Dolfin fell in beside him, and they walked, slowly, along the arcade towards the Porta della Carta. Two men in black, talking business. Anyone watching would have thought Matteo had come up in the world. \"And how many are there?\"\n\n\"Three. On the lower passage.\"\n\n\"And how is the boy?\"\n\n\"He has the gaol fever.\"\n\n\"Ah.\" Dolfin nodded, regretfully. \"Yes. It's the spring tides. It comes up through the floor.\" They walked a few paces. \"Well, it hardly matters now, does it?\"\n\nMatteo stopped.\n\n\"Excellency?\"\n\n\"Walk, Matteo. People are looking at us.\" Dolfin took his elbow, quite gently, and walked him on. \"I said it hardly matters. The Capi have agreed, on my word, to come to the Rialto on the evening of the Feast. I have promised them a widow selling the secrets of the Art to a Fleming, and they are very excited, in the way that old men are excited, which is to say they have stopped listening to me and started composing their speeches. When she is taken, the widow will give me the leaf. And when she has given it to me, the Council will consider the matter of her son, who tried to carry the Art abroad, and whose mother has now been taken trying to do the same. And they will send him to the galleys, Matteo, because there will be no other possible thing for them to do. It will not be my decision. I shall argue for clemency. I shall be very moving.\" He smiled. \"I've always been told I'm very moving.\"\n\n\"You told her he'd walk out on the Sensa.\"\n\n\"I told her he would walk out if she brought me the leaf. She has not brought me the leaf. She has chosen to steal from the Republic instead. People make their choices.\" Dolfin patted his arm. \"You look ill. Is it the wells? They take some people that way. One breath and they're ill for a week.\"\n\nThey had come to the Porta della Carta. Beyond it, in the Piazzetta, the sun was blazing on the two columns and on the water, and men were already putting up the stands for the Sensa fair, a forest of poles and canvas and shouting.\n\n\"One thing more,\" Dolfin said, and took a letter from his sleeve. It was folded small, in the Chancery fold, and sealed with plain red wax and no device. \"For our friend at the Rialto. He's anxious, and anxious men talk. Take it to him tonight. He'll know who it's from. Don't let him keep you.\"\n\nMatteo took it. It went into his pocket with the bead and the coral.\n\n\"Excellency,\" he said. \"The boy. Supposing she did bring you the leaf. Supposing she came tomorrow.\"\n\n\"Then she'd be a fool,\" said Dolfin, \"to bring it to me before the Feast instead of after it, wouldn't she, when the price will be so much lower after.\" He looked at Matteo, curiously, the way he had looked at him across the well-head the day he bought his debts. \"Why do you ask?\"\n\n\"I like to know the odds, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Dolfin. \"That's always been your trouble.\" He lifted a hand, and went back across the courtyard to the Scala d'Oro, and up it, into the dark of the Palace, where the gold on the vault caught the light and held it as the rest of the stair went into shadow.\n\nMatteo did not go to the Rialto that night. He went home and sat at his table and took the three things out of his pocket and laid them in a row: the coral, and the bead, and the letter in the Chancery fold. He looked at them until the candle had burned down a finger's width.\n\nThen he took his penknife, and warmed the blade in the flame, and very carefully, the way he had done a hundred times in the old days when he was the best notary in the Republic and had not yet been caught, lifted the seal.\n\n# Confession\n\n[\n  Place: #The Ferro furnace\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Fra Anselmo @@Lucia Zen\n  When: +4 days\n  Facts: ?Vos is working with Dolfin:@Isotta Ferro ?Dolfin promised Vos a copy of the calcedonio receipt:@Isotta Ferro ?Only four people knew the day of the theft:@Isotta Ferro\n]\n\nThe friar came on the Monday before the Feast, with Lucia, in the middle of the afternoon, when the light lay in long gold bars across the cold floor of the workshop and the dust stood up in them like smoke.\n\nIsotta was cleaning the crucibles. She had begun it on Saturday because she could not sit still, and it was work that wanted nothing from her head: a scraper, a bucket of water, a brush, and twenty pots crusted with the glass of the last firing, Bortolo's last firing, a green-gold skin of it that came away in flakes. She had done eleven. When she heard the boat she did not get up. When she saw Lucia's face in the water-door, she put down the scraper.\n\n\"Tell me,\" she said.\n\nThey told her. Lucia told the stair and the open door and Orsato's voice and the Fleming asking about the leaf. She told the storeroom and the pepper and the black shoe on the bottom step, and the ring. She told it well, Isotta thought; in order, without decoration, the way Matteo Lion would have liked a witness to tell it. Then the friar told the rest, sitting on the bench by the marver with his hands on his knees: the four people who knew the day; the fig tree; eight years and the rent.\n\nIsotta listened to all of it with her hands folded on her apron. When they had finished she stood up and went to the rack of blowpipes and took down the fourth from the left, the oldest, and stood holding it, as though it were the only thing in the room that weighed what it should.\n\n\"When did you know?\" she said to Lucia.\n\n\"Sunday. A week.\"\n\n\"A week.\"\n\n\"Zia, I was afraid you'd stop. I was afraid you'd go to Dolfin. Fra Anselmo said—\"\n\n\"I know what Fra Anselmo said. Fra Anselmo was right.\" Isotta turned the pipe in her hands. \"I would have stopped. On Sunday I would have gone to Dolfin in my good black with my mother's coral and given him your collar and thanked him for his kindness.\" She looked at the pipe. \"I haven't got my mother's coral. I gave it to the notary.\"\n\n\"The notary,\" said Fra Anselmo, \"is coming tonight.\"\n\n\"Yes. I asked him to come. To go over the way once more, he said.\" She set the pipe back in the rack, carefully, the fourth from the left. \"Brother. Can you copy a book?\"\n\nThe friar raised his eyebrows. The good one and the bad one went up together, and for a moment he looked like a startled owl.\n\n\"That depends very much on the book.\"\n\n\"Boards. Calf. Brass corners, one missing, the bottom left. Swollen at the lower edge from the damp, the way all our books swell. Two hundred and eleven leaves of rag paper, foxed. My husband's hand, which I can give you in forty letters he wrote me when he was in Florence and I was not, and which I have kept, God knows why, because they are mostly about money.\" She looked at him. \"Not all two hundred and eleven. Nobody could do that in three days. The first gathering and the last, so that a man who opened it at the front or at the back would see what he expected to see. The middle you can fill with anything. Old accounts. Prayers. Recipes for jam.\"\n\n\"And the last leaf? The one he's waiting for?\"\n\n\"Put one in,\" said Isotta Ferro. \"Sewn into the stub, where the cut one was. Calcedonio. In my husband's hand. With every weight wrong.\"\n\nLucia made a small sound. The friar did not; he only looked at her for a long time with his one good eye, and then, slowly, with the side of his face that worked, he smiled.\n\n\"Every weight?\"\n\n\"Not so wrong that a glassmaker would laugh. Wrong enough that it would make something the colour of mud and crack in the lehr. I'll write it out for you.\" She almost smiled herself. \"I know exactly how to make calcedonio go wrong. We did it forty times in February.\"","wordCount":1531,"createdAt":1792361756080},{"id":"m0k7fXwAzmvEaNkMaI0Hx","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"0YoSvhnQYY2tWtmUCGQdm","text":"\n\nThe sirocco had been blowing for three days, and everything in the house on the Rio dei Vetrai was damp to the touch: the bread, the linen, the ash in the furnace mouth, which no longer glowed. Isotta Ferro had let the fire die on the ninth day after the funeral because there was no one left to tend it but her, and a widow who keeps a furnace lit is a widow who means to work it, and the Guild had already sent a man to remind her what that would cost.\n\nShe was sweeping the cold floor when the boat came.\n\nShe heard it before she saw it: oars, and no singing. Murano boats sang. The ferrymen sang, the sand-barges sang, even the priest's boy sang when he rowed the Host across to the sick. A boat that came up the canal in silence was a boat from the Piazza, and she set the broom against the marver and wiped her hands on her apron and went to the water door to meet it, because there was no point in being found.\n\nThere were four of them. Three wore the grey of the Ten's fanti, swords on short belts. The fourth wore black, and a cap of black felt, and he climbed out onto the step with the care of a man who did not expect to be helped.\n\n\"Madonna Ferro.\"\n\n\"Messer Orsato.\" She knew him. Everyone on the island knew him. He had come for the Barovier boy's tools the winter before, and for old Seguso's moulds, and he would come, one day, for hers. \"You are early for a condolence.\"\n\n\"I am not here for a condolence.\" He did not smile; it was not a face that had been built for it. \"The Most Excellent Council of Ten instructs me to take into its keeping the book of receipts of the late master Bortolo Ferro, glassmaker of this island, which by the statutes of the Art is the property of the Republic upon the death of a master without a sworn heir.\"\n\n\"He has a sworn heir.\"\n\n\"He had a son who did not swear.\" Orsato looked past her into the workshop, at the cold glory-hole and the racked blowpipes and the shelf of clay crucibles cracked from the last firing. \"And the son is not, at present, in a position to swear.\"\n\nShe did not understand that, and she did not let him see that she did not understand it. She stood aside.\n\nThey were not rough. She had expected them to be rough, and it was worse that they were not. One of the fanti lifted the cloth from the cooling-oven as though there might be a child sleeping under it. Another opened the chest of oxides and closed it again without looking, because Orsato had not told him to look. Orsato himself went straight to the shelf above the bench, where the book had sat for twenty-two years between the jar of manganese and the image of San Donato, and took it down with both hands.\n\nIt was a thick book, bound in boards and calf, swollen with damp at the lower corners. Bortolo's father had begun it and Bortolo had filled it, and it held, in a cramped brown hand and a cipher of his own that was no cipher at all to anyone who had stood beside him at the pot, everything the Ferro family knew about making glass clear. The weights of the ash. The washing of the sand from the Ticino. How long to hold the batch at the first heat and how long at the second. The cristallo that had made the house rich and the lattimo, milk-white, that had made it famous. Two hundred and eleven leaves.\n\nOrsato turned them with his thumb, quickly, the way a man counts coin he has already been told is correct. He stopped near the end. He turned back. He looked at the stub of paper sewn into the gathering where the last leaf should have been, a sliver no wider than a fingernail, cut close with a sharp knife.\n\n\"There is a leaf missing.\"\n\n\"There are several.\" Isotta kept her voice tired. That was not difficult. \"He tore them out when a receipt failed. He would not keep a failure in the book. He said it would teach the book bad habits.\"\n\n\"This one was cut.\"\n\n\"Then he cut it.\"\n\nOrsato looked at her for a long moment. Behind him the sirocco shoved at the shutters, and somewhere down the canal a dog was barking at the wind, the high complaining bark of a dog that has been barking for three days.\n\n\"The Council will want to know what was on it,\" he said.\n\n\"The Council may ask him. He is in San Pietro Martire, under the third slab on the left. He was always a good listener in church.\"\n\nOne of the fanti laughed and turned it into a cough. Orsato did not laugh. He wrapped the book in an oilcloth he had brought for the purpose, which told her he had known it would be raining, or sweating, or whatever it was the air did in this wind, and he tucked it under his arm.\n\n\"You will be told,\" he said, \"when the Council wishes to speak to you.\" And then, because he was not a cruel man, only an exact one, he added: \"You should go to the Palace in any case. Before they send for you. Ask at the door of the Pozzi. It is better if a mother asks.\"\n\nHe was in the boat before she had found her voice, and by the time she reached the step the oars were moving, and the boat was going away down the Rio dei Vetrai in silence, past the Seguso works and the Barovier works and the little bridge where the children sat to watch the furnace-glow at night, though there was no glow now, there had been no glow for nine days.\n\nThe Pozzi. The wells. The cells under the Palace where the water came up through the floor at high tide.\n\nShe went back inside and stood in the cold workshop with her hands in her apron. She did not sit down; if she sat down she did not think she would get up. After a while she crossed to the rack of blowpipes and took down the fourth from the left, the oldest, the one with the brass ferrule Bortolo had never let anyone else use, and unscrewed the mouthpiece, and tipped it.\n\nA roll of paper slid out into her palm, tight as a spill for lighting candles, singed brown at one edge where the heat had come too close. She did not unroll it. She knew what was on it. She had watched him write it, and she had corrected the weights while he wrote, and he had let her, which was the nearest thing to an apology for twenty years that she had ever had from him.\n\nCalcedonio. The stone-glass. The streaked and clouded glass that looked like agate, like chalcedony, like something dug out of a mountain and not breathed into shape on a pipe. The Venetians had made it once, a hundred years ago, and lost it. Bortolo had found it again in the last winter of his life. Nobody else knew. Nobody but her.\n\nShe screwed the mouthpiece back on and put the roll inside her bodice, against her skin, where it would be warm, and went to find her cloak.","wordCount":1263,"createdAt":1792360668751},{"id":"mQa3IQCxUVu1RRwaoyd79","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"OBzJf-CqpTS80cJKVT4cR","text":"[\n  Place: #Santa Maria dei Servi\n  Characters: @@Matteo Lion @@Fra Anselmo @@Isotta Ferro\n  When: +2 days\n  Items: ^The forged warrant:@Fra Anselmo\n  Facts: ?The Chancery is entered by the Scala dei Censori:@Isotta Ferro ?The Chancery is entered by the Scala dei Censori:@Fra Anselmo ?The Chancery is entered by the Scala dei Censori:@Matteo Lion:before\n]\n\nThe Servite brothers had their house on the far side of Cannaregio, where the city ran out of grandeur and began to smell of the tanneries, and Fra Anselmo had a cell at the end of the dormitory with a window on the orchard and a lock on the door that the Prior did not have the key to. Matteo had never asked how that had been arranged. In eight years he had learned that the friar's arrangements were like his seals: if you looked at them closely enough, you could see they were perfect, and that was how you knew they were false.\n\n\"Sit, sit,\" the friar said. \"On the bed. The stool is for the work.\"\n\nThe work was laid out on a board under the window: a block of pear-wood the size of a walnut, a row of little gravers on a strip of leather, a dish of lampblack, a candle stub, and, pinned flat under a sheet of horn so that nothing could breathe on it, a letter. Matteo knew the seal at the foot of it before he was close enough to see it properly. Everyone in Venice knew it. The lion in a circle, with the book open under his paw, and round the circle in tiny capitals the words that made men lose their sleep: CONSILIVM DECEM.\n\n\"Where did you get that?\"\n\n\"It came to the convent,\" said Fra Anselmo placidly, \"two years ago, to inform the Prior that a brother of ours was to be questioned about a pamphlet. The brother was questioned. The Prior was distressed. The letter was filed. I am the convent's archivist.\" He sat down at the stool and picked up a graver. \"I am very good at filing.\"\n\nIsotta Ferro had come with Matteo across the city in silence, and now she stood in the corner by the door with her arms folded, and looked at the seal, and then at the friar, and Matteo saw her understand for the first time what she was paying for.\n\n\"It is not enough to have the seal,\" Matteo said, for her benefit. \"A seal alone is nothing. Anyone can carry a seal. What opens a door in the Palace is a warrant: a sheet of the Chancery's own paper, in a Chancery hand, under a Chancery seal, naming the bearer and his business. The guards cannot read, most of them. But they know what a warrant looks like, the way a dog knows what its master's boots smell like.\"\n\n\"And the paper?\"\n\n\"I have the paper,\" said Matteo. \"I kept some. When they struck me off.\" He shrugged. \"Notaries are magpies.\"\n\n\"And the hand?\"\n\n\"That is mine,\" said the friar, and smiled with the side of his face that worked. \"I have the hands of forty-one secretaries of the Chancery in my files. Not all are alive. All of them are very easy.\"\n\nHe bent over the pear-wood. The graver made a sound like a mouse in a wall.\n\n\"Tell me the way,\" Isotta said to Matteo. \"From the beginning. As if I were a fool.\"\n\nSo he told her. He had drawn it the night before, on the back of a will, and he unfolded it now on the bed between them: the Palace, the courtyard, the Scala dei Giganti where the Doge was crowned, and beside it, much humbler, the Scala dei Censori that the clerks used, up three turns to the floor of the Secret Chancery. The guard at the stairhead. The long panelled passage. The doors: the Sala della Bussola on the left, where people waited to be accused, and on the right the door to the Chancery itself, and beyond that, through a smaller door that most people did not know was a door, the little room where the iron chests were kept.\n\n\"On the Sensa,\" he said, \"the Doge leaves the Palace at the third hour of the morning by the Porta della Carta, with the Signoria behind him. They walk to the Molo and go aboard the Bucintoro. Everyone who can be seen with them goes with them, and everyone who cannot be seen with them goes to watch. The Chancery is left with one secretary of the lowest grade and the guards. The secretaries take it in turns. This year it is a boy called Sanudo, who is twenty and has a mistress at San Polo and has been heard to complain about the duty.\"\n\n\"How do you know that?\"\n\n\"Because I asked him.\" Matteo smiled. \"In a malvasia, while I lost to him at primero. He is a very lucky boy.\"\n\n\"And the chest? The three keys?\"\n\n\"There,\" said Fra Anselmo without looking up, \"is where the warrant comes in.\"\n\nThey had worked it out between them the week before, he and Matteo, over the friar's terrible wine. A warrant under the Ten's seal, in the hand of a senior secretary, ordering the officer on duty to deliver to the bearer the book of receipts of the late Bortolo Ferro, to be carried to the Capi of the Ten on the Bucintoro for an urgent question of the Art. Sanudo would not have the three keys. But Sanudo would know that the Ferro book was not in the iron chests at all, because it had been brought in a month ago and not yet entered; it would be in the press of new matters, behind the Chancery door, under one lock, with one key, on Sanudo's own belt. Matteo had seen that press a hundred times when he was a notary. He had seen what went in it and how long it stayed.\n\n\"He'll look at the warrant,\" said Matteo, \"and he'll look at me, and he'll think: either this is real, and if I refuse it I will be explaining myself to the Capi tomorrow; or it is false, and the only person who could have forged it is a man I beat at primero, which is not possible. And then he'll open the press.\"\n\n\"And if he doesn't?\"\n\n\"Then we run,\" Matteo said. \"Very fast.\"\n\nIsotta looked at the map for a long time.\n\n\"Who carries it?\" she said. \"The warrant. Who goes in?\"\n\n\"I do.\" He had expected the question. \"They know my face on the Censori, from the old days. It's an advantage. A struck-off notary running errands for a secretary is a thing that happens. A widow from Murano is not.\"\n\n\"And who carries the book out?\"\n\nHe hesitated. That part he had not decided, because the answer he wanted to give was not one he could give her.\n\n\"Someone who could carry a book,\" said Fra Anselmo, into the pause, \"without anyone thinking it was a book.\" He held up the pear-wood to the light from the window. The lion was there now, half-born, his paw on the open page. \"Your niece, Madonna. The lacemaker. She carries a pillow and a basket everywhere, does she not? Every girl from Burano carries a pillow. Nobody in the world has ever looked inside one.\"","wordCount":1231,"createdAt":1792361271959},{"id":"mSbLThT8qXgxKlWG7-eP-","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"WfzecJCXcnkhuVLjye4PH","text":"\"A girl,\" Dolfin said. \"How delightful.\"\n\nThe vine on the pergola had put out its first leaves since Matteo's last visit, small and crumpled and very green, like the hands of a newborn. Dolfin was not sitting under it this time; he was walking, slowly, round and round the well-head, with his hands behind his back, and Matteo had to stand in the middle of the gravel and turn to keep facing him, like a horse on a lunge rein.\n\n\"Her niece. Lucia Zen. A lacemaker from Burano. Nineteen.\"\n\n\"Pretty?\"\n\n\"I've never seen her.\"\n\n\"Then you'll have that to look forward to.\" Dolfin completed a circuit. \"And the friar is cutting the seal. Our seal. On the Sensa. Up the Censori.\" He said each thing as though he were laying it on a table, to look at it. \"The boy Sanudo. You've been thorough, Matteo. I'd forgotten how thorough you were. It's a pity about the cards.\"\n\n\"Excellency.\" Matteo had not meant to speak. \"The girl. When you come to the Rialto with the Capi—\"\n\n\"The girl will be with her aunt and her aunt will be selling the Republic's property to a foreigner. What becomes of her depends on what her aunt does next.\" Dolfin stopped walking. \"You're not growing a conscience, I hope. At your age it's like growing teeth; it hurts, and nobody admires the result.\"\n\n\"No, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Good.\" Dolfin resumed his circuit. \"There's one thing more I want. When you have the girl in the Chancery with the book in her pillow, I want you to bring her out by the Censori. Not the Scala d'Oro, not the Giganti, not some other little rat-hole of your own. The Censori, and across the courtyard, and out by the Porta del Frumento to the Molo. I'll have a man in the courtyard to see her go. I don't want her taken. I want her watched, all the way to the Rialto, so that when I come in at the Fleming's door the book is on his table and her hands are still warm from it.\"\n\n\"As you say.\"\n\n\"As I say.\" Dolfin smiled at him across the well-head. \"And Matteo. That debt of yours. Two hundred and twelve, you said? I took the liberty of buying it. From the Greek, and from that other fellow at the Frari. I hold all of it now. It seemed tidier.\"\n\nMatteo stood very still on the gravel. Above him a sparrow came down onto the pergola and looked at the new leaves and decided against them and flew away.\n\n\"That's very kind, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Isn't it?\" said Dolfin. \"I thought so.\"\n\n# The Fleming\n\n[\n  Place: #The Fleming's lodging\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Lucia Zen @@Pieter Vos\n  When: +3 days\n  Facts: ?Vos will pay four hundred ducats and passage for the ledger:@Isotta Ferro ?Vos will pay four hundred ducats and passage for the ledger:@Lucia Zen ?Vos will pay four hundred ducats and passage for the ledger:reader ?Isotta means to steal the ledger back:@Pieter Vos\n]\n\nPieter Vos lodged above a spice-merchant's on the Ruga dei Spezieri, so that the stair up to his rooms smelled of pepper and cloves and something sweeter underneath that Lucia could not name, and by the time they reached his door her eyes were watering and she had sneezed twice. Her aunt did not sneeze. Her aunt went up the stair as though it were the stair of her own house, one hand on the rail and the other holding her skirts out of the dust, and knocked on the Fleming's door with the knuckle of her middle finger, twice, the way you knock on a pot to hear if it is cracked.\n\nLucia had never met a Fleming. She had expected something large and pale and bearded, a sort of walking cheese. Vos was small and neat and brown, with a short grey beard cut close and a doublet of black silk better than anything a Venetian merchant would have worn on a weekday, and he bowed over her aunt's hand like a gentleman, and then over Lucia's, and said in very good Venetian, \"The lacemaker. Your cousin spoke of you. He said you were the only one in the family with any sense.\"\n\n\"My cousin is in the Pozzi,\" said Lucia, \"because of you.\"\n\n\"Lucia,\" said her aunt.\n\n\"No, no. She is right.\" Vos straightened. \"He is in the Pozzi because of me, and because of the Ten, and because of the law, and because he is seventeen and in a hurry. I am sorry for all of those except the last, which he will grow out of if they let him.\" He gestured to the room behind him. \"Please. Come in. I have wine from the Rhine which is terrible and wine from Candia which is not, and I am too old a merchant to make you choose.\"\n\nThe room was full of light from two windows on the Grand Canal and full of things from everywhere: bolts of cloth, a Turkish carpet on the table instead of on the floor, a brass astrolabe, a little lute with a broken string, a stack of ledgers bound in vellum, a box of clear glass beakers in straw that Lucia saw her aunt see, and look away from. There was a writing-desk under one window with a letter half-written on it, and a stick of red wax, and a candle, and a seal. Vos moved between them and the desk as he poured, without seeming to, and turned the letter face down.\n\n\"So,\" he said when they were sitting. \"The widow Ferro. I had hoped to meet you in happier days. Your husband's cristallo is on the tables of four princes in the Low Countries. My masters in Antwerp have been trying to copy it for ten years and they make something very fine that looks like the water in a ditch. I take it you have not come to discuss the weather.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then what have you come to discuss?\"\n\nHer aunt drank a little of the Candia wine. Lucia, watching, saw her hands were perfectly steady, and knew that meant she was terrified.\n\n\"My son told you there was a book.\"\n\n\"He did.\"\n\n\"There is. The Ten have it. They took it from my house the day after he was taken.\" Her aunt set down the cup. \"I can get it back.\"\n\nVos did not move, but something in the room did; it was as though the light from the canal had shifted on the ceiling. Lucia had seen men at the lace-market do the same when a nun brought in a piece of real Venetian point and pretended not to know what it was worth.\n\n\"From the Ten,\" he said.\n\n\"From the Ten.\"\n\n\"May I ask how?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nHe laughed, then, delighted, and poured more wine. \"Good. Good, Madonna. Never tell a buyer how you came by the goods; it only makes him think he could have done it himself. And when you have the book?\"\n\n\"Four hundred ducats,\" said her aunt. \"In gold, not paper. Passage to Antwerp for two, on a ship that will sail the day after the Feast and not one day later. And a place for my son in your masters' glasshouse, as a journeyman, not a sweeper. A bench of his own, in three years.\"\n\n\"And for you?\"\n\n\"I'll keep house for him.\"\n\nVos studied her over his cup. \"The book has a missing leaf,\" he said. \"Your son told me that too. He said his father kept the last thing he made out of it, because he did not trust the book to keep it.\"\n\nLucia felt her hands go cold in her lap. She did not look at her aunt. She looked at the box of glass beakers in their straw, and counted the beakers, seven, and counted them again.\n\n\"My son,\" said Isotta Ferro, \"tells everyone everything. It's why he's in the Pozzi. Two hundred and eleven leaves, Messer Vos. Everything the Ferro made for three generations. Cristallo, lattimo, the aventurine, the blue of Saint Mark, the rose that my husband's father made for the Pope. You may have the whole of it, and if there is some leaf that is not in it, it is a leaf that cost my husband four crucibles and his peace of mind and was not worth it.\" She stood up. \"Four hundred, Messer Vos. Or I find another Fleming. There are a great many of you about.\"\n\nHe did not answer at once. Then he stood too, and bowed again, and when he came up he was smiling the way he had smiled at the door.\n\n\"Four hundred,\" he said. \"And passage on the Zeeridder, which sails from Malamocco on the morning after the Sensa, as you wish. I'll have the gold here, in this room, on the evening of the Feast. Bring me the book when the Doge has had his wedding.\" He took her hand again. \"I would like to have known your husband, Madonna.\"\n\n\"No,\" said her aunt. \"You wouldn't.\"","wordCount":1515,"createdAt":1792361422079},{"id":"mV1iL5wnAr-cRfTtrrzv8","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"MsLhDkYbkLalyMtBs3Twg","text":"[\n  Place: #Santa Maria dei Servi\n  Characters: @@Lucia Zen @@Fra Anselmo\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?Vos is working with Dolfin:@Fra Anselmo ?Dolfin promised Vos a copy of the calcedonio receipt:@Fra Anselmo ?Only four people knew the day of the theft:@Fra Anselmo ?Only four people knew the day of the theft:@Lucia Zen ?Only four people knew the day of the theft:reader\n]\n\nShe did not go to her aunt.\n\nShe thought about it the whole way from the Rialto. She thought about it crossing the Strada Nova, and on the bridge at the Misericordia where the boys were fishing for eels with bent pins, and she knew exactly what would happen. Her aunt would listen with her hands folded. Her aunt would say thank you, Lucia. And then her aunt would say: it's over, we stop. And she would go to Dolfin and give him what he wanted, because there would be nothing else left to do, and Nane would come home, and the calcedonio would go into the Guild's chest and Zio Bortolo would be nobody, and her aunt would be nobody, and every time her aunt looked at a piece of glass for the rest of her life she would think: I gave it to them.\n\nOr else she would not stop. She would say: we go on, and we are cleverer. And she would ask Lucia to go into the Palace anyway, knowing.\n\nLucia did not know which was worse. So she went to the Servi.\n\nFra Anselmo was in the orchard behind the dormitory, in his shirt-sleeves, with a hoe, among rows of something green and feathery that turned out to be fennel. He did not seem surprised to see her. He did not seem anything, at first; he looked at her with his one good eye and went on hoeing until she had finished, and then he leaned on the hoe and looked at the fennel for a while.\n\n\"Orsato,\" he said. \"Of all people. He has a sister at the Celestia. He brings her oranges.\" He shook his head. \"Everyone is someone's brother.\"\n\n\"What do we do?\"\n\n\"We?\" He smiled with the working side of his face. \"You've come to me instead of your aunt. I take that as a great compliment, and also as a sign that you're frightened of what she'll do, which is sensible of you. She is a very brave woman. Brave people are the most dangerous ones to tell things to.\"\n\n\"Will you tell her?\"\n\n\"Yes. Not today.\" He set the hoe against the wall. \"Sit down, child. On the well. No, it won't fall in, it's been here since the Lombards.\" She sat. He sat beside her, slowly, the way old men sit, in stages. \"Tell me again who knows the day. The Sensa. Who knows we go on the Sensa?\"\n\nLucia counted on her fingers. \"Zia. You. Me. And the notary. Messer Lion.\"\n\n\"And who knows the girl from Burano will be the one to carry it?\"\n\n\"The same.\"\n\n\"Not Nane? Not your mother?\"\n\n\"Nobody else. Zia made me swear.\"\n\n\"Four, then.\" The friar looked at the orchard wall, where a fig tree had pushed its way out between the bricks and was hanging there in the sun as though it had been crucified. \"And the Fleming knew the day before your aunt told him. And Orsato and the Fleming were talking about the Capi coming in at the door, which means Dolfin knows not only the day but the end of the plan as well, which your aunt has told to nobody except the four of us.\" He was quiet a moment. \"So it is you, or your aunt, or the notary, or me.\"\n\n\"It isn't me.\"\n\n\"No. I don't think it is.\"\n\n\"And it isn't Zia.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nLucia looked at him. He was looking at the fig tree. The sun was on the milky side of his face and it made the bad eye look like a pearl.\n\n\"It might be you,\" she said.\n\n\"It might.\" He nodded, as if she had said something very sensible about fennel. \"You have only my word that it is not. That is the trouble with all of this. In the end, you have only someone's word.\" He stood up, in stages, and picked up the hoe. \"I have known Matteo Lion eight years. I like him very much. He is the best notary I ever met and the worst card player. And in eight years, child, I have never once asked him how a man who loses every Sunday at the Greek's goes on paying his rent.\" He began to hoe again. \"Go home. Say nothing. Make your aunt a collar. I'll come to Murano before the Feast.\"","wordCount":789,"createdAt":1792361558901},{"id":"mv7-vhAvyh72hUwSskK6Z","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"VvxSkA1OXIEZRFdcspW_i","text":"They came down the Censori into the courtyard, and Orsato was there.\n\nHe was not pretending to be a guard any more. He was standing by the great bronze well-head in the middle of the courtyard in his black cap, with his hands behind his back, looking at nothing, the way the Capi had looked at nothing as they walked to the boat; and as Lucia came down the last steps with her basket he turned his head and looked at her, and she felt it go right through the pillow and the linen and the boards and the calf, and she thought, he knows. He can see it. He can see the book through the basket the way he saw me through the storeroom door.\n\n\"Don't stop,\" said Matteo pleasantly beside her. \"Smile at him. He's a friend of mine.\"\n\nShe did not smile. She walked. Across the courtyard, past the well-head, past Orsato, who did not move, through the Porta del Frumento, where the sun hit her in the face like a slap and the noise of the Molo came back all at once, trumpets, bells, the roar of the people as the Bucintoro, far out on the water, came about in a great slow curve towards the Lido.\n\n\"He's following,\" said Matteo. \"Good. Don't look.\"\n\nThey went along the Molo, through the crowd, past the column of San Todaro where the man in the grey coat had finished his fritter and was licking his fingers, past the Zecca, to the place at the end of the Molo where the boats were, hundreds of them, gondolas and sandoli and barges and the big painted peote of the guilds, all jostling and rocking at the steps waiting to follow the Doge to the sea. And in among them, very small, a sandolo from Murano with Piero at the oar and a woman in black sitting in the bottom of it, with a basket on her knee.\n\nIt was the same basket. Lucia had made sure of that; she had bought two from the same man at the Rialto on Monday and rubbed the new one with ash and grease until it looked as old as hers. And the pillow on top of it was the same, or near enough, with a half-made border pinned to it and bobbins hanging down.\n\n\"Zia!\" She waved. It was the most natural thing in the world. A girl from Burano, in the crowd on the Sensa, seeing her aunt in a boat. \"Zia, hold this, I want to go and see the Doge come back—\"\n\nShe went down the wet steps. Her aunt reached up. There was a moment, one, with a gondola bumping on one side and a fat man from the butchers' guild shouting on the other, when both baskets were in the air between them; and then Lucia was climbing back up the steps with a basket on her arm that weighed exactly what hers had weighed when she came out of the Censori, because Fra Anselmo had weighed them on his scales, and her aunt was sitting in the bottom of the boat with a basket on her knee and her hand flat on the pillow, the way she had put her hand flat on the false leaf at dawn.\n\nPiero pushed off. The sandolo slid out between the gondolas, and turned, and went away towards the Arsenal and the open water and Murano, small and black and unremarkable among a thousand boats.\n\nLucia did not watch it go. She turned round on the top step and looked straight across the Molo at Orsato, who had stopped ten paces off by a stall selling glass beads, and was holding one up to the light as though he were thinking of buying it.\n\nHe looked at her. He looked at the basket on her arm. He looked, for a long moment, out at the water where the Murano boat had gone.\n\nThen he put the bead down, and paid for it anyway, and walked away along the Molo towards the Rialto, without hurrying, to tell his master where the girl was going.","wordCount":692,"createdAt":1792364116981},{"id":"oCTFJdJlo-lJksT8eF5zW","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"2ELvnobYYBoagGZKaAtCN","text":"She did not open it in the boat.\n\nShe sat in the bottom of Piero's sandolo all the way across the lagoon with the basket on her knees and her hand flat on the pillow, and she did not lift the pillow, and she did not look under the linen, and she did not let herself think the thing she was thinking, which was that it might not be there. That the girl might have lost her nerve in the Chancery, at the last, and dropped the bobbins and picked them up and walked out with the friar's book still in her basket and the real one still in the press. That she was carrying three days of an old man's forgery across the water like a relic. Piero rowed, and said nothing, and once, off San Michele, where the dead of the city lay behind their red wall, he crossed himself, and she did too.\n\nAt home she barred the water-door and the street door both and carried the basket into the workshop and set it on the marver and lifted the pillow off.\n\nIt was there. Of course it was there. Boards and calf, swollen at the lower edge, and one brass corner missing, the bottom left, exactly as the friar had made it. She opened it at the front and read the first page, in her husband's father's hand, a receipt for a green glass for bottles that nobody had made since she was a girl. She opened it at the back. And then she turned to the end, to the gutter, where Anselmo's false leaf had been sewn in so neatly that she had had to put her nail to the stitches to find them, and there was no leaf. There was a stub of cut paper no wider than a fingernail. There was Bortolo's knife-cut, slightly ragged at the bottom where his hand had slipped, the night he did it, in February, with her holding the candle.\n\nShe put her hand flat on the stub and stood like that for a long time.\n\nThen she wrapped it again in the oilcloth Orsato had brought it away in, which she had kept, and carried it to the great furnace, and lay down on her side on the cold floor, and reached into the ash pit under the grate as far as her arm would go, past the old ash and the grit and the bones of a mouse, to the ledge at the back where Bortolo's father had kept his money in the plague year, and pushed it in. When she stood up she was grey to the shoulder. She washed her arm in the bucket by the marver, and washed her face, and changed into the black wool and the good collar; and she put on, for the first time, the little collar of punto in aria that Lucia had made, with the vine and the pomegranates and the birds and the border of knots like a fence round a garden, and pinned it at her throat with a pin of Murano glass.\n\nThen she went to sell the Fleming nothing.","wordCount":526,"createdAt":1792364142348},{"id":"pTlZZ4hQnUBWA6nAELpTt","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"FCcx9EVdPWwkA6032mhg0","text":"She lit the furnace for him.\n\nNot the great furnace; there was not wood enough on the island for that, and no reason. The small one, the glory-hole by the bench, where Bortolo had warmed the pieces between gathers; she lit it at dusk with the kindling and then the beech, and fed it until the mouth of it stood open on a round of white-orange light that hurt to look at, and the workshop, which had been cold for five weeks, began to tick and creak around her as the walls remembered what they were for.\n\nWhen Matteo Lion came up the step from his boat she was standing at the bench with a blowpipe in the glory-hole, turning it slowly, as though there were glass on the end of it. There was no glass. There was only the iron, going red, then orange.\n\n\"Madonna.\" He stopped in the water-door. The heat met him there; she saw it in his face. \"You've lit the fire.\"\n\n\"Come in, Messer Lion. Shut the door. The draught's bad for the glass.\"\n\nHe shut it. He came across the floor, and stopped at the end of the bench, where the light from the hole lay over him and made him look younger, or else older, it was hard to tell. He had a roll of paper under his arm. The way, she supposed. He laid it on the marver.\n\n\"The friar's not here?\" he said.\n\n\"The friar's in the lehr-room with my niece. They're listening.\" She turned the pipe. \"I wanted them to hear what you say. In case I have to tell it afterwards to someone, and they ask for witnesses.\"\n\nHe did not move. She watched him not move; she watched him understand, and add it up, and arrive at the figure. It did not take him long. He had always been quick.\n\n\"Orsato,\" he said.\n\n\"Orsato. At the Fleming's. On Sunday last. Talking about gold on a table and the Capi at the door, and a widow taken with the book in her hands.\" She drew the pipe out of the hole and held it up between them. The last hand's length of it was the colour of an apricot. \"Four people knew the day, Messer Lion. I didn't tell Dolfin. Lucia didn't tell Dolfin. Fra Anselmo didn't tell Dolfin, though he's the only one of you I can't prove it of.\"\n\n\"It wasn't the friar.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe pipe hissed faintly in the air between them. Neither of them looked at it.\n\n\"Four years,\" he said. \"Since they struck me off. He came to me the week after, at the Greek's, and bought me a drink, and paid my rent, and asked me to tell him now and then what I heard. In the malvasie. In the courts. Who was in debt, who was in love, who was leaving the city. I told myself it was nothing. I told myself I was a notary still, taking depositions. That's what I told myself.\" He looked at the glowing iron. \"I'd tell you I'm sorry, but you wouldn't believe me, and you'd be right.\"\n\n\"I don't want you to be sorry. I want to know what you're going to do.\"\n\nHe put his hand in his pocket, then, and she lifted the pipe an inch, and he stopped, and slowly drew the hand out and opened it on the marver, in the light.\n\nThere were three things on his palm. Her mother's coral, the little red branch. A letter, folded very small, in a way she had seen once, on Dolfin's table, with its seal lifted cleanly and pressed back. And a bead.\n\nShe knew the bead before she knew she knew it. White, lattimo, round as a pea, with a single thread of blue wound once about it. She had sent back eleven of them, the spring he was fourteen, because they were not round, or the blue had run, or the white was not white enough. The twelfth she had kept. Or she had thought she had. It was on the shelf by San Donato.\n\nNo. It was not. It was here.\n\n\"He said to tell you he's keeping the blanket dry,\" Matteo Lion said. \"He has the gaol fever. Not the worst kind, I think; I've seen the worst kind. And Dolfin told me on Thursday, at the foot of the Scala d'Oro, that when you've been taken at the Fleming's and given him the leaf, the Council will send your son to the galleys regardless, and Dolfin will speak very movingly for clemency, and lose.\" He pushed the letter towards her with one finger. \"That's to the Fleming. In Dolfin's own hand. He gave it me to carry and I didn't carry it. It promises Vos the receipt, a fair copy, when you've given it up. If the Capi saw it, they'd know what he is. A secretary of the Ten selling the secrets of the Art to Antwerp for gold.\"\n\nIsotta put the pipe back in the glory-hole. Her hand was not steady and she did not want him to see it.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\nHe thought about it. He actually thought, standing there with the heat on his face; she watched him do it, and somehow that was what she believed, in the end, more than anything he said.\n\n\"Because your son told me he'd kill me with the hot end of a pipe if I got you killed,\" he said. \"And I believed him. And I found, when I thought about it, that I'd very much rather he didn't have to.\"\n\nFrom the lehr-room door, behind her, there came a small sound, as of an old man trying not to laugh.\n\n\"Come out, Brother,\" said Isotta, without turning round. \"Lucia. Bring the lamp.\" She picked up the bead and closed her hand on it, so hard she felt the little thread of blue against her palm, and looked at the notary across the marver. \"Sit down, Messer Lion. You can sit down here. We're going to steal my husband's book on Thursday exactly as you planned it. Up the Censori, with the friar's warrant, and out in my niece's pillow. And when the boy Sanudo shuts the press behind you, there will still be a book in it.\"\n\n# The Marriage of the Sea\n\n[\n  Place: #The Molo\n  Characters: @@Lucia Zen @@Matteo Lion @@Orsato\n  When: Thursday 19 May\n  Items: ^The false ledger:@Lucia Zen ^The forged warrant:@Matteo Lion\n]\n\nOn the morning of the Sensa the whole city walked to the sea.\n\nLucia had seen it before, every year of her life, from a boat on the edge of the crowd with her father swearing at the other boats; she had never seen it from the inside. From the inside it was not a crowd. It was weather. It moved in gusts and eddies along the Riva and across the Piazzetta, it sang, it shouted, it sold things, it smelled of fried fish and orange-water and incense and the sweat of ten thousand people in their best clothes, and it carried her along the Molo with her basket on her arm and her pillow on top of the basket the way the tide carries a cork.\n\nThe basket was heavy. Nobody would have known. She carried it the way she carried it every day, on the crook of her left arm with her hand over the pillow, and the pillow on top had a half-made border pinned on it with the bobbins hanging down, and under the pillow, wrapped in a cloth of unbleached linen, was a book in boards and calf, swollen at the lower edge, with one brass corner missing, the bottom left.\n\nFra Anselmo had brought it to Murano at dawn. He had not slept, he said, for three nights, and looked it; his good eye was red and his bad eye was redder, and his fingers were black to the second joint. Her aunt had opened it at the front and read the first page and closed it and opened it at the back and read the last, and then turned to the end, where the stub was, and found the leaf sewn in there, in Zio Bortolo's cramped brown hand, CALCEDONIO, with the weights all wrong. She had put her hand flat on it for a moment. Then she had kissed the friar on both cheeks, which Lucia had never seen her do to anyone, not even Zio Bortolo, and he had gone pink to the edge of his tonsure.\n\nAt the third hour the bells began.\n\nAll of them. San Marco first, the great bell, the Marangona, so deep Lucia felt it in her teeth rather than heard it; and then San Zaccaria and San Giorgio across the water and the Pietà and San Moisè, and the ones further off, Santi Apostoli and the Frari and the Madonna dell'Orto, until the whole city was ringing like a struck glass. And out of the Porta della Carta, between the guards, came the Doge.\n\nLucia had never been so close. He was an old man in cloth of gold, with the horned cap on his head and a face like a walnut, and he walked very slowly because of the weight of the gold, between two men holding up the train, and behind him came the Signoria in crimson, and behind them the Capi of the Ten in black, three old men in a row looking at nothing. The crowd went down on its knees. Lucia went down on her knees with her basket in her arms, and through the legs of the people in front she watched the procession go along the Molo to the water, where the Bucintoro lay against the steps like a great golden animal, and all its oars stood up in the air at once in a salute, two hundred of them, white, with a sound like a flock of birds rising.\n\nWhen she stood up, Matteo Lion was beside her.\n\nShe had seen him only once, the night of the glory-hole, in the bad light of the furnace. In the morning sun he looked older and more respectable, in a notary's black gown with a roll of papers in his hand and a cap like a pudding, and he was smiling at the Bucintoro as though he had paid for it.\n\n\"You're very punctual,\" he said, without looking at her. \"Don't look round. There's a man by the column of San Todaro in a grey coat eating a fritter. He's been there since the second hour. And Orsato is at the Porta della Carta, behind the guard, pretending to be a guard.\"\n\nLucia did not look round.\n\n\"The Capi are on the boat,\" Matteo said. \"All three of them. And Dolfin isn't.\"\n\n\"Where is he?\"\n\n\"He sent his excuses this morning. A fever.\" Matteo smiled more widely at the Bucintoro, which was moving now, very slowly, out from the steps into the basin, with the trumpets going on the poop and the oars rising and falling. \"I expect he'll recover by noon. Have you got it?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Have you got the other thing?\"\n\nShe moved her right hand on the basket handle, and felt, in the pocket of her apron, the little bag of bobbins that she had wound the night before: forty of them, old ones, boxwood and bone, every one she could find in her mother's house.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then walk with me,\" said Matteo Lion, \"like a girl from Burano who's been asked to carry something for a notary and doesn't think much of it, and when we're on the stair, don't speak at all. Your accent is very charming and very Burano and the guard on the Censori would remember it for the rest of his life.\"","wordCount":1974,"createdAt":1792361921350},{"id":"pmRD1-NfyOIuPdysrQ15w","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"WfzecJCXcnkhuVLjye4PH","text":"\"A girl,\" Dolfin said. \"How delightful.\"\n\nThe vine on the pergola had put out its first leaves since Matteo's last visit, small and crumpled and very green, like the hands of a newborn. Dolfin was not sitting under it this time; he was walking, slowly, round and round the well-head, with his hands behind his back, and Matteo had to stand in the middle of the gravel and turn to keep facing him, like a horse on a lunge rein.\n\n\"Her niece. Lucia Zen. A lacemaker from Burano. Nineteen.\"\n\n\"Pretty?\"\n\n\"I've never seen her.\"\n\n\"Then you'll have that to look forward to.\" Dolfin completed a circuit. \"And the friar is cutting the seal. Our seal. On the Sensa. Up the Censori.\" He said each thing as though he were laying it on a table, to look at it. \"The boy Sanudo. You've been thorough, Matteo. I'd forgotten how thorough you were. It's a pity about the cards.\"\n\n\"Excellency.\" Matteo had not meant to speak. \"The girl. When you come to the Rialto with the Capi—\"\n\n\"The girl will be with her aunt and her aunt will be selling the Republic's property to a foreigner. What becomes of her depends on what her aunt does next.\" Dolfin stopped walking. \"You're not growing a conscience, I hope. At your age it's like growing teeth; it hurts, and nobody admires the result.\"\n\n\"No, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Good.\" Dolfin resumed his circuit. \"There's one thing more I want. When you have the girl in the Chancery with the book in her pillow, I want you to bring her out by the Censori. Not the Scala d'Oro, not the Giganti, not some other little rat-hole of your own. The Censori, and across the courtyard, and out by the Porta del Frumento to the Molo. I'll have a man in the courtyard to see her go. I don't want her taken. I want her watched, all the way to the Rialto, so that when I come in at the Fleming's door the book is on his table and her hands are still warm from it.\"\n\n\"As you say.\"\n\n\"As I say.\" Dolfin smiled at him across the well-head. \"And Matteo. That debt of yours. Two hundred and twelve, you said? I took the liberty of buying it. From the Greek, and from that other fellow at the Frari. I hold all of it now. It seemed tidier.\"\n\nMatteo stood very still on the gravel. Above him a sparrow came down onto the pergola and looked at the new leaves and decided against them and flew away.\n\n\"That's very kind, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Isn't it?\" said Dolfin. \"I thought so.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Zen house on Burano\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Lucia Zen\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?Lucia will carry the ledger out:@Lucia Zen ?Lucia will carry the ledger out:@Isotta Ferro\n]\n\nIt was nearly dark when Isotta came to Burano, and she did not knock. She came up the step from Piero's boat and through the water-door and into the little room at the back where her niece sat working by the last of the window, and stood in the doorway until Lucia looked up.\n\n\"The friar thinks you should carry it,\" she said. \"The book. Out of the Chancery, under your pillow, on the Sensa. With the notary beside you and half the Ten on the water and the other half, God knows, anywhere at all.\" She heard her own voice and did not like it; it was the voice of the Guild men in the yard. \"I said I would ask you. I'm asking you. You can say no. I'd think better of you, I think, if you said no.\"\n\nLucia put down her bobbins.\n\n\"Then you'd have to find another girl from Burano with a basket,\" she said, \"and teach her the stairs in two weeks, and trust her. And you don't trust anybody, Zia. You told me so on the bridge.\"\n\n\"I trust you.\"\n\n\"Then let me.\" Lucia got up and went to the corner by the bed, where her work-basket stood, the old one, the rush gone dark with the grease of her hands and patched at the rim with a strip of red cloth. She lifted it and turned it round, so that her aunt could see the patch. \"But not with one basket. With two.\"\n\n\"Two.\"\n\n\"Everybody knows my basket. Every boatman from here to the Fondamente. If they see it going into the Palace on the Sensa and coming out again, they'll remember it. So there should be two the same. I'll buy one at the Rialto, from the man by the fish-market, and rub it with ash and lard until it looks like this, and sew the same red cloth on the rim.\" She was talking quickly now, the way she did when a pattern came to her all at once and she had to get it down before it went. \"And when I come out of the Palace I'll give you mine, with the book in it, and you give me yours, with lace in it. In the crowd. On the Molo, at the boats, where everyone's handing things up and down. And whoever's watching me will follow a girl with a basket of lace all the way to wherever they like.\"\n\nIsotta looked at the basket for a long time. Then at her niece.\n\n\"Who taught you to think like that?\"\n\n\"Suor Benedetta,\" said Lucia. \"She says the first thing a lacemaker learns is that the pattern people look at is never the one that's holding the thing together.\"\n\n\"Is that true?\"\n\n\"No,\" said Lucia, and smiled for the first time in two weeks. \"She tells everybody something different.\"","wordCount":949,"createdAt":1792364155153},{"id":"r6aGJCP44Sne-6gMPMMlt","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"MsLhDkYbkLalyMtBs3Twg","text":"She did not go to her aunt.\n\nShe thought about it the whole way from the Rialto. She thought about it crossing the Strada Nova, and on the bridge at the Misericordia where the boys were fishing for eels with bent pins, and she knew exactly what would happen. Her aunt would listen with her hands folded. Her aunt would say thank you, Lucia. And then her aunt would say: it's over, we stop. And she would go to Dolfin and give him what he wanted, because there would be nothing else left to do, and Nane would come home, and the calcedonio would go into the Guild's chest and Zio Bortolo would be nobody, and her aunt would be nobody, and every time her aunt looked at a piece of glass for the rest of her life she would think: I gave it to them.\n\nOr else she would not stop. She would say: we go on, and we are cleverer. And she would ask Lucia to go into the Palace anyway, knowing.\n\nLucia did not know which was worse. So she went to the Servi.\n\nFra Anselmo was in the orchard behind the dormitory, in his shirt-sleeves, with a hoe, among rows of something green and feathery that turned out to be fennel. He did not seem surprised to see her. He did not seem anything, at first; he looked at her with his one good eye and went on hoeing until she had finished, and then he leaned on the hoe and looked at the fennel for a while.\n\n\"Orsato,\" he said. \"Of all people. He has a sister at the Celestia. He brings her oranges.\" He shook his head. \"Everyone is someone's brother.\"\n\n\"What do we do?\"\n\n\"We?\" He smiled with the working side of his face. \"You've come to me instead of your aunt. I take that as a great compliment, and also as a sign that you're frightened of what she'll do, which is sensible of you. She is a very brave woman. Brave people are the most dangerous ones to tell things to.\"\n\n\"Will you tell her?\"\n\n\"Yes. Not today.\" He set the hoe against the wall. \"Sit down, child. On the well. No, it won't fall in, it's been here since the Lombards.\" She sat. He sat beside her, slowly, the way old men sit, in stages. \"Tell me again who knows the day. The Sensa. Who knows we go on the Sensa?\"\n\nLucia counted on her fingers. \"Zia. You. Me. And the notary. Messer Lion.\"\n\n\"And who knows the girl from Burano will be the one to carry it?\"\n\n\"The same.\"\n\n\"Not Nane? Not your mother?\"\n\n\"Nobody else. Zia made me swear.\"\n\n\"Four, then.\" The friar looked at the orchard wall, where a fig tree had pushed its way out between the bricks and was hanging there in the sun as though it had been crucified. \"And the Fleming knew the day before your aunt told him. And Orsato and the Fleming were talking about the Capi coming in at the door, which means Dolfin knows not only the day but the end of the plan as well, which your aunt has told to nobody except the four of us.\" He was quiet a moment. \"So it is you, or your aunt, or the notary, or me.\"\n\n\"It isn't me.\"\n\n\"No. I don't think it is.\"\n\n\"And it isn't Zia.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nLucia looked at him. He was looking at the fig tree. The sun was on the milky side of his face and it made the bad eye look like a pearl.\n\n\"It might be you,\" she said.\n\n\"It might.\" He nodded, as if she had said something very sensible about fennel. \"You have only my word that it is not. That is the trouble with all of this. In the end, you have only someone's word.\" He stood up, in stages, and picked up the hoe. \"I have known Matteo Lion eight years. I like him very much. He is the best notary I ever met and the worst card player. And in eight years, child, I have never once asked him how a man who loses every Sunday at the Greek's goes on paying his rent.\" He began to hoe again. \"Go home. Say nothing. Make your aunt a collar. I'll come to Murano before the Feast.\"\n\n# The Wells\n\n[\n  Place: #The Pozzi\n  Characters: @@Matteo Lion @@Nane Ferro\n  When: +4 days\n  Items: ^The lattimo bead:@Matteo Lion\n  Facts: ?Nane has the gaol fever:@Matteo Lion ?Nane has the gaol fever:reader\n]\n\nHe told himself he went down to the wells to count the guards.\n\nThat was true, as far as it went. On the Sensa they would come up the Censori, and a man who came up the Censori could also be made to go down it, and down was the Pozzi; it was worth knowing how many men were on the lower passage on a feast day and whether they could hear the Chancery from where they stood. He had a pass. He had kept it, as he had kept the paper. It said that Matteo Lion, notary of the Avogaria, was to be admitted to the prisons to take depositions in the matter of the Republic's business, and it was four years out of date, and the guard on the stool by the low arch could not read it, and let him through for two soldi.\n\nHe counted the guards. There were three, on the lower passage, and one of them was asleep.\n\nThen he went to the third cell, because he had known all along that he would.\n\nThe boy was lying down. That was the first thing. A boy of seventeen does not lie down in the middle of the day unless he has been told to or cannot help it, and nobody had told him to. He was on the plank with the blanket over him up to the chin, and when Matteo lifted the little lamp he had bought from the guard and held it to the bars, the face that turned towards the light was shining wet, and the eyes were too bright.\n\n\"Who are you?\"\n\n\"A friend of your mother's.\"\n\n\"My mother hasn't got any friends.\" The boy laughed and the laugh went into a cough that went on a long time. \"She's got my father's friends, and they all came to the funeral and looked at the furnace. Are you from the Guild?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"From Dolfin?\"\n\nMatteo did not answer quickly enough.\n\n\"Get out,\" the boy said. Then: \"No. Wait. Wait.\" He pushed himself up on one elbow. In the lamplight Matteo could see the hand, the burnt one, gripping the edge of the blanket, the scar shiny as glass. \"Is she all right? Has he done anything to her?\"\n\n\"She's all right.\"\n\n\"She came once. She said she'd come again.\" The boy lay back. \"That was three weeks ago. It's hard to count. They don't tell you the days down here. They give you bread twice and that's a day, but sometimes they forget and give it once, and then you don't know.\" He was quiet for a moment. \"There's a man in the next cell who's been here since the war of Cyprus. He talks to me through the wall. He says it gets easier. He says after the first year you stop hearing the water.\"\n\n\"Your mother's coming for you,\" Matteo said.\n\nHe had not meant to say it. It was the kind of thing one did not say, in the Pozzi, with three guards on the passage and one of them perhaps not asleep.\n\nThe boy turned his head and looked at him. It was Isotta's look. It was exactly her look, the one she had given him in the back room of the Greek's, adding him up.\n\n\"You're the notary,\" Nane said. \"Fra Anselmo's notary. She told me about you. Not your name. She said there was a man who knew the stairs, who was clever and unlucky.\" He was breathing hard. \"Are you going to get her killed?\"\n\nMatteo opened his mouth. Nothing came out of it.\n\n\"Because if you are,\" said Giovanni Ferro, seventeen, lying on a plank under the Palace of the Doges with the gaol fever in him, \"I'll kill you. When I get out. I'll come and find you and I'll do it with a pipe from the glory-hole, the hot end, and it will take a long time.\" He coughed again. \"That's a promise. My father always kept his promises. Ask anybody.\"\n\nThen, before Matteo could say anything, he fumbled under the blanket and brought out something small and held it through the bars.\n\n\"Here. Give her this. I made it before. Before the ship. I had it in my shoe and they didn't find it.\" His fingers opened. On the palm, among the calluses, lay a bead: a little sphere of white glass the size of a chickpea, milk-white, perfectly round, with a single thread of blue wound once about its middle like a ring on a finger. \"Lattimo. The first one I ever made that she didn't send back. Tell her I'm keeping the blanket dry.\"\n\nMatteo took it. It was warm from the boy's hand. He put it in his pocket, where the coral was, and they lay there together, the two small hard things, the white and the red.\n\n\"I'll tell her,\" he said.","wordCount":1578,"createdAt":1792361634381},{"id":"rvPnD-F-2hxIO0uFELAqP","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"2rW2A0wi2wIlkCEMMTq6j","text":"[\n  Place: #The Zen house on Burano\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Lucia Zen\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?Lucia will carry the ledger out:@Lucia Zen ?Lucia will carry the ledger out:@Isotta Ferro\n]\n\nIt was nearly dark when Isotta came to Burano, and she did not knock. She came up the step from Piero's boat and through the water-door and into the little room at the back where her niece sat working by the last of the window, and stood in the doorway until Lucia looked up.\n\n\"The friar thinks you should carry it,\" she said. \"The book. Out of the Chancery, under your pillow, on the Sensa. With the notary beside you and half the Ten on the water and the other half, God knows, anywhere at all.\" She heard her own voice and did not like it; it was the voice of the Guild men in the yard. \"I said I would ask you. I'm asking you. You can say no. I'd think better of you, I think, if you said no.\"\n\nLucia put down her bobbins.\n\n\"Then you'd have to find another girl from Burano with a basket,\" she said, \"and teach her the stairs in two weeks, and trust her. And you don't trust anybody, Zia. You told me so on the bridge.\"\n\n\"I trust you.\"\n\n\"Then let me.\" Lucia got up and went to the corner by the bed, where her work-basket stood, the old one, the rush gone dark with the grease of her hands and patched at the rim with a strip of red cloth. She lifted it and turned it round, so that her aunt could see the patch. \"But not with one basket. With two.\"\n\n\"Two.\"\n\n\"Everybody knows my basket. Every boatman from here to the Fondamente. If they see it going into the Palace on the Sensa and coming out again, they'll remember it. So there should be two the same. I'll buy one at the Rialto, from the man by the fish-market, and rub it with ash and lard until it looks like this, and sew the same red cloth on the rim.\" She was talking quickly now, the way she did when a pattern came to her all at once and she had to get it down before it went. \"And when I come out of the Palace I'll give you mine, with the book in it, and you give me yours, with lace in it. In the crowd. On the Molo, at the boats, where everyone's handing things up and down. And whoever's watching me will follow a girl with a basket of lace all the way to wherever they like.\"\n\nIsotta looked at the basket for a long time. Then at her niece.\n\n\"Who taught you to think like that?\"\n\n\"Suor Benedetta,\" said Lucia. \"She says the first thing a lacemaker learns is that the pattern people look at is never the one that's holding the thing together.\"\n\n\"Is that true?\"\n\n\"No,\" said Lucia, and smiled for the first time in two weeks. \"She tells everybody something different.\"","wordCount":510,"createdAt":1792364156579},{"id":"sCdO5R7OsxIsmVSwGDdD3","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"Fydy7EaqZZr40hKtbAF0P","text":"He came up out of the wells into the courtyard and there was Dolfin, at the foot of the Scala d'Oro, in a gown of black damask with a fur at the collar though it was May, talking to two senators as though he had been waiting there all morning for nobody in particular.\n\nHe had not been waiting for nobody. He finished with the senators and turned and came across the flagstones, and Matteo felt the stones under his own feet go soft, the way the floor of the lower passage had been soft.\n\n\"Matteo. You've been visiting.\"\n\n\"I wanted to count the guards, Excellency. For the day.\"\n\n\"Of course you did.\" Dolfin fell in beside him, and they walked, slowly, along the arcade towards the Porta della Carta. Two men in black, talking business. Anyone watching would have thought Matteo had come up in the world. \"And how many are there?\"\n\n\"Three. On the lower passage.\"\n\n\"And how is the boy?\"\n\n\"He has the gaol fever.\"\n\n\"Ah.\" Dolfin nodded, regretfully. \"Yes. It's the spring tides. It comes up through the floor.\" They walked a few paces. \"Well, it hardly matters now, does it?\"\n\nMatteo stopped.\n\n\"Excellency?\"\n\n\"Walk, Matteo. People are looking at us.\" Dolfin took his elbow, quite gently, and walked him on. \"I said it hardly matters. The Capi have agreed, on my word, to come to the Rialto on the evening of the Feast. I have promised them a widow selling the secrets of the Art to a Fleming, and they are very excited, in the way that old men are excited, which is to say they have stopped listening to me and started composing their speeches. When she is taken, the widow will give me the leaf. And when she has given it to me, the Council will consider the matter of her son, who tried to carry the Art abroad, and whose mother has now been taken trying to do the same. And they will send him to the galleys, Matteo, because there will be no other possible thing for them to do. It will not be my decision. I shall argue for clemency. I shall be very moving.\" He smiled. \"I've always been told I'm very moving.\"\n\n\"You told her he'd walk out on the Sensa.\"\n\n\"I told her he would walk out if she brought me the leaf. She has not brought me the leaf. She has chosen to steal from the Republic instead. People make their choices.\" Dolfin patted his arm. \"You look ill. Is it the wells? They take some people that way. One breath and they're ill for a week.\"\n\nThey had come to the Porta della Carta. Beyond it, in the Piazzetta, the sun was blazing on the two columns and on the water, and men were already putting up the stands for the Sensa fair, a forest of poles and canvas and shouting.\n\n\"One thing more,\" Dolfin said, and took a letter from his sleeve. It was folded small, in the Chancery fold, and sealed with plain red wax and no device. \"For our friend at the Rialto. He's anxious, and anxious men talk. Take it to him tonight. He'll know who it's from. Don't let him keep you.\"\n\nMatteo took it. It went into his pocket with the bead and the coral.\n\n\"Excellency,\" he said. \"The boy. Supposing she did bring you the leaf. Supposing she came tomorrow.\"\n\n\"Then she'd be a fool,\" said Dolfin, \"to bring it to me before the Feast instead of after it, wouldn't she, when the price will be so much lower after.\" He looked at Matteo, curiously, the way he had looked at him across the well-head the day he bought his debts. \"Why do you ask?\"\n\n\"I like to know the odds, Excellency.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Dolfin. \"That's always been your trouble.\" He lifted a hand, and went back across the courtyard to the Scala d'Oro, and up it, into the dark of the Palace, where the gold on the vault caught the light and held it as the rest of the stair went into shadow.\n\nMatteo did not go to the Rialto that night. He went home and sat at his table and took the three things out of his pocket and laid them in a row: the coral, and the bead, and the letter in the Chancery fold. He looked at them until the candle had burned down a finger's width.\n\nThen he took his penknife, and warmed the blade in the flame, and very carefully, the way he had done a hundred times in the old days when he was the best notary in the Republic and had not yet been caught, lifted the seal.\n\n# Confession\n\n## Fra Anselmo comes to Murano\n[\n  Place: #The Ferro furnace\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Fra Anselmo @@Lucia Zen\n  When: +4 days\n  Facts: ?Vos is working with Dolfin:@Isotta Ferro ?Dolfin promised Vos a copy of the calcedonio receipt:@Isotta Ferro ?Only four people knew the day of the theft:@Isotta Ferro\n]\n\nThe friar came on the Monday before the Feast, with Lucia, in the middle of the afternoon, when the light lay in long gold bars across the cold floor of the workshop and the dust stood up in them like smoke.\n\nIsotta was cleaning the crucibles. She had begun it on Saturday because she could not sit still, and it was work that wanted nothing from her head: a scraper, a bucket of water, a brush, and twenty pots crusted with the glass of the last firing, Bortolo's last firing, a green-gold skin of it that came away in flakes. She had done eleven. When she heard the boat she did not get up. When she saw Lucia's face in the water-door, she put down the scraper.\n\n\"Tell me,\" she said.\n\nThey told her. Lucia told the stair and the open door and Orsato's voice and the Fleming asking about the leaf. She told the storeroom and the pepper and the black shoe on the bottom step, and the ring. She told it well, Isotta thought; in order, without decoration, the way Matteo Lion would have liked a witness to tell it. Then the friar told the rest, sitting on the bench by the marver with his hands on his knees: the four people who knew the day; the fig tree; eight years and the rent.\n\nIsotta listened to all of it with her hands folded on her apron. When they had finished she stood up and went to the rack of blowpipes and took down the fourth from the left, the oldest, and stood holding it, as though it were the only thing in the room that weighed what it should.\n\n\"When did you know?\" she said to Lucia.\n\n\"Sunday. A week.\"\n\n\"A week.\"\n\n\"Zia, I was afraid you'd stop. I was afraid you'd go to Dolfin. Fra Anselmo said—\"\n\n\"I know what Fra Anselmo said. Fra Anselmo was right.\" Isotta turned the pipe in her hands. \"I would have stopped. On Sunday I would have gone to Dolfin in my good black with my mother's coral and given him your collar and thanked him for his kindness.\" She looked at the pipe. \"I haven't got my mother's coral. I gave it to the notary.\"\n\n\"The notary,\" said Fra Anselmo, \"is coming tonight.\"\n\n\"Yes. I asked him to come. To go over the way once more, he said.\" She set the pipe back in the rack, carefully, the fourth from the left. \"Brother. Can you copy a book?\"\n\nThe friar raised his eyebrows. The good one and the bad one went up together, and for a moment he looked like a startled owl.\n\n\"That depends very much on the book.\"\n\n\"Boards. Calf. Brass corners, one missing, the bottom left. Swollen at the lower edge from the damp, the way all our books swell. Two hundred and eleven leaves of rag paper, foxed. My husband's hand, which I can give you in forty letters he wrote me when he was in Florence and I was not, and which I have kept, God knows why, because they are mostly about money.\" She looked at him. \"Not all two hundred and eleven. Nobody could do that in three days. The first gathering and the last, so that a man who opened it at the front or at the back would see what he expected to see. The middle you can fill with anything. Old accounts. Prayers. Recipes for jam.\"\n\n\"And the last leaf? The one he's waiting for?\"\n\n\"Put one in,\" said Isotta Ferro. \"Sewn into the stub, where the cut one was. Calcedonio. In my husband's hand. With every weight wrong.\"\n\nLucia made a small sound. The friar did not; he only looked at her for a long time with his one good eye, and then, slowly, with the side of his face that worked, he smiled.\n\n\"Every weight?\"\n\n\"Not so wrong that a glassmaker would laugh. Wrong enough that it would make something the colour of mud and crack in the lehr. I'll write it out for you.\" She almost smiled herself. \"I know exactly how to make calcedonio go wrong. We did it forty times in February.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Ferro furnace\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Matteo Lion @@Fra Anselmo @@Lucia Zen\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The lattimo bead:@Isotta Ferro ^Dolfin's letter to Vos:@Isotta Ferro ^The false ledger:@Fra Anselmo\n  Facts: ?Matteo informs for Dolfin:@Isotta Ferro ?Matteo informs for Dolfin:@Lucia Zen ?Matteo informs for Dolfin:@Fra Anselmo ?Dolfin means to send Nane to the galleys regardless:@Isotta Ferro ?Matteo has turned against Dolfin:@Isotta Ferro ?Matteo has turned against Dolfin:reader ?The false ledger will be left in the press:@Isotta Ferro ?The false ledger will be left in the press:@Matteo Lion ?The false ledger will be left in the press:@Fra Anselmo ?The false ledger will be left in the press:@Lucia Zen ?The false ledger will be left in the press:reader\n]\n\nShe lit the furnace for him.\n\nNot the great furnace; there was not wood enough on the island for that, and no reason. The small one, the glory-hole by the bench, where Bortolo had warmed the pieces between gathers; she lit it at dusk with the kindling and then the beech, and fed it until the mouth of it stood open on a round of white-orange light that hurt to look at, and the workshop, which had been cold for five weeks, began to tick and creak around her as the walls remembered what they were for.\n\nWhen Matteo Lion came up the step from his boat she was standing at the bench with a blowpipe in the glory-hole, turning it slowly, as though there were glass on the end of it. There was no glass. There was only the iron, going red, then orange.\n\n\"Madonna.\" He stopped in the water-door. The heat met him there; she saw it in his face. \"You've lit the fire.\"\n\n\"Come in, Messer Lion. Shut the door. The draught's bad for the glass.\"\n\nHe shut it. He came across the floor, and stopped at the end of the bench, where the light from the hole lay over him and made him look younger, or else older, it was hard to tell. He had a roll of paper under his arm. The way, she supposed. He laid it on the marver.\n\n\"The friar's not here?\" he said.\n\n\"The friar's in the lehr-room with my niece. They're listening.\" She turned the pipe. \"I wanted them to hear what you say. In case I have to tell it afterwards to someone, and they ask for witnesses.\"\n\nHe did not move. She watched him not move; she watched him understand, and add it up, and arrive at the figure. It did not take him long. He had always been quick.\n\n\"Orsato,\" he said.\n\n\"Orsato. At the Fleming's. On Sunday last. Talking about gold on a table and the Capi at the door, and a widow taken with the book in her hands.\" She drew the pipe out of the hole and held it up between them. The last hand's length of it was the colour of an apricot. \"Four people knew the day, Messer Lion. I didn't tell Dolfin. Lucia didn't tell Dolfin. Fra Anselmo didn't tell Dolfin, though he's the only one of you I can't prove it of.\"\n\n\"It wasn't the friar.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe pipe hissed faintly in the air between them. Neither of them looked at it.\n\n\"Four years,\" he said. \"Since they struck me off. He came to me the week after, at the Greek's, and bought me a drink, and paid my rent, and asked me to tell him now and then what I heard. In the malvasie. In the courts. Who was in debt, who was in love, who was leaving the city. I told myself it was nothing. I told myself I was a notary still, taking depositions. That's what I told myself.\" He looked at the glowing iron. \"I'd tell you I'm sorry, but you wouldn't believe me, and you'd be right.\"\n\n\"I don't want you to be sorry. I want to know what you're going to do.\"\n\nHe put his hand in his pocket, then, and she lifted the pipe an inch, and he stopped, and slowly drew the hand out and opened it on the marver, in the light.\n\nThere were three things on his palm. Her mother's coral, the little red branch. A letter, folded very small, in a way she had seen once, on Dolfin's table, with its seal lifted cleanly and pressed back. And a bead.\n\nShe knew the bead before she knew she knew it. White, lattimo, round as a pea, with a single thread of blue wound once about it. She had sent back eleven of them, the spring he was fourteen, because they were not round, or the blue had run, or the white was not white enough. The twelfth she had kept. Or she had thought she had. It was on the shelf by San Donato.\n\nNo. It was not. It was here.\n\n\"He said to tell you he's keeping the blanket dry,\" Matteo Lion said. \"He has the gaol fever. Not the worst kind, I think; I've seen the worst kind. And Dolfin told me on Thursday, at the foot of the Scala d'Oro, that when you've been taken at the Fleming's and given him the leaf, the Council will send your son to the galleys regardless, and Dolfin will speak very movingly for clemency, and lose.\" He pushed the letter towards her with one finger. \"That's to the Fleming. In Dolfin's own hand. He gave it me to carry and I didn't carry it. It promises Vos the receipt, a fair copy, when you've given it up. If the Capi saw it, they'd know what he is. A secretary of the Ten selling the secrets of the Art to Antwerp for gold.\"\n\nIsotta put the pipe back in the glory-hole. Her hand was not steady and she did not want him to see it.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\nHe thought about it. He actually thought, standing there with the heat on his face; she watched him do it, and somehow that was what she believed, in the end, more than anything he said.\n\n\"Because your son told me he'd kill me with the hot end of a pipe if I got you killed,\" he said. \"And I believed him. And I found, when I thought about it, that I'd very much rather he didn't have to.\"\n\nFrom the lehr-room door, behind her, there came a small sound, as of an old man trying not to laugh.\n\n\"Come out, Brother,\" said Isotta, without turning round. \"Lucia. Bring the lamp.\" She picked up the bead and closed her hand on it, so hard she felt the little thread of blue against her palm, and looked at the notary across the marver. \"Sit down, Messer Lion. You can sit down here. We're going to steal my husband's book on Thursday exactly as you planned it. Up the Censori, with the friar's warrant, and out in my niece's pillow. And when the boy Sanudo shuts the press behind you, there will still be a book in it.\"","wordCount":2708,"createdAt":1792361755750},{"id":"sirMR0GrDmjcGCHi2bWwZ","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"eGefyjbO4ydqoB5RwpDOc","text":"[\n  Place: #The Secret Chancery\n  Characters: @@Matteo Lion @@Lucia Zen @@Sanudo\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The Glass Ledger:@Lucia Zen ^The false ledger:#The Secret Chancery ^The forged warrant:@Sanudo\n  Facts: ?The false ledger is in the press:@Matteo Lion ?The false ledger is in the press:@Lucia Zen ?The false ledger is in the press:reader\n]\n\nThe guard at the stairhead of the Censori was a Dalmatian with a broken nose who had been a guard at the stairhead of the Censori, Matteo was fairly sure, since the war of Cyprus. He did not read the warrant. He looked at the seal on it, the lion with his paw on the book, and at Matteo's gown and cap, and at the girl with the basket, and spat out of the window into the Rio di Palazzo, and waved them on.\n\nMatteo's legs had been fine on the stair. They went to water in the passage. It was the smell that did it: ink, and beeswax on the panelling, and the faint sharp smell of the sand they shook over wet signatures, which he had not smelled for four years and which went into him like a hook into a fish. The clerks' desks along the wall were empty. The pens lay on them in a row. Somewhere far off, through the Palace, he could hear the trumpets on the water, thin as a gnat.\n\nSanudo was in the Chancery with his feet on a desk, eating cherries out of his hat.\n\n\"Lion!\" He took his feet down. He was twenty, and plump, and had a downy beginning of a beard that he touched when he was nervous; he touched it now. \"What are you doing here? You're not supposed to be here. You're not anything any more, are you? They struck you off.\" He seemed pleased with himself for remembering. \"And who's she?\"\n\n\"My sister's girl. She carries my papers.\" Matteo held out the warrant. He did it the old way, the way he had once held out depositions to the Avogadori, with the seal uppermost and his thumb not quite touching it, so the eye went to the wax and stayed there. \"His Excellency Dolfin's compliments, Sanudo. He's sick, poor man, and he's sent me for something, and if you don't help me I shall have to go out to the Bucintoro in a sandolo and explain to the Capi in the middle of the Doge's wedding why the Ferro receipt isn't on their table, and I'll give them your name. Read it.\"\n\nSanudo read it. It took him some time. His lips moved. Matteo, who had written it, could have recited it: the officer on duty in the Secret Chancery to show the bearer the book of receipts of the late Bortolo Ferro, received the twenty-first of April, so that the bearer might copy from it the receipt for lattimo, of which the Capi had need on the Bucintoro for a question raised by the Most Serene Prince touching the making of the cups for the wedding feast. Signed, in a hand that had been dead for six years, by a secretary of the first grade; under it, the lion.\n\nIt was, Matteo thought, a deeply silly warrant. That was why he loved it. Nobody would forge anything so silly.\n\n\"Lattimo,\" said Sanudo.\n\n\"The Doge wants white cups. The Patriarch wants clear ones. There's been a quarrel. You know what they're like.\" Matteo shrugged. \"Twenty lines. I copy them, you lock it up again, I go out to the boat, you go back to your cherries. Or you send me away and tomorrow you explain.\"\n\nSanudo looked at the warrant, and at Matteo, and touched his beard; and Matteo watched him think exactly what he had told Isotta Ferro, in the friar's cell, that the boy would think. Either it was real; or the only man who could have forged it was a man he had beaten at primero.\n\nHe took the key off his belt.\n\nThe press stood against the wall of the little room beyond the Chancery door, the room most people did not know was a room: a cupboard of walnut, tall as a man, with a lock like a fist. Inside, on three shelves, were the new matters, the things that had come in and not yet been entered and sent to their proper chests. Bundles of letters tied with tape. A box of seized pamphlets. A dagger in a bag with a label on it. And on the middle shelf, by itself, a book in boards and calf, swollen at the lower edge, with one brass corner missing, the bottom left.\n\nMatteo heard the girl behind him breathe in, once.\n\n\"There,\" Sanudo said, and lifted it out, and put it on the Chancery desk, and opened it, and stood over it. \"Lattimo. Go on, then.\"\n\nMatteo sat. He took out his own paper and ink, and uncapped the ink, and turned the leaves, slowly, as a notary does, looking for the place, and the boy stood at his shoulder and watched every leaf turn.\n\nThat was the moment, and they had known it would be. Sanudo had to look away. They had argued about it for an hour in the friar's cell and for another hour at the marver with the glory-hole roaring, and in the end it was the girl who had said, quite calmly, turning a bobbin over in her fingers: Nobody can watch a book when a girl drops forty bobbins on a stone floor. Not even a priest. I've seen it.\n\nMatteo found the lattimo. He dipped his pen.\n\nAnd Lucia Zen, standing behind him with her basket, put her hand into her apron for her handkerchief, and brought out the little bag of bobbins instead, and somehow, as she did so, the drawstring came undone.\n\nThey went everywhere. Forty bobbins of boxwood and bone on a floor of red and white marble, bouncing and rolling, clattering like hail on a roof, under the desks, under the press, into the corners, a sound that went on and on as though it would never stop; and the girl gave a cry of real distress, and went down on her knees with her basket, and Sanudo, who was twenty and kind and had a mistress at San Polo and would be a good husband to someone one day, said \"Oh, God, wait, let me,\" and went down on his knees too.\n\nIt took them, afterwards, Matteo calculated, about as long as a Paternoster and two Aves to pick them all up. The girl kept finding more, and holding them up, and thanking him. Sanudo crawled under the press and came out with dust on his hat.\n\nWhen they stood up, Matteo was copying the receipt for lattimo, very neatly, in his best hand, and the book was open on the desk beside him at the right page, and the girl's basket was on her arm, and her pillow was on top of it, with the half-made border pinned to it, and the bobbins hanging down.\n\nIt was heavier on the left now, Matteo thought, watching her settle it; she had to brace her hip. It was not heavier. It was precisely the same weight. He had weighed them both, on the friar's scales, the night before.\n\nHe sanded his copy, and folded it, and stood.\n\n\"My thanks, Sanudo. I'll tell His Excellency you were very helpful.\"\n\n\"Will you?\" The boy looked absurdly pleased. He shut the book, and lifted it, and put it back on the middle shelf of the press, by itself, and locked the lock like a fist, and hung the key on his belt, and patted it. \"There. Safe as the Mint.\"\n\n\"Safer,\" said Matteo.","wordCount":1293,"createdAt":1792361923241},{"id":"szUd5gKzSgNWXVNHLGi1E","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"ElASJa2Vxm6oNK1C3eGhQ","text":"[\n  Place: #The Doge's Palace\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Zuane Dolfin @@Orsato\n  When: +1 day\n  Items: ^The Glass Ledger:@Zuane Dolfin\n  Facts: ?The last leaf of the ledger is missing:@Zuane Dolfin ?The last leaf of the ledger is missing:@Orsato ?Bortolo found the lost calcedonio receipt:@Zuane Dolfin ?Dolfin will trade Nane for the leaf:@Isotta Ferro ?Dolfin will trade Nane for the leaf:reader\n]\n\nThe bora had come down off the mountains in the night and swept the sirocco out to sea, and the morning was so clear that from the Fondamenta dei Vetrai Isotta could see snow on the Alps, a white rind laid along the top of the world. The lagoon was the colour of a knife. Piero the boatman rowed her across without singing either, out of respect, and set her down at the Ponte della Paglia among the fishwives and the clerks.\n\nShe had dressed for it. The black wool, the good collar, the coral her mother had left her; not so much that she looked like a woman who could pay, not so little that she looked like a woman who could not. She had thought about it for an hour in the cold kitchen before dawn, and then she had thought that thinking about it was what the Palace wanted from people, that they should come to the door already half on their knees, and she had nearly gone in her apron out of spite.\n\nThe door of the Pozzi was a low arch at the bottom of a stair, with a guard on a stool beside it eating bread. She told him her name, and her son's name, and he went on eating bread and looked at her as though she were weather.\n\n\"Ferro,\" he said at last. \"From Murano.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Wait.\"\n\nShe waited. She waited long enough to watch the light move across the courtyard flagstones from one drain to the next. People went up and down the stair past her: a priest with a basket, a notary with a sheaf of papers under his chin, two women who were plainly also mothers, from the way they did not look at each other. Then the guard came back with someone else, and it was not a gaoler. It was Orsato, in his black cap, and he did not take her down. He took her up.\n\nUp the Scala dei Censori, up again, through a door so plain she understood it was important, along a passage panelled in dark wood where the clerks' pens made a sound like rain, and into a room no larger than her kitchen, with one window on the Rio di Palazzo and one man at a table under it.\n\n\"Madonna Ferro. Please. Sit.\" He rose for her. Nobody in the Palace had risen for her. \"Zuane Dolfin. I have the honour to be one of the secretaries of the Most Excellent Council. You will forgive the room. The great rooms are for great business, and this, I hope, is a small business, between ourselves.\"\n\nHe was perhaps fifty, grey at the temples, with a pleasant tired face and very clean hands. On the table in front of him, unwrapped from Orsato's oilcloth, lay Bortolo's book.\n\nShe sat because her knees had decided it.\n\n\"Your son is well,\" Dolfin said. \"I asked, before you came. He is in the third cell on the lower passage, which is not the best cell, but it is not the worst; it is dry except at the spring tides. He is fed. He has not been put to the question and I see no reason he should be.\" He let that lie a moment, the way a man lets a coin lie on the table. \"You know, of course, what the law says.\"\n\n\"I know what it says.\"\n\n\"Then you know I have been generous already.\" He touched the book with one finger, on the boards, not the pages, as though it might be warm. \"I will be plain with you, Madonna, because you seem to me a plain woman, and plainness saves time. The Guild of the Art has, as you know, its ears. One of those ears belongs to a man who carried wood for your husband last winter. He tells us that the furnace was lit at hours when no orders were being filled. He tells us that master Bortolo burned through four crucibles in February making something he would not let the boys see. He tells us that on the second Sunday of Lent your husband went to San Donato and paid for a Mass of thanksgiving, which he had never done in his life.\"\n\nIsotta said nothing.\n\n\"And here,\" said Dolfin, and now he did open the book, at the end, where Orsato had opened it, and turned it round so she could see the sliver of cut paper in the gutter, \"here is a leaf that is not here.\"\n\n\"He cut out his failures.\"\n\n\"So Orsato told me you said. I think it is a good answer. I think it is the answer I would give.\" He closed the book. \"Calcedonio, Madonna. That is what the Guild thinks. Glass like stone. They have not made it on this island since my grandfather's time and I do not need to tell you what the Republic would give to make it again, or what the Emperor would give, or the King of France, or a certain house in Antwerp that your son was so eager to visit.\"\n\n\"My son knows nothing about it.\"\n\n\"Then he has nothing to sell, and he will go to the galleys for trying to sell it, which seems to me a waste of a good pair of hands.\" Dolfin folded his own. \"On the Feast of the Sensa the Most Excellent Council will sit after the Doge returns from the Lido, as it does every year, and it will pass sentence on those subjects of the Republic who were taken trying to carry the Art abroad. There are three this year. Your son is one. I would like him not to be. Bring me the leaf before the Sensa, and he walks out of the Pozzi on the Feast and goes home with you to Murano, and no one ever writes his name in a book again.\"\n\n\"And if there is no leaf?\"\n\n\"Then I am sorry for you.\" He meant it, she thought; that was the worst of him. He meant every word. \"Orsato will take you down now. You may have a little while at the grille. I would not tell him about our conversation. Boys of that age are proud, and pride is very expensive in the Pozzi.\"","wordCount":1113,"createdAt":1792360972542},{"id":"vlWT-_QgDKUEEtPIqX7iv","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"gnMam_XH4_nPQ3uIi2eK8","text":"[\n  Place: #The Pozzi\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Nane Ferro @@Orsato\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?Vos is the Fleming at the Rialto:@Isotta Ferro ?Vos is the Fleming at the Rialto:@Nane Ferro:before\n]\n\nThe passage under the Palace smelled of the sea and of what the sea left behind. Orsato carried a lamp and went ahead of her and did not speak, and she was grateful, because she did not think she could have answered. The walls sweated. Somewhere water was running, not loud, a thread of it, and the floor under her shoes was soft with something she did not look at.\n\nThe third cell on the lower passage had a door of black oak with a square cut in it at the height of a man's face, and across the square three iron bars, and behind the bars, when Orsato lifted the lamp, her son.\n\nHe had grown. That was her first stupid thought. He had grown in the eleven days since the funeral; or else she had never looked at him properly, or else the cell made him tall by being so low. His hair was flat with damp. He had a blanket round his shoulders like an old woman at a fire.\n\n\"Mamma.\"\n\n\"Don't,\" she said, because his voice had cracked on it, and if he cried she would, and Orsato was standing four paces off with his lamp and his patience. \"Don't. Are you hurt?\"\n\n\"No.\" He put his fingers through the bars, the burnt ones. She took them. They were cold as the canal. \"They haven't done anything. They ask me things. A man comes with a book and asks me what my father put in the batch for lattimo, and I tell him, because everyone in Murano knows what goes in lattimo, and he writes it down very slowly and goes away.\"\n\n\"Nane, why?\"\n\nHe looked at her, and for a moment he was seven again, holding out his blistered hand and asking if it would make him a master.\n\n\"Because they were going to take the book,\" he said. \"Because the Guild had already been to see you, I saw them, I saw old Briati in the yard with his hat in his hands looking at our furnace like a man looking at a widow. Because I'm not going to blow beads for them for thirty years while some secretary keeps Babbo's book in a chest and sells it a page at a time.\" He lowered his voice. \"There's a Fleming. Vos. He's at the Rialto. He said if I came to Antwerp with what was in my head he'd give me a furnace of my own. A furnace, Mamma. Not a bench in someone else's.\"\n\n\"And what was in your head?\"\n\n\"Everything I'd seen.\" His fingers tightened. \"Not the last thing. Babbo never let me see the last thing. But Vos didn't know that.\"\n\nSo he had meant to cheat the Fleming too. He was his father's son. She closed her eyes for a moment.\n\n\"Who else did you tell?\"\n\n\"Nobody. Lucia. Only Lucia.\" He tried to smile. \"She told me I was an idiot.\"\n\n\"She was right.\"\n\n\"Time,\" Orsato said, not unkindly.\n\nNane held on. \"Mamma, the man with the book said something. He said if you were sensible I'd be home for the Sensa. What does that mean? What does he want from you?\"\n\nShe looked at her son through the bars, at his flat wet hair and the old woman's blanket and the hand that had been burnt into the shape of a master's hand, and she thought of Dolfin saying pride is very expensive in the Pozzi.\n\n\"It means he is a liar,\" she said, \"like all of them. Eat what they give you. Keep the blanket dry. Say your prayers and say them out loud, so the guard hears you and thinks you're a good boy. I'll come back.\"\n\n\"When?\"\n\n\"When I can.\"\n\nOrsato was already lowering the lamp. In the last of its light she saw her son's face go back into the dark of the cell, not all at once but by parts, the eyes last, and then there was only the square in the door and the three bars and the sound of the water running somewhere it should not be.\n\nOn the stair, in the daylight again, she had to stop and put her hand against the wall. Orsato stopped too, a step above, and waited, and looked out over the courtyard where the light had moved on to the next drain.\n\n\"He is right,\" he said, to nobody. \"It is a waste of good hands.\"\n\nThen he took her back to the Ponte della Paglia, and handed her down into Piero's boat, and lifted his black cap to her, once, which she would remember a long time afterwards, when she had reason to wonder what side he was on.","wordCount":816,"createdAt":1792360972542},{"id":"xI2Mn1t833Qb9FuxmoZPM","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"zIrHkBr1jO8kZ1DDBUd9J","text":"Lucia came across from Burano at dusk, rowing herself, which she was not supposed to do.\n\nIsotta heard the oars from the kitchen and knew them, because they were bad. Lucia rowed the way she did everything that was not lace, which was impatiently and with too much shoulder, and the little sandolo slapped the water like a hand slapping a table. By the time Isotta reached the step, her niece was already tying up, crooked, to the ring that had been meant for the sand barges.\n\n\"You'll have your father after you.\"\n\n\"My father is in Chioggia until Sunday, and my mother thinks I am at the Ursulines learning the punto in aria from Suor Benedetta, who is ninety and asleep.\" Lucia came up the step two at a time. She was nineteen and small and her hands were never still; even standing on the step she was turning the end of the painter over and over in her fingers. \"Zia. Have they come?\"\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Don't. Don't do that, you do that to the Guild men. Have the Ten come?\"\n\n\"This morning.\" Isotta stood aside to let her in, as she had stood aside for Orsato. \"They took the book.\"\n\n\"Only the book?\"\n\n\"What else is there?\"\n\nLucia stopped in the passage. The lamp in the kitchen behind Isotta threw both their shadows up the wall, long and wavering, and Isotta saw in her niece's face that she had come to say something and had been saying it to herself all the way across the lagoon and now did not know how to begin.\n\n\"Sit down,\" Isotta said.\n\n\"I won't. Zia, it's Nane.\"\n\nThe kitchen was warm, at least. She had lit the small fire for the soup, and the sirocco, which made every other room in the house sweat, made the kitchen merely close. Lucia would not sit, so Isotta did, on the bench by the table, and folded her hands, and waited.\n\n\"Piero the boatman was at the Riva degli Schiavoni on Tuesday,\" Lucia said. \"With a load for the Arsenal. There was a Flemish ship at the moorings, the Sint Joris, out of Antwerp, loading oil and currants. And the fanti came along the Riva at first light, eight of them, with a secretary, and they went aboard, and they brought off a boy. A tall boy with a burnt hand. Piero says he did not fight. Piero says he said his name when they asked it, very clearly, as though he wanted it written down right.\"\n\nIsotta looked at her hands.\n\nThe left one had the same burn, the old shiny pucker across the base of the thumb where every glassmaker's child learned, once, that a pipe fresh out of the glory-hole is hot along its whole length and not only at the end. Nane had got his at seven. He had not cried. He had looked at it with great interest and then at her, and asked if it would make him a master.\n\n\"He was going to Antwerp,\" she said.\n\n\"Zia, it's the law. A glassmaker who leaves the Republic—\"\n\n\"I know the law.\"\n\n\"—they can send someone after him. They can kill him in Antwerp if they like and nobody will say a word. And if he is caught before he goes—\"\n\n\"I know the law, Lucia. I was married to it.\" She heard her own voice, flat as a marver, and was ashamed of it. Lucia was crying now, quietly, without stopping turning the rope's end in her fingers, which she had carried in from the boat without noticing. \"He never told me. He never said one word.\"\n\n\"He told me,\" Lucia said.\n\nIsotta looked up.\n\n\"Not when. Not the ship. Only that he would not stay and blow beads for the Guild while they kept his father's book in a chest. He said the Ten would take the book the moment Zio Bortolo died, because Zio Bortolo had found something, and the Guild knew he had found something, and the Ten always know what the Guild knows.\" She wiped her face with the back of her wrist. \"He said there was a Fleming who would pay. For the book. Or for him, if he had the book in his head. He said you'd be better off without him to feed.\"\n\n\"When did he say this?\"\n\n\"At the funeral. In the cloister, while the Guild men were talking to you.\"\n\nSo he had known, at the funeral, with his father's body still on the bier, that the Ten would come. He had known about the Fleming. He had known, or guessed, about the last winter's work, and he had said nothing to her and everything to his cousin, because his cousin was nineteen and made lace and could not stop him.\n\nIsotta put her hand flat on her bodice, where the paper was.\n\n\"Is he in the Pozzi?\"\n\n\"Piero thinks so. They took him to the Palace by the Ponte della Paglia, not to the Arsenal.\"\n\n\"Then tomorrow,\" Isotta said, \"I am going to the Palace.\"\n\n\"They won't let you see him.\"\n\n\"Orsato said a mother should ask.\" She stood. The soup had boiled down to a brown sediment at the bottom of the pot and she lifted it off the fire and set it on the stone and stood looking at it. \"Orsato knew this morning. He knew when he took the book. He said the son is not at present in a position to swear. I thought he meant Nane was a child.\"\n\nLucia said nothing.\n\n\"You'll stay tonight. I'll take you back at first light and you can tell your mother Suor Benedetta kept you for vespers.\"\n\n\"Suor Benedetta has not kept anyone for vespers since the war of Cyprus.\"\n\n\"Then tell her something better. You're the one with the clever hands.\" Isotta reached up and took the rope's end gently out of her niece's fingers and laid it on the table. \"And Lucia. Whatever Nane told you, about the Fleming and the book. You will not tell anyone else.\"\n\n\"Who would I tell?\"\n\n\"Anyone who asks,\" Isotta said. \"That is who people tell.\"\n\nOutside, the wind had dropped at last. In the sudden quiet she could hear the water lapping the step, and the dog down the canal had stopped barking, and somewhere over toward the Fondamenta dei Vetrai a furnace that was still lit was being stoked for the night shift: the deep exhalation of the bellows, and a man's voice calling for more wood, and the small sharp ring of glass touching glass.\n\n# The Secretary\n\n## The door of the Pozzi\n[\n  Place: #The Doge's Palace\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Zuane Dolfin @@Orsato\n  When: +1 day\n  Items: ^The Glass Ledger:@Zuane Dolfin\n  Facts: ?The last leaf of the ledger is missing:@Zuane Dolfin ?The last leaf of the ledger is missing:@Orsato ?Bortolo found the lost calcedonio receipt:@Zuane Dolfin ?Dolfin will trade Nane for the leaf:@Isotta Ferro ?Dolfin will trade Nane for the leaf:reader\n]\n\nThe bora had come down off the mountains in the night and swept the sirocco out to sea, and the morning was so clear that from the Fondamenta dei Vetrai Isotta could see snow on the Alps, a white rind laid along the top of the world. The lagoon was the colour of a knife. Piero the boatman rowed her across without singing either, out of respect, and set her down at the Ponte della Paglia among the fishwives and the clerks.\n\nShe had dressed for it. The black wool, the good collar, the coral her mother had left her; not so much that she looked like a woman who could pay, not so little that she looked like a woman who could not. She had thought about it for an hour in the cold kitchen before dawn, and then she had thought that thinking about it was what the Palace wanted from people, that they should come to the door already half on their knees, and she had nearly gone in her apron out of spite.\n\nThe door of the Pozzi was a low arch at the bottom of a stair, with a guard on a stool beside it eating bread. She told him her name, and her son's name, and he went on eating bread and looked at her as though she were weather.\n\n\"Ferro,\" he said at last. \"From Murano.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Wait.\"\n\nShe waited. She waited long enough to watch the light move across the courtyard flagstones from one drain to the next. People went up and down the stair past her: a priest with a basket, a notary with a sheaf of papers under his chin, two women who were plainly also mothers, from the way they did not look at each other. Then the guard came back with someone else, and it was not a gaoler. It was Orsato, in his black cap, and he did not take her down. He took her up.\n\nUp the Scala dei Censori, up again, through a door so plain she understood it was important, along a passage panelled in dark wood where the clerks' pens made a sound like rain, and into a room no larger than her kitchen, with one window on the Rio di Palazzo and one man at a table under it.\n\n\"Madonna Ferro. Please. Sit.\" He rose for her. Nobody in the Palace had risen for her. \"Zuane Dolfin. I have the honour to be one of the secretaries of the Most Excellent Council. You will forgive the room. The great rooms are for great business, and this, I hope, is a small business, between ourselves.\"\n\nHe was perhaps fifty, grey at the temples, with a pleasant tired face and very clean hands. On the table in front of him, unwrapped from Orsato's oilcloth, lay Bortolo's book.\n\nShe sat because her knees had decided it.\n\n\"Your son is well,\" Dolfin said. \"I asked, before you came. He is in the third cell on the lower passage, which is not the best cell, but it is not the worst; it is dry except at the spring tides. He is fed. He has not been put to the question and I see no reason he should be.\" He let that lie a moment, the way a man lets a coin lie on the table. \"You know, of course, what the law says.\"\n\n\"I know what it says.\"\n\n\"Then you know I have been generous already.\" He touched the book with one finger, on the boards, not the pages, as though it might be warm. \"I will be plain with you, Madonna, because you seem to me a plain woman, and plainness saves time. The Guild of the Art has, as you know, its ears. One of those ears belongs to a man who carried wood for your husband last winter. He tells us that the furnace was lit at hours when no orders were being filled. He tells us that master Bortolo burned through four crucibles in February making something he would not let the boys see. He tells us that on the second Sunday of Lent your husband went to San Donato and paid for a Mass of thanksgiving, which he had never done in his life.\"\n\nIsotta said nothing.\n\n\"And here,\" said Dolfin, and now he did open the book, at the end, where Orsato had opened it, and turned it round so she could see the sliver of cut paper in the gutter, \"here is a leaf that is not here.\"\n\n\"He cut out his failures.\"\n\n\"So Orsato told me you said. I think it is a good answer. I think it is the answer I would give.\" He closed the book. \"Calcedonio, Madonna. That is what the Guild thinks. Glass like stone. They have not made it on this island since my grandfather's time and I do not need to tell you what the Republic would give to make it again, or what the Emperor would give, or the King of France, or a certain house in Antwerp that your son was so eager to visit.\"\n\n\"My son knows nothing about it.\"\n\n\"Then he has nothing to sell, and he will go to the galleys for trying to sell it, which seems to me a waste of a good pair of hands.\" Dolfin folded his own. \"On the Feast of the Sensa the Most Excellent Council will sit after the Doge returns from the Lido, as it does every year, and it will pass sentence on those subjects of the Republic who were taken trying to carry the Art abroad. There are three this year. Your son is one. I would like him not to be. Bring me the leaf before the Sensa, and he walks out of the Pozzi on the Feast and goes home with you to Murano, and no one ever writes his name in a book again.\"\n\n\"And if there is no leaf?\"\n\n\"Then I am sorry for you.\" He meant it, she thought; that was the worst of him. He meant every word. \"Orsato will take you down now. You may have a little while at the grille. I would not tell him about our conversation. Boys of that age are proud, and pride is very expensive in the Pozzi.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Pozzi\n  Characters: @@Isotta Ferro @@Nane Ferro @@Orsato\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?Vos is the Fleming at the Rialto:@Isotta Ferro ?Vos is the Fleming at the Rialto:@Nane Ferro:before\n]\n\nThe passage under the Palace smelled of the sea and of what the sea left behind. Orsato carried a lamp and went ahead of her and did not speak, and she was grateful, because she did not think she could have answered. The walls sweated. Somewhere water was running, not loud, a thread of it, and the floor under her shoes was soft with something she did not look at.\n\nThe third cell on the lower passage had a door of black oak with a square cut in it at the height of a man's face, and across the square three iron bars, and behind the bars, when Orsato lifted the lamp, her son.\n\nHe had grown. That was her first stupid thought. He had grown in the eleven days since the funeral; or else she had never looked at him properly, or else the cell made him tall by being so low. His hair was flat with damp. He had a blanket round his shoulders like an old woman at a fire.\n\n\"Mamma.\"\n\n\"Don't,\" she said, because his voice had cracked on it, and if he cried she would, and Orsato was standing four paces off with his lamp and his patience. \"Don't. Are you hurt?\"\n\n\"No.\" He put his fingers through the bars, the burnt ones. She took them. They were cold as the canal. \"They haven't done anything. They ask me things. A man comes with a book and asks me what my father put in the batch for lattimo, and I tell him, because everyone in Murano knows what goes in lattimo, and he writes it down very slowly and goes away.\"\n\n\"Nane, why?\"\n\nHe looked at her, and for a moment he was seven again, holding out his blistered hand and asking if it would make him a master.\n\n\"Because they were going to take the book,\" he said. \"Because the Guild had already been to see you, I saw them, I saw old Briati in the yard with his hat in his hands looking at our furnace like a man looking at a widow. Because I'm not going to blow beads for them for thirty years while some secretary keeps Babbo's book in a chest and sells it a page at a time.\" He lowered his voice. \"There's a Fleming. Vos. He's at the Rialto. He said if I came to Antwerp with what was in my head he'd give me a furnace of my own. A furnace, Mamma. Not a bench in someone else's.\"\n\n\"And what was in your head?\"\n\n\"Everything I'd seen.\" His fingers tightened. \"Not the last thing. Babbo never let me see the last thing. But Vos didn't know that.\"\n\nSo he had meant to cheat the Fleming too. He was his father's son. She closed her eyes for a moment.\n\n\"Who else did you tell?\"\n\n\"Nobody. Lucia. Only Lucia.\" He tried to smile. \"She told me I was an idiot.\"\n\n\"She was right.\"\n\n\"Time,\" Orsato said, not unkindly.\n\nNane held on. \"Mamma, the man with the book said something. He said if you were sensible I'd be home for the Sensa. What does that mean? What does he want from you?\"\n\nShe looked at her son through the bars, at his flat wet hair and the old woman's blanket and the hand that had been burnt into the shape of a master's hand, and she thought of Dolfin saying pride is very expensive in the Pozzi.\n\n\"It means he is a liar,\" she said, \"like all of them. Eat what they give you. Keep the blanket dry. Say your prayers and say them out loud, so the guard hears you and thinks you're a good boy. I'll come back.\"\n\n\"When?\"\n\n\"When I can.\"\n\nOrsato was already lowering the lamp. In the last of its light she saw her son's face go back into the dark of the cell, not all at once but by parts, the eyes last, and then there was only the square in the door and the three bars and the sound of the water running somewhere it should not be.\n\nOn the stair, in the daylight again, she had to stop and put her hand against the wall. Orsato stopped too, a step above, and waited, and looked out over the courtyard where the light had moved on to the next drain.\n\n\"He is right,\" he said, to nobody. \"It is a waste of good hands.\"\n\nThen he took her back to the Ponte della Paglia, and handed her down into Piero's boat, and lifted his black cap to her, once, which she would remember a long time afterwards, when she had reason to wonder what side he was on.","wordCount":3036,"createdAt":1792360970797},{"id":"zRFHoM07Y3pk-aXHJwlxy","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"3Ca9TRPohYxSK0z4dLlo_","text":"[\n  Place: #The Zen house on Burano\n  Characters: @@Lucia Zen @@Isotta Ferro\n  When: +3 days\n  Items: ^The calcedonio leaf:destroyed ^The calcedonio lace:@Lucia Zen\n  Facts: ?The calcedonio leaf has been burned:@Isotta Ferro ?The calcedonio leaf has been burned:@Lucia Zen\n]\n\nIt took her four.\n\nNot because the numbers were hard. The numbers were the easy part; she made a key on the first night, by candle, on the back of an old pricking: a twist for an ounce, a crossing for a pound, a pin for an hour, a picot for each heat, and the four-petalled flower Suor Benedetta called the Sant'Andrea for the turn of a line. It was the hiding that was hard. A pattern that was only numbers looked like numbers: it had a stiffness to it, a lack of grace, and anyone who had ever made lace would see at a glance that it had been made by someone who was thinking about something else. So she had to make it beautiful. She had to make the zafre into a vine and the copper into a pomegranate and NOT TO STIR into a little border of knots that ran round the whole thing like a fence round a garden, and she had to make it so that the vine and the pomegranate and the fence were the reason for the collar and the numbers were only how many stitches it happened to need.\n\nOn the fourth morning there was no fog. The sun came in flat and gold over the lagoon and lit up the window like a lamp, and she unpinned the collar from the pillow and laid it on the black cloth and sent the boy from next door to Murano for her aunt.\n\nZia Isotta looked at it a long time without touching it.\n\nIt was a small thing, a woman's collar, no wider than two hands. A vine ran round it, heavy with fruit. In the corners there were flowers. Along the edge there was a border of tiny knots, and here and there among the leaves a bird, because Lucia had needed somewhere to put the second heat and a bird had seemed right.\n\n\"Read it to me,\" her aunt said.\n\nSo Lucia read it. She put her finger on the first leaf of the vine and counted the twists aloud and said \"four pounds of ash of Levant,\" and moved along the vine and said \"six of the sand, washed thrice,\" and went round the collar as if it were a clock, through the copper and the silver and the hours and the heats, through the birds, until she came to the border of knots and said, \"and not to stir.\"\n\nWhen she looked up her aunt was crying. Not loudly. The way a pot weeps on the outside when it is very cold and the room is warm.\n\n\"He would have hated it,\" Isotta said. \"He would have said lace is for nuns.\" She wiped her face with the heel of her hand. \"Give me the leaf.\"\n\nThey burned it in the kitchen fire with Lucia's mother at Mass. Her aunt held it by one corner over the coals and it went brown, then black, then a curl of bright orange ran along the edge and for a moment, in the last of the paper, Lucia saw the numbers stand out white, every one of them, as if the fire were reading them too. Then it was ash, and her aunt crumbled the ash in her fingers and dropped it into the coals and dusted her hands, and that was the end of the last leaf of the Glass Ledger.\n\n\"Now,\" Isotta said. \"Now it's in two places. Your hands and mine. And nobody looks at a girl's collar.\"\n\n\"The Grimani will look at it.\"\n\n\"The Grimani will never see it. Make them another.\" Her aunt folded the collar into a square of linen and put it into Lucia's hands and closed Lucia's fingers over it. \"Keep it in your work-basket, under the bobbins, the way you'd keep anything you hadn't finished. Don't hide it. Hidden things get found.\"\n\n\"Zia.\" Lucia held the little bundle. It weighed nothing. \"What are you going to do? About Nane?\"\n\nHer aunt was quiet for a moment, looking at the fire.\n\n\"I'm going to steal your uncle's book back from the Council of Ten,\" she said, \"on the Feast of the Sensa, while the Doge is marrying the sea. With a notary who drinks and a friar who forges seals. I am going to sell it to a Fleming. And then I am going to buy your cousin out of the Pozzi and take him a very long way away.\"\n\nLucia laughed, because it was so absurd. Then she saw her aunt's face and stopped.\n\n\"Can I come?\" she said.","wordCount":806,"createdAt":1792361179903},{"id":"zfDnLyveBmWjF1Dpc4ZVE","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","eventId":"FCcx9EVdPWwkA6032mhg0","text":"She lit the furnace for him.\n\nNot the great furnace; there was not wood enough on the island for that, and no reason. The small one, the glory-hole by the bench, where Bortolo had warmed the pieces between gathers; she lit it at dusk with the kindling and then the beech, and fed it until the mouth of it stood open on a round of white-orange light that hurt to look at, and the workshop, which had been cold for five weeks, began to tick and creak around her as the walls remembered what they were for.\n\nWhen Matteo Lion came up the step from his boat she was standing at the bench with a blowpipe in the glory-hole, turning it slowly, as though there were glass on the end of it. There was no glass. There was only the iron, going red, then orange.\n\n\"Madonna.\" He stopped in the water-door. The heat met him there; she saw it in his face. \"You've lit the fire.\"\n\n\"Come in, Messer Lion. Shut the door. The draught's bad for the glass.\"\n\nHe shut it. He came across the floor, and stopped at the end of the bench, where the light from the hole lay over him and made him look younger, or else older, it was hard to tell. He had a roll of paper under his arm. The way, she supposed. He laid it on the marver.\n\n\"The friar's not here?\" he said.\n\n\"The friar's in the lehr-room with my niece. They're listening.\" She turned the pipe. \"I wanted them to hear what you say. In case I have to tell it afterwards to someone, and they ask for witnesses.\"\n\nHe did not move. She watched him not move; she watched him understand, and add it up, and arrive at the figure. It did not take him long. He had always been quick.\n\n\"Orsato,\" he said.\n\n\"Orsato. At the Fleming's. On Sunday last. Talking about gold on a table and the Capi at the door, and a widow taken with the book in her hands.\" She drew the pipe out of the hole and held it up between them. The last hand's length of it was the colour of an apricot. \"Four people knew the day, Messer Lion. I didn't tell Dolfin. Lucia didn't tell Dolfin. Fra Anselmo didn't tell Dolfin, though he's the only one of you I can't prove it of.\"\n\n\"It wasn't the friar.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe pipe hissed faintly in the air between them. Neither of them looked at it.\n\n\"Four years,\" he said. \"Since they struck me off. He came to me the week after, at the Greek's, and bought me a drink, and paid my rent, and asked me to tell him now and then what I heard. In the malvasie. In the courts. Who was in debt, who was in love, who was leaving the city. I told myself it was nothing. I told myself I was a notary still, taking depositions. That's what I told myself.\" He looked at the glowing iron. \"I'd tell you I'm sorry, but you wouldn't believe me, and you'd be right.\"\n\n\"I don't want you to be sorry. I want to know what you're going to do.\"\n\nHe put his hand in his pocket, then, and she lifted the pipe an inch, and he stopped, and slowly drew the hand out and opened it on the marver, in the light.\n\nThere were three things on his palm. Her mother's coral, the little red branch. A letter, folded very small, in a way she had seen once, on Dolfin's table, with its seal lifted cleanly and pressed back. And a bead.\n\nShe knew the bead before she knew she knew it. White, lattimo, round as a pea, with a single thread of blue wound once about it. She had sent back eleven of them, the spring he was fourteen, because they were not round, or the blue had run, or the white was not white enough. The twelfth she had kept. Or she had thought she had. It was on the shelf by San Donato.\n\nNo. It was not. It was here.\n\n\"He said to tell you he's keeping the blanket dry,\" Matteo Lion said. \"He has the gaol fever. Not the worst kind, I think; I've seen the worst kind. And Dolfin told me on Thursday, at the foot of the Scala d'Oro, that when you've been taken at the Fleming's and given him the leaf, the Council will send your son to the galleys regardless, and Dolfin will speak very movingly for clemency, and lose.\" He pushed the letter towards her with one finger. \"That's to the Fleming. In Dolfin's own hand. He gave it me to carry and I didn't carry it. It promises Vos the receipt, a fair copy, when you've given it up. If the Capi saw it, they'd know what he is. A secretary of the Ten selling the secrets of the Art to Antwerp for gold.\"\n\nIsotta put the pipe back in the glory-hole. Her hand was not steady and she did not want him to see it.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\nHe thought about it. He actually thought, standing there with the heat on his face; she watched him do it, and somehow that was what she believed, in the end, more than anything he said.\n\n\"Because your son told me he'd kill me with the hot end of a pipe if I got you killed,\" he said. \"And I believed him. And I found, when I thought about it, that I'd very much rather he didn't have to.\"\n\nFrom the lehr-room door, behind her, there came a small sound, as of an old man trying not to laugh.\n\n\"Come out, Brother,\" said Isotta, without turning round. \"Lucia. Bring the lamp.\" She picked up the bead and closed her hand on it, so hard she felt the little thread of blue against her palm, and looked at the notary across the marver. \"Sit down, Messer Lion. You can sit down here. We're going to steal my husband's book on Thursday exactly as you planned it. Up the Censori, with the friar's warrant, and out in my niece's pillow. And when the boy Sanudo shuts the press behind you, there will still be a book in it.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #Santa Maria dei Servi\n  Characters: @@Lucia Zen @@Fra Anselmo @@Matteo Lion\n  When: +2 days\n  Items: ^The false ledger:@Fra Anselmo\n]\n\nOn the third night Lucia carried the last of the weights across the city in her bodice, as her aunt had once carried the leaf, and found the friar's cell lit like a chapel.\n\nThere were candles everywhere. On the sill, on the floor, on the lid of the chest at the foot of the bed, stuck in their own wax on the corner of the board under the window, so many that the little room was warm as a kitchen and smelled of honey and hot tallow and, under that, of the ink, which Fra Anselmo made himself from oak-galls and copperas and something he would not name, and which came out of the pot brown, exactly the brown of her uncle's hand. The friar sat at the board in the middle of it all in his shirt, with his sleeves tied back and his spectacles on the end of his nose and his bad eye shut, writing.\n\nMatteo Lion was sitting on the bed with a pair of scales on his knees.\n\n\"Ah,\" said the friar without looking up. \"The weights. Good. Give them to the notary. He reads your aunt's hand better than I do. I think she writes it badly on purpose.\"\n\nLucia gave them. Matteo unfolded the paper and held it to the nearest candle, and she watched his lips move as he read, and saw him smile, once, at a line near the bottom.\n\n\"Twelve ounces of the zafre,\" he said. \"Twelve. In a batch that size. She means it to come out the colour of a bruise.\"\n\n\"She means it to crack in the lehr,\" said Lucia. \"She said a bruise was too good for them.\"\n\nOn the board in front of the friar lay the false ledger, open. She had not seen it before. She had imagined something rough, a thing made in a hurry, and it was not; it was her uncle's book. Not like it. It. The boards were the boards, swollen at the lower edge, because Fra Anselmo had soaked them in the canal and dried them in the bread-oven at the convent bakery. The calf was rubbed at the spine where a thumb would rub it. One of the brass corners was gone, the bottom left, and where it had been the leather was darker, the way leather is darker where it has been covered for twenty years. She put her hand out to touch it and stopped.\n\n\"Go on,\" said the friar. \"Touch it. If it doesn't fool you it won't fool the Ten.\"\n\nShe touched it. It was her uncle's book. She took her hand away quickly, as if it were hot.\n\n\"The first gathering is done,\" Anselmo said, writing. \"Your great-grandfather's green for bottles, and the cristallo, and the lattimo, all true, every grain, because a man who opens a book at the front reads the first page with care and nothing after it. The last gathering is done. The middle—\" he waved his pen \"—is accounts. Sand bought, ash bought, wages, the price of beech in the winter of the plague. Your aunt kept your uncle's day-books. I have copied two years of them. Nobody alive could read them without falling asleep.\" He blotted a line. \"And this is the last leaf.\"\n\nHe lifted it from under the horn: a single sheet of old rag paper, browned at the edges, with eleven lines of figures in the cramped brown hand, and at the top, underlined twice, CALCEDONIO.\n\n\"Your aunt's weights,\" he said, \"in your uncle's hand, on paper from the back of a missal printed in the year your uncle was born. It wants sewing in. To the stub. Where the knife went.\" He looked at her over the spectacles with his good eye. \"I can cut a seal. I can write any hand in the Republic. I cannot sew to save my soul.\"\n\nSo that was why she had been sent with the weights, and not Piero.\n\nShe sat on the stool he gave up to her and opened her own little case of needles and found the finest, the one she used for the picots on the Grimani's border, and a thread of unbleached linen she waxed with the end of a candle. The stub in the gutter of the false book was there, a sliver of cut paper no wider than a fingernail; the friar had cut it himself, with Bortolo's own knife, which her aunt had given him, so that it would be slightly ragged at the bottom where a hand had slipped. Lucia laid the leaf against it. She made her stitches through the old holes of the gathering, under the stub, so that from the outside they could not be seen at all, and drew the thread so gently that the paper did not pucker, and tied it off inside the fold with a knot so small it was only a thickening of the thread.\n\nWhen she sat back, the two men were watching her.\n\n\"Again,\" said Matteo softly. \"I didn't see where you began.\"\n\n\"That's the point,\" said Lucia.\n\nThey weighed the books after that, the false one on one pan and on the other a block of cut stone Fra Anselmo had brought up from the cloister, which Matteo said was the weight of the real book to within an ounce, because he had held the real book in his hands in the old days and a notary's hands remember weights the way a glassmaker's remember heat. The false book was lighter. The friar tore out a leaf of the accounts and Matteo shook his head; he put a thin sheet of lead under the endpaper, as they used to do in the Chancery to keep the big registers from blowing open, and the pans hung level and trembled and were still.\n\nIt was past midnight. The candles had burned down into puddles. Lucia packed her needles away and stood, and found that Matteo Lion had stood too, and was holding the door for her, which nobody had ever done.\n\n\"I'll walk you to the Fondamente,\" he said. \"The ferry for Burano goes at the first bell. There are men by the Misericordia at this hour who'd be glad of a girl alone.\"\n\nShe went out past him into the dark of the dormitory. Halfway down the corridor she stopped and turned round.\n\n\"Messer Lion. Why did you change?\"\n\nHe was a long time answering. In the light of the friar's door behind him she could see him put his hand in his pocket and turn something over and over, the way she turned the end of a rope, the way she turned a bobbin.\n\n\"Your cousin told me something,\" he said at last. \"In the wells. I'll tell you what it was one day, when it's finished. If it's finished well.\"\n\n\"And if it isn't?\"\n\n\"Then,\" said Matteo Lion, \"you were right not to trust me.\" He held out his arm. \"Come along. You have to sleep. On Thursday you have to drop forty bobbins on a marble floor, and I'm told it's harder than it sounds.\"","wordCount":2267,"createdAt":1792364201582}],"tombstones":[{"id":"PJ6DzvNBrZ-APx0EfNaqV","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","entityType":"knowledgeReveal","entityId":"7POkdRtpOLZ66wkc8tg8W","version":1,"deviceId":"P8uTBLZqMsMg7sUDrigEa","deletedAt":1792362280639},{"id":"mdMUIi-PnPgwOcBbHhDfe","worldId":"U9mo43OyNFRbbaPDICLDY","entityType":"knowledgeReveal","entityId":"hgQ_QylDWKZfROtVYnMZG","version":1,"deviceId":"P8uTBLZqMsMg7sUDrigEa","deletedAt":1792365021466}],"blobs":[]}